tgs:Concept.TheSenecaEffect a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.clubofrome.org/publication/the-seneca-effect-2017/> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Seneca Effect"@en ;
    skos:definition "The observation that complex systems tend to grow slowly but collapse rapidly — named after the Roman philosopher Seneca's own observation that fortune grows slowly but ruin is swift. Developed by Ugo Bardi as a general model for why decline, once it starts, tends to be much faster than the growth that preceded it."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.UgoBardi ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.TheSenecaEffect ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical .

tgs:Concept.LandscapeOfPossibleFutures a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Landscape of Possible Futures"@en ;
    skos:definition "Futures pictured as a landscape of valleys and ridges, where the path depends on initial conditions and small pushes at the right time can change which valley we roll into."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Hagens' own application of the landscape metaphor (valleys, ridges, switchbacks, erosion) to civilizational futures — see tgs:Monologue.Frankly_145_Part3UphillFutures for the full framework. Adapts Conrad Waddington's epigenetic landscape (originally a model of cellular differentiation) to a different domain entirely — a genuine reframing, not a direct reuse, hence HagensConcept despite the explicit external inspiration."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.ConradWaddington .

tgs:Concept.PerpetualMulligans a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perpetual Mulligans"@en ;
    skos:definition "Hagens' own framing for how cheap, abundant fossil energy has functioned historically as an economic 'do-over' — letting mistakes, inefficiencies, and bad decisions get absorbed and corrected via sheer energy surplus rather than genuine adaptation, a buffer that thins as energy surplus declines."@en .

tgs:Concept.UnicornStrategies a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/151-protect-the-irreversible> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Unicorn Strategies"@en ;
    skos:definition "Hagens' term (Frankly_151) for deliberately focused commitments -- a single cause someone cares about 'with an intensity that organizes their entire life' -- pursued even though they matter much more in some composite futures than in others, rather than hedged across all of them. His examples: ocean conservation (actions that matter most in futures where the biosphere can still be protected; a shout-out to his friend DJ), nuclear disarmament (preserving a taboo that matters enormously in some worlds and less in others), and preserving an Indigenous language (something that cannot be rebuilt once lost, whatever future it is lost in). They make sense because humans 'are not interchangeable planning units' but beings with particular loves and callings: someone whose life's work is protecting wild salmon runs should not divert that energy to community resilience because a framework told them to hedge. For most people he suggests a mix: a base of robust actions plus one or two unicorn commitments."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Corrected 2026-10-13 against the Frankly_151 transcript. The earlier definition described unicorn strategies as actions robust across all four composite futures, which inverts Nate's meaning; actions that pay off in every future are tgs:Concept.RobustActions, and unicorn strategies are the deliberately focused, unhedged complement. Coda question from the same episode: 'Is there a unicorn commitment deep in you, something you love enough to focus on, even if the work is narrower?'"@en .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — RR-26 "Why Community Fails" bootstrap.
#############################################

tgs:Concept.Communing a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Communing"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nora Bateson's framing of 'communing' as the necessary precursor to 'community' — the practice of genuine mutual relating (giving before being owed, radical hospitality, tending to the whole as a way of tending to the self) that most attempts at community-building skip past by going straight to shared values, organizing, and logistics."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NoraBateson .

tgs:Concept.Symmathesy a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:source <https://journals.isss.org/index.php/proceedings59th/article/view/2720> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Symmathesy"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nora Bateson's 2015 coinage (Greek 'sym' + 'mathesis,' together + learning) for an entity composed through contextual mutual learning by interaction — a living system (a family, an ecosystem, a conversation) that is genuinely shaped by its own internal relationships and interdependencies, not just a collection of independently-acting parts."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NoraBateson ;
    skos:scopeNote "dcterms:source UPGRADED 2026-08-13 from a generic WordPress blog post to the real ISSS (International Society for the Systems Sciences) academic conference proceedings — a strictly more authoritative citation for this same 2015 coinage, surfaced via RR-26's own real linked show notes."@en .

tgs:Concept.Schismogenesis a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Schismogenesis"@en ;
    skos:definition "Relationships that escalate through their own interaction -- matching (an arms race) or opposite (dominance and submission) -- until something interrupts them."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Gregory Bateson's term (from his 1930s anthropological fieldwork) for a process in which relationships escalate through cumulative interaction — either symmetrically (both parties responding to each other's same behavior with more of the same, e.g. an arms race) or complementarily (parties responding with opposite behaviors that reinforce each other, e.g. dominance and submission), each pattern intensifying over time unless deliberately interrupted."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.GregoryBateson .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — Berman 5-interview batch (TGS_101).
#############################################

tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Maximum Power Principle"@en ;
    skos:definition "Self-organizing systems that capture and use the most energy per unit of time tend to prevail -- the energetic logic behind the growth of the superorganism."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Howard T. Odum's principle (building on Alfred Lotka's earlier work) that self-organizing systems tend to develop and prevail when they maximize their intake and useful transformation of energy, not merely their efficiency — a foundational idea in ecological energetics directly connected to EROI (Charles Hall, a notable Odum student, is a central figure in that literature too)."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.HowardOdum ;
    skos:scopeNote "Nate first explains it to Daniel in Part 1 (TGS_5) -- Daniel replies 'Please explain it' -- with aerial thermal images of a forest transect (the road hottest, old-growth redwoods coolest because they dissipate the most energy) and Kleiber's law. In Bend Not Break Part 2 (TGS_20) Nate defines it as organisms self-organizing to maximize power (energy per unit time), noting that Americans use about 100 times as much energy outside their bodies as inside; Daniel restates it for markets -- any positive return, energy or financial, will be exploited even as the maximum declines -- and generalizes it to agents maximizing returns on agency (tgs:Concept.HyperAgents, tgs:Concept.MaximizingReturnsOnPower). Added 2026-10-18."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — surfaced via TGS_10's own real linked show notes.
#############################################

tgs:Concept.KleibersLaw a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kleiber%27s_law> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Kleiber's Law"@en ;
    skos:definition "An organism's metabolic rate scales with roughly the 3/4 power of its mass; Nate uses it as an analogy for how economies scale with energy."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Max Kleiber's 1932 empirical finding that an organism's basal metabolic rate scales to roughly the 3/4 power of its body mass, not the 2/3 power a simple surface-area argument would predict — one of the few genuine quantitative laws in biology, still without a fully settled theoretical explanation, and used in this graph's own source material as a real biological analogy for economic/systemic energy-scaling arguments."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.MaxKleiber ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical .

