tgs:Concept.RadicalHospitality a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Radical Hospitality"@en ;
    skos:definition "A practice of genuine, unscripted welcome — extended both to others and to oneself — treated in this graph's source material as a necessary ingredient for real community, distinct from the transactional, role-bound interactions that ordinarily substitute for it. No single originator credited in the source material (etymological, not attributional, citations given for both words) — modeled as umbrella Concept, not thinkr:HagensConcept."@en .

tgs:Concept.Amonging a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.combining.life/> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Amonging"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nora Bateson's own neologism for the edge of the self as genuinely porous and relational rather than fixed — being fundamentally constituted 'among' one's relationships and context, not separately from them. Directly connects to both her book Combining and her newer book Belly (whose own publisher description uses the phrase 'the among-ing of all life') — not confidently attributable to one specific book over the other from the sources checked."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NoraBateson .

tgs:Concept.DunbarsNumber a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dunbar%27s_number> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dunbar's Number"@en ;
    skos:definition "The roughly 150 stable relationships a human brain can maintain, a limit set by our evolution in small bands -- and an argument for depth over breadth in community."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Robin Dunbar's proposed cognitive limit (roughly 150) on the number of people with whom one can maintain genuinely stable social relationships, derived from a correlation between primate brain/neocortex size and social group size — used in this graph's source material as evidence for prioritizing depth over breadth in relationships."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RobinDunbar ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — Concept.DarkTriad, previously discussed
# substantively across RR-19, RR-20, and RR-26 but never itself
# formally minted, closing a real gap surfaced while processing
# Frankly_85. Concept.LightTriad, Nate's own explicit antonym framing
# for it, surfaced via Frankly_85 directly.
#
# ARCHITECTURAL RESOLUTION, same day: this is the first real case
# testing whether thinkr:AlternateTermType.Antonym (built 2026-08-13,
# earlier this session, flagged then as unresolved and NEVER actually
# used on any real individual since) or the already-existing
# thinkr:contrastsWith is the right mechanism for a genuine antonym.
# RESOLVED: contrastsWith wins, clearly — Light Triad and Dark Triad
# are NOT two names for the same referent (which is what AlternateTerm
# is for, e.g. EROI/"Energy Return on Investment"); they are two
# DISTINCT, separately-defined constructs that stand in opposition.
# Confirmed independently by the very paper that established this
# contrast: Kaufman et al.'s own 2019 title is literally "Contrasting
# Two Very Different Profiles of Human Nature." contrastsWith's own
# existing definition ("useful tension... does not imply influence in
# either direction") fits this case exactly. See
# thinkr:AlternateTermType.Antonym's own scopeNote for the formal
# resolution note.
#############################################

tgs:Concept.DarkTriad a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0191886902001459> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dark Triad"@en ;
    skos:definition "Narcissism, Machiavellianism and psychopathy together; Nate estimates about 10% of people have these traits, far more among those who rise to the top of institutions, which shapes how power behaves."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "A cluster of three socially aversive personality traits — narcissism, Machiavellianism, and psychopathy — originally proposed by Delroy Paulhus and Kevin Williams (2002). A recurring analytical lens across this graph's source material for understanding how individuals with these traits gain disproportionate influence over institutions and culture, distinct from mere individual pathology."@en ;
    thinkr:contrastsWith tgs:Concept.LightTriad ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    skos:scopeNote "Nate's own figure, first stated in the graph in Frankly_53 (January 2024): about 10 percent of people have dark-triad traits, probably more because such people answer surveys strategically, and far more among senior officials and CEOs -- 'the gauntlet to run requires those traits to get to the top.' He cites it again to Daniel Schmachtenberger in TGS_126 (tgs:Concept.PowerSeekingAndComplicity). Related: tgs:Concept.TraitsOutcompeteValues (Frankly_85), tgs:Concept.HavesAndHaveNots. In Frankly_78 (November 2024) he adds that the share of dark-triad people will be no larger at the bend-versus-break moment than now, but they will be 'out in the open' rather than hidden (tgs:Concept.ThreeTimelinesOfResponse)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.LightTriad a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:source <https://doaj.org/article/6a8567c65a0a46cbb579e2a93b07acd0> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Light Triad"@en ;
    skos:definition "Scott Barry Kaufman's proposed counterpart to the Dark Triad (2019, with David Bryce Yaden, Elizabeth Hyde, and Eli Tsukayama) — a cluster of three prosocial traits: Kantianism (treating people as ends in themselves, not means), humanism (belief in the inherent dignity of others), and faith in humanity (a basic trust that most people are good). Hagens' own explicit framing treats it as a real but structurally weaker force than the Dark Triad — a value-based orientation up against trait-based behavior, requiring deliberate group-level support (not just individual goodness) to hold its own."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.ScottBarryKaufman ;
    thinkr:contrastsWith tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    skos:scopeNote "Added 2026-10-18 from Frankly_85: Nate first met a value-based 'light triad' (sustainable stewardship, interconnectedness, respect for all beings) in a LinkedIn essay, then found Kaufman's more trait-like version; his point is that values alone lose to traits (tgs:Concept.TraitsOutcompeteValues), so light-triad people need group support. He asks how many supporters are enough, whether the downslope of the carbon pulse will favor light or dark traits, and whether dark-triad people can be flipped. Daniel Schmachtenberger's system-level account of dark-triad selection is tgs:Concept.PowerSeekingAndComplicity (TGS_126)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

