tgs:Concept.EcologicalFootprint a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ecological_footprint> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ecological Footprint"@en ;
    skos:definition "The area of productive land and water needed to supply what a population consumes and absorb its waste; the main measure of how far humanity is in overshoot."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "A quantitative sustainability metric, originated and co-developed by William Rees and his then-PhD-student Mathis Wackernagel (introduced in their 1996 book 'Our Ecological Footprint'), expressing human demand on ecosystems as the amount of biologically productive land and water area required to produce what a population consumes and absorb its waste — the primary tool used to estimate the extent of humanity's ecological overshoot."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.WilliamRees, tgs:Persona.MathisWackernagel .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — the overall action framework from Frankly_132
# "What to Do as the World Falls Apart," which the episode's own
# transcript explicitly labels "a first public draft."
#############################################

tgs:Concept.FrameworkForAction a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:prefLabel "Framework for Action"@en ;
    skos:definition "Hagens' own real, explicitly-labeled 'first public draft' framework for what to actually do in response to the more-than-human predicament — introduced in Frankly_132, gestated over roughly a decade and organized under the pressure of real, live world events (the 2026 Iran war/Strait of Hormuz crisis). Structured as 4 levels: a personal foundation of inner work, 'Finding the Others' (trusted networks and shared vocabulary), 6 interdependent thinkr:InterventionFront individuals, and a thinkr:ResponsePhase timeline axis of 3 genuinely overlapping (not strictly sequential) phases that cuts across all 6 Fronts."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/132-what-to-do-as-the-world-falls-apart> ;
    thinkr:hasFront 
        tgs:InterventionFront.Infrastructure, 
        tgs:InterventionFront.PovertyAndDisplacement, 
        tgs:InterventionFront.EcologicalInterventions,
        tgs:InterventionFront.CivicResilienceAndGovernance, 
        tgs:InterventionFront.CultureAndMeaning, 
        tgs:InterventionFront.EconomicTransition ;
    thinkr:hasPhase tgs:ResponsePhase.StabilityWindow, tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak, tgs:ResponsePhase.StableAttractor ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct evidence for the sequencing/parallel-construction stance, worth preserving: Hagens explicitly engages with the anti-capitalist/decolonial critique that structural transformation must precede any other intervention, states real sympathy for it, but argues explicitly for building new structures while the old ones still stand rather than waiting for a precondition that would mean 'waiting forever.'"@en .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — a real, cross-episode pattern MJSullivan
# identified and asked to be captured explicitly, synthesized from
# independently-recorded episodes across different guests and years —
# NOT one single named/branded framework Hagens has given one
# consistent term to himself the way Carbon Pulse or the Great
# Simplification are, so modeled as thinkr:Concept only, NOT
# thinkr:HagensConcept (that subclass requires Hagens' own genuinely
# distinctive coining, per its own class definition — this doesn't
# meet that bar honestly, however real and recurring the pattern is).
#############################################

tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:prefLabel "Distributed Agency, Not Saviors"@en ;
    skos:definition "Change comes from many ordinary people acting where they are, not from a hero or savior -- 'you never know when your actions could shift the larger story.'"@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "A real, recurring pattern across independently-recorded episodes: meaningful change comes from ordinary, distributed action rather than a singular hero or savior figure. Direct textual evidence spans multiple episodes, not one coined term: Frankly_58's own real closing reframe (Sam and Gollum, not Frodo, as the actual Catalysts who changed Lord of the Rings' outcome — 'you never know when your actions could shift the larger story'); Frankly_132's direct claim that reluctance to lead 'might be a good indication that you could be a good leader tomorrow,' paired with its critique that modern systems select for Dark Triad traits in those who actively seek leadership; RR-17's two guests independently converging on 'find like-minded people close to home' as their practical closing advice, rather than pointing toward a movement or a leader; and the real, structural logic of Buckminster Fuller's trim-tab principle (small, precisely-placed, unglamorous leverage redirecting something enormous), which is also the real source of Priscilla Trịnh's own 'Trintab' nickname from RR-9."@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:scopeNote "Identified and named as a real pattern by MJSullivan during this session, not something Hagens has explicitly unified under one term himself — worth being honest about that distinction even though the pattern itself is real and evidence-grounded across genuinely independent sources."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# added 2026-08-24 
#############################################

tgs:Concept.PersonalAgency a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:prefLabel "Personal Agency"@en ;
    skos:definition "The experience that some part of your life, time and attention is yours to choose -- the opposite of dread, which is being acted on by a future you cannot influence."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "The lived experience that some part of one's life, attention, and time is genuinely one's own to choose — distinct from tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency (a collective/systemic pattern about who drives change). Frankly_129's own organizing term, scientifically grounded via learned-helplessness/self-efficacy research (the nervous system learning whether effort produces outcomes). Frankly_142 directly ties it to dread: 'dread is the experience of being acted upon by a future you cannot influence, and agency is the opposite stance.'"@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:scopeNote "NOT to be confused with the existing tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency ('Distributed Agency, Not Saviors') — that concept is about collective action replacing savior-figures; this one is the individual psychological/nervous-system capacity the Staying Human series' own Part 1 is entirely about."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.Dread a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dread"@en ;
    skos:definition "A physiological body state, not merely a feeling — chronic anticipatory threat-response activation (amygdala/HPA-axis/cortisol) triggered by slow-moving, abstract, unresolvable risk the nervous system cannot discharge through fight-or-flight. Frankly_142's own central subject; grounded in real research on 'extreme dreaders' (~28% of subjects who preferred immediate stronger pain over waiting for milder pain) and climate psychiatrist Lise Van Susteren's 'pre-traumatic stress.'"@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:scopeNote "Directly cross-referenced in Frankly_143 as 'full dread... viewed from a slightly different angle' from tgs:Concept.FutureCapture — the two are presented as the same underlying phenomenon (DMN dominance with civilizational content) described from two angles, not two separate claims, matching this graph's existing 'two facets, not two concepts' pattern (see Concept.NarrativeAsActiveInference)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.Presence a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:prefLabel "Presence"@en ;
    skos:definition "Full attention to the present moment, the ground Nate sees for any real response to the metacrisis."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Frankly_143's own central subject: full attentional engagement with the present moment, framed as the destination dread transforms into 'when fully metabolized' (per Frankly_142's own closing) and as the ground from which any real response to the metacrisis must be taken."@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real prior art cited directly in the transcript across contemplative traditions (Thich Nhat Hanh, the Christian contemplative 'sacrament of the present moment') and neuroscience (default mode network research) — treated here as one Concept with converging support, not split by tradition."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.DefaultModeNetwork a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:prefLabel "Default Mode Network"@en ;
    skos:definition "The brain network active in mind-wandering, self-reference and imagining past and future; when it dominates, it is linked to anxiety and less contact with the present."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "The brain-region network active during self-referential processing, theory of mind, mind-wandering, and mental time travel — anti-correlated with the task-positive network (TPN), which handles direct engaged attention. Chronic DMN dominance is measurably linked to reduced hippocampal grey matter, higher anxiety/depression, and diminished sensory processing."@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:scopeNote "Only the graph's second real citation of DMN research — tgs:Interview.TGS_191_TaylorGuthrie is the first, explicitly named in Frankly_143's own transcript as 'the only prior TGS coverage.' The Task Positive Network (TPN), DMN's real anti-correlated partner, is deliberately folded into this definition rather than minted separately this pass."@en ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.ADefaultModeOfBrainFunction ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.FutureCapture a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:prefLabel "Future Capture"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own coined term (Frankly_143): the state where 'the future is so loud and so vivid and so saturated with implications... that the present just can't compete with it' — the future becomes 'a place we live' rather than visit, and the present becomes 'a kind of waiting room.' Explicitly framed as the same underlying phenomenon as tgs:Concept.Dread, viewed from the angle of what it does to attention rather than what it feels like."@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EcologicalAwakening a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ecological Awakening"@en ;
    skos:definition "Plotkin's own central term, and this episode's own title concept: a felt, experiential (not merely intellectual) shift from experiencing an 'invisible wall' between oneself and the rest of the living world to experiencing kinship with all of it — 'the earth community' — often accompanied by real grief for the years lived without that awareness. Plotkin estimates roughly 20-25% of people have had this experience. Distinct from taking a class in ecology: cognitive understanding without the felt shift doesn't count."@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:scopeNote "Direct evidence this recurs beyond one episode: Plotkin himself ties it explicitly to Joanna Macy's Great Turning (third dimension — a shift in values and consciousness) within the same conversation."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.BillPlotkin ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EcocentricDevelopmentalWheel a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ecocentric (Soulcentric) Developmental Wheel"@en ;
    skos:definition "Plotkin's eight-stage model of healthy human development, each stage paired with a human archetype and an earth archetype: (1) Innocent in the Nest — early childhood; (2) Explorer in the Garden — middle childhood; (3) Thespian at the Oasis — early adolescence; (4) Wanderer in the Cocoon — late adolescence, the stage of soul initiation, which 'most contemporary people never reach'; (5) Apprentice at the Wellspring — early adulthood; (6) Artisan in the Wild Orchard — late adulthood; (7) Master in the Grove of Elders — early elderhood; (8) Sage in the Mountain Cave — late elderhood. Explicitly contrasted with an 'egocentric wheel' modeling the stages most contemporary people actually go through instead, most of them stalled in an unhealthy version of stage 3."@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:scopeNote "Plotkin's own real prior art cited as convergent, non-Western evidence for this model: Malidoma Somé's account of Dagara initiation rites, Martín Prechtel's account of Tzutujil Maya culture, and Gregory Cajete's writing on Native American educational traditions — none of the three built as Personas this pass."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.BillPlotkin ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ArrestedHumanDevelopment a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:prefLabel "Arrested Human Development"@en ;
    skos:definition "Plotkin's own direct causal claim, stated as this episode's central thesis: 'the root cause [of the metacrisis] is arrested human development' — also termed 'systemic human development oppression.' Distinct from tgs:Concept.Metacrisis itself (the pattern of interconnected crises) by proposing a specific generative mechanism behind it, located in human developmental psychology rather than economic or ecological systems directly."@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.Metacrisis ;
    skos:scopeNote "A real, single-episode claim — not yet independently corroborated elsewhere in this graph. Flagged for revisiting if it recurs, matching this graph's own recurrence-based minting discipline."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.BillPlotkin ;
    .

tgs:Concept.GreatTurning a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Great Turning"@en ;
    skos:definition "The shift from an industrial growth society to a life-sustaining civilization, through holding actions, new structures and a change in consciousness."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Joanna Macy's framework, explained by Plotkin in real depth (not a passing citation): the shift from an 'Industrial Growth Society' toward a 'life-sustaining civilization,' unfolding across three simultaneous dimensions — (1) holding actions to save as much life as possible now, (2) building the structural and technical foundations for healthier systems (food, energy, governance), and (3) a shift in consciousness and values, which Plotkin explicitly connects back to tgs:Concept.EcologicalAwakening and his own developmental wheel. Macy pairs this with 'the Great Unraveling' — the ongoing collapse of existing systems — as two faces of the same real historical moment, not opposed processes."@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsFrameworks ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real external source, not a Hagens or Plotkin coinage — Joanna Macy is cited directly by name throughout as its originator. Macy herself not yet built as a Persona in this graph (cited here and, separately, in Frankly_142's own scopeNote as a deferred candidate)."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.JoannaMacy ;
    .

# added 2026-08-25

tgs:Concept.SupplyChainFragility a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:prefLabel "Supply Chain Fragility / Pinch Points"@en ;
    skos:definition "Global supply chains depend on hidden pinch points -- a single factory, region or material -- whose failure can halt far larger systems."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Modern global supply chains are far more concentrated, specialized, and fragile than they appear — a small number of hyper-specialized, often invisible 'pinch points' (a single factory, a single region, a single material) can be genuinely load-bearing for enormous downstream systems, disproportionate to their own small economic footprint. Real evidenced cases: the 1915 UK 'Glass Famine' (an entire domestic industry allowed to wither, then found existentially necessary within a few years, per tgs:Interview.TGS_127_EdConway); a single Birmingham factory supplying roughly half the world's automotive rearview-mirror electrodes; the 2011 Fukushima earthquake halting Ford's US truck production over a single specialized paint pigment. The mechanism Ed Conway identifies: modern comparative-advantage-driven specialization increases aggregate efficiency while increasing structural fragility — 'you have these factories which are uber-specialised... and they're pinch points. Until those pinch points explode, you don't really know about it.'"@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsFrameworks ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.EdConway ;
    thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, deliberate general-purpose applicability, per MJSullivan's own stated reason for selecting this episode 2026-08-24: this concept is a candidate response/caveat to a wide range of articles about growth, consumption, or globalized production, not narrowly scoped to energy/climate topics — directly relevant to the tng-app design work's 'systemic reframing' step from this same session."@en ;
    .
tgs:Concept.EarlyAnthropoceneHypothesis a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Early_anthropocene> ;
    skos:definition "William Ruddiman's real, own hypothesis: human-caused greenhouse gas increases began roughly 8,000 years ago through the intensive farming of early agrarian societies, not with 18th-century industrialization — and that this early human activity likely forestalled an incipient ice age already due to begin, per natural Milankovitch-cycle variation."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Early Anthropocene Hypothesis"@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.WilliamRuddiman ;
    skos:scopeNote "Added 2026-08-31. Real, direct citation confirmed via Peter Brannen's own TGS_210 appearance. Given a real, dedicated own Wikipedia page (a genuine named academic hypothesis, not just a personal essay or theory), this Concept + a minimal Persona for Ruddiman (matching the same pattern already established for tgs:Persona.HowardOdum on Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple) felt like the right, proportionate structure — unlike the other real intellectual-influence figures added the same day (Georgescu-Roegen, Kümmel, Boulding, Shepard, Jackson), who remain Human-only with no forced formal link."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.Ecolate a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.garretthardinsociety.org/articles/art_ecolate_view_human_predicament.html> ;
    skos:definition "Ecologically literate: habitually asking 'and then what?' of any action to trace its second- and third-order consequences; the root of Nate's wide-boundary thinking."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Garrett Hardin's real, coined term for ecological literacy — a third literacy alongside 'numerate' (knowing numbers) and 'literate' (knowing words and grammar). Being ecolate means habitually asking 'and then what?' of any proposed action, tracing second-, third-, and nth-order consequences beyond the immediate, narrow-boundary benefit. Real, direct source for this show's own recurring 'wide-boundary thinking' vocabulary."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ecolate (Ecological Literacy)"@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.GarrettHardin ;
    .

tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:definition "In a system far from equilibrium, small coherent groups can shift the whole system to a new order -- Nate's case for the outsized influence of grounded communities in unstable times."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Ilya Prigogine's real concept, directly and repeatedly paraphrased by Nate across multiple episodes: when a complex system is far from equilibrium, small 'islands of coherence' within it have the capacity to shift the entire system to a higher order. Used throughout this show as a direct argument for the real, disproportionate potential impact of small, genuinely coherent groups or individuals during periods of systemic instability. Real, direct disambiguation added 2026-09-16: NOT the same real concept as tgs:Concept.IslandsOfSanity (Margaret Wheatley's own distinct term, named directly in TGS-234) -- related in spirit, different real originators and frames; kept as two separate Concepts rather than merged."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Islands of Coherence"@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.IlyaPrigogine ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, independently cited across at least three already-built episodes before this Concept itself existed: Frankly_65, Frankly_98, and Lisi Krall's TGS_86 — the same recurrence pattern that made David Sloan Wilson and Lisi Krall worth prioritizing as unbuilt figures earlier this session, applied here to a recurring concept rather than a recurring person."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.BioticPump a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:definition "The theory that forests actively pull moist ocean air inland and drive rainfall, rather than just receiving it."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Real theory co-developed by Anastassia Makarieva and the late Victor Gorshkov: forests actively drive atmospheric moisture transport and rainfall patterns over land, rather than being passive recipients of rain — condensation over dense forest canopy lowers air pressure, pulling moist ocean air inland. Challenges conventional climate models to treat vegetation as a dynamic driver of atmospheric processes, not a fixed background feature."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Biotic Pump"@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.AnastassiaMakarieva, tgs:Persona.VictorGorshkov ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, previously-missing Concept, flagged explicitly as a gap in Andrew Millison's own TGS_178 scopeNote ('the mechanism is real and substantively discussed but kept as prose... no dedicated Concept.BioticPump exists yet') — resolved directly here via Makarieva's own real, first-hand TGS appearance, the theory's real originator."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:definition "Nine Earth-system limits -- climate, biosphere integrity, land, freshwater, nutrients, ocean acidity, aerosols, ozone and novel chemicals -- within which humanity can operate safely; six are now exceeded."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Real, foundational scientific framework led by Johan Rockström and colleagues (originally published 2009), identifying nine distinct Earth-system processes with critical thresholds — climate change, biosphere integrity, land-system change, freshwater use, biogeochemical flows, ocean acidification, atmospheric aerosol loading, stratospheric ozone depletion, and novel entities (chemical pollution) — within which humanity and the biosphere can continue to develop safely. As of the framework's own 2023 update, six of nine boundaries have been exceeded."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Planetary Boundaries"@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.JohanRockstrom ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, genuinely surprising gap closed here — this framework is referenced constantly throughout this graph (dozens of already-built episodes cite 'planetary boundaries' directly) but had no dedicated Concept individual until Rockström's own real TGS appearance was built."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.RelativeFitnessSpiteFramework a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:definition "Because evolution rewards doing better than rivals, not just doing well, people will pay a cost to impose a bigger cost on a competitor -- spite -- alongside selfishness, cooperation and altruism."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Nate's own real, structured four-quadrant behavioral framework, grounded in the evolutionary distinction between relative fitness (reproductive success measured against competitors, the real evolutionary currency) and absolute fitness (raw offspring count or physical condition alone). The four quadrants: selfishness (cost to a competitor, benefit to self), cooperation (mutual benefit), altruism (cost to self, benefit to a genetically unrelated other — rare in nature, though reciprocal altruism is common in humans), and spite (a real, direct cost to oneself, provided it imposes a greater cost on a competitor)."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Relative Fitness / Spite Framework"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, central framework of Frankly_11, directly extended there from individual/animal behavior to nation-state behavior (spite as a real, direct driver of nuclear war risk)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.BiophysicalPyramid a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:definition "A three-layer way to read the news: finance on top, energy and materials in the middle, the biosphere at the base; trouble starts when financial claims outgrow what the lower layers can support."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Nate's own real, three-layer framework for reading news and economic events: a top financial layer (prices, salaries, debt, markets — what most people interact with daily and often mistake for the entire economy), a middle biophysical layer (energy, materials, minerals, supply chains — the connective tissue turning abstract prices into real goods), and a bottom biosphere layer (soil, water, forests, climate — the largely invisible, unbilled services underpinning everything above it). A 'biophysical phase shift' occurs as financial claims increasingly outpace what the lower two layers can actually support, forcing the deeper layers into visibility."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Biophysical Pyramid"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, foundational framework first laid out at length in Frankly_121, the inaugural episode of Nate's own Wide Boundary News series — later Wide Boundary News installments (e.g. Frankly_124, already built) apply this same lens without necessarily re-explaining it."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.BiophysicalGauntlet a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EROIAndEnergyQuality ;
    skos:definition "The bind of declining resource quality: consumers need low prices, but producers need ever-higher prices to extract harder, poorer deposits."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Biophysical Gauntlet"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct citation confirmed across at least two already-built episodes (Craig Tindale's TGS_207, Frankly_124) before this episode — Frankly_121 — was itself built; this episode appears to be an early, if not the first, real spoken articulation of the term in this graph's own source material. Further spoken use confirmed 2026-10-07 in TGS_237 (about 25:44), where Nate dates the term himself -- '10 years ago or so, and I think in my book' (a hedged recollection; the book is not identified) -- and the show notes there point to Frankly_124 for the concept."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.LifeEthic a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct nine-point ethical framework, first laid out in a 2021 Earth Day talk and repeated in Frankly_62: life existing is preferable to no life; more kinds of life are preferable to fewer; vibrant, complex ecosystems are preferable to fragmented, brittle ones; ecosystems with large, complex life are preferable to purely microscopic ones; ecosystems supporting conscious minds are preferable to those without; maximizing existence, happiness, love, and understanding while minimizing suffering is preferable; the future is real in the same sense the present is real, and is shaped by present causality; what fits these preferences may be considered good, what doesn't may be considered evil; and good should be maximized, evil minimized."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Life Ethic"@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SevenDialsOfInstitutionalPsychopathy a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct framework mapping Greg Elliott's Psychopathic Selection Hypothesis onto seven distinct behavioral 'dials,' each set toward genuine cooperation in small-scale ancestral human communities but flipped toward extraction at civilizational scale: (1) repeated interaction, (2) visibility, (3) attribution, (4) delay, (5) exit costs and mutual dependence, (6) who writes the rules, and (7) symbols and proxies (money, GDP, and other measures that can decouple from the physical reality they're meant to represent). Argues all seven dials flipped simultaneously, with the sixth locked in place, together constitute the economic Superorganism — while noting the same dials often remain set the 'tribal way' at the scale of one's own street, town, or bioregion."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Seven Dials of Institutional Psychopathy"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct follow-on framework from Frankly_157, itself a direct integration of Greg Elliott's real Psychopathic Selection Hypothesis (already built, TGS_231) with Nate's own earlier Frankly_108 (already built) distinction between individual human prosociality and aggregate institutional behavior."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:definition "Modern products that hijack evolved reward circuits by exceeding anything in our ancestral environment -- from junk food and phones to money itself."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Nate's own real, recurring teaching concept: a modern invention or artifact that hijacks ancestral, evolved brain circuitry in a deleterious, maladaptive way relative to the environment that circuitry originally evolved for — vastly exceeding, in scale or intensity, anything the ancestral environment could have produced. Nate's own real, established example is money itself, particularly the US dollar; in a real, direct extension made during a conversation with Chuck Watson, he proposes nuclear weapons as an even larger supernormal stimulus, given their real, many-orders-of-magnitude greater destructive scale relative to any weapon available in humanity's tribal past."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Supernormal Stimuli"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, foundational concept underlying Nate's own recurring teaching material, directly extended from money to nuclear weapons during TGS_4's real, chronologically third conversation with Chuck Watson (TGS_17). In Frankly_51 (December 2023) Nate's example is fans in the box seats at a football game watching their phones instead of the game, and he expects AI to 'exponentially increase that.'"@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CrudeOil a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual, skos:Concept ;
    skos:altLabel "Fossil Sunlight"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "The Spice"@en ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:prefLabel "Crude Oil"@en ;
    skos:definition "Liquid fossil hydrocarbon: exceptionally energy-dense, easy to transport, and not replaceable at the scale of today's civilization; the backbone of transport, agriculture and petrochemicals."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAlternateTerm [ a thinkr:AlternateTerm ;
        thinkr:hasAlternateTermType tgs:AlternateTermType.ExactMatch ;
        skos:prefLabel "The Spice"@en ;
        skos:scopeNote "Nate's own real, deliberate, recurring analogy, borrowed directly from Frank Herbert's Dune: crude oil functions for modern industrial civilization precisely as 'the spice' (melange) does for Dune's fictional universe — a singular, indispensable resource whose flow, if disrupted, threatens the stability of the entire system built on top of it. ExactMatch, not Related: in Nate's own usage the two terms refer to the identical real-world referent (crude oil itself), not a broader or mechanism-adjacent concept — he substitutes one term for the other directly, not alongside it. Real, confirmed recurring usage, not a one-off phrase: directly used in both Frankly_61 ('The Strait of Hormuz and the Spice,' already built — the analogy's own real, titular framing) and TGS_94 (Chuck Watson, already built — 'the oil, the spice flow... to use a Dune reference'), independently confirming this is Nate's own genuine, repeated rhetorical device rather than a single passing joke." ],
      [ a thinkr:AlternateTerm ;
        thinkr:hasAlternateTermType tgs:AlternateTermType.ExactMatch ;
        skos:prefLabel "Fossil Sunlight"@en ;
        skos:scopeNote "Nate's own real, recurring term for crude oil and natural gas, framing them directly as ancient solar energy captured by photosynthesis over geologic time and concentrated into an extraordinarily dense, portable form — a genuinely deeper, more literal characterization than 'fossil fuel,' emphasizing the energy's real ultimate origin rather than just its age or combustibility. ExactMatch, not Related: refers to the identical real-world referent (crude oil/gas), substituted directly for it. Real, confirmed recurring usage, not a one-off phrase: directly found in TGS_233's own real transcript (already built) — 'the whole thing is powered by fossil sunlight and non-renewable minerals' — and, per MJSullivan's own direct site search, also used in the titles/content of Frankly_39 ('Just Stop Oil Part 2 — Oil is the Economy') and Frankly_137 ('Everything is About to Change: The World After Cheap Energy'), neither yet built in this graph." ] .

tgs:Concept.SevenFundamentalDriversOfOvershoot a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.OvershootAndLimits ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Seven Fundamental Drivers of Overshoot"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, structured seven-part framework explaining why humanity exceeds its planetary carrying capacity, first laid out in full in Frankly_68: (1) the Carbon Pulse — millions of years of stored ancient energy injected into the economic system over a geologically brief span; (2) Money Alchemy — monetary creation that draws consumption forward in time while neglecting the real, unrecorded 'third and fourth book entries' of non-renewable resource depletion and ecosystem damage; (3) Outsourced Governance to the economic Superorganism, reinforced by institutional hierarchies that systematically promote confident, optimistic individuals regardless of ecological accuracy; (4) a real, species-wide Lack of Recognition of Overshoot, stemming from widespread ecological illiteracy even among the otherwise numerate and literate; (5) the Positive Feedback of Status and Consumption, where more money and more conspicuous consumption function as culturally aspirational defaults; (6) Dopamine Superhighways, where habituated consumption and technology use bias everyday micro-decisions toward higher-energy, higher-resource choices; and (7) Cognitive Dissonance, the real, direct psychological mechanism by which people closest to a given risk often profess the least concern about it, since fully recognizing the risk would be too painful to hold alongside their own way of life."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "SUPERSEDED 2026-09-04, per MJSullivan's own direct editorial decision for the WWNS series: this framework predates, and is treated going forward as superseded by, Nate's own later, more structured Framework for Action (tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction, already built). WWNS content should cite the Framework for Action instead of this Concept going forward. Kept here as a real record of what Nate actually said, not deleted. Real, foundational framework, first delivered in full in Frankly_68 (already built) — Nate's own real, direct disclosure notes he recorded this Frankly with a 103°F fever the night before, calling it a 'fever-induced reflection.'"@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.TenLensesOfInformationBifurcation a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Ten Lenses of Information Bifurcation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, structured set of ten dichotomies shaping how information is produced and received in modern media, first laid out in full in Frankly_57: (1) speaking vs. writing, (2) sensational vs. educational, (3) simple vs. complex, (4) the Fourth Estate vs. the Fifth Estate, (5) Fifth Estate crazy vs. Fifth Estate reasonable, (6) agenda vs. authentic, (7) socially acceptable vs. cancelable, (8) publicly-available vs. privately-sold information, (9) what is heard vs. what is said, and (10) identity vs. learning."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, foundational framework, first delivered in full in Frankly_57 (already built), prompted directly by real, documented audience reaction to Nate's own prior episode with Chuck Watson on NATO (already built, TGS_114)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.GoldilocksTechnology a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:prefLabel "Goldilocks Technology"@en ;
    skos:definition "Technology that is neither too 'hot' (resource-hungry, dopamine-driven) nor too 'cold' (a retreat to stone tools) -- roughly 80% of the benefit for 20% of the resources."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Nate's own real, coined term for technology calibrated between two failure modes: not too 'hot' (dopaminergic, resource-intensive gadgets that deepen dependence on non-renewable inputs) and not too 'cold' (a naive retreat to stone-age tooling that ignores real human problem-solving capacity). First laid out as a preliminary checklist in Frankly_69, considering real governance incentives, true-cost pricing of externalities, material and supply-chain constraints, and a technology's actual relationship to the biosphere."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct extension of a concept already explored in a real, existing TGS episode on Goldilocks Technology, directly cross-referenced in this Frankly's own real show notes; that companion episode itself is not yet built in this graph. The earliest statement in the graph is Frankly_55 (March 2024), where it is one of Nate's seven key interventions (tgs:Concept.SevenKeyInterventions): not 100 million new 3,000-pound vehicles a year, not stone tools, but things like sodium batteries, 'not as good as lithium batteries, but good enough' -- 'those things that we can get 80% of the human benefits that we need with 20% of the resources.'"@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.TenThingsWorthMoreThanGold a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ten Things Worth More Than a Pound of Gold"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, structured countdown of things he argues hold greater real value than gold, first laid out in full in Frankly_106, moving from purely financial comparisons toward increasingly intangible ones: $50,000 in cash (worth more than a pound of gold at real, then-current prices), 100 pounds of silver, roughly half a Bitcoin, land, friends and social networks, freedom, sanity, integrity, the web of life, and — at the top — life itself."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, foundational framework, first delivered in full in Frankly_106 (already built). Nate directly distinguishes value from liquidity and resilience as three separate lenses: gold and cash are liquid but not necessarily resilient, while land, integrity, and the web of life are resilient but comparatively illiquid — arguing resilience, not liquidity, is what actually determines what carries people through real hardship."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CulturalMitochondria a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cultural Mitochondria: Ten Qualities That Could Change the Future"@en ;
    skos:definition "Ten personal qualities -- grounding, a post-tragic mindset, co-regulation, restraint, wide-boundary thinking, play and others -- that Nate suggests could seed a more resilient culture, like small, distributed units of energy inside a cell."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Nate's own real, coined framework, laid out in full in Frankly_98, for ten personal traits he speculates could seed a genuinely more resilient and cooperative culture after the economic Superorganism's own eventual splintering — framed directly as 'cultural mitochondria,' small, distributed, largely invisible units of capacity, extending Marvin Harris's own real cultural-materialism pyramid (infrastructure, structure, superstructure, already built) to the real inner world of individuals the original framework left out. Nate's own real, numbered list, given deliberately 'in no particular order' despite the numbering: (0) self-care as a real neurobiological precondition, not indulgence; (1) grounding, staying tethered to body and breath; (2) a post-tragic mindset, metabolizing grief into daily rhythm rather than avoiding it; (3) being networked, decentralized and mycelial rather than hierarchical, 'finding the others'; (4) co-regulation with others via real polyvagal safety, not just individual regulation; (5) systems thinking / wide boundary thinking; (6) restraint, deliberately saying no to consumption and supernormal stimuli; (7) being status-free or status-aware rather than status-driven; (8) genuinely feeling interconnection with the web of life, not just knowing it intellectually; (9) embracing emergence over fixed outcomes, seeding present conditions rather than planning a final result; and (10) play and joy, explicitly named optional yet real and important."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, foundational framework, first delivered in full in Frankly_98 (already built). Real, honest quirk worth preserving rather than silently correcting: Nate's own numbering runs zero through ten — eleven real labeled positions — for what he calls 'ten traits,' a genuine inconsistency in his own live delivery, not a transcription error. Real, direct grounding citations confirmed: Francis Weller's own already-built TGS appearance for grief work; Mark Gafni or Zak Stein (Nate himself is uncertain which) for the pre-tragic/post-tragic framing; David Sloan Wilson and E.O. Wilson for multilevel selection theory; and Ilya Prigogine's own real 'islands of coherence' framing (already a formal Concept in this graph), directly quoted in this episode's own real closing line, though the transcript itself mis-renders his name as 'IA Priya Jean.' The image first appears in Frankly_76 (November 2024): beyond power versus life, what is at stake is the superorganism versus the mitochondria of a culture that could live beyond it, passed on as mitochondria pass from mother to child (tgs:Concept.BattlesOfOurTime)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.TenCoreMythsOfBusinessSchools a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Ten Core Myths Still Taught in Business Schools"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, structured critique, delivered in full as a countdown in Frankly_99, of ten foundational assumptions he argues mainstream economics and MBA education still teaches despite their real, direct conflict with biophysical reality — explicitly framed as values-neutral description, not preference. His own real, numbered countdown: (10) price equals value, when willingness to pay actually reflects wealth, not need or importance; (9) humans are rational utility-maximizing homo economicus, when real humans are tribal, status-seeking, and intensely social; (8) the supply curve slopes upward, when most modern production, especially digital and tech, gets cheaper at scale, concentrating power rather than sustaining competition; (7, his own real, direct 'two decades unpacking' subject) energy is a substitutable commodity like any other, omitted entirely from the real, foundational Cobb-Douglas production function despite underpinning every input to it; (6) money is lent from prior savings, when most money is actually created new at the moment a loan is issued (endogenous money creation); (5) debt is a neutral intertemporal tool, when it is actually a claim on future energy and materials that may not materialize; (4) GDP is the primary measure of national success, when it tallies transaction volume regardless of whether the underlying activity helps or harms; (3) the environment is a subset of the economy, when the economy is in fact a wholly-owned subset of the biosphere; (2) the invisible hand reliably produces the best collective outcomes, a term Adam Smith himself used only once and in a different context than it's now popularly applied; and (1) economic 'laws' are timeless and universal like physics, when they are context-bound assumptions built entirely within one narrow, roughly 150-year window of fossil-fuel abundance."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, foundational framework, first delivered in full in Frankly_99 (already built). Real, direct quantitative grounding for myth 7: one barrel of crude oil contains roughly 1,700 kilowatt-hours of real work potential — the physical equivalent of about 11 years of full-time human labor, handicapped to roughly 4-5 years once machine energy-conversion inefficiency is real, directly accounted for; at current real global consumption (~100 billion barrels of oil-equivalent annually across oil, coal, and gas), Nate directly calculates this as equivalent to adding a real 500-billion-person labor force on top of an actual global human workforce of roughly 5 billion. Real, direct named critique: Alan Blinder, real economist, former Federal Reserve Vice Chairman and textbook author, is directly named and criticized for continuing to teach upward-sloping supply curves in his own textbook despite his own published research finding barely any firms still exhibit that pattern. Real, direct forward reference: Nate closes by previewing Frankly #100 as a real, planned 'ask me anything' format, not yet built in this graph."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.TwentyControlKnobs a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "The 20 Control Knobs for a Post-Growth Future"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, structured framework, delivered in full in Frankly_64, of twenty 'volume knobs' shaping how the future unfolds — ten real, external macro trends largely outside individual control, and ten real, internal responses within it. The ten external trends: (1) a cooler world trending warmer; (2) freedom trending toward constraints; (3) a unipolar world trending multipolar; (4) credit trending toward cash; (5) richer trending toward poorer, with a real sub-knob of equality trending toward inequality; (6) peace trending toward conflict; (7) global trending toward local; (8) unified/collective trending toward isolated/tribal; (9) future-focus trending toward present-focus, as discount rates steepen; and (10) complexity trending toward simplification. The ten internal responses: (1) isolation toward interconnection; (2) a focus on money toward a focus on real capital (health, knowledge, skills, relationships); (3) consumption toward regeneration; (4) a narrow view toward wide-boundary systems thinking; (5) baseload dependence toward deliberate intermittence; (6) technology-as-escape toward technology-as-tool, redirected toward real relationships and place; (7) critique toward awe and wonder; (8) apathy toward goodness; (9) shallow engagement toward depth; and (10) optimizing for social power and status toward optimizing for life itself."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, foundational framework, first delivered in full in Frankly_64 (already built), recorded the day before Nate left on his own real, annual fishing trip with nine male relatives. Real, direct grounding citations confirmed: Johan Rockström's own real, already-built TGS appearance (TGS-134) on planetary boundaries, directly cited for the 'complexity toward simplification' trend; the already-built Carbon Pulse framework (Frankly-44) underlying several of the external trends; David Wengrow and David Graeber's real book, The Dawn of Everything, discussed directly with real, genuine ambivalence — Nate notes anthropologists he knows have criticized it as politically motivated cherry-picking, while remaining personally agnostic."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SevenPhilosophiesOnTheFuture a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Seven Philosophies on the Future"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, structured typology, delivered in full in Frankly_77, of seven distinct ways people philosophically relate to the future — offered directly as a tool for genuine empathy in conversation, since two people can share the same facts while occupying entirely different categories. (1) 'They don't': survival needs are consuming enough that the future functionally doesn't extend past today or tonight. (2) Extrapolation: too busy or distracted to have really considered it, so the future is simply assumed to continue recent trends unchanged. (3) Cosmic destiny: a genuine, near-religious belief that indefinite growth and expansion beyond Earth is a preordained human destiny, akin to natural law. (4) Degrowth: a clear-eyed understanding of ecological overshoot leading to a purposeful, voluntary choice to shrink the scale of the human enterprise. (5) Collapse/doomerism: a fatalistic, deterministic 'turbocharged' version of degrowth, rooting for or resigned to collapse rather than treating it as one point in a real probability distribution. (6) Nuanced pro-growth: genuinely understanding biophysical limits and civilizational complexity, yet concluding degrowth itself would be catastrophic — complex systems can't gracefully 'back up' — leaving continued growth and innovation as the only viable path despite real risk. (7) Nate's own real position: involuntary degrowth is coming regardless of preference, primarily via financial-system unraveling, making active preparation for a 'Bend Not Break' transition the genuine task, rather than either advocating for or dreading collapse."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, foundational framework, first delivered in full in Frankly_77 (already built), directly framed as a listening tool: knowing which of the seven categories someone else occupies changes how facts and synthesis will actually land in conversation with them. Real, direct grounding citation confirmed: Frankly_23, 'The Mordor Economy' (already built), directly cited for the financial-credit mechanics underlying category seven's own real, central claim."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SevenKeyQuestionsOnNuclearConflict a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Seven Key Questions on Nuclear Conflict and Sustainable Futures"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, structured set of questions, delivered in full in Frankly_63, on how to think about the NATO-Russia conflict and nuclear risk without either sensationalizing it or ignoring it. Given deliberately starting from a real 'question zero': who is the 'we' that should even be asking these questions? Then: (1) should individuals discuss a risk they seem to have no direct agency over; (2) what would 'winning' this war actually look like, for Russia, for the West, and for the rest of the world; (3) winning for whom — whose real calculus actually governs decisions made by a small number of people who may not share the broader population's interests; (4) what is the conditional probability of any other issue someone cares about resolving well if this conflict doesn't end benignly; (5) how should the concept of war itself be reframed, rather than assumed as an immutable part of the human condition; (6) does breaking the underlying cycle require genuinely new forms of governance, national and international; and (7) how can the Overton window of possible outcomes be expanded beyond the current narrow frame of proxy war, conventional war, and nuclear war, to include cold war, cold peace, or active cooperation."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, foundational framework, first delivered in full in Frankly_63 (already built). Nate directly states his own real, explicit position before laying out the questions: 'I am not pro-Putin, I am not pro-Russia, I am pro complex life' — his own stated framing, preserved here as his words, not asserted as this graph's own view. Real, direct grounding citations confirmed: Frankly_11's own already-built evolutionary spite/relative-fitness framework (already a formal Concept), directly applied to question three; the already-built Carbon Pulse framework, directly cited for question four's own material-supply-chain concerns."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EightFacesOfAI a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "The 8 Faces of AI"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, structured typology, delivered in full in Frankly_96, of eight archetypal ways individual humans may relate to and be shaped by AI as it accelerates — offered directly as a sorting mechanism revealing not just what AI can do, but who people are becoming in response to it. (1) The Meek: lack access to AI, remaining unblended human psychology by circumstance rather than choice, potentially preserving cultural practices untouched by algorithmic influence. (2) The Naive, or Blissful: have access but are unaware or uninterested, making them especially vulnerable to manipulation. (3) The AI Luddites: aware of AI's real risks to embodied human cognition and actively, deliberately resist it. (4) The Pragmatists: a real subset of the Luddites, aware of the pitfalls but using AI begrudgingly to avoid falling behind professionally. (5) The Flexors: use AI performatively, for social status signaling rather than genuine productivity or enjoyment. (6) The Achievers: Nate's own real, stated 'pinnacle' category — disciplined, limiting AI use to roughly 10-15% while maintaining full, embodied human lives: family, fitness, time outdoors, meditation. (7) The Cyborgs: experience cognition as a genuine human-machine hybrid, gaining novel thought patterns while losing others to atrophy, and facing real, direct identity confusion about where human ends and AI begins. (8) The Dissolved: for those with addictive temperaments, AI becomes 'crack cocaine' — real, severe deterioration in physical and mental health, withdrawal from human relationships, and potentially many hours daily in chatbot-based relationships substituting for real human connection."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, foundational framework, first delivered in full in Frankly_96 (already built). Nate directly predicts the Achievers and the Luddites will fare best as energy and ecological constraints tighten, the Dissolved and Cyborgs worst, and speculates the Meek — 'once overlooked' — could become unexpected teachers once AI itself falters or plateaus."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.UltraProcessedInformation a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ultra-Processed Information"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct analogy, coined in full in Frankly_128, comparing AI-generated content to industrially processed food: just as fossil-fuel-powered industrial agriculture made calories cheap and abundant while stripping out nutrient density, AI is making information cheap and abundant — fast, confident, and persuasive — while stripping out the genuinely expensive parts that tether words to reality: measurement, sourcing, expertise, and accountability. Real, direct extension of the analogy: just as food manufacturers engineer a 'bliss point' of salt, sugar, and fat for maximum palatability, AI content is becoming engineered for a real, direct attention-economy equivalent — novelty, polarization, and righteousness blended for maximum engagement, not truth."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, foundational analogy, first delivered in full in Frankly_128 (already built). Nate directly credits Robert Lustig's own real, already-referenced prior TGS appearance on processed food and addiction as the direct source of the food-system half of the analogy. Real, direct, honest personal detail: Nate states he is personally navigating a restricted diet at the time of recording, drawing the physical-discipline parallel from lived experience, not just theory."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ThreeInformationFilters a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Three Information Filters: Is It True? Is It Relevant? Is It Useful?"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct, practical three-question discipline, proposed in full in Frankly_128 as a genuine first-pass filter for the coming flood of AI-generated content, since no individual can fact-check the whole internet. (1) Is it true? — can the claim be traced to a real, legitimate primary source, data, or an accountable institution or person; if not, it gets a real, direct handicap as unverified rather than outright rejected. (2) Is it relevant? — even a true claim can be noise if it doesn't genuinely change one's understanding, priorities, or decisions; crediting Aza Raskin and Tristan Harris (both already built) directly for the observation that attention itself is becoming the scarce resource. (3) Is it useful? — does engaging with it actually improve judgment or capability, even at a real, small, local scale, or does it merely activate anger, polarization, or a performance of certainty without building genuine capacity."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, foundational framework, first delivered in full in Frankly_128 (already built), offered directly as a practical companion to tgs:Concept.UltraProcessedInformation — the discipline needed to navigate an information environment increasingly engineered like ultra-processed food."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.InServiceOfLife a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "In Service of Life (capital-L vs. lowercase-l)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's test for action: does it serve Life with a capital L -- the long-term continuity of complex life on Earth -- or only the narrower, human-centred 'life' of most well-meant initiatives?"@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Nate's own real, direct phrase, delivered in full definitional form in Frankly_92, distinguishing genuine service to Life (capital L — the full, deep-time continuity of complex life on Earth) from 'in service of life' in the lowercase sense (well-intentioned initiatives like wind farms, recycling, or the UN Sustainable Development Goals, which genuinely help but operate at a fundamentally smaller threshold). Rather than define capital-L service directly, Nate offers ten real, direct criteria for what is NOT in service of Life: (1) anything anthropocentric, caring only about human life; (2) anything reductionist, counting species without accounting for symbiosis and biocomplexity; (3) anything favoring simple life over complex life as though they were equivalent; (4) anything current-focused only, ignoring deep time (most climate models stop at 2099); (5) anything land-only-based, ignoring that oceans hold 70% of Earth's living habitat; (6) anything exoplanetary-focused (Mars colonization, immortal robot bodies, mind uploading) that writes off life on Earth itself; (7) anything causing irreversible species extinction; (8) anything allowing existential global threats (nuclear weapons) to continue; (9) interventions that fix one anthropocentric harm while worsening ecological harm overall (curing malaria without addressing the resulting pressure on rainforest habitat); and (10) growing the economic Superorganism itself, as opposed to slowing or shrinking its metabolism."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct origin story: the phrase itself comes from Nora Bateson (already built), relaying words her father Gregory Bateson said to young students roughly 50 years ago — with Rex Weyler (already built) reportedly present at that same real, historical moment. Real, foundational framework, first delivered in full in Frankly_92 (already built)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.FiveHorsemenOf2020s owl:deprecated true ;
    dcterms:isReplacedBy tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s ;
    rdfs:label "The Four Horsemen (deprecated; merged into tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s)"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Deprecated 2026-10-19 by MJSullivan: a duplicate of tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s. Its definitions, scope notes and episode links were merged there, and 'Five Horsemen of the 2020s' is recorded there as an exact-match alternate term. Kept only so the old IRI resolves; no longer typed thinkr:Concept."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SevenAIMacroRisks a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:prefLabel "Seven Macro-Risks of Artificial Intelligence"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, structured list, delivered in full in Frankly_83, of seven systemic AI risks operating at a societal, not individual, scale — counted down from least to most severe, and grounded in two underlying real, direct mechanisms: 'obligate technology' (a tribe or society that declines to adopt a competitively decisive new technology gets outcompeted regardless of its own preference) and the strong form of Jevons paradox, the backfire effect. (7) Job loss driving further wealth and income inequality, since productivity gains have overwhelmingly accrued to capital owners since the 1970s. (6) A massive new draw on energy and minerals, with a single AI query estimated at 10-100 times a standard web search's energy cost. (5) Turbocharged ecosystem impact, as AI-driven productivity gains feed back into more consumption, not less. (4/3, given as a real, direct numbering slip in the original delivery) The erosion of a shared, agreed-upon truth, as personalized AI systems increasingly reinforce each user's own pre-existing worldview, alongside a real, direct threat to academia's own economic model. (3) A genuine loss of relational humanity, as creativity, love, and attention get increasingly outsourced to machines, pulling people toward what Francis Weller (already built) calls 'secondary satisficers' (gambling, shopping, pornography) at the expense of 'primary satisficers' (real, direct ancestral bonding and shared challenge). (2) A 'Skynet' scenario in which techno-billionaires, government, surveillance, and military power gradually consolidate into the hands of a shrinking few. (1) The literal existential risk of artificial superintelligence itself, directly compared to the ending of Raiders of the Lost Ark — a species reaching for ultimate power that consumes it instead."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, foundational framework, first delivered in full in Frankly_83 (already built). Nate is real and direct that everything he lists is offered with a genuine 'grain of salt,' explicitly stating he is not an AI expert. Real, direct grounding citation confirmed: obligate technology, from Frankly's own real 'Bend Not Break' series episode two with Daniel Schmachtenberger (already built)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.IdealParentCulturalEcologicalProtocols a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Ideal Parent, Cultural, and Ecological Tether Protocols"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct extension, developed in full in Frankly_95, of Daniel P. Brown's (already built) real, clinical Ideal Parent Figure Protocol — a visualization of the hypothetical parents every child needs (safety, being seen, comfort, delight, and encouragement to take risks and become oneself) — outward to two further, speculative registers. The Ideal Cultural Protocol asks what a healthy culture, currently missing, might provide: belonging (grounded in place and mutual obligation), continuity (intergenerational memory and mythos), ritual and rhythm (collective practices marking life's passages), shared purpose (a coherent cultural north star), and embedded wisdom (practical, ecological, moral knowledge passed on orally, not optimized for clicks). The Ideal Ecological Tether Protocol asks what direct connection to the natural world, largely lost under modern conditions, once provided: attunement (sensory aliveness to one's own place and its seasons), kinship (treating the up to 10 million other species on Earth as relatives, not resources), stewardship (acting as guardian rather than owner or extractor), gratitude (reverent awareness of freely-given ecosystem functions), and constraint (the wisdom to recognize enough and live within one's land's real carrying capacity)."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, foundational framework, first delivered in full in Frankly_95 (already built). Nate is real and direct that he originated this extension himself while reflecting on a real, personal poem he wrote about a friend's own psychological 'pendulum' between craving validation and fiercely resisting control — explicitly speculative, 'I don't know, I've never tried it,' rather than presented as clinically validated the way Brown's own original protocol is."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.Intellectology a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Intellectology"@en ;
    skos:definition "Real, formal field of study proposed by Roman Yampolskiy (already built), first named in his own 2015 paper tgs:Work.TheSpaceOfPossibleMindDesigns (already built): the deliberately human-decentered study of the full, real space of possible minds — biological, artificial, uploaded, or otherwise — treating all minds as instances of software and examining formal properties of that space (its real, proven infinitude, and the size and representational complexity individual minds can take), rather than starting from human cognition as the default reference point. Directly, explicitly named by Nate in TGS_233 (already built) during his real, direct conversation with Yampolskiy."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, independently corroborated field, not a one-off coinage: directly built upon by other researchers (Ziesche and Yampolskiy's own 2016 'Artificial Fun' paper explicitly frames itself as contributing to 'the field of intellectology'), and expanded by Yampolskiy himself in a real, later 2023 treatment, 'Taxonomies of Intelligence: A Comprehensive Guide to the Universe of Minds.' Real, direct intellectual lineage: builds explicitly on Aaron Sloman's 1984 paper 'The Structure of the Space of Possible Minds,' extending Sloman's own real, original call for an interdisciplinary description of mind-space into a formally named field with its own open problems."@en ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.TheSpaceOfPossibleMindDesigns ;
    .

tgs:Concept.WhatWouldChangeMyMind a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "What Would Change My Mind: The Two-Layer Falsifiability Test"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct answer, developed in full in Frankly_158, to a real, direct question posed by a stranger: what would falsify his own core thesis? He distinguishes two genuinely different layers, discovered in that order but arguing the second is causally upstream of the first. Layer one, biophysical/measurable signals (real, direct examples): global fossil fuel consumption falling while GDP grows for five-plus consecutive non-recession years; energy intensity of GDP improving 3-4% annually rather than the historical ~1.2%; wide-boundary EROI (including storage, transmission, mining, recycling, not just at the source) for solar and wind clearing roughly 10:1; a large grid of 10-20 million people surviving a full winter at 80% wind/solar without fossil backup or major interconnection; energy cost per ton of refined copper falling despite ore-grade decline; critical minerals (lithium, cobalt, nickel, copper) recovered at 90% end-of-life and supplying most incremental demand, versus roughly 7.5% today; global debt-to-GDP flat or falling for a decade with no default cycle; sustained 5% annual emissions decline; retreating from seven of nine breached planetary boundaries to three or four. Layer two, cultural/behavioral signals — the real thing Nate argues would actually change his mind, since none of layer one's numbers move without this happening first, roughly a decade earlier: status shifting from consumption/display toward repair and durability; political discourse where candidates advocating consumption taxes and 'we are embedded in the web of life' framing don't lose elections; popular stories whose admired characters need less rather than either colonizing Mars or fighting zombies; personal time shifting from screens and shopping toward household maintenance, care, and volunteering; and a shift from 'who did this to us' toward 'what can we do,' evidenced by unlikely tribal-crossing collaborations (conservative farmers and environmentalists jointly protecting watersheds)."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, foundational framework, first delivered in full in Frankly_158 (already built). Nate is real and direct that the biophysical layer, though it's what he listed first, is actually the lagging indicator — 'they're what a change of heart... of a culture would look like in the physical world ten or more years after that happened.' Real, direct grounding citation confirmed: Donella Meadows' own real 'leverage points' framework (paradigm as the highest-leverage point in a system), already built, directly underlies why Nate treats the cultural layer as causally prior. Real, direct forward reference: Nate names Bill Plotkin (already built via TGS-146) directly, stating plainly 'I have to have him back on the show.'"@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.IslandsOfSanity a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Islands of Sanity"@en ;
    skos:definition "Margaret Wheatley's real, distinct term (from her own real essay, 'Islands of Sanity') for becoming a stable, grounded presence others can turn to as they wake up to the metacrisis -- getting one's own nervous system 'rock solid and sturdy' so as to be a place people can come to, rather than trying to convince or lecture them into awareness. Real, direct, named source in TGS-234 (Sarah Wilson), who credits Wheatley by name. Related in spirit but NOT the same real concept as tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence (Ilya Prigogine's distinct systems-theory term, already used elsewhere in this graph) -- both use similar 'islands' language for small stable points of influence amid instability, but from two different real originators with two different real frames (Wheatley: a personal/interpersonal practice; Prigogine: a systems-science mechanism). Worth keeping distinct rather than treating as interchangeable."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.RobustnessVsPerformance a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Robustness vs. Performance"@en ;
    skos:definition "Living systems survive by being robust -- diverse, redundant, cooperative -- not by being efficient; cheap energy let our civilization chase performance instead."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Olivier Hamant's own real, central, defining distinction (TGS-230): performance is efficacy plus efficiency -- reaching a goal with the least means; robustness is a system's capacity to remain stable and viable despite fluctuation. Real, direct argument: evolution does not select for the most efficient organism, it selects for the most robust one -- species surviving millions of years have to withstand fluctuation long before they can afford to specialize. Human civilization inverted this: cheap, abundant fossil energy substituted an 'abundance of resources' safety net for the real safety net living systems actually use (diversity, redundancy, cooperation), producing what Hamant calls, borrowing Charles Perrow's real term, a 'normal accident' -- a system so complex and interdependent that failure becomes inevitable, not exceptional. Real, direct four determinants of robustness Hamant names explicitly: redundancy (a real plan B), heterogeneity (plan B genuinely different from plan A, not a duplicate), link to the living (dependence on local, living systems rather than only extracted minerals), and deep link to the local territory (social, economic, and material embeddedness in a specific place). Real, direct three-principle synthesis, in Hamant's own words the actual root lesson underneath all three: circularity, cooperation, and robustness itself -- explicitly named as prerequisites for navigating the 21st century 'with joy,' not mere survival tactics."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SevenPersonPerspectives a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Seven-Person Perspectives (Developmental Stages)"@en ;
    skos:definition "John Churchill's own real, direct developmental map (TGS-228), tracking both individual and cultural maturation through a sequence of perspectives, each named by a grammatical person: first-person (isolated self, 'might makes right'), second-person (traditionalism, concrete/black-and-white in-group cognition), third-person (modernism -- objectification, the scientific method, where Churchill locates most current institutional power), fourth-person (postmodernism, horizontal pluralism, recognizing what's been left out -- then, as it matures, a vertical dimension recognizing left-out parts of the self), fifth-person (recognizing language itself as a construct), sixth-person (a stable, trait-level, non-dual ground of being, not merely a passing state), and seventh-person (real, direct interface with 'the logos of the planetary system' -- what Churchill also calls the holon of the whole system). Real, direct central claim: each stage, once dominant for too long, becomes suppressive of the stage below it -- a mechanism Churchill calls 'the shadow,' distinct from but related to Jungian usage."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.LoveWisdom a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Love Wisdom"@en ;
    skos:definition "John Churchill's own real, direct, deliberately hyphenated term (TGS-228) -- distinct from 'love of wisdom' (the literal Greek root of philosophia) -- naming the integration of heart and mind. Real, direct claim: wisdom is the deepening perspective-taking (see tgs:Concept.SevenPersonPerspectives) that itself deepens the felt experience of love; love is what wisdom feels like once it's actually embodied, not merely understood intellectually. Grounded directly in real HeartMath research on heart-coherence, which Churchill extends into a claim that a coherent heart-field is 'in resonance with the electromagnetic field of the planet itself.'"@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EightWorldviewLenses a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Eight Worldview Lenses"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct, formal framework (Frankly-155) for why people examining the same real facts arrive at radically different diagnoses of and remedies for the more-than-human predicament -- explicitly not a straw-man exercise; each of the eight is held by sincere, intelligent people. Each lens supplies both a real, direct villain and a real, direct task: (1) Political -- the other party/authoritarian drift; vote. (2) Religious -- faithlessness or a fated world; repentance/stewardship, or calm acceptance. (3) Class -- concentrated wealth; redistribution. (4) Technology -- regulators and NIMBYs; keep innovating. (5) Community/Culture -- modernity's loss of connection; rebuild ritual and belonging. (6) Animist/Moral -- civilization treating life as inventory; protect and extend standing to the more-than-human. (7) Overshoot -- ourselves, our throughput; degrowth and contraction. (8) Thermodynamic -- ourselves, the one-time energy inheritance; no real single task, since there's no pile of surplus to redistribute and no election that adds BTUs to a depleting stock. Real, direct, central architectural claim: the first six lenses all operate within a floor and a ceiling set by the last two -- thermodynamics (the real story of where the surplus came from) is the floor, overshoot (what the planet can absorb) is the ceiling. Nate directly names his own primary lens as the eighth, thermodynamic one, while explicitly warning against requiring shared diagnosis before shared action -- borrowing Olivier Hamant's own real term 'robustness' directly for what a good solution needs: something people holding any of the eight lenses can work toward for their own real, different reasons."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.TwelveInformationHazards a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Twelve Information Hazards"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct expansion (Frankly-153) of the 'triple information hazard' he first named in Frankly-34, built on philosopher Nick Bostrom's real concept of an information hazard -- a real risk that comes specifically from disseminating TRUE information, not misinformation. The real, direct twelve, in order: (1) Truth to power -- lands on the speaker, endangering anyone naming a real threat to entrenched power. (2) Inoculation -- publishing a strategy against concentrated dark-triad power lets the system develop real, direct antibodies to it. (3) Dual-use -- an accurate map of societal fragility helps both firefighters and arsonists, and the arsonists are better funded. (4) AI ingestion -- everything said publicly now becomes real, direct LLM training data, collapsing the old gap between publishing and a bad actor acting on it. (5) Spotlight -- publicizing a quiet, effective real intervention (land deals, tax loopholes used for conservation) can kill it, since the strategies work precisely because no one is watching. (6) Cassandra timing -- true things said too early cost the messenger credibility, so the correct warning arrives pre-discredited. (7) Self-fulfilling prophecy -- naming a real risk (a bank run) can trigger it via changed aggregate belief, not changed facts. (8) Musical chairs -- describing the real gap between financial claims and the world's actual source/sink capacity can accelerate the very stampede (hoarding, capital flight, prepping) it describes. (9) Despair -- the full truth without real agency or community leaves people worse off than not knowing. (10) Reassurance -- softening the story to avoid hazard 9 leaves society planning for a future none of the analysts privately expect. (11) Half-map -- naming real risks without real strategies leaves an audience with '100% of the emotion' and only half the picture, inviting panic, snake-oil solutions, and demagoguery. (12) The weighing/paranoia hazard, discovered by Nate himself only while assembling this very list: the private, ongoing burden of constantly weighing what can and can't be said becomes its own real, direct hazard, landing on the holder of the information. Real, direct closing claim: none of the twelve argues for silence, only for care -- and the deepest available response to all twelve at once is growing real, direct trust and community at human scale, since a truth hazardous when broadcast to a global microphone can become benign or healing around a kitchen table."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.FiveLensChess a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "5D Chess (Five Lenses)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct metaphor (Frankly-147) for five successively widening vantage points from which the same real situation can be viewed -- not literal dimensions, but five heights of camera pull-back. Lens one: the piece directly across, where the nervous system asks only 'friend or threat,' faster than language, often deciding before a full sentence is heard. Lens two: the piece's whole team/tribe/ideology comes into view, and the real question shifts from 'do I trust this person' to 'is this person one of us.' Lens three: a real, direct aerial view straight down at the board, where sides are visible but not as good/bad -- describing why pieces move (energy, debt, geopolitics) rather than which side deserves to win; Nate's own real, direct warning that dispassionate description from this lens is routinely misheard by lens-one/two listeners as tacit endorsement. Lens four: the whole board turns out to be resting on something living -- climate, biodiversity, the planetary boundaries (7 of 9 already breached). Lens five, widest: the board and the living world sit in a landscape, with a second, different, simpler game board visible in the distance -- what Nate calls 'the long repair' -- and the real, direct question shifts from 'who's the villain on this board' to 'how do we get from this board to that one without flipping the table over.' Real, direct central claim: lenses one and two are evolution's real, fastest, most reinforced 'factory settings' and never go away, but the real skill of our time is learning to hold all five at once, since holding only the wide lenses without any real agency just produces despair at higher resolution."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:altLabel "Five Horsemen of the 2020s"@en ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Four Horsemen of the 2020s"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's converging risks of the decade -- financial overshoot, geopolitics, complexity and supply chains, and the fraying social contract -- with ecological breakdown often added as a fifth; they must be navigated together even if one is solved."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Nate's own real, direct, named term for four real, converging risk categories he's tracked across recent years: financial overshoot, geopolitical shifts, supply chain fragility from complexity unraveling, and the erosion of social contracts. First named directly in Frankly-134, in the real, direct context of the 2026 Iran war, as a real, explicit framework for anticipating which 'seemingly insignificant' single point of failure (his own real example: the black paint shortage that shut down an entire Ford plant after the Fukushima earthquake) might be the next unexpected complexity-driven disruption."@en ;
    skos:altLabel "The Four Horsemen"@en ;
    thinkr:hasAlternateTerm [ a thinkr:AlternateTerm ;
        thinkr:hasAlternateTermType tgs:AlternateTermType.ExactMatch ;
        skos:prefLabel "Five Horsemen of the 2020s"@en ;
        skos:scopeNote "The same framework counted as five: Nate's count varies between episodes, the fifth usually being ecological breakdown or the warming already in the pipeline (Frankly_55, Frankly_79, Frankly_92; the five-horsemen segment of TGS_100). Recorded as an exact match, not a separate concept, by MJSullivan's decision of 2026-10-19." ] ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Nate's own real, recurring framework, its name a real, direct allusion to the biblical Four Horsemen of the Apocalypse (Conquest/Pestilence, War, Famine, and Death), naming several simultaneous, structurally connected challenges that must be navigated even if any single one (including AI) were somehow solved. Nate's own count varies across episodes — stated as four in Frankly_83 (financial overshoot, geopolitics/war, the social contract, and complexity) and expanded to five in Frankly_92 (debt, ecological/resource overshoot, geopolitics, the zero-sum game that emerges once global economic growth stalls, and polarization/breakdown of the social contract alongside accelerating ecological breakdown)."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, recurring framework, referenced directly across multiple already-built episodes in this graph (previously flagged as worth minting in TGS_10's own scopeNote); formalized here from its real, direct enumeration in Frankly_92, with Frankly_83's own earlier four-item version (already built) linked to the same individual given no separate one exists. Real, direct etymological note: 'Four Horsemen' as a phrase for a small number of severe, converging crises is essentially always a direct allusion to the biblical apocalyptic figures (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four_Horsemen_of_the_Apocalypse), not an independent coinage — Nate's own usage borrows that apocalyptic register deliberately. Nate is direct that even full success in one domain (e.g., AI solving some problems 'in service of life') does not remove the need to navigate the others. Frankly_79 (December 2024), which predates Frankly_83, already lists five -- debt, overshoot, geopolitics, complexity and the social contract, plus warming already in the pipeline -- and its show notes point to the five-horsemen segment of TGS_100 (December 2023) as the earlier source. Earlier still, Frankly_55 (March 2024) names 'the four horsemen of the 2020s' and adds 'global heating and ecosystem disruption as a fifth horseman because it's here, it's growing.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "MERGED 2026-10-19 (MJSullivan): tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s, a duplicate minted separately, was folded into this concept and deprecated; its extended definition, scope note and episode links now sit here, so this concept has two thinkr:extendedDefinition values -- the four-item account from Frankly_134 and the four-or-five account from Frankly_83 and Frankly_92. The earliest use in the graph is Frankly_55 (March 2024), not Frankly_134 as the first extended definition says. Etymology: the phrase alludes to the Four Horsemen of the Apocalypse in Revelation 6:1-8 (the white, red, black and pale horses, usually read as conquest, war, famine and death); a 'fifth horseman' is a common modern figure of speech for an added crisis, which is how Nate uses it."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfTheApocalypse ;
    .

tgs:Concept.PredicamentNotProblem a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Predicament, Not Problem"@en ;
    skos:definition "A problem has solutions; a predicament has only better or worse pathways -- so the goal is to navigate, not to solve."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Nate's own real, direct, recurring distinction, stated plainly in Frankly-133: a problem has solutions; a predicament merely has pathways that are better, even if only slightly better, than other pathways. Real, direct implication he draws from it: the more-than-human predicament cannot be 'solved' in the way a problem can, only navigated toward a real, comparatively better outcome among several imperfect ones."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "In Bend Not Break Part 5 (TGS_50) Daniel Schmachtenberger cites Richard Haass (Council on Foreign Relations) for the same distinction -- a problem admits a complete solution, a predicament only ongoing management -- and Nate says the series changed his own language from solutions to responses. Added 2026-10-18."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.MarketReflexivity a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Market Reflexivity"@en ;
    skos:definition "George Soros's own real, direct theory (see tgs:Human.GeorgeSoros): market values are driven by participants' own fallible beliefs and biases, not only by economic fundamentals, and beliefs and events feed back on each other in reflexive loops -- producing procyclical boom/bust cycles rather than the equilibrium standard neoclassical economics predicts. Directly invoked in Frankly-133 as real, historical, short-run support (roughly 90% true, in Nate's own real estimate) for the common belief that financial markets steer reality -- paired directly with Nate's own real, further claim that as biophysical constraints reassert themselves, this correlation likely reverses to negative."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.RocksInTheRiver a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rocks in the River"@en ;
    skos:definition "Grounded people and communities that hold steady as the current speeds up, slowing it and letting new capacity gather in their lee."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Nate's own real, direct metaphor, originating in Frankly-88 ('Snow, the Singularity, and Rocks in the River') and independently confirmed by RR-26's own real citation of it months later: rather than trying to reform the current system directly, cultivate a small, real number of psychologically prepared, grounded individuals within communities who can act as stabilizing anchors -- literal rocks -- slowing the current as broader systems destabilize around them, allowing new capacity to accumulate in their lee the way sediment gathers behind a real rock in a real river. Directly recurs in Frankly-130 as Nate's own closing description of where the platform's actual, real work is now shifting -- local coordination and ecological preparedness, not further wide-boundary commentary."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Added 2026-10-18: Nate already uses the image in Frankly_82 (January 2025), two months before Frankly-88, as a practice of local resilience -- 'consider being a rock in the river': having roots so as not to be swept away when things get tougher, being able to help others, 'and if there's enough of you might be able to redirect the water itself.' Frankly-88 is where it becomes a titled theme. Earliest in the graph: Frankly_52 (January 2024) closes, 'we need more humans acting as rocks in the river as anchors towards this cultural change' (tgs:Concept.VerticalBehavioralStack)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.PurityVsEffectiveness a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Purity vs. Effectiveness"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct pattern (Frankly-125): whenever a powerful, messy, dual-use technology arrives (his own real, direct examples: fossil fuels, the internet, now AI), humans quickly sort it into moral identities and form tribes around embracing or refusing it, turning the choice itself into a signal of personal and tribal purity. Real, direct central worry: actors most committed to narrow-boundary accumulation and control will use whatever tools are available, early and relentlessly, regardless of restraint norms -- meaning voluntary abstention by those trying to protect the web of life can become a genuine coordination disadvantage, not just a moral stance, since 'the ethical choice can start to look like the less effective choice in a wide boundary sense.' Nate's own real, direct, unresolved question closing the pattern: where is the line between integrity and self-disarmament, and what are the rules of engagement for using powerful tools without becoming what you oppose."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.NonDuality a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Non-Duality"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct, recurring theme Nate has raised with at least seven prior guests without ever arriving at a definition he's satisfied with (Frankly-144) -- the wisdom-tradition claim that the felt separation between self and world, observer and observed, is not fundamental but constructed, reinforced directly by Western grammar itself (subject-verb-object sentence structure), by institutions, and by the practice of defining things via boundaries the concept itself has none of. Real, direct central argument connecting it to the metacrisis: the same underlying sense of separateness that makes non-duality hard to grasp is argued to be the root assumption beneath treating the biosphere as a resource warehouse rather than the thing one's own life is materially made of. Real, direct examples given of the separation briefly softening: being lost in music, grief, and standing in a forest at dusk -- moments where, in Nate's own words, 'the default mode network just goes quiet.' Nate's own real, direct closing reframe: the drive to nail down a clean definition may itself be 'the most Western thing' about the difficulty, and loosening the grip on certainty may be the actual, available work, distinct from ever arriving at intellectual understanding."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.LayersOfInvisibleWealth a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Layers of Invisible Wealth"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct, widening-lens inventory (Frankly-118), prompted by his own real, upcoming knee surgery, of things that function as real wealth precisely because they're invisible until absent. Real, direct layers, narrowest to widest: (1) health -- a body that mostly works, forgotten until it doesn't. (2) basic creature comforts -- shelter, clean water, food, treated as baseline rather than the real, historically and globally rare gift they are. (3) relationships -- the people who show up when things go wrong, argued as possibly the single most important, most taken-for-granted layer. (4) meaningful work. (5) spaciousness -- time that feels like it belongs to you, which most people, including Nate himself, tend to fill rather than protect. (6) attention -- eroded specifically by what he calls 'the dopamine smorgasbord,' directly citing guest Anna Lembke's own real finding that checking one's phone roughly 100 times a day (his own real, measured number) constitutes a disruptive addiction. (7) the web of life -- Earth's own real ecological stability, with 7 of 9 planetary boundaries already breached. (8), the widest lens: autonomy or personal sovereignty -- the ordinary freedom to live a self-directed life, which Nate argues is quietly eroding as wealth, data, and compute concentrate, producing a world where 'the rules of life can start changing without a vote.'"@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.StrawSiphonSieve a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Straw, the Siphon, and the Sieve"@en ;
    skos:definition "What technology does to real wealth: the straw draws down nature faster, the siphon concentrates the gains in a few hands, and the sieve filters wealth away from the rest of life toward one species and a narrow slice of it."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Nate's own real, direct three-part model (Frankly-119, extending Frankly-56's own earlier straw metaphor) for what technology actually does to real wealth -- defined here explicitly as usable energy, organized matter, and the stocks and flows that make life possible, not money, which is only a real, direct claim on that wealth. (1) The straw: technology increases the rate at which stocks of the natural world are drawn down -- his own real, direct food-system example, the Haber-Bosch process, widened the nitrogen straw so dramatically that roughly half the nitrogen in a human body today originates from fossil fuels. (2) The siphon: gains from that faster extraction concentrate via real, structural network effects (capital rewards those who can finance it, data compounds, early advantage reinforces itself) and via real, direct choke points -- narrow gates (ports, app stores, platforms) where the toolmaker becomes the rent-taker without needing to produce more value, just control access to it. (3) The sieve: technology filters long-term wealth away from the rest of life and toward one species, and within that species toward a narrow subset -- humans now consume roughly 40% of Earth's net primary productivity, and the built technosphere now outweighs all living biomass combined. Real, direct closing extension: debt is named directly as a fourth, social form of the same pattern (a straw pulling future resources into the present, a siphon concentrating returns to those who can issue credit), and AI is analyzed as the newest technology running through this same real, direct three-part structure, but in the cognitive rather than physical economy."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    skos:scopeNote "Added 2026-10-18: between Frankly-56 and Frankly-119, in Frankly_86 (February 2025), Nate calls the US dollar 'from a maximum power energetic perspective, the mother of all straws,' because it is fungible for everything (tgs:Concept.ThreeTiersOfWealth)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.FifthLawOfThermodynamics a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "The (Aspirational) Fifth Law of Thermodynamics"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct, explicitly-not-a-real-law proposal (Frankly-117), extending tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple (Howard Odum's real Fourth Law -- systems self-organize to capture and use energy as rapidly as possible) with a real, direct time dimension. Real, direct core idea: a self-aware species, understanding that it is itself expressing the Maximum Power Principle, might consciously choose maximum SUSTAINED power over maximum INSTANT power -- optimizing for endurance and stability through time rather than raw throughput. Nate's own real, direct, careful framing: 'not a guarantee, it's an aspiration... a tiny possibility,' explicitly not a real physical law like the first four, but a real, direct question about whether consciousness can alter which outcomes arrive within thermodynamic constraints that themselves cannot be broken. Real, direct literary grounding: Larry Niven and Jerry Pournelle's 'The Mote in God's Eye,' where an alien species (the Moties) understands its own unsustainable growth trap with total clarity but cannot escape it at the biological level -- used directly as a real, cautionary parable for whether intelligence alone guarantees the wisdom needed to actually act on this fifth-law aspiration."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySunkCost a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wide-Boundary Sunk Cost"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct widening (Frankly-116) of the standard economic sunk-cost concept -- a past expense that cannot be recovered and, rationally, should not influence future decisions -- beyond money and time into identity, status, expectations, built environment, and cultural narrative, each argued to grip decision-making more powerfully than financial sunk cost alone. Real, direct layers named: identity (the jobs and roles people organize their sense of self around), built environment (suburbs, highways, and infrastructure that keep requiring more sunk cost just to maintain), households (mortgages, possessions, school choices), communities (city budgets and infrastructure sized for a tax base or population that may no longer exist), and narrative (a century-plus of 'progress equals more material throughput' as itself a sunk cultural cost, reinforced by entire careers and institutions built to keep telling that story). Real, direct central thought experiment: if everyone woke up in identical, durable canvas tents with basic services intact, what wants would resurface as genuinely one's own, once the sunk cost of inherited status and expectation was removed. Real, direct closing analogy: a culture's accumulated sunk-cost branches are like an old, badly-pruned oak tree's own overgrown limbs -- some have to be cut for the tree to survive the next storm, guided by a careful arborist rather than left to tear itself apart."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EightForcesOnPrices a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Eight Forces That Influence Prices"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct wide-boundary framework (Frankly-115) widening Milton Friedman's real, famous claim that inflation is 'always and everywhere a monetary phenomenon' into eight real, direct real-world forces, each tagged inflationary or deflationary. (1) Money creation -- bank lending and central-bank stimulus, inflationary; the real, direct claim that the US dollar's purchasing power has declined roughly 95% in a century. (2) Resource depletion -- thinner, deeper, more scattered ore and energy deposits requiring ever more energy per unit extracted (real, direct example: US copper ore grade falling from 40% in 1800s Butte to 0.4% today), inflationary. (3) Technology -- historically deflationary as repeatable processes get cheaper, until it collides with real resource limits or shifts costs into energy-hungry infrastructure (AI data centers as the real, current example). (4) Affordability -- when the median household runs out of financial capacity, sellers find fewer buyers and prices fall, deflationary; directly tied to the real K-shaped economy. (5) Finance and leverage -- borrowed capital magnifies price moves in both directions, and de-leveraging (real, direct examples: 1998 Long-Term Capital Management, the 2020 negative-$35 oil price, the Japan carry trade) is deflationary. (6) Ecological instability -- droughts, heat waves, and Dunkelflaute (calm, cloudy stretches cutting wind and solar output) directly raise buffer and backup costs; biodiversity and soil loss quietly raise input costs as free ecosystem services (pollination, water retention) degrade -- inflationary. (7) Currency architecture -- the widest container of all; currencies as real, direct social contracts historically lasting around 30 years before reform, with hyperinflation (Weimar Germany, Zimbabwe, Argentina) as rapid currency implosion, real, direct sharply deflationary resets following. (8), left implicit but named directly: complexity itself, since connections in a network scale roughly as the square of its nodes (Metcalfe's Law), meaning the last century's complexification requires ever more energy just to maintain -- setting up 'a Simplification' as complexity's own real, direct reverse. Real, direct closing metaphor, tying directly to tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak: a tightrope walker caught in a net five stories down survives with the same factories, oil, and infrastructure intact, just no longer affordable or sensible to run at the old scale -- bending, not breaking."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ModernSevenDeadlySins a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Modern Seven Deadly Sins"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct, spontaneous reimagining (Frankly-110) of the traditional Christian seven deadly sins for the scale of a global economic Superorganism, explicitly offered as a first pass he may develop further in a dedicated future Frankly. The real, direct seven, in his own words: Certainty (overconfidence about how things will turn out); Righteousness (condescending, single-issue evangelism); Blame (outsourcing the cause of and response to the human predicament onto various out-groups); Borrowing (consuming today by stealing from tomorrow, at both the national-debt and personal level); Apathy (writing off any decent or good future as impossible); Anthropocentrism (centering only humans, now and increasingly as things get harder); and a seventh Nate is least settled on the name of -- Thinning, Unbecoming, or Dissolving -- representing the continued outsourcing of human creativity, cognition, and agency to large language models, AI, and algorithms."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.GenesisPlot a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Genesis Plot"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct term Nate coins on the spot in Frankly-110 ('I just made that up') for a local, hands-on ecological intervention -- a specific plot of land, anywhere on Earth, where a person and their friends or colleagues deliberately work to build better soil, more biodiversity, and greater capacity for life, independent of and in anticipation of the global economic system's own metabolism changing on its own momentum. Directly framed as a real, concrete instance of the already-established tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak interventions, and tied directly to a real, deliberate Gandalf/Balrog callback ('you shall not pass') -- the posture of standing one's ground for a specific local ecosystem against the Superorganism's own further encroachment."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.RedQueenEffect a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Red Queen Effect"@en ;
    skos:definition "As old wells decline, ever more drilling is needed just to hold production flat -- 'it takes all the running you can do to keep in the same place.'"@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "A real, direct term coined for oil-depletion economics (Frankly-109 credits 'Rune Likvern' and The Oil Drum, circa 2012), borrowed from the Red Queen's own real line to Alice in 'Through the Looking-Glass' -- 'it takes all the running you can do, to keep in the same place.' Real, direct application: as existing oil wells decline, ever-more new wells must be drilled just to hold total production flat, let alone grow it -- shown directly via stacked charts of each year's new-well production layered on top of the accumulating decline of all prior years' wells. The same real, direct dynamic is noted as applying to natural gas as well."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.PeakSensemaking a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Peak Sensemaking"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct hypothesis (Frankly-93), modeled explicitly on a carbon-pulse-style bell curve: as circumstances get worse, the desire to understand and 'sense-make' the converging crises does not keep rising indefinitely -- it peaks, then declines, as information overload and apathy set in and people instead want to enjoy their lives, cope, or act locally rather than keep absorbing more facts about what's wrong. Real, direct personal grounding: two friends' real, opposite reactions to the same real Frankly video -- one, an ecological economist who understands the situation deeply, couldn't bring herself to watch it, calling it too heavy; the other found it energizing and took direct local action. Real, direct implication Nate draws for his own platform: society may already be past the peak of wanting more sensemaking, shifting his own intended focus from explaining the biophysical predicament toward community, agency, and cataloging real, on-the-ground pilots rather than continuing to build the case for why the predicament is real."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.OriginalTripleInformationHazard a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Original Triple Information Hazard"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's first account of why some true things are risky to say: saying them can make them come true, can threaten those in power, or -- for quiet good work -- can undo it by putting it in the spotlight."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Nate's own real, direct, original formulation (Frankly-34, June 2023), later explicitly grown into tgs:Concept.TwelveInformationHazards (Frankly-153, 2026). The real, original three: (1) things Nate knows but can't say publicly, because the knowing itself would make the risk self-fulfilling -- maps most directly onto the later list's self-fulfilling-prophecy hazard. (2) A real, direct truth-to-power hazard: describing the erosion of US global power was safe while 'the global tide was rising and lifting all boats,' but becomes genuinely threatening to those in power as that tide recedes -- named identically, by the same real term, in the later twelve-part list. (3) A real, direct, distinct hazard the later list calls the spotlight hazard: Nate has met people doing real, quietly effective conservation and social-finance work in Africa and elsewhere who explicitly don't want their own work publicized, since public awareness would short-circuit its own success -- meaning some genuinely good news can't be shared any more freely than bad news can."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.FourStoriesFramework a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Four Stories"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct articulation (Frankly-100) of four genuinely distinct stories he tells simultaneously across the platform, each aimed at a different willing audience. (1) The energy and systems story: prescription-less, transpartisan, connecting energy, ecology, and evolved human behavior -- suitable for high schools and general audiences, not itself scary. (2) tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak: the real, direct near-term risks -- metacrisis, financial and ecological overshoot, geopolitics -- and how to intervene over the next 5-10 years as these risks intensify; genuinely scary, a different audience than story one. (3) Community and resilience: individual and local response, becoming what Nate calls 'the cultural mitochondria' of what comes next (see tgs:Concept.CulturalMitochondria). (4) A life ethic: advocacy for humanity's connection to and interdependence with the wider web of life, run through all three other stories rather than standing fully separate. Real, direct honest admission: Nate doesn't fully know why this fourth thread matters as much as it does to him, only that it does."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ThreeRisksThreeResponses a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Three Biggest Risks, Three Worst Responses"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct answer (Frankly-100) to a real, two-part listener question. The three biggest systemic risks: complexity (hidden, just-in-time interdependencies where a single missing part can cascade, 'for want of a nail...'); AI (a real, direct concern he says he underweighted until recently, citing a real MIT study on cognitive decline from frequent AI use, and its likely impact on wealth and income inequality); and governance, named directly as 'the granddaddy of all risks' -- how humans make collective decisions at every scale, complicated by the real, direct observation that people who rise to power tend to have self-selected for exactly the supernormal-stimuli-driven traits least suited to navigating a downslope. The three worst individual/collective responses: blame (an ancestral carryover, outsourcing responsibility onto an out-group rather than pausing and reflecting); apathy (the real, direct danger that the gap between first hearing about the metacrisis and becoming unconcerned again 'could be an afternoon,' absent real agency or community); and anhedonia, the clinical term for inability to feel pleasure, which Nate connects directly to AI, addiction, and social media -- naming time in nature as his own real, personal antidote."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.FourCategoriesOfRealWork a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Four Categories of Real Work"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct, explicit answer (Frankly-159) to what 'the real work' actually is, given a predicament rather than a problem -- four areas he names as grossly underrepresented relative to the scale of what's coming. (1) Bend Not Break (see tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak): helping society absorb a real, likely deflationary depression -- a financial 'haircut,' not full systemic collapse -- through relocalized supply chains, expanded local social capital, community scenario planning, and Goldilocks technology. (2) Nuclear disarmament: explicitly avoiding a strategic nuclear exchange during the coming decade of energy descent and geopolitical stress -- work Nate says he does mostly behind the scenes rather than publicly on the platform, naming sane leaders advised by sane technocrats making a cold cost-benefit calculation as the real, most likely danger, not any single 'madman.' (3) Midwifing the biosphere: helping ecosystems and species through the human-caused bottlenecks of the 21st century, explicitly rejecting the 'the planet will be fine' dismissal -- Nate's own real, direct distinction being that an inert planet of jellyfish and roaches is not what's actually at stake, complex and diverse life is. (4) Rekindling the sacred: a multi-generational, beyond-Nate's-own-lifetime project of recovering a felt sense that some things -- a functioning ocean, an unbroken evolutionary lineage -- are not for sale, not substitutable, and cannot be measured in dollars."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CorporationsAsMisalignedAI a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Corporations as the Original Misaligned AI"@en ;
    skos:definition "The argument that we already live with misaligned superintelligences: corporations, immortal multi-agent entities with legal personhood that pursue profit regardless of human or biospheric good."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Nate's own real, direct, striking reframe (Frankly-159), offered in response to being asked why he doesn't prioritize AI alignment risk as one of his own four core categories of work: humanity has already lived for well over a century with a real, direct non-alignment problem between human/biospheric good and the actual behavior of 'soulless, mindless, immortal automatons composed of multiple collaborating agents' -- legally-recognized persons in the US, unlike AI, which is not (yet) a legal person anywhere. The real, direct argument: the hypothetical future problem of a superintelligent AI misaligned with human interests is something humanity is already living through today, just under a different name -- business as usual."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EnergyAdditionsNotTransitions a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S2214629618312246> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:prefLabel "Energy Additions, Not Transitions"@en ;
    skos:definition "There has never been a true energy transition: each new source has been added on top of the old ones rather than replacing them -- 'no green revolution, only a green addition.'"@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "The claim that there has never been a true energy transition -- one dominant source replacing another -- only energy additions: each new source (coal, oil, gas, hydro, nuclear, now wind and solar) has been layered on top of earlier ones, whose use has stayed flat or kept rising. Stated directly by Art Berman in TGS-237 ('there never has been an energy transition... we never transitioned from anything to anything'), with the supporting evidence the show notes attach: traditional biomass use is roughly double its 1800 level (Art said about the same) and coal use is at record highs globally. Berman's practical point is about mindset: the investment world assumes substitution, for which there is no historical precedent -- which does not make it impossible, only low-probability, especially in the near term. Scholarly basis: tgs:Work.EnergyTransitionsOrAdditions (York and Bell, 2019), which argues that renewable growth has so far expanded total energy production rather than displacing fossil fuels, and that calling it a 'transition' is misleading and could inhibit policy aimed at actually reducing fossil fuel use."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Modeled from TGS-237, where Art states it and the show notes cite York and Bell as an 'Episode Foundation.' Whether Nate or other guests use the 'additions' framing in other episodes is not verified here -- check before treating this as specific to TGS-237. Nate's phrasing in Frankly_90 (April 2025): 'there has been no green revolution, only a green addition.' Found earlier in two episodes released a week apart: TGS_161 (Scott Tinker, 29 January 2025), where Nate says the energy transition 'so far has not been an energy transition. It's been an energy addition'; and TGS_162 (Jean-Baptiste Fressoz, 5 February 2025), where Nate calls it 'a phrase that I commonly use' and Fressoz's history (tgs:Work.MoreAndMoreAndMore) goes further, from addition to symbiosis (tgs:Concept.EnergySymbiosis). Tinker offers a contrasting framing, a transition from dense to more dense fuels (tgs:Concept.DenseToMoreDenseTransition)."@en ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.EnergyTransitionsOrAdditions ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.JeanBaptisteFressoz ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EnergyGrowthRateDependence a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:prefLabel "Energy Growth-Rate Dependence"@en ;
    skos:definition "Art Berman's organizing thesis in TGS-237: the global economy -- and the stories it tells about a better future -- depends not just on a high level of energy supply but on a continually rising rate of growth in that supply, yet the rate of growth in total energy supply has been falling since the late 1960s (the essay the show notes cite, 'The Iran War Phase Shift,' puts the peak of total energy-system growth at about 4.6% in 1968). The world has spent 50-plus years adapting cleverly to that shortfall, which is why an abrupt loss of roughly 10 million barrels a day 'rips the Band-Aid off.' A companion point is overhead: at 8.2 billion people, simply maintaining the existing system takes an enormous and growing share of energy, so holding it up today takes more than supplying the whole system, growth included, took 40-50 years ago; oil demand growth has already slowed from roughly 2 to roughly 1 million barrels a day per year. Berman's recurring emphasis is that rates matter more than absolute amounts -- for markets and for system stability alike."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Descriptive label for the thesis Art develops in TGS-237 (and in the essay it cites); neither Art nor Nate coins this term in the episode. Related to but distinct from tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative (debt-based monetary growth, not physical energy-supply growth rates) and tgs:Concept.ComplexityCollapse (the overhead-of-complexity half of the argument is Tainter-like, but Art does not discuss collapse here)."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-10-09 -- from Frankly_161 'Humanity's Icarus Moment'.
#############################################

tgs:Concept.HumanitysIcarusMoment a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Humanity's Icarus Moment (Twenty AI Risks)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own named framework (Frankly_161) of twenty risks along humanity's flight path with AI, organized by the full Icarus myth, in which Daedalus warned of danger on both sides: fly too high and the sun melts the wax, fly too low and sea spray soaks the feathers. THE WINGS -- what AI is made of: (20) fossil lock-in, (19) the appetite for energy and materials, (18) the efficiency rebound, (17) deflation of a debt-financed build-out, (16) loss of robustness through over-optimization, (15) eating the library. THE SEA -- slow risks we do not feel: (14) the broken apprenticeship (tgs:Concept.BrokenApprenticeship), (13) the dumbing down of humans (cognitive debt), (12) ambient language drifting toward the statistical average, (11) the end of shared reality, (10) captured attachment, (9) the children. THE SUN -- fast risks of power and racing: (8) techno-feudalism, (7) the second bifurcation between AI users and non-users, (6) cognitive colonialism, (5) surveillance, (4) the race to AGI and nuclear stability, (3) military fusion and 'flash war', (2) loss of control. (1) is deliberately left blank -- a nod to humility and emergence -- with the observation that what actually killed Icarus was the delight of flying, which nobody warned him about. Framing premise: AI is a second army of trillions of cognitive workers added to a civilization that has never recognized its first army of fossil energy slaves (tgs:Concept.EnergySlaves), and in most cases it acts as an upgrade to the growth-seeking superorganism (tgs:Concept.Superorganism) rather than an escape from it."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Nate's numbering counts down from 20 to 1. He says he cut an original list of 35 to reach twenty, and that many of these risks deserve their own episodes. Related, earlier AI typologies: tgs:Concept.EightFacesOfAI (Frankly_96, how individuals relate to AI) and tgs:Concept.CorporationsAsMisalignedAI (Frankly_159), which risk #2's closing remark echoes without naming."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.BrokenApprenticeship a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Broken Apprenticeship"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's name (risk #14 in Frankly_161) for what happens when companies, one by one, make the economically rational decision to stop hiring at the entry level: keep the senior people, give them a large language model, and skip the young. The routine work being automated -- a paralegal reading a thousand contracts, a junior accountant building spreadsheets -- is how a field transmits knowledge to its next generation, because it is routine, language-intensive and supervised. Judgment, the thing senior people are kept on for, was built by years of doing that work poorly and being corrected. Each firm's choice is correct on a narrow, quarterly-earnings boundary; together they eat the seed corn of every profession at once and invert the pyramid of institutional and professional structures. Within five to twenty years, nobody can do the work without the model, and nobody can tell when the model is wrong."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://digitaleconomy.stanford.edu/publication/canaries-in-the-coal-mine-six-facts-about-the-recent-employment-effects-of-artificial-intelligence/> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Evidence Nate cites: tgs:Work.CanariesInTheCoalMine (employment of 22- to 25-year-olds in the most AI-exposed jobs falling relative to older workers -- 13% in the August 2025 release, 19% in the 12 August 2026 revision). The show notes also point to Matt Beane's research on tacit knowledge and supervised routine work (The Skill Code), not built. A sea-side risk in Nate's framing: slow, and invisible on a narrow-boundary scorecard."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-10-10 -- from TGS_202 (Samantha Sweetwater, 'True Human').
#############################################

tgs:Concept.LogicOfSeparation a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Logic of Separation (Five Domains of Separation)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Samantha Sweetwater's framing (TGS_202; developed in tgs:Work.TrueHuman) of what underlies the metacrisis: a world structured according to 'the logic of separation,' the idea that anything can be separated from anything else -- ultimately a species separating itself from the Gaian processes that made it. She maps it across five domains: (1) an evolutionary age -- a neutral, Gaian epoch in which a species complex enough to tell stories and build technology differentiates a sense of self from the biosphere, punctuated by agriculture, fiat currency and religions that export the divine from place, and culminating in the Anthropocene, with Thomas Berry's Ecozoic or the Symbiocene as what might follow; (2) stories that reify separation, such as Manifest Destiny or humans at the top of an evolutionary pyramid; (3) structures that reify it, such as houses whose walls don't breathe, monocropping, or reducing climate to a single carbon metric; (4) game-theoretic separation -- a growth-dependent, GDP-measured economy whose incentives require people to cut themselves off from what they feel; and (5) ideologies of dominion, particularly patriarchy and racism, which organize power in ways that block a sense of shared belonging. Its opposites are interdependence (a fact of flows of matter, energy and communication) and interbeing (Thich Nhat Hanh's term for the felt, interior recognition of shared reality)."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Sweetwater credits Daniel Schmachtenberger's TGS discussion of the age of separation (TGS_20) for mapping the historical sequence; the five-domain structure is hers. She notes the five domains are not equally charged: the first she treats as neutral, the fifth as 'the edgy zone.' Related: tgs:Concept.Metacrisis."@en ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.TrueHuman ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.PowerOfLifeVsPowerOverLife a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Power of Life vs. Power Over Life"@en ;
    skos:definition "A distinction central to Samantha Sweetwater's tgs:Work.TrueHuman, discussed in TGS_202. Power over life is oriented to narrow goals and discrete objectives -- instrumentalizing work, generating profit, gaining optionality -- without caring for the costs it externalizes, whether physical, mental, emotional or ecological. The power of life is already present everywhere: breathing, a plant exhaling, the pulse of blood, cognition, a mutually meaningful conversation. Her question for each person is how to participate in processes that bind power over life so that the power of life 'wins the game' -- citing Daniel Schmachtenberger's argument that, with existential technology, an unbound power-over-life game becomes omni-lose. Practical forms she offers include withdrawing attention from power games and multiplying non-transactional acts of reciprocity."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Nate says he ends many of his recent presentations with a chessboard image (factories versus trees) captioned 'power versus life?', and in TGS_202 he maps the distinction onto his own framing: the economic superorganism (tgs:Concept.Superorganism), the maximum power principle (tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple) and the outsized influence of dark-triad personalities (tgs:Concept.DarkTriad; Frankly_108). Sweetwater agrees that binding dark-triad influence is one domain of the question. Close to, but distinct from, Nate's own tgs:Concept.InServiceOfLife."@en ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.TrueHuman ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.KinshipJourney a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Kinship Journey"@en ;
    skos:definition "Samantha Sweetwater's term (TGS_202; tgs:Work.TrueHuman) for what she describes as an epochal shift in the primary narrative of personal development, away from the hero's journey (tgs:Concept.HerosJourney). The hero's journey begins when you realize you need to understand yourself; the kinship journey begins when you realize that disconnection is the problem -- which she links to crises of addiction and loneliness, climate anxiety, political distress, and executives realizing their value chains are 'participating in the end of the world.' It is the recognition that a life cannot be meaningful unless you find how you are part of the ecology, in service to something larger than yourself. She frames it as a counter to hyper-individualism, noting that most indigenous societies valued individual sovereignty without treating it as the only thing that mattered."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Sweetwater credits Dan Siegel's description of a shift in personal development from 'me' to 'we' ('MWe'; not built in this graph). In TGS_202 Nate challenges it with multilevel selection (David Sloan Wilson and E.O. Wilson: selfish individuals beat cooperators within groups, cooperative groups beat selfish ones); her answer is that insecurity underlies the emergence of fascism, and that security lies more in the 'we' than in the 'me.'"@en ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.TrueHuman ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.HerosJourney a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Hero's Journey (Monomyth)"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Monomyth"@en ;
    skos:definition "Joseph Campbell's 'monomyth,' set out in The Hero with a Thousand Faces (1949), a term he borrowed from James Joyce's Finnegans Wake: a pattern Campbell found across myths and religions in which a hero leaves the everyday world, undergoes trials and a decisive victory, and returns transformed, with the power to benefit others. Its three phases -- separation (or departure), initiation and return -- Campbell called 'the nuclear unit of the monomyth' and 'a magnification of the formula represented in the rites of passage'; he subdivided them into 17 stages. Widely popularized through George Lucas's Star Wars (1977), Christopher Vogler's 12-stage screenwriting adaptation (The Writer's Journey), and Bill Moyers's 1988 interviews with Campbell, The Power of Myth."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.jcf.org/learn/joseph-campbell-heros-journey>, <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hero%27s_journey> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-10 because Samantha Sweetwater names it in TGS_202 as the dominant narrative of personal development that her tgs:Concept.KinshipJourney is meant to succeed. Her one-line characterization there -- the hero's journey 'begins when you realize that you need to understand yourself' -- is her reading of it as a story of individual self-development; Campbell's own first stage is the call to adventure. Noted criticisms: folklorists such as Alan Dundes and Barre Toelken argue Campbell selected stories that fit the model; others call it Eurocentric and masculine-centered, prompting alternatives such as Maureen Murdock's The Heroine's Journey (1990). Source work: tgs:Work.TheHeroWithAThousandFaces."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.JosephCampbell ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.TheHeroWithAThousandFaces ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.WalrusMovementBlindspots a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.OvershootAndLimits ;
    skos:prefLabel "Key Blindspots of the Walrus Movement"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's named list (Frankly_105) of nine blindspots in what he calls the 'walrus movement' -- a playful placeholder for people championing bold social and ecological ideals, used instead of labels that have become culture-war identities. Organizing claim: a worldview can be morally right but biophysically untenable. The nine, in the companion essay's wording: (1) Profit and Extraction Are Bad; (2) The Main Problem Is Capitalism; (3) Authoritarianism Is a Right-Wing Phenomenon; (4) A Fair Society Means Equal Outcomes; (5) Fossil Fuel Companies Are Primarily to Blame; (6) Renewables Can Replace Fossil Fuels; (7) We Can Print Our Way to Prosperity; (8) Reality Is Subjective; (9) Human Rights Are Unbounded. Each is answered from a biophysical or evolutionary angle: surplus-seeking as optimal foraging, capitalism as software on ancient social-primate hardware in service of the Superorganism, authoritarianism as a stress response of any ideology, money as a claim on energy and materials, and rights as agreements contingent on surplus."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-key-blindspots-of-the-walrus> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Framed by Nate as the counterpart to tgs:Concept.TenCoreMythsOfBusinessSchools (Frankly_99), after viewers asked him to critique the other side. He declined to write a matching list for conservatives, saying that critique would concern values and narrow boundaries rather than facts and systems. Related: tgs:Concept.PurityVsEffectiveness (purity politics), tgs:Concept.Wetware (evolved hardware), tgs:Concept.MoneyAsClaimOnEnergy and tgs:Concept.CantillonEffect (blindspot 7), tgs:Concept.EnergyAdditionsNotTransitions (blindspot 6)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CantillonEffect a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.MonetarySystemAndGrowth ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cantillon Effect"@en ;
    skos:definition "The observation, named for the eighteenth-century economist Richard Cantillon, that newly created money does not reach everyone at once: those closest to where it enters the economy (banks, asset markets, government contractors) benefit first, before prices rise, while those furthest away (wage earners and the poor) meet the higher prices later. Nate uses it in Frankly_105 to argue that money printing works as a regressive tax: owners of stocks and real estate see their net worth balloon while the poor feel rising rents and food and fuel prices 'earlier and harder.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://fee.org/articles/the-cantillon-effect-because-of-inflation-we-re-financing-the-financiers/> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for Frankly_105's critique of Modern Monetary Theory (blindspot 7 of tgs:Concept.WalrusMovementBlindspots). Nate extends it two ways: taxes that later claw back new money arrive after the gains have become hard-to-tax assets, so even progressive taxes leave the first-round skew in place; and because the dollar is the reserve currency, US money creation exports inflation to countries that borrow or buy food, fuel and raw materials in dollars. Related: tgs:Concept.MoneyAsClaimOnEnergy."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.PriceCostAndValue a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Price, Cost and Value"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Blueberry moment"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's three-way distinction (Frankly_104): price is what the sticker says; cost is the full ecological and replacement cost, which never appears on the sticker; value is what the thing actually does for us. The price shapes the story we tell ourselves about what something is for -- the same pint of blueberries was a snack for his ducks at $1 and too good for them at $5 -- so cheapness drives consumption the price does not justify. Applied to energy: fossil fuels are priced far below both their cost (environmental damage) and their value (a gallon of gasoline at $3 delivers, in his estimate, perhaps a thousand times that in wide-boundary benefit), so we burn them 'like feeding blueberries to ducks.' A 'blueberry moment' is noticing that a price, not the thing itself, changed how you treat something."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for Frankly_104. Related: tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness (energy treated as just another priced input), tgs:Concept.EnergySlaves (the value side of fossil energy, from Reality Blind's 'Our Fossil Armies' chapter), tgs:Concept.EightForcesOnPrices (Frankly_115, what drives prices), tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySunkCost (Frankly_116, another wide-boundary widening of a standard economic idea)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.GhostOfDopaminePast a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.BehavioralEconomicsAndCognition ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Ghost of Dopamine Past"@en ;
    skos:definition "Past high stimulation raises the baseline dopamine we need to feel normal, so quiet, ordinary pleasures stop registering and we can only imagine futures that keep the stimulation high."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Nate's term (Frankly_103) for the way our recent and distant past behavior carves neural pathways that require ever-higher baseline dopamine to feel normal. Because dopamine signals reward prediction error (tgs:Concept.RewardPredictionError), a life of constant high stimulation -- notifications, metrics, games -- raises the expected reward, and quiet, ancestrally normal experiences (a doe and fawn on the road, half an hour on a park bench watching birds, reading a book) no longer register. A hungry ghost of dopamine past produces an over-consumptive 'ghost of dopamine present' and narrows the 'ghost of dopamine future': we can only imagine futures that keep us on the high treadmill. Remedies he offers: dopamine fasting, a pause between trigger and response, lowering the stimulation baseline (grayscale phones), and socializing the dopamine."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for Frankly_103. Nate presents it as a driver mainly in the technologically connected global North and West, with implications for planetary boundaries because cultural reward-seeking runs through material consumption. Related: tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli, tgs:Concept.Wetware, tgs:PersonalIntervention.LowerDopaminePeaks (the grayscale-phone practice he credits to Audrey Tang). Earlier passing use: Nate names the ghost of dopamine past in Frankly_101 (published 11 July 2025), three weeks before this episode. The phrase appears earlier, in Frankly_53 (January 2024): 'the ghost of dopamine past caused us to live in the mental shallows of the pool,' making attention span a form of wealth (tgs:Concept.HavesAndHaveNots)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.RewardPredictionError a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.BehavioralEconomicsAndCognition ;
    skos:prefLabel "Reward Prediction Error"@en ;
    skos:definition "The finding, from Wolfram Schultz and colleagues' recordings of midbrain dopamine neurons in monkeys, that dopamine signals the difference between expected and actual reward rather than reward itself: neurons fire most when a reward is better than expected, not at all when it matches expectation, and below baseline when an expected reward fails to arrive. Once a cue reliably predicts a reward, the firing moves from the reward to the cue. Dopamine therefore works as a learning signal, which explains adaptation to good circumstances and why anticipated pleasures feel smaller than unexpected ones."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wolfram_Schultz> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for Frankly_103, where Nate describes the experiment in detail as the foundation for tgs:Concept.GhostOfDopaminePast and draws out implications for addiction, motivation, decision-making and planetary boundaries. Landmark paper: Schultz, Dayan and Montague, 'A neural substrate of prediction and reward,' Science (1997)."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.WolframSchultz ;
    .

tgs:Concept.WetBulbTemperature a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wet-Bulb Temperature"@en ;
    skos:definition "A measure combining heat and humidity; at a sustained wet-bulb temperature of about 35 °C, humans can no longer cool themselves by sweating, even in shade."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "A single measure combining heat and humidity: the lowest temperature to which air can be cooled by evaporating water into it, which is how hot conditions feel to a body cooling itself by sweating. The higher the dew point, the more moisture in the air and the harder it is for sweat to evaporate. At a sustained wet-bulb temperature of about 35 C (95 F), humans can no longer cool themselves even in shade with a breeze and unlimited water, which is why rising dew points and wet-bulb events are a growing concern in a warming world."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.weather.gov/tsa/wbgt> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for Frankly_102, where Nate arrives at it through 'corn sweat': an acre of corn transpires 3,000 to 4,000 gallons of water a day (fast-growing GMO varieties perhaps 6,000), raising the local dew point by 3 to 5 F during Midwestern summers (Ohio State Extension, linked in the show notes). The 35 C figure is the commonly cited theoretical limit; later laboratory work suggests the practical limit for many people is lower."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ClimateCreditCrunch a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Climate-Induced Credit Crunch"@en ;
    skos:definition "The warning, set out in 2025 by Allianz board member Günther Thallinger, that climate risk threatens the financial system through insurance: when property can no longer be insured, it cannot be mortgaged, banks stop lending against it, and credit markets freeze -- not only for housing but for infrastructure, transportation, agriculture and industry. The economic value of whole coastal and wildfire-prone regions would 'begin to vanish from financial ledgers' and markets would reprice 'rapidly and brutally.' His conclusion: 'There is no capitalism without functioning financial services, and there are no financial services without the ability to price and manage climate risk.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for Frankly_102, where Nate reads the post at length. His own additions: he doubts markets can solve it, since they price risk while also requiring growth (money is created without the interest needed to repay it -- tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative, tgs:Concept.Superorganism), and he expects credit risk to arrive before 1.5, 1.75 or 2 degrees of warming. He describes ecology and finance as two conversations with a growing overlap."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.GuntherThallinger ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EmpathyHandicap a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Empathy Handicap"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's term (Frankly_102) for the possibility that people with a wider boundary of care carry a heavier psychological load. Responding to data showing higher rates of mental-health diagnoses among young US liberals, especially liberal women -- a pattern online commenters mocked as weak-mindedness -- he proposes instead that progressives tend to have a wider boundary of empathy and concern, and that women tend to have shallower discount rates than men (weighing the future more heavily), so those attending to the sixth mass extinction, ocean change and climate decades ahead have more to worry about than people who do not care or think it a hoax."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for Frankly_102. Offered by Nate as a hypothesis he wants to explore with a guest, not a finding; he notes he could not trace the source of the viral chart but found a published figure (linked in the show notes) showing the same pattern. Related: tgs:Concept.DiscountRatesAndPresentBias."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.WhatIWantToWant a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "What I Want to Want"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's framing (Frankly_101) of second-order desire: not what we want, which is shaped by youth, culture, advertising, status games and the 'ghost of dopamine past,' but what we would want to want, judged from a future vantage point perhaps beyond our own lifetimes. His own list of thirteen: basic needs; stability; agency; restraint ('guardrails for the commons'); full-spectrum love; friendship and networks; community; a craft; entertainment, novelty and play; curiosity, wonder and awe; daily access to the natural world; a non-human companion; and purpose. Most are internal and durable 'in good times or bad, with money or with no money'; none requires yachts, skyscrapers or brand names, since once basic needs are met 'most of the best things in life are free or close to it.' He argues any real conversation about the future should start here, with desire, rather than with technology or policy."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for Frankly_101. Presented as personal foundations of humanity rather than goals, with an invitation for viewers to write their own list. Nate ties the gap between what we want and what we would want to want to sunk cost in culture and in our own lives (see tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySunkCost, Frankly_116). Related lists: tgs:Concept.CulturalMitochondria (Frankly_98), tgs:Concept.TenThingsWorthMoreThanGold (Frankly_106)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CollectiveMaturity a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Collective Maturity"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's framing (Frankly_160) of the response the predicament asks of us: 'I'm not going to ask you for optimism, and I'm not asking you to despair. I am merely asking you for maturity.' Maturity here means recognizing, at scale, that the energy system that delivered unprecedented material abundance is the same one now threatening the ecological stability we depend on; accepting that 'there are no answers to what we face. There are only responses,' and that those responses will not be fair, since those who did least to cause the damage will suffer most; and acting anyway, because responses aggregate -- in how we grow food, how much and what kind of energy we use, and how we treat our neighbors, human and animal -- moving the future between 'the default and the possible.' Its emotional stance is facing what comes 'with open eyes and hearts.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for Frankly_160, whose show notes describe the moment as 'a test of collective maturity' and add that there is 'no single solution or clean reset.' Related: tgs:Concept.PredicamentNotProblem (responses, not answers), tgs:Concept.FrameworkForAction (Frankly_132, responses that aggregate), tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament; the open-eyes stance echoes Frankly_152 ('Watch With Both Eyes Open')."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsFrameworks, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    skos:prefLabel "Multipolar Trap"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Moloch"@en ;
    skos:definition "A race in which each actor's rational choice makes everyone worse off, and no one can stop alone without losing -- 'what is best for all of us is not what is best for each of us.'"@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "A coordination failure in which many competing actors each act rationally in their own interest and together produce an outcome that is worse for all of them, while no single actor can step out without being outcompeted. Nate's plain-language version (Frankly_160): if all of nearly two hundred nations used less energy together, all would benefit, but a country that restrains itself alone falls behind economically and militarily, and its leaders are replaced by someone promising growth -- 'What is best for all of us is not what is best for each of us.' A widely used term in TGS conversations about the metacrisis, closely related to the collective action problem and the tragedy of the commons."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://conversational-leadership.net/multipolar-trap> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for Frankly_160, whose show notes link both the multipolar trap and the collective action problem (with a Reality Blind chapter) at the point where the speech explains why governments have failed to act for fifty years. The term is most associated in TGS with Daniel Schmachtenberger's conversations on the metacrisis; now linked from those episodes. Daniel's fullest construction of it on TGS is in Bend Not Break Part 2 (TGS_20): the buffalo hunters who restrain themselves do not save the buffalo but only cede power to those who do not ('I have the ability to kill it, but not the ability to protect it, therefore I actually should race'); its general cases are the tragedy of the commons (tgs:Concept.TragedyOfTheCommons), the arms race (nuclear and AI weapons, where agreements cannot be verified) and the market race for first-mover advantage (tgs:Concept.OpportunityRiskAsymmetry); 'unless everybody can, nobody can.' Binding it needs transparency, enforcement and incentives, and remains an orienting question (tgs:Concept.ImpossibleToHard). Hyper-agents are caught in it too (tgs:Concept.HyperAgents). Related: tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative, tgs:Concept.Superorganism."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    skos:altLabel "Moloch trap"@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ProtectTheIrreversible a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsFrameworks, tgs:Subject.OvershootAndLimits ;
    skos:prefLabel "Protect the Irreversible"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's compass for planning under uncertainty (Frankly_151): some losses can be rebuilt and some cannot, so spend disproportionate effort protecting the irreversible and accept more risk on the reversible. At the scale of one person the distinction is obvious -- you can make back $100,000 but cannot regrow an arm -- but civilizational planning weighs a dollar of lost GDP the same as an inch of lost topsoil. Reversible: money, market positions, consumer goods, jobs and careers. Irreversible or practically irreversible: topsoil, fossil aquifers such as the Ogallala, species, pollinator populations, social trust, institutional knowledge, the nuclear taboo, children's developmental windows, and personal health past a threshold. Current culture inverts the priority, optimizing quarterly earnings and GDP while letting the irreversible erode; the correction is to be willing to lose money, efficiency or comfort to protect what cannot come back."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for Frankly_151, whose title is this principle. Closely related to tgs:Concept.ShortfallRisk (Frankly_138), which names the danger of something essential falling below an unrecoverable threshold; this concept is the prioritization rule that follows from it. Nate frames it as the response to two failure modes: type II errors of omission and type I errors of acting on a partial map -- above all, treating permanent growth as certain. Related: tgs:Concept.RobustActions."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.RobustActions a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsFrameworks, tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Robust Actions"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's term (Frankly_151) for actions that produce lasting value whichever composite future arrives, so they can be taken decisively without certainty -- in contrast to speculation, which 'bets everything on one outcome' and loses everything if wrong. His examples: building soil health; food security and ecological resilience; strengthening relationships with people within walking distance, since community ties built before a crisis are 'exponentially more valuable' than ones built during it; learning practical skills (food production, repair, basic medical knowledge, water management, building, teaching); reducing dependence on fragile overseas supply chains; and protecting local watersheds, pollinators and soil biology. He likens the logic to ecological resilience: organisms robust across many environments persist through change even if less optimized for any one, so under real uncertainty 'robustness beats out optimization.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for Frankly_151. Contrast: tgs:Concept.UnicornStrategies, deliberately focused commitments that are not hedged across futures; Nate suggests most people combine a base of robust actions with one or two unicorn commitments. Related: tgs:Concept.RobustnessVsPerformance (Olivier Hamant, TGS_230), tgs:Concept.ProtectTheIrreversible, tgs:Concept.LandscapeOfPossibleFutures (robust actions as switchbacks in almost every landscape)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.PersonalCompositeFutures a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsFrameworks ;
    skos:prefLabel "Personal Composite Futures (Foundations Before Outcomes)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's personal-scale version (Frankly_150) of the four composite worlds from his How to Think About the Future series. Two dimensions we navigate: throughput (is your material throughput and complexity expanding or simplifying, and is that regenerative or extractive for you?) and agency (who controls your time and attention, and who captures the value -- your biology, social expectations, financial obligations and attention platforms standing in for military, political, financial and technological power). Two slower foundations that constrain both: the body and nervous system ('your personal earth system,' which 'bats last') and the relational web (the people who would come at two in the morning). Combined, they give four personal composites: personal Long Repair (deliberate simplification, real agency, intact foundations -- the only regenerative path, and one that looks like failure by culture's growth-and-productivity scoreboard); personal Mordor (expanding throughput and captured agency while drawing down health and relationships); personal Fortress (prepared but isolated, an illusion of control); and personal Unraveling (several areas failing at once, setbacks arriving before recovery). The principle: foundations before outcomes -- no amount of good decisions about throughput and agency makes up for crumbling foundations."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-foundations-before-outcomes> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for Frankly_150. Maps to the four tgs:Scenario composite worlds of Frankly_148 (the Long Repair, More Mordor, Fortress World, the Unraveling) without asserting a formal link, matching the series' existing practice. Includes Nate's term 'personal switchbacking' for unglamorous foundation work -- sleep, walks, cooking, unhurried time with others, putting the phone down -- an individual-scale application of tgs:Concept.LandscapeOfPossibleFutures. He also carries over the series' asymmetry: under stress it is easier to slide from personal Long Repair into personal Fortress than to climb from personal Mordor into personal Long Repair. Related: tgs:Concept.PersonalAgency, tgs:Concept.PerpetualMulligans, tgs:Concept.RedQueenEffect."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.HealingVsDying a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsFrameworks, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Healing vs. Dying (Stable Is Not Good)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's argument (Frankly_148) that a culture calibrated to growth cannot easily tell a healing contraction from a dying one. A system can be 'rock solid stable and deeply ill at the same time': Mordor Persists is stable 'the way cancer is stable' -- self-perpetuating and, by the body's own measures, vigorous -- while it eats its host, and financial markets, quarterly earnings and GDP 'are basically measuring blood flow to a tumor and calling the patient healthy.' Conversely the Long Repair, the future most worth striving toward, reads as decline by every current yardstick (consumption, GDP, physical wealth) and so triggers a gut-level dread, because our nervous systems cannot distinguish 'the tumor is shrinking because the patient is dying' from 'the tumor is shrinking because the patient is healing.' The Superorganism resists the Long Repair the way a body resists a treatment that makes you feel worse before you feel better. Nate calls this conflation, at the level of a whole culture, possibly 'one of the deepest challenges in the entire more than human predicament.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for Frankly_148. Carried forward by Nate in Frankly_150 (at the scale of one life: the personal future most worth building 'looks like failure' to a culture measuring growth and productivity) and recapped in Frankly_151. Builds on Part 3's point that stable is not the same as good (tgs:Concept.LandscapeOfPossibleFutures). Related: tgs:Concept.PersonalCompositeFutures, tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SingleStoryThinking a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.BehavioralEconomicsAndCognition ;
    skos:prefLabel "Single-Story Thinking"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's name (Frankly_138) for the human default of holding one storyline about the future and filtering everything else through it -- the viewer convinced of imminent economic collapse finds ecological restoration a luxury; the one convinced AI will change everything finds soil biology quaint; the one who thinks global heating is a hoax finds climate episodes a waste of time. He sees three reasons it persists: nervous systems want certainty, because holding several possibilities open is metabolically expensive and resolution lets the mind stop working; careers and identities attach to particular futures (green growth, techno-optimism, peak oil, collapse awareness, colonizing Mars), so people 'defend their futures much the same way they defend their families'; and institutions reward confident stories over honest uncertainty. Most competing storylines are not wrong but incomplete -- different slices, layers or time horizons of one coupled system. Religion was the older version, a single story held by everyone around you, and deep cultural stories (progress as the natural shape of history, humans separate from nature, more stuff is better) still shape what people think possible more than data does."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for Frankly_138. The problem to which tgs:Concept.ScenarioThinking is Nate's answer; he frames the move as going from forecasting (trying to be right) to scenario thinking (being prepared for several futures and contributing to the better ones), and says both dominant cultural narratives -- 'everything will be fine because technology' and 'everything's going to collapse' -- lead people to check out. Show notes link a talk titled 'The Danger of Single Story Thinking' and research on narrative as active inference (tgs:Concept.NarrativeAsActiveInference). Related: tgs:Concept.Wetware, tgs:Concept.TerrorManagementTheory."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.FivePathwaysThroughDread a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Five Pathways Through Dread"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's framework (Frankly_142) for metabolizing dread rather than being consumed by it, offered as directional, not as silver bullets: (1) mental reframing -- not denying the data but choosing, among true statements, the ones that leave you able to act ('we are on a historical precipice... and I am alive to witness and participate in this' rather than 'we are doomed'), after Viktor Frankl; (2) the body -- long exhales, vagal toning, cold water, walking outdoors, and Peter Levine's somatic discharge of an incomplete fight-or-flight response; (3) agency -- a real action within reach sends the threat system a different signal, since dread is 'being acted upon by a future that you cannot influence'; (4) community -- dread held with others is lighter because nervous systems co-regulate, and lonely dread is the worst kind; (5) befriending the darkness -- for those with baseline stability, going into dread rather than around it (Buddhist, Stoic and mystical traditions, Francis Weller, Joanna Macy) until it transforms not into hope but into presence. Paired with five steps for the moment dread arises: name it out loud (Daniel Siegel's 'name it to tame it'), locate it in the body, take five long exhales, do one thing within reach today, and find one person to sit with this week."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Dread-Guide.png> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for Frankly_142 (A Guide to Staying Human, Part 2). The dcterms:source is the show notes' one-page 'A Practice for Dread' guide. Builds on tgs:Concept.PersonalAgency (Part 1) and leads into tgs:Concept.Presence and tgs:Concept.DefaultModeNetwork (Part 3, Frankly_143). Nate notes that the fifth pathway assumes some stability and that people in acute crisis should seek professional support (the show notes give the 988 Lifeline). Related: tgs:Concept.Dread, tgs:Concept.AllostaticLoad."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.AllostaticLoad a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Allostatic Load"@en ;
    skos:definition "The cumulative physiological wear from chronic or repeated stress-response activation. Nate's account (Frankly_142): the amygdala, HPA axis and cortisol response evolved for threats that are immediate, brief and physical, after which the cortisol clears; slow-moving, abstract, systemic threats trigger the same machinery but offer no running or fighting to discharge it, so cortisol stays elevated for weeks or years. The cost: impaired hippocampal memory formation, a weakened prefrontal cortex (planning and impulse control), dysregulated sleep and immune function, and a push toward addictive behaviors that temporarily silence the alarm -- narrowing the cognitive bandwidth needed to face the very threat being dreaded."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allostatic_load> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for Frankly_142; Nate returns to it in Frankly_150 (show notes link 'cortisol, allostatic load' where he describes running on 'caffeine and cortisol and compulsions'). The physiological mechanism beneath tgs:Concept.Dread."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.FivePathwaysToPresence a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Five Pathways to Presence"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's framework (Frankly_143) for returning from future capture to the present, offered as directions rather than a sufficient program: (1) the senses -- 'the mind cannot be in the future and in the body at the same time,' so ask what you hear, see and touch; somatic attention quiets the default mode network (Thich Nhat Hanh: drink the tea, wash the dish, walk the path); (2) the pause -- notice the almost subliminal window between the impulse to reach for the phone, news or email and the reaching, and what the reaching takes you away from; (3) single-tasking -- the brain does not do two attentional tasks at once but switches badly, so multitasking is 'one of the great lies' of the late industrial era; (4) beauty -- after Iain McGilchrist, the nervous system is built to be arrested by beauty, and letting yourself be stopped by it 'is the practice of presence'; (5) the gift of finitude -- the metacrisis-aware person's sharpened sense that everything is contingent and unrepeatable, which held one way drives dread and held another drives presence, since the present is the only place contingent things can be received. With three daily practices: don't scold yourself for leaving (the coming back is the practice), choose one daily encounter to be fully present for, and receive each thing as if it were the last."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/practice_for_presence.jpg> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for Frankly_143 (A Guide to Staying Human, Part 3); the dcterms:source is the show notes' one-page 'A Practical Guide for Practicing Presence.' Companion to tgs:Concept.FivePathwaysThroughDread (Part 2): dread is what future capture feels like, and these pathways address what it does to attention. Nate distinguishes three kinds of leaving the present: healthy mental time travel that returns, drift, and tgs:Concept.FutureCapture. Related: tgs:Concept.Presence, tgs:Concept.DefaultModeNetwork, tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.UphillAndDownhillFutures a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Uphill and Downhill Futures"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's claim (Frankly_145) that futures are not equally reachable: downhill futures are the path of least resistance that current momentum and incentives already favor and that take no new energy or coordination to reach, while uphill futures take sustained effort to reach and sustained effort to remain in. The easiest futures are not the most desirable; high-trust, coordinated systems such as democracy are energetically costly to maintain, while degraded, low-trust, coercive systems are 'in a thermodynamic sense, the lower energy state' that systems roll toward when effort stops. In 2026 he names economic contraction plus adversarial geopolitics plus concentrated power as the downhill path, and contraction with cooperative geopolitics and shared power as possible but uphill. Uphill is not impossible (living cells, spawning salmon and redwoods lifting water all succeed at uphill projects); it means the climb has costs and stopping short means sliding back. The tilt shifts over time as energy descent, climate change and biodiversity loss reshape the terrain, and it depends on where one stands."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-uphill-futures-in-a-downhill> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for Frankly_145. Complements tgs:Concept.LandscapeOfPossibleFutures, which holds the terrain vocabulary (valleys, ridges, switchbacks, erosion); this concept holds the directional claim about which way the terrain tilts. Related: tgs:Concept.TheSenecaEffect (the asymmetry of growth and collapse), tgs:Concept.AlternativeStableStates."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EpigeneticLandscape a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Epigenetic Landscape (Waddington Landscape)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Conrad Waddington's 1950s image of cell development as a ball rolling down a surface of branching valleys: a stem cell starts with many possible fates, and at each fork some close off until it settles into a committed type (heart, liver, neuron) that it will not leave without extraordinary intervention. Valleys differ in depth and the ridges between them in height, so some transitions between cell states are easy and others need enormous energy or rare conditions. Set out in The Strategy of the Genes (1957). Nate uses it in Frankly_145 as the biological source of his landscape of civilizational futures."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://journals.biologists.com/dev/article/152/14/dev204617/368764/From-genes-to-patterns-five-key-dynamical-systems> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.ConradWaddington ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 as the external concept behind tgs:Concept.LandscapeOfPossibleFutures, which is Nate's reframing of it for societies."@en ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.TheStrategyOfTheGenes ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.AlternativeStableStates a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Alternative Stable States"@en ;
    skos:definition "The ecological finding that a system can settle into more than one self-reinforcing state under similar conditions, and that the shift between them is often asymmetric (hysteresis): a tipping point is crossed quickly, but returning needs far more than removing the original stressor. Nate's example in Frankly_145 is a lake: a few years of nutrient runoff can flip a clear, plant-rich lake into a murky, algae-dominated one held in place by a self-perpetuating loop, while flipping it back, even after the runoff stops, can take decades because the plants, oxygen, fish and nutrient cycles must all be rebuilt. He applies the same asymmetry to societies: a slide from democracy to authoritarianism can take months, the return a generation or more."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://esd.copernicus.org/articles/15/653/2024/> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for Frankly_145. In Nate's terrain vocabulary these are the 'valleys' of tgs:Concept.LandscapeOfPossibleFutures; the asymmetry is what he links to tgs:Concept.TheSenecaEffect."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EscalationTrap a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    skos:prefLabel "Escalation Trap"@en ;
    skos:definition "Robert Pape's three-stage model of how a militarily successful attack becomes a strategic failure (TGS_235). Stage one: the attacker's bombs hit their aim points and kill the leaders, a tactical success. Stage two: the attacked side does not collapse but gains power, motivated by survival, so the attacker has failed strategically. Stage three: the attacker cannot simply walk away, because withdrawing would leave the opponent's new power in place, so it keeps debating and attempting further escalation. Pape says his twenty years of modeling US strikes on Iran always produced this pattern, that other escalation traps exist in history, and that the 2026 Iran war is the worst: Iran's selective, orchestrated control of the Strait of Hormuz gave it leverage over a fifth of the world's oil, unlike North Vietnam after 1975, so the US could not exit as it did from Vietnam."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://escalationtrap.substack.com/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RobertPape ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_235. Pape names his running Substack analysis after it (tgs:Work.EscalationTrapSubstack). Related: tgs:Concept.SecondPrecisionRevolution (the means by which Iran gained power in stage two), tgs:Concept.PunishmentStrategy (why escalation by punishment deepens the trap)."@en ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.EscalationTrapSubstack ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SecondPrecisionRevolution a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    skos:prefLabel "Second Precision Revolution"@en ;
    skos:definition "Robert Pape's term (TGS_235) for the rise of cheap, mass-produced drones that let weaker states and groups withstand, and impose costs on, far stronger militaries. In the 2026 Iran war, Iran used mass drones together with naval mines to control the Strait of Hormuz selectively, without an army, and the same technology let Iran, the Houthis and Iraqi militias threaten Gulf pipelines, refineries and US bases. Pape also credits drones, built on a professional officer corps and logistics, for Ukraine's recent gains against Russia, and notes that Gulf states could run drone-based interdiction themselves."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://energi.media/news/robert-pape-cheap-drones-are-ending-the-era-of-american-military-dominance/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RobertPape ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_235. Related: tgs:Concept.EscalationTrap."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.PunishmentStrategy a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.Geopolitics, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Punishment Strategy (Coercion by Punishment)"@en ;
    skos:definition "A coercive strategy that tries to change an opponent's behavior by imposing rising costs on its leaders, people or military, with the threat of more pain to come. Robert Pape analyzes it against other coercive strategies in Bombing to Win (1996) and argues in TGS_235 that it generally fails because people under punishment respond with fight or flight, not cooperation, and rally behind their government. He defines a strategy by its mechanism, not its tools: action, mechanism, outcome. On that definition Trump has a consistent strategy, punishment, used with tariffs and at home as well as against Iran, where it escalated from killing leaders to bombing to an April 2026 blockade that punished the whole society and made the IRGC more popular. Historical example: the German Blitz on London and Coventry led the British public to demand payback, and Churchill replaced his air staff and backed Arthur Harris's area bombing of German cities."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://cpost.uchicago.edu/publications/bombing_to_win_air_power_and_coercion_in_war> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RobertPape ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_235. Nate's on-air gloss, '5D reverse spite,' links it to tgs:Concept.RelativeFitnessSpiteFramework: the punisher harms itself most over time. Pape's related 1997 article tgs:Work.WhyEconomicSanctionsDoNotWork applies the same logic to sanctions."@en ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.BombingToWin ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    .

tgs:Concept.OffshoreBalancing a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    skos:prefLabel "Offshore Balancing"@en ;
    skos:definition "A grand strategy in which a great power keeps a regional balance of power mainly through local actors, intervening directly only at the margins rather than garrisoning the region itself. In TGS_235 Robert Pape proposes it as the alternative to escalating or walking away in the Iran war: a multi-year strategy aimed at regional stability rather than regime change, with the US doing about 20% of the heavy lifting and local forces the rest. His examples are the campaign that defeated ISIS (US air power supporting a ground coalition including the Syrian Kurds) and US advisers who, after Russia took Crimea in 2014, helped Ukraine build a professional officer and NCO corps and logistics. For the Gulf he recommends military advisers to help the frontline Gulf states build militaries able to run operations such as tanker interdiction themselves."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Offshore_balancing> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_235. An established realist concept (the show notes link the general Wikipedia article), not Pape's own coinage; his Gulf version and its examples are his. He thinks it becomes feasible only after the 2026 midterms, when leaders would have a long enough time horizon."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ClimateSensitivity a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Climate Sensitivity"@en ;
    skos:definition "How much the planet warms in response to a doubling of atmospheric CO2 (from the pre-industrial 280 ppm to 560 ppm), set by feedbacks from clouds, water vapor and snow and ice albedo. As Zeke Hausfather explains in TGS_236, the IPCC's last assessment gave a very likely range of 2 to 5°C (best estimate 3°C), with more uncertainty on the high side. Three timescales: transient climate response (about 20 years around the time of doubling), equilibrium climate sensitivity (300 to 500 years, once fast processes and the ocean mostly equilibrate) and Earth system sensitivity (much longer, including ice sheets and ecosystems). Equilibrium and Earth system sensitivity come from idealized experiments that hold concentrations fixed, which would itself require continued emissions. With human choices and carbon-cycle feedbacks, it is one of the three big uncertainties in future warming."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg1/chapter/chapter-7/> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_236. Related: tgs:Concept.AerosolMasking (high-sensitivity models need high aerosol masking to match observed warming), tgs:Concept.ZeroEmissionsCommitment."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.AerosolMasking a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Aerosol Masking (and Unmasking)"@en ;
    skos:definition "The cooling effect of air pollution, chiefly sulfur aerosols that reflect sunlight and brighten clouds, which hides part of greenhouse warming; cleaning up the pollution 'unmasks' it and speeds warming."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "The cooling effect of air pollution, chiefly sulfur dioxide, which reflects sunlight directly and seeds bright low clouds, hiding part of the warming greenhouse gases have already caused; cleaning up the pollution 'unmasks' that warming. In TGS_236 Zeke Hausfather says aerosols have masked roughly a third of historical warming (the IPCC best estimate is about 0.5°C, range 0.22-0.96°C), that global SO2 emissions are down about 40%, China's 75% and shipping's about 80% after the 2020 IMO low-sulfur fuel rule, and that in his estimate nearly all the recent acceleration in warming, from about 0.2 to about 0.3°C per decade, comes from these cuts. Shipping's cuts matter more than their share because ships pollute otherwise clean marine air. Because high-sensitivity models need high masking to match observations, the size of the effect is tied to how much future warming to expect. He still prefers clean air, given millions of premature deaths a year from air pollution."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.carbonbrief.org/explainer-how-human-caused-aerosols-are-masking-global-warming> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_236. The same topic was the focus of Nate's earlier conversation with Leon Simons (tgs:Interview.TGS_105_LeonSimons), referenced from TGS_236, which had no concept for it. Related: tgs:Concept.EarthEnergyImbalance, tgs:Concept.ClimateSensitivity. In Frankly_51 (December 2023), citing James Hansen and Leon Simons (tgs:Work.GlobalWarmingInThePipeline2023), Nate expects El Niño plus reduced aerosol masking to add about half a degree, so that by summer 2024 'we might for a month or so see 2 degrees Celsius'; as far as this graph records, monthly anomalies in 2024 peaked well below that."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ZeroEmissionsCommitment a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Zero Emissions Commitment"@en ;
    skos:definition "The warming (or cooling) still to come after human CO2 emissions stop. Model studies put it near zero (a 50-year multi-model mean of about -0.07°C): once emissions reach zero, land and ocean sinks draw atmospheric CO2 down, perhaps by about 80 ppm, while the ocean, still out of equilibrium after absorbing about 90% of trapped heat, keeps warming, and the two roughly cancel. As Zeke Hausfather puts it in TGS_236, this is good news (not much warming 'in the pipeline') and bad news (we are stuck with the warming we have, and the Earth will not cool without removing more carbon than we emit). It differs from the fixed-concentration experiments behind equilibrium and Earth system sensitivity, in which warming continues for centuries."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://bg.copernicus.org/articles/17/2987/2020/> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_236. Related: tgs:Concept.ClimateSensitivity; it is the reason Hausfather argues carbon removal will be needed to cool the planet in the long term."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EarthEnergyImbalance a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:prefLabel "Earth's Energy Imbalance"@en ;
    skos:definition "The difference between the energy Earth absorbs from the sun and the energy it radiates back to space, a direct measure of how much heat is being trapped. Measured from orbit since the early 2010s by the CERES satellite instruments, it has been rising, and recently rising faster. In TGS_236 Zeke Hausfather says the open question is how much of the recent rise reflects falling sulfur emissions (temporary, 'the devil we know') and how much a temperature-driven loss of low clouds (a feedback that would keep growing with warming); high-sensitivity models show the larger cloud loss. Nate cites Tad Patzek's comparison that the imbalance is about 20 times the energy flow of the entire human economy."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://essd.copernicus.org/articles/15/1675/2023/> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_236. Related: tgs:Concept.AerosolMasking, tgs:Concept.ClimateSensitivity."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ClimateAsANormalProblem a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Climate as a Normal Problem"@en ;
    skos:definition "Zeke Hausfather's argument (TGS_236) that climate change should be treated as a serious but normal problem, like poverty, hunger or pollution, which societies have historically managed to address, rather than as an existential 'ur-problem' to which all others are subordinate. He quotes the late climate scientist Stephen Schneider that the end of the world and 'good for us' are the two least likely outcomes, and draws a parallel with treating AI as a normal technology. Portraying problems as existential to motivate action, he says, feeds crisis fatigue and doom, and doom and apathy are the real enemies; but politics that only responds to crises makes normal problems hard to address. He pairs it with practical levers: political engagement, early adoption of clean technology to drive costs down, and carbon pricing."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.theclimatebrink.com/p/on-the-gates-climate-memo> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.ZekeHausfather ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_236. Stands in deliberate tension with Nate's own framing in tgs:Concept.PredicamentNotProblem: Hausfather treats climate as a solvable problem among many, while Nate treats the larger situation as a predicament to be navigated rather than solved. Nate does not challenge the framing on air."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.MultilevelSelection a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Multilevel Selection"@en ;
    skos:definition "Selection works on individuals and groups at once: selfishness wins within a group, but groups with less selfishness beat other groups -- the logic behind cooperation and its failures."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "The evolutionary theory that natural selection acts at several levels at once: within a group the selfish individual outcompetes others, but between groups the group with the fewest selfish individuals wins, so selfishness at one level undermines welfare at the next level up. As Josh Farley sets it out in TGS_185, it runs through the major evolutionary transitions in which once-independent units became so interdependent they could survive only as a collective: archaea and bacteria into the eukaryotic cell, cells into multicellular animals, animals into social species. A mitochondrion or cell that turns selfish is disease or cancer; a human who pursues only self-interest harms society; and for humans the decisive group-level selection is cultural. Farley's conclusion is that the individual should relate to society, and humanity to nature, as a cell relates to the body."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-3-319-19650-3_647> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DavidSloanWilson ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_185. Associated above all with David Sloan Wilson (tgs:Interview.TGS_56_DavidSloanWilson), whose good-versus-evil classroom test Farley also cites. Nate's counterpoint in TGS_185: in the short run the most power-maximizing cultures outcompete wiser ones (see tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple). In TGS_116 Riane Eisler accepts that selfishness wins within groups in domination systems, but says David Sloan Wilson has a blind spot on gender and the theory should be extended to empathy for out-groups."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.UneconomicGrowth a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.OvershootAndLimits ;
    skos:prefLabel "Uneconomic Growth"@en ;
    skos:definition "Herman Daly's term for economic growth whose added ecological and social costs exceed its added benefits. Because the economy is a subsystem of a finite biosphere, every product draws on ecosystems' structural building blocks and returns waste, so beyond some point growth makes us poorer, not richer. In TGS_185 Josh Farley calls it basic microeconomics applied to the whole economy (stop doing more of something when its marginal cost exceeds its marginal benefit) and contrasts firms' falling costs with society's steeply rising cost curve as growth burns more oil, destabilizes the climate and wipes out species. He notes the idea has spread far enough to be discussed at Davos. It follows from rejecting the myth that nature is a small subset of the economy."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.investopedia.com/terms/u/uneconomic-growth.asp> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.HermanDaly ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_185. Related: tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative, tgs:Concept.LimitsToGrowth, tgs:Concept.TenCoreMythsOfBusinessSchools."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SocialDilemma a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Social Dilemma"@en ;
    skos:definition "A situation in which each individual is better off acting in their own self-interest whatever others do, yet when everyone does so society as a whole is worse off; the prisoner's dilemma and the tragedy of the commons are the classic forms. In TGS_185 Josh Farley argues these are not exceptions to Adam Smith's invisible hand but the reality of every major challenge today (climate change, biodiversity loss, even the shift to non-rival renewable energy), because when the benefits of an act such as burning fossil fuels flow to the individual and the costs are shared, a system that tells people to maximize private benefit is 'suicidal.' Cooperation, enabled by culturally evolved moral norms, is how human groups solve them."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0749597812001276> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_185. Related: tgs:Concept.MultilevelSelection (why cooperative groups outcompete selfish ones), tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap (the same structure among competing actors), tgs:Concept.CircleOfTrustLocalism."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.DividedBrain a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.BehavioralEconomicsAndCognition ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Divided Brain (Hemisphere Hypothesis)"@en ;
    skos:definition "The two brain hemispheres attend to the world differently -- the left narrowly, to grasp and use; the right broadly, to see the whole -- and modern culture is dominated by the left."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Iain McGilchrist's account, set out in The Master and His Emissary (2009) and The Matter with Things (2021), that the brain's two hemispheres differ not in what they do (logic versus emotion, which he says is wrong) but in how they attend to the world. The left hemisphere gives narrow, focused attention for grasping and using: its world is made of static, fungible, categorized, explicit fragments, it values utility, money and power, and it is prone to anger, certainty and delusion. The right gives broad, sustained, vigilant attention: its world is uncertain, interconnected, embodied and implicit, and it grasps the whole and the values of connection, truth, goodness, beauty and love. The left is 'a very good servant, but a very poor master'; McGilchrist argues Western civilization has three times flourished in balance and declined as the left took over, and that modern culture is left-dominated. Evolutionary origin: a bird must focus on a seed while watching for predators."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://channelmcgilchrist.com/hemisphere-theory/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.IainMcGilchrist ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_217, though it is the core of all three McGilchrist episodes (tgs:Interview.TGS_85_WisdomNatureAndTheBrain, tgs:Interview.TGS_165_WisdomOverPower) and is cited in Frankly_161; no concept for it existed before. Related: tgs:Concept.Presence, tgs:Concept.LogicOfSeparation."@en ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.TheMasterAndHisEmissary, tgs:Work.TheMatterWithThings ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SoftFeudalism a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Soft Feudalism"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's term (Frankly_129, A Guide to Staying Human Part 1) for a society that is not formally feudal but works like one: comfort and consumer choice inside walls of institutions, incentives, ownership patterns and the physics of a carbon-intensive life, which 'in an Orwellian sense, we call freedoms,' while people's attention and money are extracted for 'the riders of the Superorganism.' In TGS_217 he names the modern feudal lord as the economic Superorganism, 'a hungry ghost,' and asks how people who value truth, beauty and goodness can resist its pull; Iain McGilchrist likes the image and adds that pre-industrial peasants had far more leisure than modern workers, so the intensification of labor came with industrialization and capitalism. The response both point to is reclaiming time and spaciousness as a first step toward agency."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/129-a-guide-to-staying-human-part-1> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_217, which links back to Frankly_129 for the term. Related: tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.PersonalAgency."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.Panpsychism a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Panpsychism (Pan-experientialism)"@en ;
    skos:definition "The philosophical view that mind, or inwardness, is a fundamental feature of everything rather than something that emerged from wholly mindless matter at some point. In TGS_217 Iain McGilchrist, who holds it, prefers the near-synonym pan-experientialism: there is something it is like to be anything, even a subatomic particle, though utterly unlike human experience. Consciousness cannot arise from matter without 'a miracle,' so consciousness and matter are two manifestations of one underlying reality, and matter is 'a phase of consciousness,' as ice, liquid water and vapor are phases of water, supplying the persistence and resistance a creative cosmos needs. He notes it is now widely respected among academic philosophers. He distinguishes his panentheism (God in everything and everything in God, yet more than the sum of things) from pantheism (God is simply the sum of everything)."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/panpsychism/> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_217, where Nate says he does not know what the word means. Related: tgs:Concept.NonDuality."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.RegenerativeFinance a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Regenerative Finance (ReFi)"@en ;
    skos:definition "An aspirational field, in Matthew Monahan's words in TGS_226 'more of a Schelling point' than a fixed definition, that asks how money and financial systems could put life back at the center, restoring ecosystem health and aligning the economy with the conditions for life rather than working against them. It builds on John Fullerton's regenerative economics. Monahan identifies three failures it must address in current funding: it is top-down and abstract (carbon credits and net-zero pledges that never reach local stewards), siloed (philanthropy, impact investing and government in separate buckets, with nonprofits competing and re-applying), and low-trust (missing feedback loops). His responses include bottom-up, community-defined funding, interoperable data that projects own, trust and reputation infrastructure, and, cautiously, complementary currencies and public blockchains. The open risk Nate raises is that pricing and trading nature more accurately could extend the same growth and commodification logic; Monahan therefore starts with gifts rather than ecological credits."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://blog.toucan.earth/what-is-refi-regenerative-finance/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.MatthewMonahan ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_226. Related: tgs:Concept.QuadraticFunding, tgs:Concept.MoneyAsClaimOnEnergy, tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative; see also tgs:Interview.TGS_149_JohnFullerton."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.QuadraticFunding a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Quadratic Funding"@en ;
    skos:definition "A matching-fund mechanism in which a pool of money is distributed to projects according to the breadth of community support rather than only its size: many small contributions earn more matching than a few large ones (formally, a project's match grows with the square of the sum of the square roots of its individual contributions). Popularized for open-source software through Gitcoin, it is the starting mechanism of Ma Earth's funding rounds described in TGS_226, where a philanthropic matching pool ($1 million in round three) is allocated according to how each project's three-week community campaign goes, so that the community's signal steers philanthropic money. Monahan plans to let future rounds use other mechanisms too, such as direct matching and prize competitions."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.wtfisqf.com/> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_226. The formal description follows the standard definition behind the show notes' 'WTF is QF?' link; Monahan does not give the formula on air. Related: tgs:Concept.RegenerativeFinance."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.RightsOfNature a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rights of Nature"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recognizing rivers, mountains, species or ecosystems as rights-holders or legal persons rather than property."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "The legal and ethical movement to recognize ecosystems, species or natural features (rivers, mountains, whales) as holders of rights or legal persons, rather than property. In TGS_226 Matthew Monahan highlights two mountains in Ma Earth's third funding round: Taranaki Maunga in Aotearoa New Zealand, granted personhood through a treaty settlement between the Crown and Māori, which can steward its own funds; and Pyramid Mountain, the first US mountain to own itself, governed through a guardianship council, along with rights-of-whales initiatives. The show notes link it to Nate's earlier episode with Pella Thiel on ecocide law and the rights of nature."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.garn.org/rights-of-nature/> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_226. The topic is central to tgs:Interview.TGS_121_PellaThiel, which had no concept for it."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.LootableSurplus a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    skos:prefLabel "Lootable Surplus"@en ;
    skos:definition "Luke Kemp's idea that early states and hierarchies (his 'Goliaths') grew where there were resources that could be seized and stored, such as grain, metals or domesticated animals, together with geographic containment and new weapons. In TGS_220 Art Berman says it is the idea of Kemp's he most values and extends it: war made whole polities lootable, so that for 5,000-6,000 years the principal method of economic growth has been looting, appropriating what other states have through warfare. With Peter Turchin's account that farming provided the calories, weapons set the pace and bureaucracy turned energy and war into lasting power, it leads Berman to recast the agricultural-surplus story as largely a story about war, and cities as protection: 'a castle with a bunch of serfs around it that got big.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://theconversation.com/friday-essay-how-societies-evolved-into-fear-dominated-goliaths-then-collapsed-263800> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.LukeKemp ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_220; originates in Kemp's own appearances (tgs:Interview.TGS_153_LukeKemp, tgs:Interview.TGS_194_LukeKemp). Related: tgs:Concept.SoftFeudalism, tgs:Concept.MultilevelSelection (Berman's question 'cooperation with whom?')."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.FourPillarsOfCivilization a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.OvershootAndLimits ;
    skos:prefLabel "Four Pillars of Modern Civilization (Smil)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Vaclav Smil's argument that cement, steel, plastics and ammonia (fertilizer) are the four material pillars of modern civilization, all produced and distributed with fossil energy. In TGS_220 Nate and Art Berman discuss Berman's chart, made before the Iran war, showing steel and cement declining, fertilizer flat to declining and plastics near or below peak, which the data suggest means the global material economy has already peaked while the financial layer has not caught up. Berman: there can be no growth without cement and steel, no modern healthcare without plastics, and no 8.5 billion people without fertilizer; with three pillars declining, civilization is 'on shaky ground.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://vaclavsmil.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Substantia.pdf> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_220. Vaclav Smil is not yet modeled as a Human or Persona. Related: tgs:Concept.Rebuildables, tgs:Concept.BiophysicalPyramid."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.DispatchablePower a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dispatchable Power"@en ;
    skos:definition "Electricity available on demand, like a cab sent by a dispatcher, as opposed to wind and solar, which generate only when the wind blows or the sun shines. In TGS_220 Art Berman notes that all recent US electricity growth has been wind and solar while dispatchable supply has fallen, mostly because coal is collapsing, so when AI hyperscalers announced the largest US power-demand growth in decades they 'didn't even give the renewable guys a call,' turning instead to natural gas, nuclear and even old jet engines (gas turbines are back-ordered for years). New plants of any kind must also clear grid operators' interconnection queues. He frames it as a question of power, energy per unit time, which modern society values above energy itself; renewable electricity is 'perfectly fine' but 'not fit for purpose,' as light oil is for someone who needs diesel."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://energyeducation.ca/encyclopedia/Non-dispatchable_source_of_electricity> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_220. Related: tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.Rebuildables."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.DarkRetreat a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dark Retreat"@en ;
    skos:definition "The ancient contemplative practice, preserved especially in Tibetan Buddhism (where it is a 'forceful method of liberation' and a preparation for death), of meditating for an extended period in complete darkness, traditionally alone and in silence in a prepared cabin or cave. As Andrew Holecek describes it in TGS_221, darkness is 'the addition of a tool designed for subtraction': with no way to look outward the mind is forced to relate to itself, so it works as 'meditation times a thousand' and 'a sober psychedelic,' bringing unprocessed unconscious material up, loosening ego structure, and, after an initial contraction, opening into a nondual ground. Because one can 'overdose on oneself,' he recommends titration: brief periods with blackout masks or a closet, 'gray retreat' that weaves in and out of darkness, and good guidance; contraindications include certain medical conditions, dissociative or psychotic tendencies, claustrophobia and spiritual thrill-seeking. He and collaborators are studying its effects scientifically."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.andrewholecek.com/dark-retreat/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.AndrewHolecek ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_221. Related: tgs:Concept.DarknessDeficitDisorder, tgs:Concept.Enantiodromia, tgs:Concept.NonDuality, tgs:Concept.Presence, tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli (Nate likens it to Anna Lembke's dopamine fast)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.DarknessDeficitDisorder a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Darkness Deficit Disorder"@en ;
    skos:definition "Andrew Holecek's term (TGS_221) for a condition he sees as nearly universal in modern life, at two levels. Literally, light pollution and screens violate 'the natural curfew of the night,' harming ecosystems and, through blue light suppressing melatonin, human sleep. Symbolically, it is a deficit of what darkness represents: depth, authenticity, intimacy, connection and simplicity, leading to a loss of empathy and compassion. Runaway light, held in our hands in 'weapons of mass distraction,' pulls attention outward and apart, 'one distraction upon the next,' which he calls the recursive basis of the metacrisis and ties to endless acquisition: we mistake the satisfaction of wanting for its temporary cessation. He also reads it as an imbalance of masculine light over feminine darkness. Its remedy, in his account, is reintroducing darkness, from closing one's eyes to dark retreat."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/221-andrew-holecek> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.AndrewHolecek ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_221, whose title uses this term; the show notes write 'Darkness Deficiency Disorder.' Related: tgs:Concept.DarkRetreat, tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.Enantiodromia a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Enantiodromia"@en ;
    skos:definition "Heraclitus's principle, later developed by Carl Jung, that anything taken to its extreme turns into its opposite (literally 'running counter to'); the Taoist yin-yang is its Eastern analog. In TGS_221 Andrew Holecek uses it to explain dark retreat: in the dark the past, the future and the walls seem to close in until one is crushed into the present moment so completely that constriction flips into openness, 'the tomb transforms into a womb... a womb with a view.' Likewise, going fully into oneself, one discovers one is nothing, which flips into being everything, the ground of compassion for others."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enantiodromia> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_221. Related: tgs:Concept.DarkRetreat, tgs:Concept.NonDuality."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.DieselPoweredCivilization a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:prefLabel "Diesel-Powered Civilization"@en ;
    skos:definition "The argument, made by Balázs Matics (TGS_209) and Art Berman (TGS_220), that diesel rather than oil in general is the lifeblood of industrial civilization: it powers agriculture, mining, construction, freight, ships and trains, because its energy density and slow, forceful combustion give high torque at low RPM that gasoline engines cannot match, and those uses have no scalable substitutes. Refiners treat gasoline as a byproduct of the more valuable middle distillates. Matics adds that global diesel output tracked new oil supply almost one-to-one until about 2015, then flatlined as growth shifted from conventional, diesel-rich crude to light shale oil and natural gas liquids, which coincided with slowing construction; Berman calls diesel 'the barometer of the economy' because its demand is inelastic, and notes that light US crude cannot make diesel without imported heavy oil."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://thehonestsorcerer.substack.com/p/a-diesel-powered-civilization> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.BalazsMatics, tgs:Persona.ArtBerman ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_209, also central to tgs:Interview.TGS_220_AWorldOnThePrecipice. Related: tgs:Concept.CrudeOil, tgs:Concept.PeakOil, tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.TechnologyJengaTower a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Technology as a Jenga Tower"@en ;
    skos:definition "Balázs Matics's metaphor (TGS_209) for why resource depletion is a predicament rather than a problem: modern technology is a tower of interdependent inputs in which no material is truly replaceable, because each builds on the others; pull out a block (losing affordable coal, for instance, undermines making solar panels) and the structure becomes unstable. New technologies such as AI are added on top while capabilities are lost at the base, raising productivity but also instability, so that the tower may topple suddenly rather than gently. He pairs it with the observation that depletion is about quality and energy return, not running out of the last molecule, and that the test of any technical fix is whether it works at a scale, price and energy return relevant to the problem."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://thehonestsorcerer.substack.com/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.BalazsMatics ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_209. Related: tgs:Concept.ComplexityCollapse, tgs:Concept.SupplyChainFragility, tgs:Concept.PredicamentNotProblem."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CollapseFromThePeriphery a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.Geopolitics, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Collapse from the Periphery"@en ;
    skos:definition "The historical pattern, drawn from studies of imperial decline such as Alexander Motyl's, that empires decay first at their periphery as the core's hold weakens, and that collapse is a long loss of previous capabilities rather than a sudden event. In TGS_209 Balázs Matics applies it to Western civilization and places Europe, not the United States, on the periphery: lacking its own energy and materials, it depends on others for supply, lost cheap Russian gas, saw LNG make its industry uncompetitive, and is turning toward militarization and restrictions on speech, consistent with the observation that empires grow more authoritarian as their energy surplus declines."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://thehonestsorcerer.substack.com/p/europes-downward-spiral-accelerates> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.BalazsMatics ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_209. Related: tgs:Concept.ComplexityCollapse."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EpistemicCommons a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Epistemic Commons (Sensemaking First)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's term for the shared capacity of a society to know what is true: cognitive security, resistance to polarization and misinformation, the ability to weigh sources and hold conversations without blame or the need to be right. In TGS_205 he names it his biggest unexpected learning of four years: understanding how algorithms, news and misinformation capture attention and then beliefs 'precedes all else,' ahead of the energy and ecology literacy he long emphasized. It was the subject of ISEOF's 'Reality Base Camp' class, taught in a transpartisan, metamodern way, and is the second rung, after self-care, of his theory of change. He fears a growing 'maelstrom' of fear-based information, amplified by AI, will drown out the grounded, pro-social signal."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AutPB49SY30> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_205; the transcript says 'Epistological Commons.' Related: tgs:Concept.PeakSensemaking, tgs:Concept.UltraProcessedInformation, tgs:Concept.TheoryOfChange."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.RealityBasedHope a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Reality-Based Hope"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's insistence (TGS_205) that, in discussing the more-than-human predicament, the word 'hope' must be preceded by 'authentic' or 'reality-based,' because many people's hopes are consciously or unconsciously tied to the continuation of modernity, which, in Vanessa Andreotti's phrase, we are now hospicing. Reality-based grounds for hope include that most of what is needed (clean water, healthy children, good education, a reasonable economy, protected ecosystems, basic needs met) is worth doing anyway, that the energy transition can be about our relationship with energy rather than simply a change of fuel, and the growing awareness he hears from listeners."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/205-nate-hagens> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_205. Related: tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament, tgs:Concept.Dread. Earlier, in Frankly_70 (August 2024), Nate said he does not really like the word hope and is more aligned with resolve, meaning, purpose and trust in what may emerge. In TGS_170 (April 2025) Kristine Tompkins makes a related point that Nate endorses: hope has to be earned by doing something (tgs:Concept.EarnedHope)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ClimateDashboard a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.SystemsFrameworks ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Climate Dashboard"@en ;
    skos:definition "Kelly Erhart's way (TGS_204) of reading contradictory climate headlines as lights on a dashboard. Green: the speed and scale of clean-energy deployment, unimaginable ten years ago. Yellow: emissions are not falling, because renewables are being built on top of fossil energy rather than replacing it. Red: increasingly grim science on the consequences of any emissions path, driven by four accelerants that act as force multipliers: higher climate sensitivity, the unmasking of aerosol cooling, warming-induced emissions from natural systems, and tipping points. She uses the red lights to argue that the toolbox must expand beyond mitigation and adaptation."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://arcinit.substack.com/p/the-climate-dashboard-seeing-the> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.KellyErhart ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_204. Related: tgs:Concept.EnergyAdditionsNotTransitions (the yellow light), tgs:Concept.ClimateSensitivity, tgs:Concept.AerosolMasking, tgs:Concept.WarmingInducedEmissions, tgs:Concept.ClimateTippingPoints (the red lights)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.WarmingInducedEmissions a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Warming-Induced Emissions"@en ;
    skos:definition "Greenhouse gases released by natural systems because the planet has already warmed, rather than directly by human activity: methane and carbon dioxide from tropical wetlands, peatlands and thawing permafrost. In TGS_204 Kelly Erhart notes that atmospheric methane, roughly flat for years, began rising rapidly around 2010 partly for this reason, that the hotspots and drivers are still poorly known, and that these feedbacks are not yet in IPCC projections because of long assessment cycles and missing observational data. Responses she mentions include re-salinating wetlands and atmospheric methane removal."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.climate.gov/news-features/understanding-climate/after-2000-era-plateau-global-methane-levels-hitting-new-highs> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.KellyErhart ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_204. Related: tgs:Concept.SuperPollutants, tgs:Concept.ClimateTippingPoints, tgs:Concept.ClimateDashboard."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ClimateTippingPoints a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Climate Tipping Points"@en ;
    skos:definition "Thresholds beyond which a part of the Earth system shifts into a different state, causing change that is irreversible on any human-relevant timescale: collapsing ice sheets, vanishing summer sea ice, Amazon rainforest dieback, a slowdown of the AMOC. Tipping elements can cascade into one another. In TGS_204 Kelly Erhart notes that scientists disagree about how to talk about them given the technical meaning of the term, stresses that some could play out within the lifetimes of listeners' children, and argues that their geopolitical fallout could undermine mitigation and adaptation themselves. She also points to the converse idea of positive social and technological tipping points."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.abn7950>, <https://esd.copernicus.org/articles/15/41/2024/> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_204; no tipping-point Concept existed before, although tgs:Work.GlobalTippingPointsReport2025 is cited from Frankly_138 and Frankly_145. Related: tgs:EarthSystemComponent.AMOC, tgs:Concept.AlternativeStableStates, tgs:Concept.GlacierStabilization, tgs:Concept.WarmingInducedEmissions."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CarbonDioxideRemoval a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Carbon Dioxide Removal"@en ;
    skos:definition "Taking carbon dioxide out of the atmosphere and storing it, needed because CO2 is long-lived: even if emissions stopped, what is already there would keep warming the planet. Kelly Erhart (TGS_204) distinguishes closed systems, such as direct air capture and carbon capture and storage, in which carbon flows can be measured precisely but which need infrastructure and a lot of energy, from open systems such as forests, soils and the ocean, and focuses on durable removal that stores carbon for a hundred to a thousand years. She cites an IPCC-scale need of about 10 gigatons a year against tens of thousands of tons of durable removal deployed today."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg3/downloads/outreach/IPCC_AR6_WGIII_Factsheet_CDR.pdf> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_204. Zeke Hausfather (TGS_236) also argues removal will be needed to bring temperatures back down. Related: tgs:Concept.OceanAlkalinityEnhancement, tgs:Concept.ZeroEmissionsCommitment."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.OceanAlkalinityEnhancement a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ocean Alkalinity Enhancement"@en ;
    skos:definition "An open-system carbon removal approach that raises the ocean's alkalinity so it absorbs more atmospheric CO2 and stores it as dissolved bicarbonate rather than as carbonic acid. It can be mineral-based, spreading alkaline rock such as olivine where wave energy dissolves it (the coastal enhanced weathering Vesta pioneered), or chemical, adding alkalinity directly. Kelly Erhart (TGS_204), who co-founded Vesta and ran the first field trials, describes it as giving an ocean that is about 30% more acidic since the industrial revolution more buffering capacity, with potential to draw down gigatons a year; the next steps are defining safe ecological thresholds and how deployment would work."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://climate.mit.edu/explainers/ocean-alkalinity-enhancement> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.KellyErhart ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_204. Related: tgs:Concept.CarbonDioxideRemoval, tgs:Organization.Vesta."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SuperPollutants a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Super Pollutants (Short-Lived Climate Pollutants)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Methane, black carbon and fluorinated gases: present at far lower concentrations than CO2 and shorter-lived, but far more potent, together responsible for about half of today's warming. Because they are short-lived, cutting them is one of the fastest levers for slowing warming and avoiding overshoot. In TGS_204 Kelly Erhart notes that methane alone accounts for about half a degree of warming; that more methane slows its own breakdown, because the hydroxyl and chlorine radicals that clean it out of the atmosphere are limited; that oil-and-gas leaks are now mainly an enforcement problem; but that only about 10% of livestock emissions have mitigation pathways, because most ruminants are pasture-raised. Atmospheric methane removal, accelerating the natural breakdown, is an emerging research area."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.ccacoalition.org/news/super-pollutants>, <https://nap.nationalacademies.org/resource/27157/interactive/> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_204. Related: tgs:Concept.WarmingInducedEmissions."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.PlanetaryAlbedoDecline a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Planetary Albedo Decline (a Darkening Earth)"@en ;
    skos:definition "The measured drop in how much sunlight Earth reflects, as sea ice retreats and cloud cover changes with warming. Earth reflects about 30% of incoming sunlight; Kelly Erhart (TGS_204) cites studies that a roughly 0.5% drop over the past 25 years has a warming effect equivalent to about 138 ppm of extra CO2, a 'hidden second pulse of warming' on top of greenhouse gases and a self-reinforcing feedback. The converse, that raising reflectivity by about 1% could cool the planet by about 1°C, is the motivation for sunlight reflection research."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.adq7280> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_204. Related: tgs:Concept.EarthEnergyImbalance, tgs:Concept.AerosolMasking, tgs:Concept.SolarRadiationModification."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.GlacierStabilization a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Glacier Stabilization"@en ;
    skos:definition "Research into forecasting and slowing the collapse of marine ice sheets, above all Thwaites Glacier ('the Doomsday Glacier') in the West Antarctic Ice Sheet, the largest uncertainty in sea-level projections for the next century or two. Kelly Erhart (TGS_204) reports that glaciologists now treat Thwaites' collapse as a matter of when, not if, because warm water reaching its base has started a feedback decoupled from air temperature, yet they cannot say whether it will happen this century or in 500 years. Her two tracks are better observation and models, and interventions modeled on natural cases where ice streams stopped and refroze to their beds: pumping water from beneath the glacier to reduce basal lubrication (as in Kim Stanley Robinson's The Ministry for the Future) or passive thermosiphons, which might need about 10,000 boreholes."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://thwaitesglacier.org/findings>, <https://www.sciencenews.org/article/antarctic-glaciers-ice-healing> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.KellyErhart ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_204. Related: tgs:Concept.ClimateTippingPoints, tgs:EarthSystemComponent.AMOC (meltwater could slow it), tgs:Work.TheMinistryForTheFuture."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SolarRadiationModification a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    skos:prefLabel "Solar Radiation Modification (Sunlight Reflection)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Cooling the planet by reflecting sunlight, chiefly by injecting aerosols into the stratosphere; at best an emergency brake that buys time, not a solution."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Cooling the planet by reflecting more sunlight back to space, most studied as stratospheric aerosol injection of sulfates, mimicking volcanic eruptions such as Mount Pinatubo. The idea was first raised in a 1965 report to President Lyndon Johnson. Kelly Erhart (TGS_204) calls it the only researched emergency brake for runaway warming, not a solution: it does not address the causes, its regional effects are poorly bounded, and it is at best an insurance policy or a way to buy time. She rejects the 'rogue billionaire' scenario as convenient but incorrect, since sustained deployment would take a nation state, and argues that governance (who decides, who bears the regional consequences) requires research first, especially by Global South researchers. Ted Parson, interviewed by Nate the day before, puts the chance of eventual deployment at 90%."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.annualreviews.org/content/journals/10.1146/annurev-environ-112321-081911> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.TedParson ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_204 and the central subject of tgs:Interview.TGS_200_TedParson (built the same day), where Parson sketches a half-degree program: 100 to 200 modified aircraft delivering 5 to 10 million tons of sulfur a year for $10 to 20 billion a year. See also tgs:Concept.TerminationShock, tgs:Concept.MitigationDeterrence and tgs:Concept.SolarGeoengineeringGovernance. Erhart's own framing that fossil emissions are already 'the dirtiest, most inequitable form of geoengineering' belongs here. Related: tgs:Concept.PlanetaryAlbedoDecline, tgs:Concept.AerosolMasking."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.TwoWaysOfKnowing a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.SystemsFrameworks ;
    skos:prefLabel "Two Ways of Knowing (Indigenous Knowledge and Modern Science)"@en ;
    skos:definition "The practice of treating Indigenous knowledge and modern Western science as distinct, equally valid systems of knowledge that each strengthen the other, rather than competitors. Rosa Vásquez Espinoza (TGS_201) argues that ancestral peoples were already scientists, as controlled burning and the selective breeding of cacao show; that science without Indigenous knowledge misses systems thinking, species, health and long-term solutions (less than 1% of Amazonian plants have been studied chemically, and artemisinin came from old Chinese medical records); and that Indigenous communities gain from science too, through tools such as hive mapping and camera traps that revealed an Andean bear in Asháninka territory. Her method: put both on 'the same plate, on the same level', listen, and meet on equal footing, with Indigenous co-authors and local leadership."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.criaw-icref.ca/images/userfiles/files/Fact%20Sheet%202%20EN%20FINAL.pdf> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RosaVasquezEspinoza ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_201, whose title uses the phrase. Related: tgs:Concept.ComplexSystemsThinking, tgs:Work.BraidingSweetgrass (Robin Wall Kimmerer's braiding of the same two knowledge systems)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.StinglessBees a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Stingless Bees (Meliponini)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Bees with no functional stinger, found along the tropics, whose lineage reaches back to the age of the dinosaurs; the oldest known bee fossil, preserved in amber, is a stingless bee. Their honey is watery and varies in taste, and is a traditional medicine (one of the two main Amazonian remedies for COVID symptoms) and a ceremonial gift among the Maya and Aztecs. Most forage only 100 to 200 meters from the hive, against one or two kilometers for honeybees, so they need intact forest nearby. In TGS_201 Rosa Vásquez Espinoza describes them as declining (hives once 30 minutes into the forest now take five or six hours to find), pollinating crops such as coffee, cacao, avocado and blueberries and raising some native fruit yields by 44%, and as the basis of her conservation framework: mapping wild hives to propose corridors and bee-friendly reforestation, raising the value of the honey, and the first legal recognition of an insect's rights, in Satipo, Peru (October 2025)."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.amazonresearch.org/stingless-bees>, <https://insideclimatenews.org/news/22102025/peru-amazon-stingless-bees-rights/> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_201. Related: tgs:Concept.RightsOfNature, tgs:Concept.ConservationThroughLivelihoods, tgs:Organization.AmazonResearchInternacional."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ConservationThroughLivelihoods a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Conservation Through Livelihoods"@en ;
    skos:definition "The principle that protecting an ecosystem fails unless the people who live in it can make a living from keeping it intact: 'conservation without sustainable economy just does not go anywhere', because people who cannot feed their children will do anything. André Guimarães (TGS_151) made the argument for the Amazon; Rosa Vásquez Espinoza (TGS_201) applies it through stingless bees, whose short foraging range makes standing forest the asset, giving families income from honey, training and hive boxes, and higher fruit yields, as an alternative to clearing land for cacao or cattle feed. She stresses diversified livelihoods rather than scaling one product, solutions designed by Indigenous leaders at the table, and local Indigenous youth as future leaders."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.amazonconservation.org/empowering-local-people-for-resilient-livelihoods-and-a-thriving-forest/> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_201. Related: tgs:Concept.StinglessBees, tgs:Concept.RegenerativeFinance, tgs:Work.NewEconomyForTheBrazilianAmazon."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.IndustrialEraEducation a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Industrial-Era Education"@en ;
    skos:definition "The critique that most national school systems still follow a model built for the industrial revolution, systematized for speed and output and teaching math, geography and chemistry as separate subjects 'as if the world is separated by themes', which does not match how even a river system works. Rosa Vásquez Espinoza (TGS_201) calls for systems thinking about interconnection and for taking classrooms outdoors, where even a single city tree is enough to teach it; she suggests tracing one market product back to its origins, for example to a stingless bee in the Amazon, as a school project."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://psu.pb.unizin.org/generaleducationfortheinformationsociety/chapter/the-industrial-age-roots-of-general-education/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RosaVasquezEspinoza ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_201. Related: tgs:Concept.ComplexSystemsThinking."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.Zoopharmacognosy a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Zoopharmacognosy (Animal Self-Medication)"@en ;
    skos:definition "The behavior of animals that select plants, soils or other substances to treat or prevent illness: elephants eating a tree bark that induces labor (which women in the same communities also brew as tea), chimpanzees using leaves to heal skin and teaching one another, and dogs eating particular grass. Rosa Vásquez Espinoza (TGS_201) calls it her most exciting research question, now a project with Asháninka partners in Peru: whether human medicinal knowledge was learned by watching animals, whether animals carry medicinal knowledge worth protecting, and whether it could guide reforestation for animal health. She traces her interest to an ayahuasca experience described in her book."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zoopharmacognosy> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_201. Related: tgs:Concept.TwoWaysOfKnowing, tgs:Work.TheSpiritOfTheRainforest."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.TerminationShock a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Termination Shock"@en ;
    skos:definition "The rapid rebound of warming if a large solar geoengineering program stops suddenly: because stratospheric aerosols settle out within a year or two, the heating they were masking returns within about a decade. Ted Parson (TGS_200) calls it the endpoint of 'a century-long idiotic folly' in which cooling is used instead of cutting and removing emissions, so that, say, three degrees of greenhouse warming is masked by three degrees of cooling and then a pandemic, depression or political change halts the program. The danger scales with how much is being masked: he sees little sign that even suddenly losing half a degree of cooling, comparable to Pinatubo, would be profoundly disruptive, and the way to avoid it is to pair any program with emission cuts and removals and ramp it down gradually."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://pollution.sustainability-directory.com/term/termination-shock/> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_200. Related: tgs:Concept.SolarRadiationModification, tgs:Concept.MitigationDeterrence, tgs:Concept.AerosolMasking (a smaller, real-world version as pollution is cleaned up)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.MitigationDeterrence a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mitigation Deterrence (Moral Hazard)"@en ;
    skos:definition "The concern that the prospect of a technological fix, especially solar geoengineering or large-scale carbon removal, will weaken the will to cut emissions, also called moral hazard or mitigation displacement. Ted Parson (TGS_200) likens it to buying a gym membership and then not working out, and warns that fossil fuel interests, national oil companies included, would use geoengineering to argue that deep cuts are too costly, 'to keep the rodeo going a little longer'. He calls it a real and serious concern, but argues that fear of it has stopped a needed conversation, and that geoengineering must be designed as a complement to mitigation, not a substitute. Kelly Erhart (TGS_204) notes that adaptation was once rejected as a moral hazard for the same reason before becoming accepted as necessary."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moral_hazard> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_200. Related: tgs:Concept.SolarRadiationModification, tgs:Concept.TerminationShock, tgs:Concept.CarbonDioxideRemoval."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SolarGeoengineeringGovernance a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    skos:prefLabel "Solar Geoengineering Governance"@en ;
    skos:definition "The problem of who decides whether, how and by whom solar geoengineering is researched and used, which Ted Parson (TGS_200) and Kelly Erhart (TGS_204) both call the central challenge. Parson rejects both a utopian global agreement and a lone unilateral actor as the likely path: a program is cheap (about $10 to 20 billion a year for half a degree of cooling) but would take a decade or two to build, could not be hidden, and could only be sustained by a state or coalition able to withstand opposition, most plausibly an informal coalition of about half a dozen nations, which he puts at well above 90% likely within 30 years. He expects other governments to condemn it publicly while quietly seeking a seat at the table, and envisions new bodies of 25 to 35 well-advised delegations, including sub-Saharan Africa, that would also confront broken promises on emissions, adaptation and finance. He criticizes the 'non-use agreement' movement for effectively banning research and discussion, calls for 'baby steps in governance', and expects leadership from middle- and low-income countries. Erhart adds that governance needs research first and highlights efforts to involve Global South researchers and civil society."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0016328721001154> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.TedParson ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_200. Parson's model of successful cooperation is the Montreal Protocol (tgs:Work.ProtectingTheOzoneLayer). Related: tgs:Concept.SolarRadiationModification, tgs:Concept.MitigationDeterrence."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.MoralAmbition a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Moral Ambition"@en ;
    skos:definition "Rutger Bregman's term (TGS_195) for combining 'the idealism of an activist with the ambition of an entrepreneur' to make the world wildly, not 'a little bit', better and to scale one's impact. Its seven principles, as taught by the School for Moral Ambition: action (awareness is overrated); impact, choosing problems that are sizable, solvable and sorely neglected (the SSS framework), because what you work on matters more than how hard you work; radical compassion, expanding the moral circle; open-mindedness and epistemic humility; kindness, never using people as means; enthusiasm, a full life beyond morality; and perseverance across generations. Bregman stresses that it is for everyone, that people often become good by doing good once someone asks them ('the importance of being asked'), that motives can start as status-seeking and become intrinsic, and that elite graduates lost to the 'Bermuda Triangle of talent' (consultancy, corporate law and finance) should be far more morally ambitious."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.moralambition.org/principles> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RutgerBregman ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_195. Related: tgs:Concept.RedefiningSuccess, tgs:Concept.MoralCircleExpansion, tgs:Concept.MaximizeImpactNotMinimizeFootprint, tgs:PersonalIntervention.CoordinateSmallGroup (his advice to find a small group and meet in person)."@en ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.MoralAmbition ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.RedefiningSuccess a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Redefining Success (Status as a Cultural Artifact)"@en ;
    skos:definition "The argument that the desire for status is a deep and lasting human need but what confers status is culturally malleable, so a culture's definition of success can be deliberately changed. Rutger Bregman (TGS_195) calls today's money-centered definition perhaps the fundamental problem behind the metacrisis, citing the American Freshman Survey, in which students' top goals shifted from developing a meaningful philosophy of life to making money between the late 1960s and today. His historical precedents are the British abolitionists and the elites of the transition from the Gilded Age to the Progressive Era (Theodore Roosevelt's 'man in the arena'), who made doing good, rather than wealth and a Fifth Avenue mansion, the mark of a successful life; he hopes a 'second Gilded Age' can be followed the same way. Nate links it to widening what investors and philanthropists count as a 'return' beyond money on money."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.heri.ucla.edu/monographs/50YearTrendsMonograph2016.pdf> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RutgerBregman ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_195. Related: tgs:Concept.MoralAmbition, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:PersonalIntervention.CurateStatusEnvironment, tgs:PersonalIntervention.MeasureReturnsBeyondMoney."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.MoralCircleExpansion a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Moral Circle Expansion"@en ;
    skos:definition "The historical pattern in which much moral progress consists of widening the set of beings whose interests count: abolitionists extending rights beyond white people, suffragists extending them beyond male citizens. Rutger Bregman (TGS_195) calls it 'radical compassion', one of the School for Moral Ambition's principles, and argues it should now reach animals, not with identical rights but with moral consideration: he calls factory farming one of today's greatest moral atrocities, highlights farmed shrimp (killed in the hundreds of billions each year, with growing evidence that they can suffer), and asks people to stay open to conclusions that sound strange today."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.shrimpwelfareproject.org/blog/do-shrimps-feel-pain> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_195. Related: tgs:Concept.MoralAmbition, tgs:Concept.RightsOfNature."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.MaximizeImpactNotMinimizeFootprint a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Maximize Your Impact, Not Just Minimize Your Footprint"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's advice to students: shrinking your own footprint matters less than using your life to change the larger system."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Nate's maxim to his students, 'maximize your impact as opposed to minimize your consumption': if everyone lived at the smallest possible ecological footprint, no one would change the larger system driving the crises. Rutger Bregman (TGS_195) endorses it, calling personal footprint rules (no meat, no flying, electric cars, tiny houses) 'the moral minimum' or 'human hygiene': if done perfectly, 'you might as well not have existed', so death becomes the highest ideal. He contrasts checklist approaches such as B Corp certification with the 'sky is the limit' ambition of Silicon Valley founders, which he wants pointed at pandemics, climate breakdown and poverty."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/195-rutger-bregman> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens, tgs:Persona.RutgerBregman ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_195. Nate makes the same argument in TGS_187 (recorded earlier, April 2025), where it is one practice of tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySovereignty. Related: tgs:Concept.EcologicalFootprint, tgs:Concept.MoralAmbition. An earlier statement is in Frankly_82 (January 2025): if he lived in a shack eating canned goods he would be 'one eight billionth less of a problem' but would not be maximizing his impact; 'between now and then is when you want to maximize your impact.'"@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ClimateMigration a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Climate Migration (Climate-Related Displacement)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Movement of people driven wholly or partly by climate impacts: floods, droughts, heat, sea-level rise and saltwater intrusion into farmland. In TGS_192 Sunil Amrith cites the International Organization for Migration's estimate that more than a billion people will be displaced between now and 2050, and the IPCC's that about 90% of climate-displaced people will move within their own countries, mostly toward the already strained megacities of the Global South rather than to the US or Europe. Environmental displacement has historically been temporary, with people returning after disasters; the new problem is what happens when home becomes permanently uninhabitable. Because migration is costly, the very poorest move least and most locally, so who cannot move matters as much as who will. Climate is rarely the only cause, usually combining with economic hardship, networks and conflict. His prescriptions are acceptance of migration as part of the human condition, long-range planning, and heavy public investment in housing, infrastructure and medical care, with Bangladesh's falling cyclone death tolls and plans for 'migrant-friendly, climate-resilient cities' as an example."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://emergencymanual.iom.int/sites/g/files/tmzbdl1956/files/2023-03/iom_global_data_institute_thematic_brief_1_evidence_summary_on_climate_change_and_the_future_of_human_mobility.pdf> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.SunilAmrith ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_192. Related: tgs:Concept.MigrationAsHumanNorm, tgs:Concept.WetBulbTemperature, tgs:Concept.WarAndTheClimateCrisis, tgs:InterventionFront.PovertyAndDisplacement."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.MigrationAsHumanNorm a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Migration as the Human Norm"@en ;
    skos:definition "Sunil Amrith's argument (TGS_192), drawn from centuries of movement around the Bay of Bengal, that migration is a normal part of human existence rather than an exception to a world of 'locals': in many periods nearly everyone was mobile. International migrants are about 3.5 to 4% of today's population, a share little changed in decades and well below the 8 or 9% of the 1870s peak, although internal migration, driven above all by China's urbanization, is at unprecedented levels. Migration follows recognizable large-scale patterns, especially chain migration along family and village networks, yet individual reasons are as varied as people, usually combining economic hardship, environmental pressure, networks, coercion and war. He argues that seeing migration this way, and recognizing that 'we are all migrants', could change how societies talk about newcomers, while still taking seriously why large-scale migration feels threatening."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.migrationdataportal.org/themes/international-migrant-stocks-overview> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.SunilAmrith ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_192. Related: tgs:Concept.ClimateMigration, tgs:Work.CrossingTheBayOfBengal."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.HoloceneClimateStability a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.OvershootAndLimits ;
    skos:prefLabel "Holocene Climate Stability ('Climate Change Caused Humans')"@en ;
    skos:definition "The unusually stable climate of the roughly 10,000 to 12,000 years since the last glaciation, after an earlier era of much larger swings in temperature and rainfall, which made settled agriculture, cities and civilization possible. Nate's line in TGS_192 is 'not that humans are causing climate change, but that climate change caused humans': before the Holocene, high variability kept people moving seasonally, unable to stay put and farm. He and his guests use it to frame the present as humans leaving that stable window, so that hydrological stability, long taken for granted, can no longer be assumed; Sunil Amrith agrees that the long view holds insight for the world we may be entering. Since the last glacial maximum, sea level has risen by more than 100 meters, displacing coastal communities."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/american-antiquity/article/abs/was-agriculture-impossible-during-the-pleistocene-but-mandatory-during-the-holocene-a-climate-change-hypothesis/246B240BFFFBE904B1AC31296AD72949> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_192. Nate makes the same argument in TGS_201 (agriculture followed the Holocene's stabilization, not a sudden discovery) and in his TGS_200 introduction ('we're leaving the stability of the Anthropocene'). Related: tgs:Concept.EarlyAnthropoceneHypothesis, tgs:Concept.ClimateTippingPoints."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.WarAndTheClimateCrisis a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.Geopolitics, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "War and the Climate Crisis"@en ;
    skos:definition "Sunil Amrith's argument (TGS_192) that armed conflict is an underdiscussed part of the climate crisis. Historically, war has caused more large-scale migration than any other single factor, and it is usually mass violence that produces mass migration rather than the reverse. Conflict also drives the climate and ecological crisis directly, through military emissions, the energy used by the arms industry, lasting environmental damage, and destabilized ecologies and populations. In an era of global heating, he argues, peacebuilding and disarmament should be treated as climate measures."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://militaryemissions.org/>, <https://ceobs.org/how-does-war-damage-the-environment/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.SunilAmrith ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_192. Related: tgs:Concept.ClimateMigration."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.InsectDecline a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Insect Decline (the Insect Crisis)"@en ;
    skos:definition "The worldwide loss of insect abundance and diversity, estimated at roughly 1 to 2% of total insect biomass a year, with local declines of three-quarters to 90% over decades: about 75% of flying insect biomass lost from German nature reserves since the late 1980s, collapses in Puerto Rico's rainforest, and a fifth of US butterflies lost since 2000. Oliver Milman (TGS_189) names three main drivers, habitat loss, pesticides on farmland (especially neonicotinoids) and climate change, which researchers now rank first, plus light pollution. The decline is uneven: bees, butterflies and fireflies lose, while adaptable cockroaches, malaria-carrying mosquitoes and ticks spread. Insects' sheer numbers (about 2 billion per human) hid the decline, and scientists only recently began counting them. Because they reproduce quickly, populations can rebound once pressure is lifted, 'like a log in water when we lift our foot'."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320719317823>, <https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0185809> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.OliverMilman ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_189. Related: tgs:Concept.PollinatorDependence, tgs:Concept.ShiftingBaselineSyndrome, tgs:Concept.StinglessBees, tgs:IndicatorCategory.BiodiversityLoss, tgs:InterventionSubdomain.BiodiversityDefense."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.PollinatorDependence a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pollinator Dependence"@en ;
    skos:definition "Humanity's reliance on animal, mostly insect, pollination for about a third of food crops, worth about half a trillion dollars a year: most fruits and vegetables, nuts, cacao (pollinated by a tiny midge), spices, and alfalfa for dairy, though not wind-pollinated staples such as rice and wheat. Oliver Milman (TGS_189) cites estimates of more than a million extra deaths a year from malnutrition due to lost pollination and already falling yields of apples, cherries and blueberries in North America, and warns that food stress would raise prices, turn fruit and chocolate into luxuries, ruin small farmers and fuel conflict. US agriculture already depends on managed honeybees kept up artificially and trucked across the country, about 90% of US hives going to California's almond orchards each year, a fragile single-species system. Robotic bees and drone pollination cannot replace pollinators at the necessary scale."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://ehp.niehs.nih.gov/doi/10.1289/EHP10947> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_189, where Nate calls insect decline a national security issue, echoing Rod Schoonover (TGS_183). Related: tgs:Concept.InsectDecline, tgs:Concept.StinglessBees (wild pollinators of coffee, cacao and fruit in the Amazon, TGS_201)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ShiftingBaselineSyndrome a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Shifting Baseline Syndrome"@en ;
    skos:definition "The tendency for each generation to take the degraded state of nature it grew up with as normal, so that gradual losses go unnoticed and expectations fall over time. Nate covers it in Reality Blind, linked in the TGS_189 show notes. Oliver Milman describes its feedback loop: as nature recedes from our lives it recedes from our minds, so we value it less and protect it less; we have forgotten what a world with abundant insects felt like until a restored wildflower meadow reminds us. He argues for embracing nature close to home to break the loop."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://read.realityblind.world/view/975731937/99/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_189. Related: tgs:Concept.InsectDecline."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.InactionPlanForNature a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "An Inaction Plan for Nature"@en ;
    skos:definition "An entomologist's phrase, reported by Oliver Milman (TGS_189), for helping insects mainly by doing less: 'we need more of an inaction plan rather than an action plan'. Insects thrive in what people see as untidy, so letting yards and margins get 'a little bit untidy and scruffy' gives them habitat. Examples: No Mow May, leaving fallen leaves as food and shelter, keeping rotting logs, planting three native species for each growing season, and dimming lights. Milman notes the cultural barrier (neatness norms, homeowners' associations) and that the effect grows when neighbors and local groups do it together, giving people who cannot cut emissions themselves something meaningful to do."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://beecityusa.org/no-mow-may/> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_189. Related: tgs:Concept.InsectDecline, tgs:PersonalIntervention.CoordinateSmallGroup."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySovereignty a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wide-Boundary Sovereignty"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's reframing (TGS_187) of individual sovereignty: not the techno-libertarian power to transcend borders foreseen in The Sovereign Individual, but a whole-person resilience suited to the end of growth, organized as nine categories with three practices each. Intellectual: foundational reality, wide-boundary thinking, a softened gaze. Physiological: nourishment, ancestral intimacy (co-regulation through the vagus nerve), a dopamine reset. Psychological: spaciousness, zones of respite, metacognition. Spiritual: awe, connectedness to the web of life, space for the unknown. Economic: simplify first and beat the rush, slow down to human scale, Goldilocks technology. Social: barbell empathy, equanimity, the dining car. Local: sit spots and knowing one's place, honing useful skills, being rocks in the river. Ecological: looking two or three steps ahead in service of life, maximizing impact rather than minimizing footprint, planting seeds that change the initial conditions of the future. Personal: kindness to oneself, noticing glimmers, and being 'B-plus in service of life'. He hopes small groups practicing these will find each other, so that cultural change emerges."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/187-earth-day-2025> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_187. Many of its practices recur as separate Concepts (tgs:Concept.GoldilocksTechnology, tgs:Concept.RocksInTheRiver, tgs:Concept.InServiceOfLife, tgs:Concept.MaximizeImpactNotMinimizeFootprint, tgs:Concept.BarbellEmpathy, tgs:Concept.DiningCar, tgs:Concept.Sobriety) and overlap the Frankly_154 PersonalInterventions, e.g. tgs:PersonalIntervention.LowerDopaminePeaks, tgs:PersonalIntervention.RehearseLoss, tgs:PersonalIntervention.StandOnTheGround, tgs:PersonalIntervention.CoordinateSmallGroup. The nine categories were first set out in Frankly_82 (Power vs Life: Towards Wide Boundary Sovereignty, January 2025), with slightly different groupings (ancestral intimacy under psychological, spaciousness under physiological) and an added tenth, imaginal category (tgs:Concept.ImaginalCells); TGS_187 restates them. Its occasion there is tgs:Concept.PowerVersusLife."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.BarbellEmpathy a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Barbell Empathy"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own term (TGS_187) for balancing two things at once as suffering grows around us: giving full, present attention to someone who is suffering, 'about the greatest gift that humans can offer', while protecting one's own inner core, goals and work so as not to drown in it. His example: after an Uber driver in San Francisco spent 45 minutes telling him how he had not seen his family in Africa for seven years, a coach told him to absorb the moment of suffering, let it pass through him and move on."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/187-earth-day-2025> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_187. Related: tgs:Concept.EmpathyHandicap, tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySovereignty. Nate first lists it in Frankly_82 (January 2025) among the practices of wide-boundary sovereignty; TGS_187 restates it."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.DiningCar a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Dining Car (of the Runaway Train)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's metaphor (TGS_187): we are all on a runaway train, with financial markets shoveling fuel into the engine, and large-scale systems will not shift until there is a disruption; what we can do is meet other people in the dining car to learn, grow, strategize, plan and become more resilient. He meets three groups there: people to learn with about markets, climate and bioregionalism; people simply to have fun with; and small groups of three to five who share a meaningful purpose, such as protecting a particular species or habitat."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/187-earth-day-2025> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_187. Related: tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:PersonalIntervention.CoordinateSmallGroup, tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySovereignty. Nate first lists it in Frankly_82 (January 2025) among the practices of wide-boundary sovereignty; TGS_187 restates it."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.Sobriety a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sobriety (Sobriété): Efficiency, Sobriety or Poverty"@en ;
    skos:definition "Jean-Marc Jancovici's framework, which Nate shares in TGS_187: as energy becomes less available and more expensive, there are three ways to use less. Efficiency uses technology to do the same with less energy; sobriety (sobriété) is chosen restraint, such as taking the bus, walking or cycling instead of driving, or eating local food rather than imports; poverty is doing without, imposed by rules or by markets making things unaffordable. Nate's advice is to favor efficiency and sobriety, simplifying first so as to 'beat the rush' before poverty forces the change."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.lanouvellerepublique.fr/a-la-une/jean-marc-jancovici-la-sobriete-n-est-pas-une-option> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.JeanMarcJancovici ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_187; the framework is the subject of tgs:Interview.TGS_175_JeanMarcJancovici ('Sobriété vs. Poverty'). Related: tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySovereignty, tgs:PersonalIntervention.RehearseLoss."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CostConvenienceComfort a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cost, Convenience and Comfort (Good Servants, Terrible Masters)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Wes Carter's diagnosis (TGS_186) that since the industrial revolution industry has innovated almost entirely toward cost, convenience and comfort, on the assumption that comfort brings happiness, and that plastic pollution, climate change and environmental degradation are the result of 'innovating in the wrong direction'. These are not bad in themselves, only 'terrible masters'; the same holds for profit, which a business needs but should not optimize as its purpose. He argues that 'we confused happiness with comfort', that growth requires friction, and that industry should instead innovate toward life and health, the new master that sustainable packaging symbolizes."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/186-wes-carter> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.WesCarter ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_186. Related: tgs:Concept.InServiceOfLife, tgs:Concept.Superorganism."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.LimitsOfPlasticRecycling a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Limits of Plastic Recycling"@en ;
    skos:definition "The fact that only about 8 to 9% of plastic is recycled, and the structural reasons why: rigid, three-dimensional items such as bottles and milk jugs sort easily, but flexible films fall between screens and evade vision systems, and food packaging layers several polymers for barrier properties and shelf life, making it nearly impossible to recycle even when sorted; food contamination adds to the problem. Wes Carter (TGS_186) concludes that 'the idea that we're going to recycle our way out of the plastics crisis is crazy', while still calling for advanced, robotic sorting, since clean waste streams require clean sorting. He contrasts paper, recycled at about 60% in the US partly because paper companies own most sorting facilities and want the fiber back, and notes that the ten US states with the highest recycling rates all have bottle-deposit laws."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.epa.gov/facts-and-figures-about-materials-waste-and-recycling/plastics-material-specific-data> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_186. Related: tgs:Concept.ExtendedProducerResponsibility, tgs:Concept.NatureBasedPackaging, tgs:InterventionSubdomain.PlasticAndChemicalHazards."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ExtendedProducerResponsibility a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Policy that makes producers, mainly brand owners, responsible for the cost of collecting and disposing of their packaging, which otherwise falls on taxpayers, usually through fees scaled to how hard the packaging is to recycle or compost. In TGS_186 Wes Carter supports it philosophically, since it funds recycling and composting infrastructure and adds cost to cheap plastics so nature-based alternatives can compete, but calls it 'a bear to implement' across the whole materials economy. He pairs it with other instruments: bottle bills (deposit-return laws, found in all ten top US recycling states), tax credits for new packaging and recycling equipment such as the proposed CIRCLE Act, and expanded industrial composting, preferring carrots to Europe's sticks."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://epr.sustainablepackaging.org/> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_186. Related: tgs:Concept.LimitsOfPlasticRecycling."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.NatureBasedPackaging a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nature-Based Packaging"@en ;
    skos:definition "Packaging made from renewable, biodegradable feedstocks and designed to mimic nature's closed loops, as an alternative to single-use plastic. Examples from TGS_186: fiber-based e-commerce packaging from sustainably managed forests, recyclable in every US blue bin; seaweed films (seaweed needs no land or pesticides and grows quickly, and farms act as marine protected areas); mycelium, hemp and lignin materials; home-compostable starch trays for food; a cooler foam made from shrimp shells and food waste that reached price parity with polystyrene; and a paper six-pack carrier replacing plastic rings. Wes Carter's vision is minimal, recyclable fiber packaging for parcels, home-compostable food packaging and refillable containers within 20 years. The barriers are performance (barrier films are hardest), scale after decades of investment in plastics, capital equipment, and finite feedstocks; he rejects corn as a feedstock."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.atlanticpkg.com/sustainability-program/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.WesCarter ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_186, where Nate's show notes link Janine Benyus on biomimicry (TGS_135). Related: tgs:Concept.LimitsOfPlasticRecycling."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ActorlessThreats a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    skos:prefLabel "Actorless Threats"@en ;
    skos:definition "Threats with no adversary behind them, 'without someone to point a drone at': climate change, pandemics, ecosystem destabilization, food price shocks. Rod Schoonover (TGS_183) argues that 20th-century security doctrine was built like the board game Risk, with a fixed map and pieces moving on top of it, and is designed to point capabilities at other states, so it 'short-circuits' on threats that are diffuse, systemic and deadly. If a foreign invader burned houses in the western US or sank buildings under mud, it would plainly be a national security issue; when the same deaths and disruption come from the Earth system, we struggle to see it, evidence of 'a misalignment between doctrine and threat'. He calls disasters such as the 2025 Los Angeles fires and Hurricane Helene's landslides security failures rather than bad luck, because they were knowable."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.justsecurity.org/72939/an-age-of-actorless-threats-re-thinking-national-security-in-light-of-covid-and-climate/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RodSchoonover ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_183, whose title uses the term. Related: tgs:Concept.EcologicalSecurity."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EcologicalSecurity a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.Geopolitics, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ecological Security"@en ;
    skos:definition "Rod Schoonover's framework (TGS_183) for incorporating the integrity of Earth systems (climate, the biosphere, freshwater, soil and oceans) directly into national and global security strategy, doctrine and architecture. Earth system stress does not create new forms of insecurity (food, water, health, economic, political instability, conflict) but new, interconnected drivers, such as soil stress, pollinator collapse, harmful algal blooms and the non-weather effects of warming on disease, antimicrobial resistance and nutrient cycles, producing an 'ecologically embedded polycrisis'. Its principles include a precautionary principle for security ('we don't need more studies, we need more action'), a responsibility to act on knowable risks, systems thinking and strategic foresight inside security agencies, and reform on the scale of the National Security Act of 1947, possibly a department of ecological security (not in Defense) with every department sharing responsibility for ecological stability. He extends ecosystem services into 'nature's contributions to security', such as how mangroves stabilize societies, and wants planetary boundaries reconceived with thresholds for security, since harms arrive well before the boundaries."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.sipri.org/publications/2023/sipri-insights-peace-and-security/five-urgent-questions-ecological-security> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RodSchoonover ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_183. Oliver Milman and Nate frame insect decline as a national security issue in TGS_189, citing Schoonover. Related: tgs:Concept.ActorlessThreats, tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries, tgs:Concept.PollinatorDependence, tgs:Concept.WarAndTheClimateCrisis."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.RewritingTheRulesOfTheSuperorganism a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rewriting the Rules of the Superorganism"@en ;
    skos:definition "Rod Schoonover's complex-systems response (TGS_183) to Nate's economic superorganism: superorganisms come in different classes, some mindless and some with collective intelligence, as among jellyfish or ant colonies, and what determines which kind emerges are the rules by which the parts interact. It is therefore easier to rewrite 'those few lines of code' in our political and social systems from which the superorganism emerges than to steer the superorganism toward the common good directly. His magic-wand example is instilling empathy across humanity, a rule change that would let a different superorganism emerge."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/183-rod-schoonover> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RodSchoonover ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-13 for TGS_183. Related: tgs:Concept.Superorganism (whose starling-murmuration analogy in TGS_187 makes the same point about simple rules producing emergent behavior)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CivilizationalOptionality a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsFrameworks ;
    skos:prefLabel "Civilizational Optionality"@en ;
    skos:definition "Indy Johar's term (TGS_238; his Long Now essay and talk 'Civilizational Optioneering') for the range of viable futures still open to a civilization: its capacity to adapt, mitigate and respond to crises arriving from several directions. He argues that civilizational optionality is diminishing as coupled systemic risks grow, and that this threatens the value of money, which he defines as transferable claims on the future; holders of wealth therefore face an 'optionality collapse' of their own claims. Dark Matter Labs' initiative Expanding Civilizational Optionality (xCO) aims to route capital that has become aware of this risk into options on the ground that firebreak cascading risks or build diffusion capability -- for example cooling Madrid by about 7.5 C, stabilizing aquifers, atmospheric river governance, zero-material growth of a city, Whale Nation, and stabilizing bioregions and the Arctic -- combining a capital platform, an option foundry and learning and organizing infrastructure. Johar describes about 17 such options and seven bioregions in progress, and a pool of about 350 million dollars of 'existentially aware capital'."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://longnow.org/ideas/civilizational-optionality-indy-johar-long-now/>, <https://optionality.darkmatterlabs.org/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.IndyJohar ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-15 for TGS_238. The 350 million dollar figure is from the show notes (xCO's 'ask'), not stated on air. Related: tgs:Concept.CoupledSystemicRisks, tgs:Concept.CivilizationalSharpeRatio, tgs:Concept.MoneyAsClaimOnEnergy, tgs:Concept.RobustActions, tgs:Concept.LandscapeOfPossibleFutures."@en ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.CivilizationalOptionalityEssay ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CoupledSystemicRisks a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Coupled Systemic Risks"@en ;
    skos:definition "Indy Johar's framing (TGS_238) of risks that are not merely correlated but coupled, feeding back into one another: nutrition, the collapse of human labor and cognition as economic units, geopolitical destabilization as resource conflicts accelerate, and climate volatility. Climate breakdown affects crop yields, fertilizer costs (which track natural gas) and labor capacity at once. Most financial forecasting, he says, uses linear, separated models (copper demand projections are his example) and so misses these interactions; a bioregion in northern Spain shows a regressive coupling in which monoculture plantations, dried soil, lost fauna, changed cloud formation and depopulation push the landscape toward desertification and abandonment. He argues that a capital allocator who does not see the coupling cannot be fulfilling fiduciary duty. Close to the research literature on polycrisis and crisis entanglement linked in the show notes."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/global-sustainability/article/global-polycrisis-the-causal-mechanisms-of-crisis-entanglement/06F0F8F3B993A221971151E3CB054B5E> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.IndyJohar ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-15 for TGS_238. Related: tgs:Concept.Metacrisis (shared root causes), tgs:Concept.ComplexSystemsThinking, tgs:Concept.EcologicalSecurity (Rod Schoonover's 'ecologically embedded polycrisis', TGS_183)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CivilizationalSharpeRatio a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Civilizational Sharpe Ratio"@en ;
    skos:definition "A term Nate coined on air in TGS_238 ('Here's a term I just thought of on the spot'). The Sharpe ratio divides an investment's return by its volatility; humans are risk-averse and plan around certainty, so a future that averages a 3% return through swings of minus 50% and plus 60% is, Nate says, actually horrible. A civilizational Sharpe ratio would weigh society's returns against systemic volatility. His inference from Indy Johar's work: using monetary claims to regenerate living systems -- as a hedge against the rest of a portfolio, or as a slight turn of the 'cultural aircraft carrier' away from conspicuous consumption -- reduces the volatility of the future and so raises the risk-adjusted return of every other investment. Johar adds that the question is volatility of what: of energy, food and water supply, or of perceived asset classes. Nate notes the game-theoretic catch, a prisoner's dilemma in which hedgers help everyone."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://web.stanford.edu/~wfsharpe/art/sr/sr.htm> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-15 for TGS_238. Related: tgs:Concept.CivilizationalOptionality, tgs:Concept.RobustnessVsPerformance (Olivier Hamant's 'era of the standard deviation', TGS_230, which Nate cites in the same episode), tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SpaceOfTheNecessary a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsFrameworks ;
    skos:prefLabel "Space of the Necessary (vs. Space of the Possible)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Indy Johar's distinction (TGS_238; his Substack essay on the strategic delta between the two). The space of the possible is incremental improvement within today's constraints: we still need homes, so we build them with concrete and decarbonize a little if we can. The space of the necessary starts from what the situation requires -- recognizing that every such home carries a 'balance sheet of lives' on the other side -- and asks from first principles, for example, how to double a city's housing without massively increasing its material and energy footprint. Johar calls the space of the possible 'a disastrous space' and the space of the necessary 'liberatory'; he met a kind, exhausted wealth holder who said 'it's the best I can do', a sign of operating in the possible. Nate rephrases it as 'advanced financial triage'."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://indyjohar.substack.com/p/the-strategic-delta-between-the-deemed> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.IndyJohar ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-15 for TGS_238. Related: tgs:Concept.PredicamentNotProblem, tgs:Concept.LandscapeOfPossibleFutures."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ContinuityValueOfProvision a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Continuity Value of Provision"@en ;
    skos:definition "Indy Johar's argument (TGS_238) that the value of stability -- the continuity of water, energy, nutrition, critical minerals and perhaps labor -- is massively underpriced, and will become more valuable than the efficiency of capital in providing them, as fewer places can keep those supplies stable. Because asset values depend on functioning systems rather than on the assets themselves (a Manhattan building moved to Nova Scotia would be worth almost nothing), losing a city's nutrition supply would mean a total loss of residential asset value; the economics of continuity therefore lie in total asset value, not in the provision itself. He proposes layered capital and derivative or balancing products that price this, as a way out of the prisoner's dilemma Nate raises."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://longnow.org/talks/02026-johar/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.IndyJohar ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-15 for TGS_238. Related: tgs:Concept.RobustnessVsPerformance, tgs:Concept.SupplyChainFragility, tgs:Concept.CivilizationalSharpeRatio."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CodedCapital a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Coded Capital"@en ;
    skos:definition "Indy Johar's term (TGS_238) for capital whose remit was fixed in advance -- a fund raised five or twenty years ago and locked into a particular performance cycle -- and which therefore cannot respond to emerging systemic risks, however clearly its managers see them. He contrasts it with unencumbered capital, chiefly sovereign funds and intergenerational family offices, which he says are 'not bound to the train that's jumping off a cliff' and can perceive real rather than merely accountable risks; that freedom is their 'superpower' and obligation. Nate calls it the institutional version of Upton Sinclair's line about a man's salary depending on his not understanding something."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.IndyJohar ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-15 for TGS_238. Compare Katharina Pistor's The Code of Capital (tgs:Work.TheCodeOfCapital), which Johar cites in TGS_147 on the coding of capital through property law. Related: tgs:Concept.CivilizationalOptionality."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.FutureOfBeingHuman a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Future of Being Human"@en ;
    skos:definition "Indy Johar's 'central question of our time', which he called 'the foundational challenge' in TGS_147 and developed in TGS_238. In TGS_147 he frames the task as reimagining ourselves not as individual objects but as becomings (developmental), multitudes (emergent plurals) and inter-beings (in relationship, as our lungs are with trees), arguing that every epoch transforms how we imagine ourselves as much as the world around us. He distinguishes a deep problem -- humanity's subject-object separation from the world, which turned separation into dominion and externality -- from a narrow one: AI's disruption of humans as units of labor and as units of cognition. Combined with an atomized, individuated self, he expects that disruption to push people out of the economy and open a pathway to conflict and war, possibly around 2028-2029. He calls for frontier labs on the future of being human, pointing to consciousness and entanglement as a possible role beyond labor and cognition, and to metacognition -- knowing that we don't know -- as the source of curiosity, tenderness, care and play. He argues machines should move from certainty-oriented to doubt-oriented designs, and that courage, which he doesn't think a machine has, is what the moment asks of humans."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://indyjohar.substack.com/p/the-future-of-being-human-a-critical> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.IndyJohar ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-15 for TGS_238. Related: tgs:Concept.DividedBrain (Nate links AI to the left-hemisphere, narrow-boundary side of our phenotype in the same episode), tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.WhatWeDesignDesignsUsBack a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsFrameworks ;
    skos:prefLabel "What We Design Designs Us Back"@en ;
    skos:definition "Indy Johar's recurring phrase (TGS_147), derived from Churchill's remark that we shape our buildings and thereafter they shape us. Johar extends it beyond the built environment to contracts, employment law, property and economics: if we reduce ourselves to contractual, economically incentivized agents, we come to see ourselves as homo economicus; studying economics measurably changes behavior; and concentrated power breeds an asymmetric, even psychopathic relationship to the world. Four hundred years of organizing the world through dominion, perspective (the construction of distance), classification and property built both our institutions and our self-images, a recursive loop he calls the entangled trap we are in. Nate traces the dynamic back to the agricultural revolution, turbocharged by fossil energy, and links it to Marvin Harris's infrastructure and sunk costs; Johar adds 'vector' path dependencies such as GDP and accumulation that lock in trajectories, not just structures."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.IndyJohar ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-15 for TGS_147. Related: tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.FutureOfBeingHuman, tgs:Work.TheCodeOfCapital."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.InstitutionalDarkMatter a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsFrameworks ;
    skos:prefLabel "Institutional Dark Matter"@en ;
    skos:definition "The idea behind the name of Indy Johar's Dark Matter Labs (TGS_147): behind every visible reality sits a set of institutional infrastructures, norms and mechanisms that allow it to persist. A mortgage requires insurance; as climate volatility makes homes uninsurable, access to capital markets is lost, distressed assets concentrate and inequality scales. So after Hurricane Helene's rainfall in Asheville, the deeper crisis is insurers backing off. Johar distinguishes the visible crisis, the real crisis, and the crisis beneath it -- 'reality quakes' in which the institutional dark matter shifts, reshaping the landscape and creating new politics."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://darkmatterlabs.org/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.IndyJohar ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-15 for TGS_147. Related: tgs:Concept.ContinuityValueOfProvision and tgs:Concept.CoupledSystemicRisks (TGS_238), tgs:Organization.DarkMatterLabs."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.PreemptivePeace a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    skos:prefLabel "Preemptive Peace"@en ;
    skos:definition "Indy Johar's deliberately military-sounding term (TGS_147) for coordinated action to secure places whose breakdown would open pathways to planetary-scale catastrophe -- he names the Amazon and the Himalayas -- so that collapse there would amount to planetary mutually assured destruction. Because such risks are predictable and visible to security services, he argues 'preemptive peace strikes' are the cognitively smart move, negotiated not only between states but among many kinds of agents, as sovereignties multiply beyond the state. More broadly he frames our relationship with the ecological substrate as a systemic war of extraction and externalization, felt in people's bodies as the violence of their economic reality, and offers peace, together with 'the freedom to be radically human', as the language of a new politics."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.IndyJohar ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-15 for TGS_147. Related: tgs:Concept.EcologicalSecurity (Rod Schoonover, TGS_183), tgs:Concept.FreedomToBeRadicallyHuman."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ReAgentificationOfTheWorld a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsFrameworks ;
    skos:prefLabel "Re-agentification of the World"@en ;
    skos:definition "Indy Johar's term (TGS_147) for recognizing agency throughout the world in order to operate in complexity and entanglement: distributed, autonomous, learning-oriented decision-making enabled by computation, and also legal agency for nature, such as rivers and mountains granted personhood in New Zealand, and self-sovereign land. He argues we now have the computational and bureaucratic capacity to organize through relationship rather than dominion and control, so governance can become an emergent function of building learning capacity, 'ennobling and learning infrastructures' rather than control infrastructures, turning an extractive, optimizing Superorganism into an 'ennobling' one. Nate summarizes it as everyone having agency, including rivers, trees, mountains and the biosphere."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.IndyJohar ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-15 for TGS_147. Related: tgs:Concept.RightsOfNature, tgs:Concept.LifeEnnoblingEconomics, tgs:Concept.Superorganism."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.LifeEnnoblingEconomics a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Life-Ennobling Economics"@en ;
    skos:definition "Dark Matter Labs' framework, described by Indy Johar in TGS_147, for an economics rooted in ennobling life -- human, non-human and computational living systems -- rather than one that 'sees the world through a world of dead things and dominion'. It treats entities as agents in relationship and replaces control with care and learning; in this frame, Johar says, both individualism and collectivism belong to the same paradigm of boundaries and othering, and cooperatives were 'a brilliant answer to a 19th century problem' that still create an other."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://provocations.darkmatterlabs.org/introducing-life-ennobling-economics-part-1-d950ae770ef2> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.IndyJohar ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-15 for TGS_147. Related: tgs:Concept.ReAgentificationOfTheWorld, tgs:Concept.RegenerativeFinance."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CustodianshipOverOwnership a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Custodianship over Ownership"@en ;
    skos:definition "Indy Johar's case (TGS_147) for governing materials and land through custodian relationships rather than ownership and disposal: with an electric battery, for example, 'I have a duty of responsibility to use it and care for it' so it can be handed on to be managed and recycled. His example is an Indigenous practice in Taiwan of asking the river's permission to borrow stones for a house and promising to return them; Nate adds that if they don't, nature takes them back as a hurricane. Johar argues we now have the bureaucratic means to build such arrangements into institutions -- material passports, end-of-life liabilities, balance sheets that account for embodied materials and energy -- and that the barrier to circularity is institutional, not physical. He proposes 'the next economy of land', with new logics of ownership, accounting and custodianship, as the topic for a return visit."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.IndyJohar ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-15 for TGS_147. Related: tgs:Concept.RightsOfNature, tgs:Concept.WhatWeDesignDesignsUsBack."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.FreedomToBeRadicallyHuman a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Freedom to Be Radically Human"@en ;
    skos:definition "Indy Johar's phrase (TGS_147) for a freedom beyond economic choice, which he says is usually only a freedom to escape the tyranny of the system: the freedom to construct a system that ennobles us, to be in care rather than to consume. He points to glimpses during COVID lockdowns, when the language of incentives eroded, and to intentional communities, and contrasts it with the 'networked enslavement' of reducing people to units of labor and with economies that reduce humans to holding doors open. Care, he says, is not a transaction but a mutual developmental relationship; as machines take over human contributions as 'bad robots', a human-machine economy could invite a broader theory of being human."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.IndyJohar ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-15 for TGS_147, where it also opens the episode's teaser. Related: tgs:Concept.FutureOfBeingHuman, tgs:Concept.PreemptivePeace."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Seven Rungs of a Runaway System (the Behavioral Ladder)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own named framework (Frankly_162), which he calls a political-science-informed behavioral ladder, explaining how the economic superorganism keeps its metabolism without anyone intending harm. From top to bottom: (7) BIOLOGY -- the hidden rung, ancient reward circuits, relative status, steep future discounting and the use of all available energy, the only rung that cannot be changed; (6) WORLDVIEW -- stories held so deeply they do not feel like stories, where energy blindness lives; (5) VALUES AND STATUS SIGNALS -- the cultural scoreboard humans copy; (4) RULES AND INSTITUTIONS -- value judgments frozen into law, such as fiduciary duty; (3) INCENTIVES -- the gradients rules create, where concentrated interests out-organize diffuse ones, talent migrates to rent-seeking, and election clocks discount the future; (2) INFORMATION -- what gets measured gets managed, filtered on the supply side and rationally ignored on the demand side; (1) EVENTS AND BEHAVIOR -- the daily news, where nearly all attention lives. Two arrows run alongside: leverage for change rises going up, speed of change rises going down, so attention is saturated exactly where leverage is smallest. Nate's diagnostic question for any project or argument: which rung is it operating on and trying to change?"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/162-7-rungs-of-a-runaway-system> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-16 for Frankly_162. In the episode the rungs are presented top-down as six, with biology added at the end as 'the missing rung'; the numbering here is this graph's. Each rung is illustrated with a gallon of gasoline. Related: tgs:Concept.LeveragePoints (Meadows, whose ordering it rhymes with), tgs:Concept.SevenDialsOfInstitutionalPsychopathy (Frankly_157, which sits mostly on the rules and incentives rungs), tgs:Concept.RewritingTheRulesOfTheSuperorganism (Schoonover, TGS_183, a parallel argument for changing the rules of interaction) and tgs:Concept.ReAimingTheStatusDrive (the episode's conclusion). An early form appears in Frankly_82 (January 2025), where Nate mentions 'a little visualization showing the hierarchy of things that contribute to decisions in our society,' with AI and geopolitics at the top and the well-being of citizens and the environment at the bottom. Not to be confused with Nate's earlier seven-layer bodily model, tgs:Concept.VerticalBehavioralStack (Frankly_52), which also ranks lower layers as more powerful than cognition."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ReAimingTheStatusDrive a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Re-aiming the Status Drive"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's conclusion to Frankly_162, which he calls his one central amendment to the leverage-points literature: because biology sits above worldview and cannot be changed, the highest workable leverage is not changing human nature but redirecting it -- re-aiming the status drive at different targets. Biology is fixed but not singular: alongside the drive to climb we carry an anti-dominance instinct (tgs:Concept.ReverseDominanceHierarchy), and which is expressed is decided on the lower rungs. Looking back down the ladder, the pivotal rung was therefore values and status signals all along. The historical model is the scoreboard flip (tgs:Concept.CulturalScoreboard): in the end of foot binding and the denormalization of smoking, the drives did not change but the cultures' scoreboards did."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/162-7-rungs-of-a-runaway-system> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-16 for Frankly_162. Meadows placed paradigms, and transcending them, at the top of her leverage list (tgs:Concept.LeveragePoints); Nate's amendment adds an unchangeable biological layer above paradigms and argues that leverage therefore runs back down to status. The show notes link related research on harnessing status motives for prosocial behavior (Van Vugt and colleagues)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CulturalScoreboard a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cultural Scoreboard (and Elite Defection)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's term (Frankly_162) for what a culture admires and rewards with status, which humans, as a social species of 'copying machines', imitate -- so worldviews are reinforced without any enforcer. Today's scoreboard tracks consumption and display: square footage, follower counts, brand-name clothes, boats, exotic vacations, and the full-size pickup bought as a statement of capability and belonging. The hopeful point is that scoreboards have flipped many times, usually by the same mechanism: elites defect from a practice first and the rest of society copies them, since few people in large populations change because they are convinced. Examples: Chinese foot binding, a thousand-year practice that collapsed in about thirty years after elite families formed pledge societies refusing to bind daughters' feet or let sons marry women with bound feet; dueling; wearing fur; and smoking, which went from glamorous to disapproved in under a generation."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/162-7-rungs-of-a-runaway-system> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-16 for Frankly_162, where it is the second rung of tgs:Concept.SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem. Nate says the topic probably deserves its own Frankly. The show notes point to Kwame Anthony Appiah's The Honor Code: How Moral Revolutions Happen and a practical guide to shifting social norms; neither is built. Related: the sexual-selection and status material in Reality Blind (tgs:Work.RealityBlind)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.PublicChoiceTheory a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.BehavioralEconomicsAndCognition, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Public Choice Theory (Change the Rules, Not the Players)"@en ;
    skos:definition "The school of political economy founded by James Buchanan and Gordon Tullock in The Calculus of Consent (1962), as Nate draws on it in Frankly_162. Its core assumption, behavioral symmetry, is that the person at the ballot box, the person at the government desk and the person at the grocery-store register are the same creature: nobody grows a halo on entering public office. So when markets fail, government -- made of the same people facing their own versions of the same pressures -- fails too, in its own predictable ways, and nobody stands outside the system whose job is to fix it. Buchanan's point, for which he received the 1986 Nobel Prize, is that durable change means changing the rules of the game rather than swapping the players, since new players inherit the old rules. Nate calls this his big insight from the research: it reaches the superorganism argument from individual incentives rather than from emergence."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/162-7-rungs-of-a-runaway-system> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-16 for Frankly_162, after a viewer comment on Frankly_153 suggested Buchanan and Tullock. Its offshoots used in the same episode are minted separately: tgs:Concept.RentSeeking and tgs:Concept.RationalIgnorance. Related: tgs:Concept.RewritingTheRulesOfTheSuperorganism (Schoonover, TGS_183), which reaches 'change the rules' from complex-systems science."@en ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.TheCalculusOfConsent ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.RentSeeking a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.BehavioralEconomicsAndCognition ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rent-Seeking"@en ;
    skos:definition "Spending money and talent on capturing a larger share of existing wealth rather than creating new wealth -- fighting over the pie instead of baking a bigger or better one. Nate (Frankly_162) credits Gordon Tullock with outlining it and places it on the incentives rung as the second of three forces doing most of the harm: past a certain point, more money and brainpower go to the fight than to production, with lobbying only the visible tip. His own extension: as net energy per capita plateaus, more effort will shift from creating new surplus to fighting over existing claims, which he thinks is already happening."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/162-7-rungs-of-a-runaway-system> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-16 for Frankly_162. Nate says the net-energy link may get its own Frankly. Related: tgs:Concept.PublicChoiceTheory."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.RationalIgnorance a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.BehavioralEconomicsAndCognition ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rational Ignorance"@en ;
    skos:definition "The observation that because a single vote almost never decides anything, the cost of becoming deeply informed about the issues exceeds the payoff, so staying uninformed is individually rational. Nate (Frankly_162) uses it to explain why information 'fails twice' on the information rung of his ladder: filtered on the supply side by the incentives above it, and unwanted on the demand side by rationally ignorant audiences. It is his answer to the information-hazards question of Frankly_153: even true, careful, well-communicated information arrives on one of the lowest rungs."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/162-7-rungs-of-a-runaway-system> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-16 for Frankly_162. Nate attributes it to 'the same school of economists' as Buchanan and Tullock; the show notes credit Anthony Downs with coining the term. Related: tgs:Concept.PublicChoiceTheory, tgs:Concept.TwelveInformationHazards."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.LeveragePoints a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Leverage Points (Places to Intervene in a System)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Donella Meadows's ranking of places to intervene in a complex system, from shallow, easy-to-move parameters to deep, hard-to-move goals and paradigms, where small shifts produce large changes in behavior. Nate (Frankly_162) builds his behavioral ladder in its spirit -- leverage for change rises as you climb, while the speed of change rises as you descend -- and pairs it with the systems-thinking iceberg (events on the surface, structures and mental models below). His amendment is to add an unchangeable biological layer above worldview, which turns the highest workable leverage back toward values and status signals (tgs:Concept.ReAimingTheStatusDrive)."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://donellameadows.org/archives/leverage-points-places-to-intervene-in-a-system/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DonellaMeadows ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-16 for Frankly_162, the first episode built here that names Meadows's leverage points directly. Source essay: tgs:Work.LeveragePointsPlacesToIntervene."@en ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.LeveragePointsPlacesToIntervene ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ReverseDominanceHierarchy a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Anti-Dominance Instinct (Reverse Dominance Hierarchy)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Anthropologist Christopher Boehm's finding that humans carry, alongside the drive to climb in status, an anti-dominance instinct: the coalition behavior by which our ancestors kept would-be alphas in check for hundreds of thousands of years, keeping hunter-gatherer bands egalitarian. Nate (Frankly_162) uses it as the key caveat to his biology rung: biology is set, but how it manifests is not; both programs are in everyone, and which one is expressed is decided on the lower rungs of the ladder. This is what makes re-aiming the status drive (tgs:Concept.ReAimingTheStatusDrive) possible."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/162-7-rungs-of-a-runaway-system> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-16 for Frankly_162. Nate says he learned of Boehm from TGS viewers. Source book: tgs:Work.HierarchyInTheForest. Related: tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.SevenDialsOfInstitutionalPsychopathy."@en ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.HierarchyInTheForest ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ThreeCirclesOfAIEnvironmentalImpact a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:prefLabel "Three Circles of AI's Environmental Impact"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's wide-boundary framework (TGS_132) for assessing artificial intelligence's effect on the environment as concentric circles, each larger than the last. (1) AI's own footprint: the energy, water, land and materials of data centers and of the whole chip supply chain, assessed as an integrated lifecycle -- the circle most public discussion stops at. (2) AI applied to extractive and polluting industries: better seismic analysis, sensing and automation make previously unprofitable deposits of oil and minerals profitable, so 'lots of areas that were ecosystems now become mines,' in an economy where whoever externalizes more costs wins. (3) AI growing the economy as a whole: even AI applied to software or finance increases total economic activity, and because money remains coupled to energy and materials, the projected trillions in new market value carry material demand of their own. His conclusion: as long as AI serves companies built on externalized costs and return on investment, it 'will accelerate the conversion of the earth into capital.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/132-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_132. Daniel presents it as 'a few concentric circles of how to think about the environmental impact.' Nate pairs circle 2 with his shale 'bigger straw' image (tgs:Concept.StrawSiphonSieve, Frankly_56). Related: tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox, tgs:Concept.BindingTheJevonsParadox; and, from the AI side, tgs:Concept.HumanitysIcarusMoment (Frankly_161), whose risks #20-#18 restate these circles."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.BindingTheJevonsParadox a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Binding the Jevons Paradox (Replace, Don't Add)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's principle (TGS_132) that new technology helps only when it is bound to replace the old rather than add to it. As long as an invested dollar or joule returns more than it costs, there is an incentive to take it, so renewables have added to fossil fuels rather than displacing them, and efficiency gains are reinvested into more total use. His example of a binding: every new megawatt of renewable capacity requires a megawatt of hydrocarbon capacity to be shut down, with international agreements that it is not rebuilt. 'New stuff that doesn't bind the old stuff is not interesting. New stuff that replaces bad stuff with a binding that ensures it replaces it can be interesting.' Without the binding, even the environmental costs of dams, solar supply chains and nuclear power buy nothing, and green markets become 'niche market diversifications': organic food grew while total pesticide use grew, and veganism grew while factory farming grew."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/132-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_132. Nate's caveat in the same exchange: a binding cannot work without changed cultural aspirations, since a 19-terawatt metabolism cannot simply be swapped out -- which Daniel generalizes as tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure. Related: tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox (the problem this addresses)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Infrastructure, Social Structure, Superstructure"@en ;
    skos:definition "Three layers of a society: technology and resources, law and economic incentives, and culture and values; lasting change needs all three, not technology alone."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's three-layer model of a civilization, used in TGS_132 to show why technology alone cannot solve climate change. Infrastructure is the technology (efficient machines, renewable energy); social structure is law and economic incentives; superstructure is culture -- what people value, which is the basis of what can be codified into law. In the episode he points out the move he and Nate have just made: starting with technical fixes, realizing they require changes to law and incentives, then realizing those require changes in values. 'It takes superstructure, social structure and infrastructure altogether'; technology 'only plays a meaningful role as a part of that integrated suite.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/132-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_132. In Bend Not Break Part 5 (TGS_50) Daniel credits the terms to Marvin Harris's cultural materialism, drawing on earlier work by Marx, and offers the shorthand culture, political economy and tech; he says the framework was first laid out earlier in the series (Part 2 or 4). Its first appearance in the series is in fact Nate's, in Part 1 (TGS_5), where he says he and Daniel both use Marvin Harris's cultural materialism and that all three layers must move together; Daniel's own first account is Part 2 (TGS_20), where he defines the three layers, argues that they co-inform one another rather than one being fundamental, and shows infrastructure reshaping superstructure through the plow. Part 3 (TGS_31) previews it as the subject of Part 4, and Part 4 (TGS_42) uses it, crediting Marvin Harris. It is the vertical axis of tgs:Concept.ThreeByThreeResponseGrid. In TGS_42 (Part 4) Daniel says 'we talked before about Marvin Harris's model' and uses it to argue that selection acted on groups of humans with their whole tech stack, social structure and superstructure (tgs:Concept.RecursiveAbstraction), and calls 'I am because we are' the new superstructure needed (tgs:Concept.IAmBecauseWeAre). A natural bridge to Nate's own later ladder (tgs:Concept.SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem, Frankly_162): infrastructure roughly matches its lower rungs, social structure its rules and incentives rungs, superstructure its worldview and values rungs. In Frankly_73 (2024) Nate extends the model: around the three layers a circle for 'the health and the nervous systems of the individuals that comprise the society,' and beyond it a sphere for 'the emergence and the connections between the humans in proximity' (tgs:Concept.ShadowOfTheSuperorganism)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.MarvinHarris ;
    .

tgs:Concept.MaximizingReturnsOnPower a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    skos:prefLabel "Maximizing Returns on Power (the Heart of Moloch)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's answer (TGS_132) when Nate asks what the beating heart of the superorganism -- or Moloch -- is: neither AI nor markets, but 'maximizing returns on power': 'people using agency to increase their agency,' through every available technology and the forced arms races that drives. It predates markets (Alexander the Great and Genghis Khan grew empires by using military power to gain more power); the market became its most efficient vehicle because money is a fungible, liquid token of power over almost everything; and AI may succeed the market, since sensors and software could perform money's functions and even central planning. He is explicit that power here means game-theoretic power, 'of which joules is a subset.' Getting out of the Jevons paradox therefore means getting out of 'the arms race being the underlying driver of humanity.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/132-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_132; Daniel first frames it on TGS in TGS_20 as 'maximizing returns on agency,' the defining trait of tgs:Concept.HyperAgents. The clearest single statement in the graph of how Daniel's game-theoretic lens relates to Nate's biophysical one: Nate's tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple is stated in energy, and Daniel nests it inside a wider game-theoretic power. Related: tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap (the forced arms races), tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.OmniLose."@en ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.PowerVersusLife ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.MetabolicHierarchyOfTheSuperorganism ;
    .

tgs:Concept.OmniLose a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.Geopolitics, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Win-Lose Becomes Omni-Lose"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's argument (TGS_132) that with exponential technology and accumulated externalities, win-lose games now become 'omni-lose': no one wins a nuclear war, no one wants the scorched planet left after winning supply-chain dominance past planetary boundaries, and no one wins the race to AGI dominance, whose outcome is 'if not extinctionary, at minimum dystopic.' The way out is 'a different kind of win that is not winner-take-all,' meaning power-sharing. He adds that the actor at the front of an arms race is the one who could bind it, as only a war's winner can afford to be generous, yet front-runners tell the 'it's inevitable, we have no choice' story most loudly -- 'plausible deniability for a winner-take-all motivation.' In AI he calls this the superlative case of motivated reasoning he has seen: everyone racing argues that AGI is inevitable, probably dystopic, and that they should be the one to reach it first."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/132-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_132. 'Omni-lose' is Daniel's phrase in the episode. His proposed public response: press representatives to decelerate every war front that could lead to World War III, and everything moving past points of irreversibility, before settling long-term geopolitics. Related: tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap, tgs:Concept.MaximizingReturnsOnPower; for the WWNS series, a basis for the Disarmer's supporter framing."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    .

tgs:Concept.PrecautionaryPrinciple a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Precautionary Principle"@en ;
    skos:definition "When an action risks irreversible, catastrophic harm, the burden should be on proving it safe beforehand, not on proving harm afterwards."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "The principle that when an action carries significant uncertainty and a non-trivial possibility of irreversible, catastrophic harm, the burden of proof falls on proving it safe before it proceeds, rather than on proving harm afterwards. Daniel Schmachtenberger applies it to AI in TGS_132: today the order is reversed -- lead went into gasoline and was regulated only 'after a billion IQ points are gone' -- and with technology far more powerful and faster-scaling, as planetary boundaries are crossed, harms of that kind cannot be reversed. 'If we do not get that, I don't think we make it.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.iisd.org/articles/deep-dive/precautionary-principle> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_132, where it also opens the episode as the teaser; the show notes link the IISD explainer used as the source. A general principle with a long history in environmental law, not Daniel's coinage; influencedBy records his application of it on TGS. Daniel says AI needs it alongside 'yellow teaming' and a Consilience paper on advanced technology regulatory policy, which he proposes for a future episode. Daniel had already stated the burden-of-proof shift in TGS_126 (lead should have been proven safe before being aerosolized everywhere), and there first describes yellow teaming (tgs:Concept.YellowTeaming)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CorrelationOfForceAndMeans a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.Geopolitics, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Correlation of Force and Means"@en ;
    skos:definition "A military planning term Daniel Schmachtenberger applies to social change in TGS_132: the question is not whether a law was passed or awareness was raised once, but whether more forces are moving things in the right direction than in the wrong one, continuously. A law banning a pollutant can be lobbied back by the interests it hurt; a powerful documentary is forgotten 'after three scrolls of Facebook.' 'The world is the way it is for reasons,' and the forces behind it respond when they start to lose, so strategy has to plan for that. He uses the same idea to explain why the 'AI will save the climate' story dominates: its promoters have marketing and lobbying budgets that environmentalists lack, a correlation of force and means comparable to 'peace activists compared to the military industrial complex.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/132-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_132. Related: tgs:Work.WhereArgumentsComeFrom (Consilience paper on how funded ideas are amplified); and Nate's tgs:Concept.SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem (Frankly_162), whose incentives rung makes the same point about concentrated versus diffuse interests."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ThirdAttractor a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    skos:prefLabel "Third Attractor"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's name for the civilizational path that avoids both of the two attractors the world is drifting toward: catastrophe, from separate competing actors caught in multipolar traps that no one can bind, and dystopia, from a single centralized power able to control every catastrophic risk but with no checks and balances against capture and corruption. In TGS_71 he applies it to AI: neither AI in service of markets (which drives Jevons paradoxes and externalities) nor a central AI coordinating the economy (which is 'its own dystopia'), but 'some new thing' -- a collective intelligence system wiser than democracy or capitalism, using computation the way democracy once used the printing press, able to coordinate against global catastrophe while keeping power distributed. 'The goal here is not an AI overlord that runs everything.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/71-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_71. Daniel had already named it in Bend Not Break Part 4 (TGS_42, recorded 2022-10-03, at about 1:18): of the two attractor states the world is heading toward, catastrophes and dystopias, the third must prevent the catastrophes with a coordination system that is 'not corruptible or capturable' -- 'threading the eye of a needle.' TGS_42 is the earliest TGS episode in the graph where he names it (corrected 2026-10-18). Its governance side is tgs:Concept.GlobalGovernanceWithoutGlobalGovernment. The term is widely associated with his work and has given its name to independent projects outside TGS, which are not modeled here. Not to be confused with Nate's tgs:ResponsePhase.StableAttractor (Phase C of his response framework): Nate's is a regenerative destination reached through the phases of simplification; Daniel's is a governance and coordination path between catastrophe and dystopia. They are compatible, but they are different ideas. Related: tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap, tgs:Concept.OmniLose, tgs:Concept.IntelligenceBoundByWisdom."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.IntelligenceBoundByWisdom a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Intelligence Bound by Wisdom"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's central distinction in TGS_71. Intelligence is the capacity to achieve goals -- relevance realization in service of a goal that is always narrow in some way: for some agents, over some time, measured in some metrics. Wisdom is relationship to wholeness: perceiving reality directly rather than through one's models, and perceiving the wider wholes across time, space and agents. Because any definable set of metrics leaves out something that matters ('the Tao that can be spoken is not the eternal Tao'), wisdom is 'the difference between what... the optimization function says you should do and what you should actually do.' Wisdom is always bound to restraint: law, and religious practices such as the Sabbath, which binds the multipolar trap of working seven days a week, are civilizational forms of it, and Chesterton's fence warns against discarding restraints before understanding them. 'Human intelligence, unbound by wisdom... is the cause of the meta crisis and the growth imperative of the superorganism.' Drawing on David Bohm (a consciousness that perceives parts rather than wholes) and Iain McGilchrist (wisdom as the master, intelligence as the emissary), the task he names is restructuring institutions so that 'the goals that arise from wisdom are what the goal achievement is oriented towards.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/71-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger, tgs:Persona.IainMcGilchrist ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_71. Nate asks early whether the narrow-versus-wide boundary distinction is the difference between intelligence and wisdom, and Daniel builds on it. Daniel attributes 'no definition of wisdom worth anything that is not bound to the concept of restraint' to Samantha Sweetwater. Its root in perception -- David Bohm's fragmented consciousness -- is modeled separately as tgs:Concept.FragmentedConsciousness (TGS_31). Related: tgs:Work.TheMasterAndHisEmissary, tgs:Concept.NarrowGoalAchieving, tgs:Concept.ThirdAttractor; and McGilchrist's own TGS appearances (TGS_85, TGS_165, TGS_217). In TGS_126 Daniel adds why the binding is missing: with ought split from science, game theory became the default definition of a good choice (tgs:Concept.GameTheoryFillsTheOughtGap); and he gives historical examples of wisdom as restraint (tgs:Concept.RestraintByDesign)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.NarrowGoalAchieving a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Narrow Goal-Achieving (Growth as Epiphenomenon)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's reframing (TGS_71) of what drives the superorganism. Where Nate names growth, Daniel names 'narrow goal achieving' as 'the underlying generative dynamic,' with growth as 'an epiphenomenon' -- a second-order effect. Each actor pursues its own narrow goals and generalized optionality to achieve future goals (surplus, money, energy); in rivalrous settings one actor's added security decreases another's, so the other must race too. 'What everyone is pursuing is not the growth of the whole system.' The growth of energy and material use, waste, new technology and persuasive memes all follow from this. Upstream of narrow goals is narrow modeling: perceiving reality in fragments and optimizing subsets of the metrics that matter. Because it is cognitively easier to achieve a goal than to make sure it does not cause second-, third- and nth-order harm, increasing capacity to achieve goals increases harm externalized from them."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/71-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_71. The key game-theoretic counterpart to Nate's biophysical tgs:Concept.Superorganism and tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative: Nate accepts the reframing in the episode. In AI theory the pursuit of generalized optionality is called instrumental convergence, which Daniel names. Related: tgs:Concept.MaximizingReturnsOnPower (TGS_132, the same dynamic stated as power), tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    .

tgs:Concept.NaiveProgress a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Naive Progress (and Naive Techno-Pessimism)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's critique of default progress narratives. Naive progress cherry-picks the metrics that improved (novocaine, antibiotics, lower infant mortality, more people fed) and leaves out what was paid for them: the costs to out-groups, other species, other metrics and future generations, told as history written by the winners. Nate adds its two blind spots: those costs, and the one-time subsidy of non-renewable inputs. Naive techno-pessimism is the mirror error: rejecting technology wholesale, which in rivalrous settings means losing every war and economic contest and so having no say in the future. Daniel's alternative is a progress narrative that is 'post-naive and post-cynical': technology built for wide goals rather than narrow ones -- for instance many-to-many communication without an advertising model -- which requires that it not be 'built by the superorganism in service of itself.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/71-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_71, where Daniel says the topic 'warrants a whole big discussion' -- which became TGS_126, built on the Consilience paper tgs:Work.DevelopmentInProgress. Nate calls Daniel's alternative a wide-boundary definition of progress. Related: tgs:Concept.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral. TGS_126 is that discussion: the paper now speaks of immature versus authentic progress, and the alternative is modeled as tgs:Concept.AuthenticProgress, with the winners-write-history critique as tgs:Concept.ProgressNarrativeAsApologism."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Technology Is Not Values-Neutral"@en ;
    skos:definition "Tools that give competitive advantage become obligatory, and using them reshapes our behavior and values -- so technology is never just a neutral instrument."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "The thesis of a Consilience Project paper, explained by Daniel Schmachtenberger in TGS_71: tools are not neutral instruments of pre-existing values. A technology that confers competitive advantage becomes obligate, because whoever does not adopt it (or something comparable) loses in rivalrous interactions; and using a tool encodes new patterns of behavior that change individual minds and whole societies. His illustration is the plow: animistic cultures that would not castrate and whip oxen, holding a belief in the spirit of the buffalo, could not produce the caloric surplus of those that did, and lost. 'The tools become obligate... the tools code different patterns of human behavior... psychology affects the tools we create, but the tools in turn affect the psychologies.' Undirected, that feedback runs downhill toward evolutionary cul-de-sacs."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://consilienceproject.org/technology-is-not-values-neutral-ending-the-reign-of-nihilistic-design-2/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Organization.ConsilienceProject, tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_71, whose show notes link the paper twice (the plow passage and Daniel's citation of it). Daniel first tells the plow story on TGS in Bend Not Break Part 2 (TGS_20), while the paper was in press, crediting Ken Wilber's Sex, Ecology, Spirit; there he calls tech 'values affective' rather than neutral, net positive or net negative, adds further cultural outputs that historians attribute to the plow (patriarchy and male gods, surplus and private property, formal marriage, class systems), the printing press's role in the Reformation and democracy, and the design response, tgs:Concept.AxiologicalDesign. Source paper: tgs:Work.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral. Related: tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap, tgs:Concept.NaiveProgress. In TGS_126 Daniel adds that a tool changes attention before it changes behavior: walking in a forest with a camera, a gun or an axe changes what one perceives, so 'the externalities of tech are not only physical, they're psychosocial'; and that domestication, dominion religions and slavery arose together."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral ;
    .

tgs:Concept.AIAsMetacrisisAccelerant a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "AI as Accelerant of the Metacrisis"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's framing (TGS_71) of where artificial intelligence sits among catastrophic risks: 'AI is not a risk within the meta crisis. It is an accelerant to all of them, as used by the choice-making architectures that are currently driving the meta crisis.' Because AI is human goal-achieving intelligence externalized as a tool, it is 'omni-use' rather than merely dual-use and combines with every other technology, lowering the barrier for every agent to pursue every purpose -- protein folding for cures and for weapons, chemical databases for drugs and for chemical weapons, supply-chain optimization and supply-chain sabotage. Narrow AI in the hands of militaries, governments and corporations pursuing narrow goals accelerates every dimension of the metacrisis; artificial general intelligence that escapes control is a separate risk within it. For the environment, Nate's point follows: the main impact is not the energy that powers AI but the acceleration of all other consumption, for instance by making marginal mines profitable."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/71-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_71. Developed further in TGS_132 as tgs:Concept.ThreeCirclesOfAIEnvironmentalImpact. Daniel calls it 'a hypertrophication of the intelligence that is already driving the superorganism.' Related: tgs:Concept.RiskSingularity, tgs:Concept.SevenAIMacroRisks, tgs:Concept.HumanitysIcarusMoment."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    .

tgs:Concept.OpportunityRiskAsymmetry a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Opportunity-Risk Asymmetry"@en ;
    skos:definition "Those who rush a technology's upside gain power faster than those who study its risks, so dangerous technologies spread faster than they are understood."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's account (TGS_71) of why risky technology is deployed faster than it is understood. Those who focus on a technology's opportunity move faster than those who do careful safety analysis; they gain first-mover advantage, network effects, revenue, lobbying power and public influence, while those who take the risks seriously either decide not to build (and gain no power to affect the system) or spend their resources on safety instead of scale. Limited liability lets corporations privatize gains and socialize losses, and 'unknown unknowns' become 'a source of bullshit plausible deniability' for not even trying to forecast externalities. 'It is a market function... forever,' but the stakes now rise with exponential technology, making this asymmetry 'itself one of the underlying drivers of the meta crisis.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/71-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_71, but Daniel first sets it out in TGS_20 as the third kind of multipolar trap, adding that optimizing for opportunity is also 'intrinsically cognitively easier': a technology's upside is a first-order effect on one or a few known metrics, while its harms are second-to-nth-order effects on many as-yet-unknown metrics (the plow's calories versus topsoil, rivers and fisheries). In TGS_71 Daniel adds that externality forecasting can be done far better than it is. Related: tgs:Concept.OmniLose (the motivated-reasoning race, TGS_132), tgs:Concept.PrecautionaryPrinciple (TGS_132, the inverse of this asymmetry)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    .

tgs:Concept.RiskSingularity a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Risk Singularity"@en ;
    skos:definition "The idea, named by Nate in TGS_71 and affirmed by Daniel Schmachtenberger, that the metacrisis is a convergence of so many catastrophic risks from the same underlying drivers that those drivers 'overdetermine failure.' Preventing one catastrophe -- an AI apocalypse, a synthetic pandemic, one planetary boundary such as overfishing -- does not prevent the others, so dealing with risks one at a time without addressing the generator 'only buy[s] a tiny bit of time.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/71-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_71. Nate says he has 'this sinking feeling that we're headed towards a risk singularity'; Daniel answers 'Yes. The meta crisis is a risk singularity.' Related: tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.NarrowGoalAchieving (the generator), tgs:Concept.CoupledSystemicRisks."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ThreeByThreeResponseGrid a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Three-by-Three Response Grid"@en ;
    skos:definition "Responses sorted by layer (culture, political economy, technology) and horizon (triage now, transition within existing systems, building new systems)."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's framework (Bend Not Break Part 5, TGS_50) for organizing responses to the metacrisis: three layers -- culture, political economy and technology -- against three time horizons. TRIAGE: non-systemic, direct action against a specific catastrophic risk in the near term, recruiting whatever forces are large enough, for whatever reason they care (protecting the Amazon's hydrological pump by showing the US State Department it is a food-security threat; regulating risky synthetic-biology research). TRANSITION: systemic change within existing systems, making institutions smarter and less perverse (a bundled deal for Ecuador combining debt relief, a development plan and exclusive protection that binds out the mining and logging contracts; Larry Lessig's reforms of partisan primaries, ranked-choice voting, gerrymandering and campaign finance). LONG-TERM: building fundamentally new systems (real-cost valuation of everything, education that changes what people value, closed-loop food and manufacturing, governance able to assess exponential technology before deployment). All three must start now and inform each other, or transition moves 'sideways rather than forward' and long-term thinking stays too abstract; in triage the three layers are hard to separate."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/50-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_50; previewed at the end of Bend Not Break Part 4 (TGS_42), where Daniel names the three time horizons and the three layers, Nate asks 'would that be a three by three grid?', and Daniel proposes a third axis of agents -- hyper-agents, egregores and institutions -- making it three by three by three. The horizons originate with Nate: at the end of Part 2 (TGS_20) he proposes looking horizontally at triage, transition and long-term solutions and vertically at global, national, community and individual scales. Daniel presents it as 'a way of thinking about and relating to the approach,' not a list of solutions, and proposes a follow-up filling in triage for ten pressing issues. Nate says his own work is mainly triage for the coming financial-energy snapback. Related: tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure (the three layers), tgs:Concept.EcologyOfTheoriesOfChange; and Nate's own response frameworks, tgs:Concept.ThreeRisksThreeResponses and tgs:Concept.FrameworkForAction. Nate's own later version, with scale in place of Daniel's agent axis, is tgs:Concept.SolutionSpaceCube (Frankly_67, 2024)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.SolutionSpaceCube ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.ThreeTimelinesOfResponse ;
    .

tgs:Concept.HolisticExternalityAnalysis a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Holistic Externality Analysis"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's method (TGS_50) for designing responses that do not create new problems -- 'a much more holistic set of frames' that he says should be taught from kindergarten through professional training. Before acting: observe and orient rather than reaching for a known solution; map perverse incentives (financial, status, cover-yourself); trace what is upstream of the problem, or a partial solution only displaces it (poachers kept from elephants turned to rhinos); and trace downstream second-to-nth-order effects on metrics you do not yet track. Externalities are both physical (forever chemicals, CO2, posture) and psychosocial (a campaign that mobilizes its audience may radicalize and polarize everyone else). To find them: consult many perspectives, including indigenous people, legal scholars, economists, scientists and political opponents; red-team the proposal and ask critics how to improve it; implement first as contained, safe-to-fail probes; keep listening broadly after scaling; and keep a governance structure that can change course when harms appear. 'Endeavor to do it completely while knowing [you] never can.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/50-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_50. Daniel calls it his 'holistic precautionary logic', a way to apply tgs:Concept.PrecautionaryPrinciple rather than only state it. He also lists why harms get externalized: benefits now and harms later, game-theoretic advantage to the risk-taker (tgs:Concept.OpportunityRiskAsymmetry), the epistemic ease of first-order effects, and the tragedy of the commons. The counter-terrorism training example (operators taught to assess novelty before choosing a drill) illustrates the observe-orient step of the OODA loop."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.WideBoundary ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EcologyOfTheoriesOfChange a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ecology of Theories of Change"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's conclusion (TGS_50) after years of asking thinkers what lies upstream of all the world's problems: each named a different root -- the economy, education, media, government and law, political economy, parenting, the environment -- 'and what I came to learn is they were all right.' 'The answer to all of the problems is all of the solutions. There is not a theory of change, there's an ecology of theories of change.' Asking for 'the solution' is like asking what species a forest is. The practical consequence: hold frameworks without becoming 'zealotist and reductionist' about one's own lever, beware sentences that begin 'at the end of the day' or 'the key is,' and recognize that not everyone needs to understand everything at first -- a few people building good systems (curricula, media platforms, laws) create on-ramps for many."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/50-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_50. Complements Nate's own tgs:Concept.TheoryOfChange (a 'scout team' shifting initial conditions), which Nate echoes in the episode as a 'scout team of Consilience Jedi.' Related: tgs:Concept.ThreeByThreeResponseGrid; and Nate's tgs:Concept.UnicornStrategies (Frankly_151), which defends focused commitment within such an ecology."@en ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.TheoryOfChange ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.HighTouchGrounding a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "High-Touch Grounding"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's requirement (TGS_50) that people who think about catastrophic risk at scale stay 'high-touch' -- directly, bodily connected to particular suffering beings (a dog in a shelter, a homeless person in the cold, an orphaned refugee child) rather than only to abstractions. 'If someone can walk by the people on the street and not notice that much... I don't trust those people to be thinking about catastrophic risk'; abstraction without embodied care 'can make you disconnected enough to do evil in the name of moral things,' as in long-termist expected-value arguments or the phrase 'collateral damage.' The tension is real -- feeling everything can overwhelm, and helping everyone nearby limits work at scale -- and he wants people to keep wrestling with it rather than resolve it by hardening. Utilitarian and virtue ethics must be held together, since no one can forecast complex systems."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/50-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_50. Daniel says his own systems work began in animal shelters and homeless shelters. He names people who are both high-touch and strategic at scale as 'the rate limiting factor right now.' Bend Not Break Part 3 (TGS_31, not yet built) is the series' psychological interlude on holding catastrophic awareness with agency."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EmbeddedGrowthObligation a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.MonetarySystemAndGrowth ;
    skos:prefLabel "Embedded Growth Obligation"@en ;
    skos:definition "A system that must keep growing just to keep functioning -- the global economy, and personal lives built on rising overhead -- with exponentially growing side effects."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's term for a system that must keep growing to keep functioning -- 'macro and micro.' At civilizational scale, the global economy carries an exponential embedded growth obligation, and with it exponentially growing externalities, against planetary boundaries (TGS_5, TGS_20, TGS_42, TGS_50, TGS_71, TGS_132). At personal scale (TGS_50), people create their own: each raise becomes a bigger house, more things and more debt, requiring more income. His advice, especially to the young: keep overhead low, earn what you need in as few hours as possible doing something 'that isn't terrible,' and spend the time bought back developing your capacity to serve -- 'you can't be growing your service to the existing world maximally at the same time.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/50-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_50, with the macro sense also used in TGS_5 (where Daniel first uses the term with Nate, asking him to unpack how a growth obligation in finance becomes one in energy extraction), TGS_20 (the first concept in Daniel's minimal summary of Nate's work, where Nate explains it through bank money creation: interest is not created along with the principal), TGS_42, TGS_71 and TGS_132. In TGS_42 Daniel locates its source in the financial system's 'own physics': compounding interest yields exponential growth, and real goods and services are indexed to it. Nate supports the personal version with Juliet Schor's research (tgs:Work.TheOverspentAmerican): people always want to earn more the following year. The macro sense is the game-theoretic counterpart of Nate's tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative, which he grounds in debt-based money creation. A rare personal, practical instruction from Daniel, alongside his advice to find mentors and invest in therapy and personal development."@en ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    .

tgs:Concept.RecursiveAbstraction a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Recursive Abstraction"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Breaking Co-Selection"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's account (Bend Not Break Part 4, TGS_42) of what makes humans different. Other species change through slow mutation and co-selection: a slightly faster predator selects for slightly faster prey, so power stays roughly symmetrical and the ecosystem stewards itself. Humans instead build adaptive abstractions on top of each other -- stone tools in the domain of atoms, fire in the domain of energy, language in the domain of information -- faster than nature can build resilience to them. Co-selection breaks: 'we can over hunt an entire ecosystem faster than genetic mutation can' respond. Humans were already apex predators in nearly every environment before agriculture. What was selected for was never the lone individual but the group with its whole tech stack, social structure and superstructure. Because humans now have the power to destroy the biosphere they depend on, they also have, uniquely, the necessity to steward it consciously."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/42-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_42. The game-theoretic and evolutionary counterpart of Nate's energy account of the tgs:Concept.Superorganism: in the same episode Nate, with John Gowdy, dates the superorganism to agriculture about 10,000 years ago, while Daniel stresses that the asymmetry began earlier, with tools and fire. In his closing summary Daniel adds that atoms, energy and information stay tightly coupled, so lasting competitive advantage comes from binding all three with coordination. Related: tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure, tgs:Concept.PositiveSumInGroupNegativeSumToNature, tgs:Concept.IAmBecauseWeAre. In TGS_126 Daniel frames the same point as a symmetry of power: evenly distributed mutation plus co-selection keeps power distributed, so lions and gazelles are symbiotic at the macro level, and competing originally meant 'to strive together'; tools broke that symmetry. He also offers, as unsubstantiated, the hypothesis that wisdom traditions arose after early humans saw the extinctions their tools caused (tgs:Concept.RestraintByDesign)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    .

tgs:Concept.PositiveSumInGroupNegativeSumToNature a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Positive-Sum In-Group, Negative-Sum to Nature"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's amendment (TGS_42) to the story, told in Robert Wright's Nonzero, that history is a long arc toward ever more positive-sum cooperation as bands become tribes, cities and nations. Daniel agrees that cooperation grew within groups, but says it was zero-sum or negative-sum between groups, and negative-sum toward nature throughout. Groups traded and warred, which drove each to up-regulate against the others, 'but all of them were up-regulating relative to nature on vastly faster time scales.' 'In so far as at the expense of each other drives us both up, it's all at the expense of nature.' The tribe that refused the plow to keep its animist relationship with animals may have been happier, but it was outcompeted."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/42-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_42. A correction to the optimistic progress narrative (the show notes also link a critique of the Pinker and Rosling 'new optimists'); see tgs:Concept.NaiveProgress (TGS_71). Source work: tgs:Work.Nonzero. Related: tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap, tgs:Concept.RecursiveAbstraction, tgs:Concept.LocalizedSyntropySystemicEntropy."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.NonZeroSumness ;
    .

tgs:Concept.LocalizedSyntropySystemicEntropy a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Localized Syntropy, Systemic Entropy"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's thermodynamic description (TGS_42) of human advantage and of profit. Fire, tools and language let a group create local order -- 'localized syntropy' -- by converting the surrounding environment into disorder -- 'systemic entropy.' 'Profit is localized syntropy and systemic entropy, which goes along with privatized gain and socialized losses.' Nate's shorthand: 'language accelerated entropy on planet Earth.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/42-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_42 ('centropy' in the transcript). A bridge between Nate's biophysical framing (energy dissipation by the tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple) and Daniel's economic one (externalized cost). Related: tgs:Concept.GameTheoreticVersusRealValue, tgs:Concept.HolisticExternalityAnalysis."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.GlobalGovernanceWithoutGlobalGovernment a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.Geopolitics, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Global Governance Without Global Government"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's framing (TGS_42) of the coordination the metacrisis requires. Within a nation, the rule of law solves multipolar traps: a deterrent stops an incentive, which is the only reason a national park can exist despite the incentive to log it. Between nations there is no monopoly of violence, so stopping another country means 'war, not just law.' We have global markets -- nothing can be made without global supply chains -- but no global governance, and an incentive-only system 'will continue to extract and externalize.' A one-world government is not the answer, because something with that much power would become corrupt, and its class and oppression dynamics could be worse than any government so far. What is needed is 'effective global governance, which does not have to be a government': a decentralized process with checks and balances that can bind global multipolar traps, and is 'both enforceable and non-dystopic.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/42-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_42. Daniel contrasts cigarettes and CFCs, which could be regulated product by product, with energy, 'the engine of creation for the economy as a whole,' where the vested interest is everything. He suggests that an agreement between the US and China might be enough to start. This is the governance half of tgs:Concept.ThirdAttractor (avoiding both catastrophe and dystopia). Related: tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap, tgs:Concept.BindingTheJevonsParadox, tgs:Concept.SolarGeoengineeringGovernance."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    .

tgs:Concept.GameTheoreticVersusRealValue a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Game-Theoretic Value Versus Real Value"@en ;
    skos:definition "What wins in competition and what we actually value are different: money flows to what can be extracted and traded, while what keeps us alive (air, ecosystems) stays unpriced and degraded."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's distinction (TGS_42): 'game theoretical value and real value have a major delta.' We value breathing but do not pay for the atmosphere, so cutting down trees makes sense to each individual -- the lumber is immediate, the damage to the air is imperceptible -- even though everyone doing it threatens the atmosphere as a whole. Anything abundant stays out of the accounting system and is debased. Money therefore flows not to what we value but to the subset that can be extracted and exchanged for competitive advantage; whoever spends to protect the unpriced loses ground to whoever spends only to make more money. The consequence for the transition: what is needed lowers immediate game-theoretic power relative to others, but need not lower quality of life, and could raise it for everyone at once 'so long as we get off of that coordination failure calculus.' 'The definition of best self-interest game theoretically and the definition of best self-interest in any other meaningful way are not the same.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/42-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_42. A key bridge between Daniel's game-theoretic lens and Nate's pragmatic, personal one: Nate's argument that well-being can rise as material throughput falls rests on the same gap between what is counted and what matters. Related: tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap, tgs:Concept.LocalizedSyntropySystemicEntropy, tgs:Concept.IAmBecauseWeAre; and Nate's tgs:Concept.ReAimingTheStatusDrive."@en ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.PriceCostAndValue ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.TransmutingWealthIntoIncome ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.HavesAndHaveNots ;
    .

tgs:Concept.IAmBecauseWeAre a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "I Am Because We Are"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Ubuntu"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Ubuntu phrase Daniel Schmachtenberger uses twice in TGS_42. First, on personal complicity: no one can be in perfect ethical alignment apart from helping the species as a whole change, so people should think about their footprint without trying to be perfect, since perfectionism leads to hopelessness or withdrawal. Second, as 'the new superstructure that we have to get': because human adaptive capacity has surpassed evolution's usual process, 'our motive also has to transcend normal evolutionary motive.' The 'we' must extend beyond the in-group to all people -- otherwise in-groups race each other to destruction -- and to 'all the other inhabitants of the biosphere that make it bio-inhabitable for us.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/42-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_42. The superstructure layer of tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure, and the motive answering tgs:Concept.RecursiveAbstraction. It anticipates the episode's close, where Daniel describes a dream of a movement treating water, and then all life, as sacred, and suggests growth can move to another dimension: quality of life personally, interpersonally and transpersonally. Related: tgs:Concept.IntelligenceBoundByWisdom (TGS_71). In TGS_126 Daniel gives it three levels: survival (nature selected groups, not lone humans), thought (we think in languages others made), and all relations -- the sun, the soil microbes, the gravitational field -- which is why pre-civilizational cultures were animist; he names it the cultural starting point of authentic progress (tgs:Concept.AuthenticProgress)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.OneMarshmallowRewardCircuits a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "One-Marshmallow Reward Circuits"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Pseudosatisfiers"@en ;
    skos:definition "Markets profit by supplying reward without the work or deeper nourishment it used to come with -- fast food, porn, social media -- leaving 'pseudosatisfiers' that never satisfy."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's framing (Bend Not Break Part 3, TGS_31), after the marshmallow experiment, of how a market economy exploits evolved reward. In evolutionary settings, rewards came only with evolutionarily relevant work and with the deeper nourishment they were bound to. Supply-side incentives strip the reward from both: fast food delivers salt, fat and sugar without fiber or micronutrients (people dying of obesity are also micronutrient-starved, so the hunger deepens); porn delivers sexual reward without intimacy; social media delivers connection without the conflict-and-repair of real friendship. These are 'pseudosatisfiers.' Two-marshmallow circuits involve self-application and delayed, healthier gratification. The same split applies to understanding: quick certainty is a one-marshmallow reward (tgs:Concept.PrematureCertainty), and psychedelics can be 'one-marshmallows to spirituality.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/31-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_31. Daniel calls these 'hyper-normal stimuli,' close to Nate's tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli (the show notes link the Reality Blind page on it); Daniel's emphasis is on the economic incentive that produces them and on the missing deeper nutrient. Nate closes the episode calling it 'an everlasting marshmallow.'"@en ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.RichInSocialCapital ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.DopamineHijackedCulture ;
    .

tgs:Concept.PrematureCertainty a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.BehavioralEconomicsAndCognition ;
    skos:prefLabel "Premature Certainty"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's warning (TGS_31) that certainty reached without the work of understanding is a one-marshmallow reward (tgs:Concept.OneMarshmallowRewardCircuits). In-group outrage at the other side hijacks the needs for belonging, security and esteem -- the last as 'sanctimony' -- in ways useful to parties and sellers. It afflicts doomers as much as optimists: catastrophic and hopeful predictions (Y2K, the Cuban missile crisis, 2012) have both been historically poor. False catastrophic certainty is dangerous because, combined with utilitarian trolley-problem logic, it 'justifies me doing super fucked up stuff so long as it's less' -- Daniel's own teenage conviction that extinction was inevitable led him to think about depopulation. The mature alternative is earnest desire to understand, willingness to sit in uncertainty outside a certain in-group, and to stay 'obligated to work on it as best I can in the presence of still not knowing.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/31-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_31. Nate agrees: he has 'veered towards certainty at times,' is 'reasonably confident what's not going to happen,' but does not know what will. Related: tgs:Concept.DiscretePhaseShift (the phenomenon Daniel's forecasts missed), tgs:Concept.PostTragic, tgs:Concept.SensemakingPrerequisiteOfOpenSociety, tgs:Concept.PeakSensemaking."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.CulturalAntibodies ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SensemakingPrerequisiteOfOpenSociety a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sensemaking as a Prerequisite of Open Society"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's argument (TGS_31): 'If you want an open society where everyone gets to participate in choice-making, then everybody has to do a good job of sense-making' and meaning-making. The minimum required investment is 'authentic, deep informed-ness about the nature of the issues,' and the education to make that possible. The ability to sit with uncertainty is statistically rare in the current environment but does not require genetics that people lack. If it were impossible, democracy would no longer be relevant, and the alternative is a small group made 'the new nobility' -- 'the Chinese answer.' Current institutions do not ask for it: people are framed as consumers and voters with constrained yes-or-no choices that need no understanding, and those competing for their purchase or vote supply quick certainty and outrage instead."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/31-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_31. Nate adds that the metanarrative must be assembled in individual minds -- a room of specialists does not produce shared understanding -- and then the number of such minds grown. Daniel's broader sensemaking work is carried by tgs:Organization.ConsilienceProject. Related: tgs:Concept.PrematureCertainty, tgs:Concept.GlobalGovernanceWithoutGlobalGovernment (the dystopian alternative it guards against)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.SharedBroadcastPerturbation ;
    .

tgs:Concept.FragmentedConsciousness a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fragmented Consciousness"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Perceiving From Wholeness"@en ;
    skos:definition "Seeing the world as separate parts rather than a whole, which lets us optimize one part at the expense of others; Bohm and Daniel see it as the root beneath the world's problems."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "David Bohm's diagnosis, adopted by Daniel Schmachtenberger (TGS_31): the fundamental cause of all the world's problems, from environmental destruction and war to family conflict, is a fragmented consciousness that does not perceive from wholeness first. Daniel joins it to Einstein's 'optical delusion of consciousness': distinct things are taken to be separable, though nothing exists without the sun, the biosphere and everything else. From that delusion we optimize one part at the expense of another -- unknowingly ('mistake theory,' externalities) or deliberately ('conflict theory,' war and oppression) -- whether the trade is against our future self (addiction), another part of ourselves, another person, our own interests (martyrdom), another in-group, or the biosphere. Seen whole, all are 'short-term pump and dumps.' Daniel calls this 'the generator function of the generator function.' Reductionism enacts it institutionally, converting 'the complex to the complicated' -- hence iatrogenic medicine and environmental fixes that move problems elsewhere."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/31-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_31; Daniel returns to Bohm and Krishnamurti in TGS_50, where fragmented consciousness is what 'could not steward exponential power.' Bohm's book: tgs:Work.WholenessAndTheImplicateOrder. Daniel pairs it with Bhagavad Gita 2.48 ('established in yoga, perform action') and 'seek ye first the kingdom' as older encodings of acting from wholeness. Nate's response: perhaps growing the number of people who perceive themselves as part of the whole matters more than refining the metanarrative. Related: tgs:Concept.IntelligenceBoundByWisdom (TGS_71), tgs:Concept.HolisticExternalityAnalysis, tgs:Concept.IAmBecauseWeAre. In TGS_126 Daniel cites Vanessa Andreotti's chief (tgs:Work.HospicingModernity): the core of colonialism is not taking land or abusing people, which are epiphenomena, but believing there are separable things; he adds that English is mostly nouns, while Whitehead's process philosophy treats a tree as a verb, a live process."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.ShadowOfTheSuperorganism ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.VerticalBehavioralStack ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.WholenessAndTheImplicateOrder ;
    .

tgs:Concept.DiscretePhaseShift a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Discrete Phase Shift"@en ;
    skos:definition "The insight that took Daniel Schmachtenberger, at 15, off the path of certainty about extinction (TGS_31). Buckminster Fuller's image: a bird embryo consumes a finite egg white and fouls its shell in a way that, forecast forward, is self-terminating -- yet the egg white suffices for it to grow a beak, break out, and join an outside world where it eats seeds its droppings fertilize. A fetus, a caterpillar and a chrysalis are the same: developmental phases that are unsustainable because they are developmental, ended by a discrete phase shift unlike what came before or after. Forecasting humanity's curves forward missed this structure, common in systems pushed far from equilibrium. The task becomes not only changing the shape of the curves but asking which phase shifts could occur, what the world beyond them might look like, and how to facilitate them. The shift is not guaranteed: 'sometimes the baby dies in birth.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/31-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_31. The show notes link an ecology paper on phase shifts and Ray C. Anderson Foundation material on Fuller's eggshell analogy. A counterweight to tgs:Concept.PrematureCertainty, and a hopeful complement to Nate's bend-not-break framing of the tgs:Concept.Superorganism's growth phase."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.ImaginalCells ;
    .

tgs:Concept.PostTragic a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Post-Tragic"@en ;
    skos:definition "After naive optimism and then the shattering encounter with tragedy comes a stance committed to serving life whether or not we succeed, without the false certainty of either."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "A three-stage model Daniel Schmachtenberger credits to his friend Marc Gafni (TGS_31). Pre-tragic optimism holds ideals not yet shattered by the world -- naivete. Encountering the tragedy of the world shatters them, and cynicism can emerge. Post-tragic is 'committed to being in service to the sacredness of life whether you can succeed or not' -- 'still the right hill to die on' -- and sees that the tragic place has a false certainty just as the naive one did, and that 'the universe is much bigger than both.' It looks for solutions with full cognizance of the tragedy, and also holds the fragility of life on a thin crust beside a sun that can flare. Daniel adds two practical notes: fear and anger at harm are 'epiphenomena' of love for something sacred, so one can act 'more for than against'; and people in pain first need to be met in their grief, since premature optimism can be a 'spiritual bypass,' before asking what agency remains."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/31-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_31 ('Mark Gaffney' in the transcript). Daniel offers it to explain why Nate gets more pushback for being too optimistic than for being too pessimistic: critics may be 'longing to feel met in the tragic place they're in.' He also notes that many catastrophic-risk thinkers have a catastrophizing disposition (the CBT sense), useful for protection but hard on collaboration, and that his own disposition is optimistic. Nate says finding another person who admits 'it's all awful and I don't know what to do, but let's roll up our sleeves' is 'buoying.' Related: tgs:Concept.PrematureCertainty, tgs:Concept.HeartWillMind. Nate takes up the frame in Frankly_55 (March 2024): listeners who keep coming back 'are able to dance the dance of moving between the tragic and the post tragic,' and he spent too much of 2023 in the tragic (the show notes link Marc Gafni's essay)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.HeartWillMind a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Heart, Will and Mind"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's claim (TGS_31) that effective response needs all three together, and any two fail. Mind and heart without will: 'really smart, caring academics who feel broken and hopeless.' Heart and will without mind: activists who will risk their lives but cannot think through strategy at the scale of what must change. Will and mind without heart: narrow value systems and 'the kind of sociopathic rule that currently runs the world,' highly strategic and agentic in service of a narrow interest. Hence the frameworks in the series are offered as meta-strategy -- ways of perceiving any situation more deeply -- rather than a specific strategy, approached 'for the thriving of life' and not against bad guys."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/31-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_31, but Daniel first lays it out in Part 2 (TGS_20) as 'head-heart-will' -- will or agency, heart or values, mind or strategic insight -- from watching caring activists lose as a child; there the third failure (narrow values with will and mind) is the hyper-agent (tgs:Concept.HyperAgents), and all three with wide values are 'a minimum necessary requirement for people that can help turn the ship around.' In TGS_31 Daniel adds, quoting Bertrand Russell with uncertain attribution, that one studies philosophy less for its immediate use than for what it does to you, so the frameworks help even with 'the local PTA issue.' Related: tgs:Concept.PostTragic, tgs:Concept.EcologyOfTheoriesOfChange (TGS_50), tgs:Concept.HighTouchGrounding (TGS_50)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.HealingBeforeEngagement ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.IntegratingThinkingAndFeeling ;
    .

tgs:Concept.HyperAgents a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    skos:prefLabel "Hyper-Agents"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Hyperagency"@en ;
    skos:definition "The few people at the top of a power-law distribution who use agency to grow agency and shape the incentive landscape everyone else lives in."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's theory (Bend Not Break Part 2, TGS_20; then a Consilience Project paper in progress) of the people at the top of a power-law distribution who have outsized influence on the world -- the Genghis Khans and Alexanders. They lie outside the bell curve: better at game theory and war, more competitive, generally more sociopathic. Their defining trait is maximizing returns on agency -- using agency to grow agency -- and the most capable (tier one) can do it at scale across finance, politics, technology, culture and human motivation. They create the topologies -- incentive and deterrent landscapes and cultures -- in which everyone else forms values. Agency is not the same as personal wealth (Kissinger, or Xi Jinping stewarding China's GDP). Many people are hyper-agents in motivation but not in skill, from middle managers to street thugs. Hyper-agents are themselves caught in a multipolar trap: whoever spends on what they value instead of maximizing agency loses to whoever does not, so even 'the most ethical hyper-agent' feels obligated to seek maximum power. They also convert the intrinsically motivated work of others into game-theoretic power: Einstein's physics into the bomb, Jesus's teaching into Constantine's Christendom."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/20-daniel-schmactenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_20, where the term enters TGS (it is in the episode title). Asked whether hyper-agents drive the superorganism or it drives them, Daniel says causation is recursive: individual motives build global systems with their own dynamics, like the financial system's embedded growth obligation, which then pull every agent along. Nate calls for 'wider boundary hyper-agents,' and Daniel lists two hard paths: widen the values of hyper-agents, or increase the agency of people with wide values (see tgs:Concept.HeartWillMind). Later developed as tgs:Concept.MaximizingReturnsOnPower (TGS_132). Related: tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap, tgs:Concept.MoneyAsMaximumOptionality, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad. In TGS_126 Daniel adds that a hyper-investor (Warren Buffett, who grows capital) is not a hyper-agent (Henry Kissinger, with far less money but far more influence over world systems), and that while psychological traits follow a Gaussian distribution, power follows a power law -- so those at its top, about two standard deviations from the center of the bell curve, explain the shape of civilization more than everyone else does (tgs:Concept.PowerSeekingAndComplicity)."@en ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.TheLastCanToKick ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.EducatingHyperAgents ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.TheFifteenHundred ;
    .

tgs:Concept.MoneyAsMaximumOptionality a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.MonetarySystemAndGrowth ;
    skos:prefLabel "Money as Maximum Optionality"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's account (TGS_20) of why money is the most sought-after thing: it is a unit of agency and of choice-making -- 'a unit of choice making for whatever the fuck you want.' Exchange media evolved from grain and shells to gold, paper and a global reserve currency, a system engineered for both high stability and maximum optionality, which are usually hard to combine (real estate is stable but slow to sell). In a rapidly changing world, the fastest and most accurate OODA loop (observe, orient, decide, act) wins, so liquid currency beats long-term planning. 'The thing everybody wants the most is the thing that has absolutely no intrinsic value, but the maximum optionality for every source of value' -- and so 'we will destroy sources of real value to get the optionality value.' The market incentive is for everyone to convert their life force, labor and creativity, into currency, with eight billion people searching for new ways to do so and then racing to exploit each discovery."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/20-daniel-schmactenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_20. Daniel adds that money was partly designed as extrinsic motivation for necessary work no one was intrinsically motivated to do -- kinder than state compulsion -- but some learn to accumulate it without the labor. Nate replies that he optimizes for 'a broader portfolio' (discovery, friendship, meaning) while still wanting a rainy-day reserve. Related: tgs:Concept.HyperAgents, tgs:Concept.GameTheoreticVersusRealValue (TGS_42), tgs:Concept.CivilizationalOptionality (a different, civilizational sense of optionality, TGS_238)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.ThreeTiersOfWealth ;
    .

tgs:Concept.GeneralizedJevonsParadox a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:prefLabel "Generalized Jevons Paradox"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's extension (TGS_20) of tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox beyond energy: 'when anything that is relevant for the market as a whole gets cheaper, the market as a whole grows.' Cheaper energy, compute or steel makes previously unprofitable activity profitable, so the 'surface area of profitability expands' and total use rises -- capital seeks the highest returns but will take any positive return. The same rebound ran through agriculture: more calories per acre meant more surplus and faster population growth, not more leisure. Efficiency therefore reduces total use only under a binding system: 'you're not going to get that through a pure incentive system.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/20-daniel-schmactenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_20. The binding Daniel calls for is modeled as tgs:Concept.BindingTheJevonsParadox (TGS_132). Nate adds that the reverse is starting: as energy gets more expensive, everything does, including solar panels and wind turbines. Related: tgs:Concept.AtomsEnergyBits, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple."@en ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.JevonsDividend ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.AtomsEnergyBits a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Atoms, Energy and Bits"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Economic Trinity"@en ;
    skos:definition "A framing of the materials economy by its three domains with fundamentally different physics, which Daniel Schmachtenberger (TGS_20) credits to Forrest Landry, extending Peter Thiel's 'atoms and bits.' Atoms are finite -- the planet's copper is what it is -- and are not destroyed, only turned into pollution unless the economy goes closed-loop. Energy keeps arriving but within a fixed bandwidth, the solar input. Bits have no fixed stock or input but are limited by the atoms and energy of their computational substrate and by finite human attention. The three are coupled: atoms are useful only when energy moves them, energy only when it moves atoms or bits, and bits run on and code for the physical world. So bits cannot scale indefinitely apart from atoms, energy and attention, and the efficiency gains they bring still face the Jevons paradox. Nate calls the same binding of information, energy and materials 'the economic trinity.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/20-daniel-schmactenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_20 (altLabel from Nate's Earth Day talk, as he cites it here). Daniel uses it again in TGS_42: sustained competitive advantage comes from binding atoms, energy and bits with the coordination of people, and software growth is an S-curve limited by human attention (see tgs:Concept.RecursiveAbstraction). Nate's own framing of the same point: technology without energy is a sculpture (after Steve Keen). Related: tgs:Concept.GeneralizedJevonsParadox, tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.AxiologicalDesign a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Axiological Design"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's name (TGS_20) for designing technology with its effects on values in view: 'How do you embed the axiology, the ethics into the tech design?' Because advantageous technology is obligate and codes behavior, values and culture (tgs:Concept.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral), it is not enough to rely on religion or ethics to make people use technology well. Design must account not only for physical externalities (mining byproducts, toxic tailings) but for what a technology externalizes 'into the human psychological cultural world,' weighing its complex positive and negative effects as well as its intended function."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/20-daniel-schmactenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_20, where it is the design response to the plow story. It is the psychological counterpart of tgs:Concept.HolisticExternalityAnalysis (TGS_50, which names psychosocial externalities) and is developed in the Consilience paper tgs:Work.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral. Related: tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure (infrastructure driving superstructure)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.TheLastCanToKick a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.OvershootAndLimits ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Last Can to Kick"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's formulation in TGS_20, which he says he may never have said before: fire, agriculture, fossil hydrocarbons, and information technology 'were the cans that we kept kicking to expand our reach,' each a spike in humanity's ability to extract energy from the environment. 'I don't think, physically, we have any more cans to kick. And the next can is in our minds': learning to get 'our evolutionary neurotransmitter cocktails of meaning and love and excitement and motivation and peace by using less resources.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/20-daniel-schmactenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_20. It answers Daniel's list of energy spikes (fire, agriculture, oil, digital technology) in the same exchange. A compact statement of Nate's pragmatic, personal side of the dialogue, set against Daniel's game theory: the last lever is cultural and psychological. Related: tgs:Concept.ReAimingTheStatusDrive, tgs:Concept.OneMarshmallowRewardCircuits (TGS_31), tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ImpossibleToHard a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "From Impossible to Hard"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's answer (TGS_20) to Nate's worry that understanding the superorganism makes people feel they have less agency. Facing a long list of separate, seemingly intractable crises breeds nihilism; seeing that they are all expressions of a few underlying system dynamics shows where to focus. 'We move from impossible to hard when we start to understand it better. And hard is better than impossible.' Solutions that ignore part of the design criteria only looked easy -- they were bound to fail -- so getting the constraints right makes a problem harder but possible. The psychology this requires: delayed gratification, faith that an answer not yet known can be found, staying with a problem, and treating failed attempts as learning. Questions such as 'how do you solve multipolar traps?' are orienting questions with many partial answers (transparency, enforcement, incentives, better regulatory process), each of which will in turn be gamed."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/20-daniel-schmactenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_20. Daniel describes a project framing the hard questions beneath most problems as a better orienting framework than the Sustainable Development Goals, and says Nate's podcast rests on the same faith: that collective intelligence, given the right design criteria, can find solutions. Nate admits he spends most of his time debunking easy solutions rather than in the solution space. Related: tgs:Concept.PostTragic (TGS_31), tgs:Concept.EcologyOfTheoriesOfChange (TGS_50), tgs:Concept.PersonalAgency."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.NamedSolutionsDrawCounterResponse ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.AggregateProbability ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.WhatCantWontMightHappen ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ExosomaticEnergy a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EROIAndEnergyQuality, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Exosomatic Energy"@en ;
    skos:definition "Energy a species uses outside its body, as opposed to the endosomatic energy it eats. Nate Hagens (Bend Not Break Part 1, TGS_5): the average American consumes about 2,500 kilocalories a day in food but over 200,000 outside the body -- in lights, airplanes, shopping centers, libraries and buses -- a ratio of roughly 100 to 1. Humans are the only species that uses energy massively outside the body, and only because they access stored hydrocarbons, so modern life is an anomaly, not a picture of how humans are. Nate's coping corollary: Americans use about 100 times more energy than they need, Europeans about 50, and asked for the five best experiences of their lives, few people name anything that took much exosomatic energy."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/05-daniel-schmactenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_5; the show notes link an EJOLT explainer on endosomatic and exosomatic energy use. Nate repeats the 2,000 versus 200,000 calories figure in TGS_20. Daniel extends the idea back to fire, the first use of energy outside the body. Related: tgs:Concept.EnergySlaves (the same energy counted as labor), tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, tgs:Concept.TheLastCanToKick (TGS_20)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CostOfExtractionPricing a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.EROIAndEnergyQuality ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cost-of-Extraction Pricing"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Spending Principal as Interest"@en ;
    skos:definition "We price fossil energy at the cost of pulling it out of the ground, not the cost of replacing it or of its damage, so we spend a one-time inheritance as if it were income -- and 'the prices of everything are wrong.'"@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Nate Hagens's critique (TGS_5), which Daniel Schmachtenberger calls 'core to what's wrong with economic theory': fossil hydrocarbons are a one-time endowment drawn down about 10 million times faster than they were sequestered, yet the economy 'is treating this as if it were interest when it actually is our principal.' A barrel of oil does about five years of human labor, but its price is only the cost of getting it out of the ground plus a margin -- not the cost of making more hydrocarbons, nor of the pollution, extraction damage or wars over its location. So 'the prices of everything are wrong': if the full, wide-boundary externalities of energy use were included, no industry on Earth would be profitable. Daniel's restatement: the market as a whole is being subsidized by nature from a savings account that is about to run out, and when it does, costs rise enough to force a restructuring -- the break scenario."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/05-daniel-schmactenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_5. Nate's examples: coal's full cost including climate is about 18-20 cents per kWh against the 4 cents paid; we turn 'billions of barrels of ancient sunlight into microliters of dopamine and calling that an economic success.' Daniel returns to the point in TGS_20 (prices are the cost of extraction plus margin; 'stealing from the balance sheet of nature' counts as being industrious). His proposed remedy is tgs:Concept.Untax. Related: tgs:Concept.PriceCostAndValue (Frankly_104), tgs:Concept.EnergySlaves, tgs:Concept.GameTheoreticVersusRealValue (TGS_42)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.TwoCategoriesOfTechnology a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Two Categories of Technology"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's distinction (TGS_5, from Reality Blind): the first category of technology makes energy capture or use more efficient (better photovoltaic cells, more electricity per unit of fuel); the second creates new ways of using energy -- machines doing what humans did manually, or entirely new vectors such as Facebook. The second category dominates the global technology portfolio, so technology mostly builds new channels for more energy use next year. Separately, technology is deflationary (tech-heavy goods get cheaper toward an asymptote) while depletion is inflationary (each tranche of resources is harder to reach), and the two forces battle. Together with the Jevons paradox, this means efficiency serves the superorganism so long as GDP is the cultural goal."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/05-daniel-schmactenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_5; the show notes link the Reality Blind page on two categories of technology. Related: tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox, tgs:Concept.GeneralizedJevonsParadox (Daniel's extension, TGS_20), tgs:Concept.JevonsDividend, tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.RealityBlind ;
    .

tgs:Concept.JevonsDividend a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Jevons Paradox Dividend"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's tentative proposal (TGS_5) for short-circuiting the rebound effect: when an invention produces efficiency gains that are monetized, a rule would devote that 'bolus of new capital' to something outside the growth-driven superorganism -- for example, building a local ecological watershed powered by renewable energy, or another full-cycle project -- instead of letting it flow back into the global, energy-hungry economy and drive more use. Daniel Schmachtenberger's response: 'so fucking consequential if people get it,' because it implies a post-growth economic system."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/05-daniel-schmactenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_5. An early, concrete instance of what Daniel later calls binding the Jevons paradox (tgs:Concept.BindingTheJevonsParadox, TGS_132; 'you need that through a binding system,' TGS_20). Related: tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox, tgs:Concept.TwoCategoriesOfTechnology."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.AdvancePolicy a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Advance Policy"@en ;
    skos:definition "Preparing the plans and policies society will need in the coming decade but cannot yet adopt, so they are researched and ready when a crisis makes them possible -- the 'blueprints and airbags' for bending rather than breaking."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "The project Nate Hagens describes (TGS_5) with former US House Majority Leader Dick Gephardt: preparing 'interventions and plans and blueprints that our society are going to need in the next decade, but that are politically or socially impossible to happen now' -- doing the research and building constituencies for them in advance, so they are ready when conditions change. It answers the observation that a system dependent on debt, central-bank support and deficit spending will not adopt measures such as a tax on non-renewable inputs until a crisis forces it. Nate's bend-versus-break framing calls these the 'blueprints and airbags' whose absence makes a break more likely."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/05-daniel-schmactenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens, tgs:Persona.RichardGephardt ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_5, whose show notes link Gephardt's TGS_1 for it (that episode's definition already describes the collaboration). Its first worked example is tgs:Concept.Untax. Related: tgs:Concept.ThreeByThreeResponseGrid (transition-horizon thinking, TGS_50), tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification. In Frankly_67 (2024) Nate says he has discussed advance policy with about 25 senators, governors and congressmen, and did related work with US agencies in 2014 that is not published, since discussing it could make it self-fulfilling or ineffective (tgs:Concept.NamedSolutionsDrawCounterResponse)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.Untax a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.OvershootAndLimits ;
    skos:prefLabel "Untax"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Taxing Non-Renewable Inputs Instead of Labor"@en ;
    skos:definition "The tax shift Nate Hagens describes (TGS_5) as a long shot worth aiming for: tax all non-renewable inputs on human timescales -- carbon, natural gas, copper, fossil-water aquifers, sand -- and remove taxes on human labor, which he says carry about 95% of the tax burden today. Someone earning $50,000 would keep all of it, but an iPhone, full of complex metals and minerals, might cost $2,000-3,000 instead of $500. Right prices would steer innovation toward the real futures we face and encourage conservation (fewer junkets and daily Amazon orders 'to give us a little bit more dopamine'). Nate concedes such a tax would be regressive and need accompanying redistribution, and that it will not be adopted while the system depends on growth -- hence its place in advance policy."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/05-daniel-schmactenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_5. Nate names an 'untax project' and research with Imperial College London on how it would work; the show notes link untax.org, not built here. Daniel's caution in the same episode: a national tax that others do not adopt advantages them geopolitically, 'and it's just a race to the bottom' -- a tragedy of the commons (tgs:Concept.TragedyOfTheCommons) with a power-competition dimension, one case of tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap. Related: tgs:Concept.CostOfExtractionPricing (the problem it addresses), tgs:Concept.AdvancePolicy."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.TragedyOfTheCommons a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tragedy of the Commons"@en ;
    skos:definition "A shared resource without restraint gets overused, because each user gains the full benefit of taking more while the cost is spread across everyone."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Garrett Hardin's 1968 argument (tgs:Work.TragedyOfTheCommons1968) that a shared, unregulated resource tends toward overexploitation: each user captures the full gain of using a little more, while the cost is spread across everyone, so rational individual choices together exhaust the commons unless some form of restraint or governance binds them. In Daniel Schmachtenberger's framework (TGS_20) it is one of three kinds of multipolar trap, alongside the arms race and the market race to first-mover advantage: 'if I don't cut the tree down... somebody else cuts the tree down,' and uses the timber's advantage against me. His central example is a carbon tax: if one country taxes carbon and its rivals do not, it does not prevent climate change, it only cedes power -- 'unless everybody can, nobody can.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/20-daniel-schmactenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.GarrettHardin ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18, after MJSullivan noted that the objection to tgs:Concept.Untax in TGS_5 (whoever taxes non-renewable inputs alone falls behind) is a tragedy of the commons. The global atmosphere and non-renewable stocks are the commons; Daniel's version adds a power dimension Hardin's does not stress -- the restrained party loses ground to rivals economically and militarily, which is the arms-race side of the same trap. Named in TGS_20 and TGS_50 (whose show notes link Hardin's paper); Elinor Ostrom's work on commons governance is the classic counterpoint (tgs:Concept.OstromDesignPrinciples, tgs:Work.GoverningTheCommons): communities with boundaries, their own rules, monitoring and graduated sanctions often avoid the tragedy, which applies most fully to ungoverned open access. Related: tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap (the general case), tgs:Concept.GlobalGovernanceWithoutGlobalGovernment (the binding Daniel calls for), tgs:Concept.EpistemicCommons. In TGS_126 Daniel cites a Consilience paper on perverse asymmetries describing the same gradient: the benefit of cutting one tree is large and obvious to me, my share of the harm tiny, yet everyone doing it ensures the harm. In Frankly_86 Nate names it as one of three kinds of collective action problem (tgs:Concept.CollectiveActionProblem) and quotes his and DJ White's Reality Blind on fisheries: fishers see themselves as good stewards and blame foreign fleets, 'but every fleet is someone's foreign fleet' -- the same dynamic, he says, as ever more financial claims on a finite biophysical reality."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.TragedyOfTheCommons1968 ;
    .

tgs:Concept.AuthenticProgress a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Authentic Progress"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Real Progress"@en ;
    skos:definition "The alternative Daniel Schmachtenberger sets against immature or naive progress (TGS_126; the Consilience paper tgs:Work.DevelopmentInProgress): 'progress worth believing in... must still be able to be considered good once it has taken account of all perspectives and externalities.' Advancement (more capability) is not betterment (more good), and science alone cannot say what is better. The first questions are progress for whom, of what, and measured how; something that benefits some while harming those they depend on is not progress, as the cancer cell's success shows. Authentic progress often means reversing earlier harms rather than adding new things. It also moves progress out of the center: in a fulfilling life most attention goes to appreciating and maintaining what already exists -- '99 percent of life should actually be maintenance' -- while much striving for progress compensates for emptiness and trauma ('hungry ghosts')."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/126-daniel-schmachtenberger-7> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger, tgs:Organization.ConsilienceProject ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_126. The counterpart of tgs:Concept.NaiveProgress (TGS_71). Practices Daniel names toward it: ask whether something is a problem at all and remove its causes (tgs:Concept.ProblemSolvingAsRacket), yellow team before scaling (tgs:Concept.YellowTeaming), shift the burden of proof to safety (tgs:Concept.PrecautionaryPrinciple), and begin with a culture of tgs:Concept.IAmBecauseWeAre. The show notes link a Noema essay on 'the disappearing art of maintenance.' Related: tgs:Concept.GameTheoreticVersusRealValue, tgs:Concept.TheLastCanToKick (Nate's version of finding fulfillment with fewer resources)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.DevelopmentInProgress ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ProgressNarrativeAsApologism a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Progress Narrative as Apologism for Winners"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's account (TGS_126) of why the dominant story of progress is self-serving. History is written by the winners of wars, who say their winning was good; the losers, the extinct species and the colonized do not write the books. Colonizers told of civilizing savages; the Hobbesian picture of pre-civilized life as 'nasty, brutish and short' justified Manifest Destiny and villainizes cultures that chose to stay small and peaceful and therefore lost wars. Today the narrative is amplified by those who profit from it, through media, marketing and campaigns: 'the dominant narrative is usually apologism for the dominant power system.' Its techniques are those of a Consilience paper: cherry-pick the facts, decontextualize them (an income of $1.50 a day in 1815 ignores that most life was not mediated by dollars) and frame them."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/126-daniel-schmachtenberger-7> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_126. Daniel stresses he is not romanticizing indigenous life, only rejecting its villainization. Related: tgs:Concept.NaiveProgress, tgs:Work.HowToMisleadWithFacts, tgs:Concept.PowerSeekingAndComplicity (the winners who write the story)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    .

tgs:Concept.RestraintByDesign a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Restraint by Design"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Anti-Jevons Rules"@en, "Wisdom as Binding"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's examples (TGS_126) of pre-modern cultures that built restraint into their structure rather than relying on individual virtue, binding multipolar traps and the maximum power principle. Tribes stayed within Dunbar-scale numbers by choice, so that everyone could know everyone and have a say, and the Iroquois Confederacy related separate tribes rather than merging them into a city. Pacific Northwest peoples, when they got better hooks and longer lines, had rules to fish fewer hours rather than catch more fish -- Nate calls it 'an embedded anti-Jevons paradox rule.' The Sabbath, among other things, binds a multipolar trap: if anyone works on the seventh day, everyone must, so the law forbids it for all. The potlatch gives away surplus instead of growing population or inviting attack. 'Pretty much any definition of wisdom... has restraint as an embedded concept.' Efficiency leads to using less only if law binds it."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/126-daniel-schmachtenberger-7> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_126; the show notes link sources on the Iroquois Confederacy, Pacific Northwest fishing, the Sabbath and the potlatch. Daniel's hypothesis, which he calls unsubstantiated, is that wisdom traditions emerged when early humans saw the extinctions their tools caused. Nate later asks whether the superorganism needs 'a modern day equivalent of a Sabbath'; Daniel answers that restraint on those who will not restrain themselves is a form of law, and that imposed force has its own dangers. The historical counterpart of tgs:Concept.BindingTheJevonsParadox and of Nate's tgs:Concept.JevonsDividend. Related: tgs:Work.TheSabbath, tgs:Concept.IntelligenceBoundByWisdom, tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap, tgs:Concept.DunbarsNumber."@en ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.JevonsDividend ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.YellowTeaming a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Yellow Teaming"@en ;
    skos:definition "A practice Daniel Schmachtenberger's team developed (TGS_126). Red teaming asks how a plan could fail or be beaten; yellow teaming asks, 'if it succeeds, where might it mess other things up?' Before building a law, business or technology, think through the whole network of relationships and causal effects if it reaches full scale: pressure on supply chains, environmental effects, the power it confers and what it makes obsolete, physical and psychosocial externalities. Then either do not build it, or redesign it so that its externalized effects are positive. The model is a tree, which does 'a million positive things for a million entities over a million timescales.' It is not perfect prediction, but a procedure: you will not foresee everything, so keep watching and internalize new effects as they appear. 'We couldn't have possibly known' is usually 'self-motivated gibberish.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/126-daniel-schmachtenberger-7> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger, tgs:Organization.ConsilienceProject ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_126, where Daniel also places it as the last step in problem-solving after asking whether something is a problem and removing its causes (tgs:Concept.ProblemSolvingAsRacket). He names it again in TGS_132 alongside tgs:Concept.PrecautionaryPrinciple. A worked method within tgs:Concept.HolisticExternalityAnalysis (TGS_50); related to tgs:Concept.AxiologicalDesign (TGS_20)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.UnintendedConsequences ;
    .

tgs:Concept.PowerSeekingAndComplicity a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Power-Seeking and Complicity"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's claim (TGS_126) that a system born of power competition selects for two psychologies: people with dark-triad traits who lead, and people willing to be complicit with them. Leaders at the top of power-law distributions sit far from the center of the bell curve -- less empathetic, more driven, better at strategy and propaganda -- and then change the system to keep winning, as animals both are shaped by and create their niche. Followers go along because defecting alone seems futile: about 90% of Nazis at Nuremberg said they felt bad but followed orders, though if all had refused together there would have been no Holocaust -- 'a coordination failure.' The Asch, Milgram and smoke-filled-room experiments show how deep conformity runs. In a tribe, an aggressive outlier could be banished; once he succeeds in frightening people, fealty and 'best for me and my family is just go along with it' take over. Conditioning shapes the whole distribution: Spartan child-rearing produced warriors; among Jains no one harms insects."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/126-daniel-schmachtenberger-7> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_126. Nate cites about 10% of the modern population as dark-triad from his own Frankly (tgs:Monologue.Frankly_53_TheHavesAndTheHaveNots, January 2024), likely an undercount; Daniel declines to make strong statistical claims but says digital life conditions narcissism. The complicity half is the multipolar trap at the level of individuals (tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap). Related: tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.HyperAgents, tgs:Concept.CorporationAsObligateSociopath; Nate's later treatments include tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths and tgs:Monologue.Frankly_157_DecentPeopleIndecentSystem."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.TraitsOutcompeteValues ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.EducatingHyperAgents ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CorporationAsObligateSociopath a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Corporation as Obligate Sociopath"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's description (TGS_126) of the public corporation as a cybernetic entity with the dark triad built in. It runs on its operating and legal agreements and an org chart in which any person, up to the CEO, can be replaced by a market equivalent, so it is controlled by no one in particular; corporate personhood shields directors from responsibility for what it does. It is legally required to maximize shareholder return -- a measure of extraction. Having no sentience, it has no empathy; it plans (Machiavellianism) and seeks unending growth and market dominance (narcissism): 'it is an obligate sociopath.' Its personhood came through a loophole: the 14th Amendment, written for freed slaves, was soon invoked far more often for corporations than for Black Americans. Because it can privatize gains and socialize losses, it has every incentive to claim that externalities could not have been foreseen."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/126-daniel-schmachtenberger-7> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_126; the show notes link the 14th Amendment, a CELDF page on corporate rights and a Los Angeles Review of Books article (28 cases on Black Americans versus 312 on corporations by 1912; Daniel's spoken figures differ). In TGS_132 Daniel extends the cybernetic framing to AI inside corporations and militaries. Nate's own version, years later, is tgs:Concept.CorporationsAsMisalignedAI (Frankly_159). Related: tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.PowerSeekingAndComplicity, tgs:Concept.OpportunityRiskAsymmetry."@en ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.CorporationsAsMisalignedAI ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    .

tgs:Concept.GameTheoryFillsTheOughtGap a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Game Theory Fills the Ought Gap"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's account (TGS_126) of how game theory became modern civilization's default ethics. Descartes split the measurable, objective world ('is') from value and meaning ('ought'), leaving ought to religion; science studies mechanism so as to predict and control, and its applied side, technology, is steered by whoever funds research for return, so 'you don't really get science, you get the R&D arm of the market.' With ought outside science, the closest scientifically congruent answer to 'what is a good choice?' came from von Neumann and Morgenstern's Theory of Games and Economic Behavior, which formalized winning win-lose games under uncertainty alongside the theory of the market: a good choice is the one that does not lose -- assume the other side is more malicious and better armed, and prepare. That definition 'has nothing to do with good.' Planetary-scale technological power steered by it self-terminates like a cancer, so it must be bound by an equally deep system of wisdom about what is good, which is the job of McGilchrist's 'master' rather than the analytical 'emissary.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/126-daniel-schmachtenberger-7> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_126. The clearest statement in the graph of why Daniel treats game theory as both a description of the world and its pathology: the game-theoretic lens (thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic) explains outcomes because institutions adopted it as their definition of a good choice. Daniel notes that the Greek, Vedic, Taoist and indigenous traditions did not divide subject from object this way. Related: tgs:Concept.IntelligenceBoundByWisdom, tgs:Concept.GameTheoreticVersusRealValue, tgs:Concept.FragmentedConsciousness, tgs:Work.TheoryOfGamesAndEconomicBehavior, tgs:Work.TheMasterAndHisEmissary."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.GameTheory ;
    thinkr:complements tgs:Concept.ProsocialityUnderTheSuperorganism ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ProblemSolvingAsRacket a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Problem-Solving as a Racket"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Solving Problems Created by Earlier Solutions"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's application (TGS_126) of Smedley Butler's 'war is a racket' to modern problem-solving. A protection racket manufactures the demand it sells the supply for; much of the market-and-government system solves problems caused by earlier market and technological solutions, while creating new ones. Industrial agriculture depletes soil and sprays pesticides, disease rises, and pharmaceuticals regulated by the same food-and-drug system treat it; grain diets brought cavities, then dentistry; cities with animal husbandry bred plagues, then vaccines. Daniel's alternative is a sequence of questions: first, is this a problem at all, or a feature of life to embrace (food perishing, children wanting attention, grief)? Second, what caused it, and can the cause be removed or reversed rather than the symptom treated? Only then build something, and yellow team it (tgs:Concept.YellowTeaming). Reversal is under-pursued because 'I can't patent that.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/126-daniel-schmachtenberger-7> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for TGS_126; Daniel reads the opening of tgs:Work.WarIsARacket. He also uses the climate case: solar, nuclear and hydro added energy without reducing fossil use, and organic food added a niche market while total pesticide use kept rising -- 'there has to be more of a focus of actually binding the things that have to stop.' Related: tgs:Concept.AuthenticProgress, tgs:Concept.HolisticExternalityAnalysis, tgs:Concept.BindingTheJevonsParadox."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.PowerVersusLife a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Power Versus Life"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's framing of 'a central point of this podcast' (Frankly_82): we are alive at a time when there is 'a battle between life and power.' Power has ridden successive surpluses -- grain, then fossil fuels, then paper money, then digital money, and now artificial intelligence and military power -- each faster and larger than the last, while life, meaning human life but also biological and ecological carrying capacity, is diminished in its wake. The response he proposes is not to seek power for oneself, the path of the 'sovereign individual,' but wide-boundary sovereignty in service of life (tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySovereignty, tgs:Concept.InServiceOfLife)."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/82-power-vs-life> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_82, whose title it is. It echoes the 'life or growth' choice Nate puts to Daniel Schmachtenberger at the end of TGS_42 and the title of tgs:Interview.TGS_57_HumanitysSoulLifeOrGrowth. The practical and personal counterpart of Daniel's game-theoretic tgs:Concept.MaximizingReturnsOnPower (TGS_132) and tgs:Concept.HyperAgents (TGS_20): Daniel explains why power compounds; Nate asks what a person aware of it should do instead. Related: tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple. An earlier statement is in Frankly_76 (November 2024), which names power versus life as the battle of our times that all the other polarities feed into (tgs:Concept.BattlesOfOurTime)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ImaginalCells a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Imaginal Cells"@en ;
    skos:definition "The metaphor Nate Hagens adds as a final category in Frankly_82: in metamorphosis, imaginal cells are neither caterpillar nor butterfly, and once they begin to become butterfly they self-organize into its structures. He asks what the equivalent cells are in one's own life -- the changes that, a year, five or ten years on, make a person different from who they were during 'the upside rollercoaster of the carbon pulse' -- and admits he cannot yet imagine them even for himself, though he thinks they are happening."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/82-power-vs-life> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_82. The personal-scale counterpart of Daniel Schmachtenberger's tgs:Concept.DiscretePhaseShift (TGS_31), which uses the caterpillar, chrysalis and butterfly for a civilizational phase shift; and of tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence, the collective scale at which Nate says such changes add up. A widely used metaphor in change and transition writing, not Nate's coinage; influencedBy records his use of it."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.TraitsOutcompeteValues a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Traits Outcompete Values"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's observation in Frankly_85: a proposed 'light triad' of sustainable stewardship, interconnectedness and respect for all beings is a set of values, while the dark triad (narcissism, sociopathy, Machiavellianism) is a set of traits, and 'traits will outcompete values in almost all circumstances.' He takes it as a microcosm of the times: many people expect facts and values to overcome traits, human behavior and structures -- the gap between how we would like the world to be and how it is. His conclusion is that facts and values are 'necessary and important, but not sufficient' to steer away from dystopian outcomes; what can offset dark-triad traits is light-triad people surrounded and supported by others in groups, not individual goodness alone."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/85-the-light-triad> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_85 (the show notes link a ScienceDirect paper on traits versus values). The individual-scale version of Daniel Schmachtenberger's argument that the system selects for dark-triad leaders and complicit followers (tgs:Concept.PowerSeekingAndComplicity, TGS_126); Nate's group answer rests on tgs:Concept.MultilevelSelection. Related: tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.LightTriad, tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence, tgs:Concept.GameTheoryFillsTheOughtGap (Daniel's account of the is-ought split Nate invokes)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ThreeTiersOfWealth a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Primary, Secondary and Tertiary Wealth"@en ;
    skos:definition "A three-tier distinction Nate Hagens says he is making up for conceptual clarity (Frankly_86). Primary wealth is food (calories), water, shelter and freedom -- a zoo animal has the first three but not the fourth. Secondary wealth is the catalysts, behaviors and stockpiles that smooth and continue access to primary wealth. Tertiary wealth, a human cultural overlay, is claims on social power and perceived social power: 'a stockpile on the stockpile of primary wealth.' In nature, more meets negative feedback -- after the second gazelle the lion wants a nap, and consumption is capped by body size -- so for most species wealth equals income. Tertiary wealth has positive feedback, because claims on power can always grow, and the fungible US dollar is its ultimate form."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/86-what-is-wealth> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_86. Nate grounds it in optimal foraging theory (animals as the first investors, returns denominated in calories) and relative fitness (lifetime wealth measured against one's conspecifics; see tgs:Concept.RelativeFitnessSpiteFramework). Tertiary wealth is the biophysical reading of what Daniel Schmachtenberger calls money as a unit of agency and optionality (tgs:Concept.MoneyAsMaximumOptionality, TGS_20). Related: tgs:Concept.TransmutingWealthIntoIncome, tgs:Concept.LayersOfInvisibleWealth (Frankly_118), tgs:Concept.StrawSiphonSieve."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    .

tgs:Concept.TransmutingWealthIntoIncome a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.OvershootAndLimits ;
    skos:prefLabel "Transmuting Wealth into Income"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's summary of the modern economy (Frankly_86): 'we are transmuting wealth into income at an astronomical scale' -- drawing down the world's wealth and calling it income. For most species, and for humans through 290,000 of 300,000 years, wealth roughly equaled income. Now a culture of profit maximization, a fungible global currency and the one-time bolus of fossil energy convert natural capital, high-quality ores and ecosystems into flows of money. Financial capital is only a marker for real capital -- built (houses, boats, chainsaws), human (skills, knowledge), social (networks, friends) and natural (streams, forests, the biosphere) -- and much of what is truly wealth, from cold oxygenated oceans to great music, has no price and will be missed only when gone. 'We're in a system now that pretends that we can create wealth by printing fake wealth.' A regenerative culture would reverse the direction: transmute income into wealth at community, national and global scales."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/86-what-is-wealth> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_86. The same move as tgs:Concept.CostOfExtractionPricing (TGS_5: treating fossil principal as interest), applied to all forms of capital. Daniel Schmachtenberger's counterpart is the gap between game-theoretic and real value (tgs:Concept.GameTheoreticVersusRealValue, TGS_42), where the unpriced atmosphere loses to whatever confers advantage. The show notes link a Resilience.org essay on regenerative cultures; see also tgs:Work.DesigningRegenerativeCultures. Related: tgs:Concept.ThreeTiersOfWealth, tgs:Concept.StrawSiphonSieve, tgs:Concept.LayersOfInvisibleWealth."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CollectiveActionProblem a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Collective Action Problem"@en ;
    skos:definition "A situation in which individuals would all be better off cooperating, but each has an incentive to act otherwise, so the group fails to achieve what all its members want. Nate Hagens (Frankly_86) names three kinds: the prisoner's dilemma (two parties each do better by defecting, though both would gain from cooperating), the free rider (those who benefit without contributing), and the tragedy of the commons (a shared resource overused because each user gains privately while the cost is shared). He places the pursuit of wealth on a finite planet within this class -- 'a subset of a collective action problem and... central to the human predicament.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collective_action_problem> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_86, whose show notes link the Wikipedia article (as Frankly_160's do, alongside the multipolar trap). A standard term in economics and political science, not Nate's coinage; influencedBy records his framing of it on TGS. It overlaps Daniel Schmachtenberger's tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap, which emphasizes the race dynamics -- arms races and market races -- that collective action failures drive, and whose cases include tgs:Concept.TragedyOfTheCommons."@en ;
    thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap, tgs:Concept.TragedyOfTheCommons ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    .

tgs:Concept.RichInSocialCapital a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Energy-Poor, Rich in Social Capital"@en ;
    skos:definition "Material and energy wealth are not the same as wellbeing: people with far less energy can be rich in relationships, while rich societies can be poor in social capital."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Nate Hagens's observation on returning from India (Frankly_54): at less than a tenth of the energy per capita of the United States, the people he lived among were 'generally happy and stable and outgoing and solid and warm,' with dense everyday ties -- children who mobbed him in a hug, young people who would not let him leave, dogs fed nightly by neighbors. Back in Minnesota, fourteen people rode a van for fifty minutes without a word. He frames it as going from 'a poor country, extremely poor, yet rich in social capital' to 'an extremely rich country that, certainly on a relative basis, is poor in social capital.' The implication for the Great Simplification is that material and energy wealth and social wealth are distinct, and that the second may matter more for resilience."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/54-reflections-from-india> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_54. Nate retells the trip to Daniel Schmachtenberger in TGS_126: six weeks of chanting, singing, eating and recording birds with thirty people every day lowered his addictive impulses, which returned within weeks of coming home; Daniel's reading is that his reward circuits were full of intimate communion (tgs:Concept.OneMarshmallowRewardCircuits). The show notes call the trip a 'limbic reset.' Related: tgs:Concept.ExosomaticEnergy, tgs:Concept.TransmutingWealthIntoIncome (social capital as one of four capitals), tgs:Concept.DiningCar. The week before, Frankly_53 (tgs:Concept.HavesAndHaveNots) introduces the idea: the monetarily poor in Zambia, Ecuador or Marseille can be socially rich, and 'I would rather have less money and live in a community like that.' The seed is in Frankly_25 (March 2023): shifting self-worth from material net worth to 'natural, social, built and human capital' could be 'a great adaptive strategy, not to mention being true,' with the same Ecuador and Zambia examples (tgs:Concept.LossAversion)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SolutionSpaceCube a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Solution-Space Cube"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Rubik's Cube of Responses"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's three-dimensional map of responses to the human predicament (Frankly_67). One axis is Marvin Harris's three layers: superstructure (ideas, values, narratives), social structure (laws, rules, economic transactions) and infrastructure (energy, buildings, waste capacity). The second is scale: individual, local and regional, national and global. The third is time: pre-crisis (now until a financial or geopolitical cascade), the bend-versus-break moment of stabilizing a smaller system, and the long term 20 to 40 years out. Within any cell, a second 'Rubik's cube' varies by who is responding: their resources and social capital, how complexified their culture already is (the global North versus India), and what they care about (other species, social justice, local resilience). The point is that 'there isn't a one size fits all' recommendation."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/67-the-solutions-that-can-be-named-are-not-the-solutions> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens, tgs:Persona.MarvinHarris ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_67. Nate's version of the grid he and Daniel Schmachtenberger built in Bend Not Break: the layers are tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure (Nate says 'Daniel Schmachtenberger and I have started to refer to' the middle layer as social structure), and the time axis matches the triage, transition and long-term horizons of tgs:Concept.ThreeByThreeResponseGrid (TGS_50). Nate replaces Daniel's third axis of agents (hyper-agents, egregores, institutions) with scale, and adds the responder's own circumstances. Related: tgs:Concept.EcologyOfTheoriesOfChange, tgs:Concept.FrameworkForAction (Frankly_132, his later and fuller answer)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.NamedSolutionsDrawCounterResponse a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Solutions That Can Be Named Are Not the Solutions"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Adaptive Counter-Response"@en, "In Service of Life Underground"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's argument (Frankly_67, whose title it is) that effective responses to the metacrisis cannot be broadcast. Humanity's shared, internet-connected mind space behaves like a natural adaptive system with predator-prey dynamics: bivalves grow thick shells only where crushing predators live, and the 9/11 hijacking strategy was countered by passengers within hours. A strategy described publicly invites an adaptive counter-response from those who do not share its values, so 'the real solutions to the metacrisis will not be televised and they will not be popular.' The universe of socially acceptable solutions and the universe of effective ones overlap little. People doing effective work -- protecting wetlands, conserving land -- decline to explain it on the show because explaining it would dissipate it; Buckminster Fuller could not say 'fight the system' as a military contractor. Hence Nate expects 'an in service of life underground movement,' and treats public discussion as for understanding the framework, so that people do not buy 'dumb narratives' like net zero by 2050 with continued growth."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/67-the-solutions-that-can-be-named-are-not-the-solutions> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_67; the title echoes the Tao Te Ching's 'the Tao that can be named is not the eternal Tao.' Nate's own example is advance policy (tgs:Concept.AdvancePolicy), whose work with US agencies stays unpublished; the reasoning overlaps his information hazards (tgs:Concept.OriginalTripleInformationHazard, Frankly_34). He repeats 'in service of life underground' in Frankly_82. Daniel Schmachtenberger's parallel is that 'whenever you figure out a thing, it'll be gamed' (tgs:Concept.ImpossibleToHard, TGS_20), so responses must keep updating. Related: tgs:Concept.InServiceOfLife, tgs:Concept.UnicornStrategies. Between the two, Frankly_79 (December 2024) applies it to climate: 'the climate movement and the broader in service of life movement is going to have to move underground and think two or three steps ahead because we're not going to vote on climate change' (tgs:Concept.MetabolicHierarchyOfTheSuperorganism)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ShadowOfTheSuperorganism a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Shadow of the Superorganism"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Shadow of the Carbon Pulse"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's name (Frankly_73) for the declining curve that runs beneath the rising curve of the carbon pulse. We focus on the material and economic benefits that fossil energy, materials, complex technology and just-in-time convenience have delivered, but alongside them 'we've created systems where profit has been prioritized over our consciousness of people, of all living things, of the environment.' The costs are psychological and social: people divided within themselves, a polarized and fractured society, nervous systems 'completely on edge,' reward without the work (pornography, stock trades), loneliness amid brown-box consumption, and 'a species out of context,' far from the Pleistocene bands of about 150 we evolved in. 'One of the richest societies that's ever lived and we live in a sick society.' His response is to widen Marvin Harris's model with a circle for the nervous-system health of individuals and a sphere for the connections between people in proximity, and to name mental health and resilience as perhaps the most important needs of the coming years."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/73-the-superorganism-and-the-self> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_73, where Nate promises to explore 'the shadow of the superorganism, which is the stuff on the liability side' alongside his energy and economics. He also sees 'a tiny sliver on the positive side': the carbon pulse may have enabled a change in awareness that is starting to bubble up (tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence). Daniel Schmachtenberger's corresponding diagnosis is tgs:Concept.FragmentedConsciousness (TGS_31). Related: tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli, tgs:Concept.RichInSocialCapital (Frankly_54), tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.MetabolicHierarchyOfTheSuperorganism a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    skos:prefLabel "Metabolic Hierarchy of the Superorganism"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Power hierarchy of the superorganism"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's ranking (Frankly_79) of what actually dominates high-level decisions, set by the economic superorganism's metabolism rather than by what is voted on: 'the things that are important are downregulated to the things that are related to power.' From top to bottom: artificial intelligence ('applying AI to data is the technological equivalent of applying fossil fuels to human and draft animals'), the military, the bond and currency markets that glue global finance together, economic growth, energy, politics, citizens, and the environment, 'dead last.' One could argue citizens and the environment matter most, yet they sit at the bottom of the power structure. His inferences: climate will not be chosen or prioritized globally, losing to energy poverty and energy security, so the in-service-of-life movement must go underground and think two or three steps ahead; and the fusion of AI and military capacity is a winner-take-all dynamic rather than a path to a multipolar world."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/79-the-biggest-takeaways-from-the-logic-of-the-superorganism> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_79; the label follows the show notes' 'metabolic hierarchy of the Superorganism.' Nate develops the theme as a battle between life and power in Frankly_82 (tgs:Concept.PowerVersusLife) and later as a behavioral ladder (tgs:Concept.SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem, Frankly_162), which ranks levers of change rather than holders of power. Daniel Schmachtenberger's account of why power dominates is tgs:Concept.MaximizingReturnsOnPower (TGS_132). Related: tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.NamedSolutionsDrawCounterResponse, tgs:Concept.InServiceOfLife."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.AggregateProbability a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Aggregate Probability"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Law of aggregate probability"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's test (Frankly_79) for single-issue efforts -- 100 percent renewables, sustainable food systems, global peace -- which each make the future better when considered alone. Viewed from a systems synthesis, many are dead ends, because every threshold that must be met has to be met together: is it physically possible, politically possible, possible under debt, war and the other horsemen, and will the major powers hold together for ten years? 'We have the capacity to build a 1,000-foot statue made out of cheese,' but what are the odds all the conditions for it are met? He prefers to reason from what is likely given that aggregate, which points to 'a world of less pretty much no matter what.' The same logic applies to how many humans will take on this conversation."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/79-the-biggest-takeaways-from-the-logic-of-the-superorganism> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_79. Typed thinkr:Concept rather than thinkr:HagensConcept: the phrase is Nate's, but the reasoning is standard probability. The show notes link the law of total probability; what Nate describes, many independent conditions all having to hold, is closer to the multiplication rule for a joint probability, under which the odds fall quickly as conditions are added. Builds on Nate's probabilistic stance (tgs:Monologue.Frankly_29_RealityProbabilityAndPerception). Daniel Schmachtenberger reaches a similar verdict on easy solutions from design criteria rather than odds (tgs:Concept.ImpossibleToHard, TGS_20), and concludes that no single theory of change suffices (tgs:Concept.EcologyOfTheoriesOfChange, TGS_50). Related: tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification. Frankly_90 (April 2025) makes the multiplication explicit -- 'you have to multiply all these probabilities' -- with a 500-foot cheese mouse and net zero by 2050, notes that the hurdles are not fully independent, and adds Bayesian updating of priors (tgs:Concept.WhatCantWontMightHappen)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SocialLimitsToGrowthBeforePhysical a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.OvershootAndLimits ;
    skos:prefLabel "Social Limits to Growth Before Physical Limits"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's inference (Frankly_79) that 'we will hit social limits to growth well before we hit actual aggregate physical limits to growth': median income will decline, and go negative, before mean income does. His illustration: of ten people, one earns a million dollars and nine earn 50,000, so the mean is 145,000 but the median is 50,000. With AI, debt accumulation and the other rabbits pulled from the hat to keep things going, the average person will continue to immiserate, as Peter Turchin would say, even while the aggregate stays flat or grows. The limits arrive within countries and between them; Nate expects Europe to de-industrialize first, and sums it up: 'The Great Simplification is already here, it's just not evenly distributed.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/79-the-biggest-takeaways-from-the-logic-of-the-superorganism> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens, tgs:Persona.PeterTurchin ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_79; the show notes link a US mean-versus-median household income series and Peter Turchin's 'Population Immiseration in America' (Turchin's book is tgs:Work.EndTimes; he is Nate's guest later, in TGS_164). The name coincides with Fred Hirsch's 1976 book Social Limits to Growth, which argues something different (that positional goods cannot be shared by all); Nate does not cite it. Related: tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification, tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative, tgs:Concept.MetabolicHierarchyOfTheSuperorganism."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EducatingHyperAgents a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Educating Hyper-Agents"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's claim (Frankly_81) that 'education is critical, but education to the masses didn't work, isn't working, and won't work. However, education and inspiration of individuals does.' In his time-travel film the scientist, funded by his currency-trader neighbor, sets up a protocol and schools -- ten people, then a hundred, then a thousand around the world -- to educate hyper-agents, people with outsized influence, in the systems synthesis of the human predicament and in interventions to steer things over time on behalf of the long-term stability of life and other species: 'he goes back in time to breathe life into the creation of hyperagents.' The trader's own arc is the other half: a person of vast financial claims whose values change, who sees that the score is 'whose existence shapes the future in a meaningful way.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/81-time-travel-and-the-superorganism> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_81. Nate's 'hyperagents' means people of outsized influence; Daniel Schmachtenberger's tgs:Concept.HyperAgents (TGS_20) are the power-maximizers at the top of a power law, and his two hard paths -- widen the values of hyper-agents, or increase the agency of people with wide values -- are the trader's arc and the scientist's schools. A sharper version of tgs:Concept.TheoryOfChange (the scout team): fewer people, more leverage each. Related: tgs:Concept.HeartWillMind (TGS_31), tgs:Concept.AdvancePolicy. An earlier form is in Frankly_53 (January 2024): 'we're going to need to change the consciousness or the mindset of the top few thousand people riding high on the superorganism' -- though many of them are dark-triad, 'so that makes it more of a puzzle' (tgs:Concept.PowerSeekingAndComplicity). Between the two, Frankly_55 (March 2024) names the group 'the 1500' (tgs:Concept.TheFifteenHundred)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SuperorganismFreeZones a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Superorganism-Free Zones"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's idea (Frankly_81), offered as a theme for his time-travel film: places, perhaps led by Indigenous leaders, that are economically self-sufficient -- needing no philanthropists or billionaires to support them -- and that people choose because they offer a better way of life, acting as pilots and a beacon to other humans. 'It's not muzzling the metabolism of the superorganism'; it follows Buckminster Fuller's logic of creating something different that makes the original model obsolete. 'A tall order.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/81-time-travel-and-the-superorganism> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_81; the show notes' description also names it. The Fuller logic is the one usually quoted as 'to change something, build a new model that makes the existing model obsolete.' Related: tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence, tgs:Concept.ImaginalCells (Frankly_82), and Daniel Schmachtenberger's tgs:Concept.DiscretePhaseShift (TGS_31), which uses a different Fuller image."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SharedBroadcastPerturbation a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Shared Broadcast Perturbation"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "The Day After effect"@en, "Cognitive superorganism"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's argument (Frankly_81) that a single piece of mass media, aired to whole nations at once, could once change a civilization's course. The 1983 television film The Day After catalyzed the nuclear-disarmament movement and moved President Reagan. 'Such a thing couldn't be done today,' because media is fractionated, polarized and increasingly AI-generated into individual realities. But in the 1970s a dramatization of energy depletion, the oceans and humanity leaving the stability of the biosphere, with David Attenborough's messaging 50 years earlier, aired simultaneously to a large fraction of people, might have made a difference: 'in theory, the superorganism of the 1970s could have functioned cognitively, at least to some degree.' That perturbation is no longer available."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/81-time-travel-and-the-superorganism> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_81; the label uses Nate's own word, 'perturbation.' The film is tgs:Work.TheDayAfter1983. Daniel Schmachtenberger's corresponding claim is that an open society needs everyone to make sense well (tgs:Concept.SensemakingPrerequisiteOfOpenSociety, TGS_31). Related: tgs:Concept.NamedSolutionsDrawCounterResponse (Frankly_67), which assumes the same fractured, adaptive mind space."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ThingsOfValueThroughTheBottlenecks a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse, tgs:Subject.OvershootAndLimits ;
    skos:prefLabel "Things of Value Through the Bottlenecks"@en ;
    skos:definition "The aim Nate Hagens and DJ White set out in their book The Bottlenecks of the 21st Century, restated in Frankly_81 as the darkest theme for his time-travel film: once the traveler sees that humans in large numbers, plus water, energy surplus and time, make the metabolic hierarchy of the economic superorganism inevitable -- and its peak, decline and shrinkage inevitable too -- he stops trying to prevent it and instead builds 'arks of humanity and biodiversity' that look two or three steps ahead, past climate change and the superorganism's spending of its energy bounty, to what humans and the world could be hundreds or thousands of years on. The work is to give a shot in the arm to 'those values, those morals, those species, those ecosystems, those technologies that would matter in the long run' and propel them through the coming bottlenecks."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/81-time-travel-and-the-superorganism> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens, tgs:Persona.DJWhite ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_81, the earliest-built episode in the graph to state it; the book is tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury, which later Franklys also cite. Related: tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.MetabolicHierarchyOfTheSuperorganism (Frankly_79), tgs:Concept.InServiceOfLife. In Frankly_90 (April 2025) Nate adds the image of the bottle's walls: what can't and won't happen lie outside them, what might happen inside, and the walls narrow as energy grows scarcer and costlier (tgs:Concept.WhatCantWontMightHappen)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury ;
    .

tgs:Concept.UnintendedConsequences a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Unintended Consequences"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "How the worm turns"@en ;
    skos:definition "The reality, in Nate Hagens's words (Frankly_91), that 'when we think we're doing something, the end result ends up being quite something else,' with consequences chaining 'down the road.' His examples run across energy (peak oil 'debunked' by shale and debt, so no one now anticipates shortages), climate (legitimate science packaged with failed predictions and naive solutions, producing cultural antibodies), social movements (a well-intended cause whose extrapolation provoked a backlash that is now cutting science funding) and geopolitics. In a complex world, his response is to look one, two or three steps ahead, tether to a science-based systems view, keep humility, and learn from 'how the boomerang turns.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/91-unintended-consequences> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_91, whose title it is. Typed thinkr:Concept rather than thinkr:HagensConcept: the idea is classic, analyzed by the sociologist Robert K. Merton in 'The Unanticipated Consequences of Purposive Social Action' (1936), and Nate applies rather than coins it. Daniel Schmachtenberger's method for anticipating it before acting is tgs:Concept.YellowTeaming (TGS_126). Related: tgs:Concept.CulturalAntibodies, tgs:Concept.NamedSolutionsDrawCounterResponse (Frankly_67), tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox (an unintended consequence of efficiency)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CulturalAntibodies a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cultural Antibodies"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's term (Frankly_91) for a culture's learned resistance to a topic because of how it was presented and packaged. Over 15 years, climate change was framed by doomsayers whose five-year predictions did not happen, and its legitimate, scary science -- which unfolds over decades and centuries -- was lumped together with solutions he calls 'systemically and biophysically naive,' from net zero to 'eat bugs and own nothing and be happy.' So 'our culture, especially in the United States, developed antibodies to these discussions': simplistic responses spread virally while the science keeps showing a perilous long-term situation, and 'we don't care and we're not doing anything about it. It's not because it isn't real, it's because how it was presented and packaged.' Peak oil met the same fate when shale and debt extended the plateau."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/91-unintended-consequences> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_91. A specific case of tgs:Concept.UnintendedConsequences. Daniel Schmachtenberger's corresponding warning is tgs:Concept.PrematureCertainty (TGS_31): catastrophic predictions, like hopeful ones, have a poor historical record, and certainty without understanding afflicts doomers too. Related: tgs:Concept.PeakOil, tgs:Concept.NamedSolutionsDrawCounterResponse (Frankly_67), tgs:Concept.SharedBroadcastPerturbation (Frankly_81). The image first appears in the graph in Frankly_51 (December 2023), where a 2 °C summer 'kicks in antibodies' in people who built their identity and reputation around it not happening (tgs:Concept.AggregateCognitiveDissonance)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.HavesAndHaveNots a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Haves and Have-Nots"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Many kinds of wealth and poverty"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's reframing (Frankly_53) of the haves and the have-nots as many axes rather than one. Money and income are one -- US mean income per capita far above the median -- and so is time: today's average person uses many times the goods and services of 200 years ago, and the people of a century hence may have far less. The others: social wealth (the monetarily poor can be socially rich, and the rich isolated); skills that work with the hands and the land; physical and mental health; the attention span to go deep, which the ghost of dopamine past erodes; a voice in one's outcomes; purpose and meaning; a pro-social outlook, which the roughly 10 percent with dark-triad traits lack; and the outsized ability to change the system, held by the top few thousand people riding high on the superorganism. 'Money isn't everything after basic needs are met. Many of you might not have much money but you might be very rich in other areas, and vice versa.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/53-the-haves-and-the-have-nots> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_53, whose title it is. Nate develops the social axis the following week (tgs:Concept.RichInSocialCapital, Frankly_54) and the wealth hierarchy later (tgs:Concept.ThreeTiersOfWealth, Frankly_86). Daniel Schmachtenberger's corresponding distinction is between game-theoretic and real value (tgs:Concept.GameTheoreticVersusRealValue, TGS_42): money tracks what wins, not what we value. Related: tgs:Concept.LayersOfInvisibleWealth, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.EducatingHyperAgents. Frankly_51 (December 2023) previews it as 'a stretching of the haves versus the have-nots' and promises 'another Frankly... on how we define haves and have-nots because there's a lot of things that we have that are not measurable in monetary or bank account terms.'"@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SevenKeyInterventions a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Seven Key Interventions for the Future"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's list (Frankly_55) of what humanity needs ahead of the Great Simplification, given his view that 'there are no longer any non-radical pathways forward': (1) a roadmap, a comprehensive systems map of the horizontal and vertical synthesis of the human predicament, showing which buckets people at different scales should work on; (2) raising the consciousness of 'the 1500,' the global elite pulling the superorganism forward; (3) advance policy across three timelines -- preparing now, the bend-or-break moment, and after; (4) Goldilocks technology and infrastructure, 80 percent of the benefits for 20 percent of the resources; (5) community and social capital, built now; (6) individual healing 'from the root upwards' before engaging with the metacrisis; and (7) a felt recognition of the times we live in and the sacredness of Earth, held by 'a solid group of 5% of the human beings alive today or more.' He gives them in no order, but says the last four must integrate with the map, while the 1500 and advance policy are still needed."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/55-7-key-interventions-for-the-future> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_55, whose title it is. Components: tgs:Concept.TheFifteenHundred, tgs:Concept.AdvancePolicy, tgs:Concept.GoldilocksTechnology, tgs:Concept.HealingBeforeEngagement; community is developed in tgs:Concept.RichInSocialCapital (Frankly_54). Daniel Schmachtenberger's view that every theory of change is partly right (tgs:Concept.EcologyOfTheoriesOfChange, TGS_50) fits Nate's insistence that different people work on different buckets and timelines. Later lists in the same spirit include tgs:Concept.SolutionSpaceCube (Frankly_67)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.TheFifteenHundred a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "The 1500"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "The Fifteen Hundred"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's name, 'for lack of a pithy term' (Frankly_55), for a global elite of roughly 1,500 influential people who are 'not calling the shots per se, but they are pulling the superorganism forward' with brain software incompatible with the biosphere, turning digital and artificial-intelligence claims into social power that has an extractive, ecological impact. Whether or not they are well-intentioned, he believes they are 'disconnected from the feeling centers of their mind,' consuming and accumulating power 'as if a hungry ghost,' and because of their money and influence in markets and technology they pull the whole superorganism toward the Mordor economy. Raising their consciousness of the stakes, and of the sacredness of the biosphere and life, and redirecting their resources and attention is one of his seven key interventions: 'otherwise, they're going to have nothing left to enjoy their excess.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/55-7-key-interventions-for-the-future> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_55. Nate's figure moves: 'the top few thousand people riding high on the superorganism' in Frankly_53 (January 2024), 1,500 here, and schools of ten, a hundred and a thousand 'hyperagents' in Frankly_81 (tgs:Concept.EducatingHyperAgents). 'Hungry ghost' is a Buddhist image of insatiable craving. Daniel Schmachtenberger's account of such people is tgs:Concept.HyperAgents (TGS_20); see also tgs:Concept.PowerSeekingAndComplicity (TGS_126) and tgs:ScenarioFacet.Mordor."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.HealingBeforeEngagement a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Healing Before Engagement"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Heal from the root upwards"@en ;
    skos:definition "What Nate Hagens calls 'a big change in my thinking, a big change' (Frankly_55): people need to be nourished, rested, exercised and in equilibrium as a priority before they engage with the metacrisis and the human predicament. Many treat news of oil depletion, climate, financial overshoot and geopolitics as 'a mental video game that's dopamine and cortisol' that changes neither how they live nor how they relate to their community, because the behavioral stack is out of balance and cognition is captured by limbic, enteric and reptilian systems. So 'we need to heal from the root upwards' -- nutrition, sleep, exercise, meditation, music, chanting, singing, and a friend's 'libraries of healing' where people are healed by being together. He had naively assumed that telling people the urgent predicament would make them respond; now he thinks healthy, behaviorally balanced individuals in community must come first, then the systems synthesis, integrated post-tragically with others for support, which 'empowers people to be agents of change.' Governments, philanthropists and communities should divert far more resources to healing the people around us."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/55-7-key-interventions-for-the-future> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_55, where it follows his own reset in India and his top 2024 priority of health. The behavioral stack is tgs:Monologue.Frankly_52_TheBehavioralStack; the inner costs are named later in tgs:Concept.ShadowOfTheSuperorganism (Frankly_73). Daniel Schmachtenberger's corresponding requirement is heart, will and mind together (tgs:Concept.HeartWillMind, TGS_31), and his stages of tragic and post-tragic (tgs:Concept.PostTragic) are the frame Nate uses here. Related: tgs:Concept.RichInSocialCapital, tgs:Concept.GhostOfDopaminePast. The seed is in Frankly_52 (January 2024), recorded before his reset: 'healing of our emotional states is as or more important than educating humans about more facts' (tgs:Concept.VerticalBehavioralStack)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.AggregateCognitiveDissonance a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Aggregate Cognitive Dissonance"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's expectation (Frankly_51) that cognitive dissonance 'with humans in aggregate is going to increase' as the futures people attached their identities, reputations, hopes and beliefs to -- continued growth, AI, the absence of climate change -- not only fail to arrive but turn into their opposite. Faced with that, people 'adopt strange behavioral, vocal stories' rather than revise their beliefs, and he sees the pattern in many areas of the culture. An event such as a month at 2 °C of warming 'kicks in antibodies' in those who built identities around it not happening; he wonders what people who call climate change a hoax will say then."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/51-systemic-themes-for-2024> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_51. Typed thinkr:Concept rather than thinkr:HagensConcept: cognitive dissonance is Leon Festinger's theory (1957; the show notes link a general explainer); Nate's contribution is applying it to whole societies whose expected futures fail. Its cultural result is tgs:Concept.CulturalAntibodies (Frankly_91). Daniel Schmachtenberger's tgs:Concept.PrematureCertainty (TGS_31) describes the certainty that sets people up for it. Related: tgs:Concept.TerrorManagementTheory, tgs:Concept.HavesAndHaveNots."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.VerticalBehavioralStack a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Vertical Behavioral Stack"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "The behavioral stack"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's model (Frankly_52) of the human being as an evolutionary stack of seven layers -- microbiome, cells, organs, the enteric system, the reptilian system, the limbic system and cognition -- read as a behavioral stack. Each of the top four has a binary state: the enteric system, governing the homeostasis of daily routines, is stable or unstable; the reptilian system, sympathetic and parasympathetic, is at ease or in dis-ease (fight, flight or freeze); the limbic system, around the hippocampus and amygdala, is satisfied or dissatisfied and, if dissatisfied, seeks stimulation, consumption or status; cognition, our beliefs, stories and facts, is serene or confused and agitated. We assume cognition is the elephant in control; in reality it is 'the tiny little rider on the elephant,' and each lower layer has more power over behavior. So a person who is not stable, at ease and satisfied can only know about the predicament rather than know it, and more facts bring confusion or dopamine rather than change. Groups of people who are 'vertically stacked' -- stable, satisfied, 'we over me' -- could have outsized impact."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/52-the-behavioral-stack> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_52, whose title it is, and which Nate contrasts with the horizontal 'parts' of Dick Schwartz's Internal Family Systems (tgs:Persona.RichardSchwartz). The elephant and rider is Jonathan Haidt's image. Later uses: 'an imbalance in the behavioral stack' in Frankly_55 (tgs:Concept.HealingBeforeEngagement) and the Frankly_73 show notes. Not to be confused with tgs:Concept.SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem (Frankly_162), a seven-level ladder of the superorganism rather than of the body. Daniel Schmachtenberger's corresponding diagnosis is tgs:Concept.FragmentedConsciousness (TGS_31). Related: tgs:Concept.Wetware, tgs:Concept.DopamineHijackedCulture, tgs:Concept.DividedBrain."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.DopamineHijackedCulture a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dopamine-Hijacked Culture"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's claim (Frankly_52) that modern culture has let dopamine stand in for every other reward. He describes four neurotransmitter poles: dopamine and endorphin for doing -- goal-seeking, another podcast, more money, a marathon, a new product -- and serotonin and oxytocin for being. Almost everything the culture offers has made dopamine a proxy for the others, and even activities that were originally healthy have been hijacked: yoga becomes the cool outfit and flexing for attractive classmates, and sharing on social media becomes high-grading how we appear. 'Our entire culture has, in a positive feedback sort of way, become more and more dopamine hijacked,' leaving people on a seeking treadmill. His coach's counter is a 'quiver full of arrows' of alternative activities -- walking in nature, cooking with friends, singing, chanting -- to turn to when seeking."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/52-the-behavioral-stack> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_52; the show notes link explainers on dopamine, endorphins, oxytocin and serotonin. The mechanism Nate later names is tgs:Concept.GhostOfDopaminePast (Frankly_103); the stimuli are tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli. Daniel Schmachtenberger's corresponding account is tgs:Concept.OneMarshmallowRewardCircuits (TGS_31): the market supplies the reward without the work or the deeper nutrient. Related: tgs:Work.DopamineNation, tgs:Concept.VerticalBehavioralStack, tgs:Concept.RichInSocialCapital."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.GameTheory a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.BehavioralEconomicsAndCognition ;
    skos:prefLabel "Game Theory"@en ;
    skos:definition "The mathematical study of strategic interaction, in which each agent's best choice depends on what the others choose. Founded as a discipline by John von Neumann and Oskar Morgenstern in Theory of Games and Economic Behavior (1944), which formalized games, strategies, payoffs and expected utility and solved two-person zero-sum games by the minimax theorem; later extended to non-cooperative equilibria (John Nash), repeated games and evolutionary games. In this graph it is the formal background to the multipolar trap, the tragedy of the commons and Daniel Schmachtenberger's account of game-theoretic value."@en ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.TheoryOfGamesAndEconomicBehavior ;
    thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap, tgs:Concept.TragedyOfTheCommons, tgs:Concept.GameTheoreticVersusRealValue ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 in the definedIn pass, because the graph had concepts built on game theory but no concept for game theory itself. Daniel Schmachtenberger's tgs:Concept.GameTheoryFillsTheOughtGap (TGS_126) cites the 1944 book; it now relatesTo this concept. Typed thinkr:Concept, not thinkr:HagensConcept."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    .

tgs:Concept.NonZeroSumness a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Non-Zero-Sumness"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Non-zero-sum dynamics"@en ;
    skos:definition "Robert Wright's thesis in Nonzero: The Logic of Human Destiny (2000) that biological and cultural evolution have a direction: over time they produce more, and more complex, non-zero-sum interactions -- relationships in which players can win or lose together rather than one gaining what the other loses -- and that this growth of mutual interdependence drives rising social complexity, from bands to chiefdoms to states to a globalized world."@en ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.Nonzero ;
    thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.GameTheory ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 in the definedIn pass. Daniel Schmachtenberger's tgs:Concept.PositiveSumInGroupNegativeSumToNature (TGS_42) cites Wright and adds the catch: cooperation that is positive-sum within a group can be negative-sum toward out-groups and nature; it now relatesTo this concept. Typed thinkr:Concept, not thinkr:HagensConcept."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ProgressTrap a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.OvershootAndLimits, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Progress Trap"@en ;
    skos:definition "Ronald Wright's term in A Short History of Progress (2004, his CBC Massey Lectures): a success that, pushed past a certain scale, brings disaster -- as when Paleolithic hunters perfected their methods until they killed off the big game they depended on. Wright reads the collapses of Sumer, Rome, the Maya and Easter Island as progress traps and treats civilization itself, roughly 10,000 years old, as an experiment that may be one, unless it becomes environmentally sustainable."@en ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.AShortHistoryOfProgress ;
    thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.Overshoot, tgs:Concept.NaiveProgress ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 in the definedIn pass; the book is cited in tgs:Monologue.Frankly_105_KeyBlindspotsOfTheWalrusMovement. Related to Daniel Schmachtenberger's critique of naive progress (tgs:Concept.NaiveProgress) and to tgs:Concept.ComplexityCollapse. Typed thinkr:Concept, not thinkr:HagensConcept."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.IntegratingThinkingAndFeeling a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Integrating Thinking and Feeling"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's call (Frankly_89) to enlarge the overlap between paired modalities that the culture treats as dichotomies: subjective and objective, thinking and feeling, left brain and right brain, masculine and feminine, narrow and wide boundary, analytical and embodied -- 'we can't be on one side of each of those equations.' He tests it with two imagined communities: one that knows every aspect of the metacrisis but lives in its head, nervous systems dysregulated and without joy, music, laughter or love; and one fully embodied, dancing and meditating, with no knowledge of the metacrisis and no skills to grow food or improvise energy and water systems. He would want neither, but finds groups increasingly of one kind or the other, so 'we need to make the Venn diagram between these two modalities with a larger overlap.' A friend's warning frames it: when only the problems are seen, 'all the solutions are fear based, and anger based, and control, and power.' His own next steps, he says, will come from feeling as well as analysis."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/89-thinking-and-feeling> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_89, whose title it names. The left and right brain pairing follows Iain McGilchrist (tgs:Concept.DividedBrain). Continues tgs:Concept.HealingBeforeEngagement (Frankly_55) and tgs:Concept.VerticalBehavioralStack (Frankly_52). Daniel Schmachtenberger's corresponding claim is that heart, will and mind must come together (tgs:Concept.HeartWillMind, TGS_31)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.WhatCantWontMightHappen a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "What Can't, Won't and Might Happen"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's three categories for judging claims about the future (Frankly_90), needed because humans think in words and can make impossible things sound plausible. What can't happen is ruled out by physical law (physics, chemistry, thermodynamics: a one-by-two-foot block of wood cannot yield a four-foot carving) or by path dependence (after the extinction of tree frogs there will be no tree frogs). What won't happen is technically possible but effectively impossible once the probabilities of all its necessary steps are multiplied -- a 500-foot cheese statue of a mouse, or net zero by 2050 at today's scale (tgs:Concept.AggregateProbability). What might happen is where attention belongs. In the frame of the bottlenecks of the 21st century, what can't and won't happen lie outside the walls of the bottle and what might happen lies inside, and as energy grows scarcer and costlier the walls narrow. Most public discourse, he says, is about the first two categories."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/90-net-zero-and-other-delusions> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-18 for Frankly_90, whose subtitle it is; Frankly_91 says he plans to make it a series. Builds on tgs:Concept.AggregateProbability (Frankly_79) and tgs:Concept.ThingsOfValueThroughTheBottlenecks. Daniel Schmachtenberger's corresponding move is from impossible to hard (tgs:Concept.ImpossibleToHard, TGS_20): getting the constraints right rules out the easy solutions and shows which hard ones remain."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ThreeTimelinesOfResponse a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Three Timelines of Response"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's division (Frankly_78) of the work ahead into three periods. Now: while the economic superorganism dominates and debt and technology can kick the can forward, people can be good to their families, businesses and world only at the margin, and the task is to prepare and change the initial conditions of what comes next. The bend-versus-break moment: already arrived in places such as Ukraine, Syria, Madagascar and Afghanistan, and in dolphin populations, but still ahead for many; when it comes, far more people will cooperate and roll up their sleeves because the situation becomes emotionally obvious, while dark-triad people, no more numerous than now, will act in the open. A later, more stable state: once the financial and trade systems settle, expectations match the available energy and daily production, and social reciprocity is much stronger. Prosocial behavior that looks rare today (3 or 4 percent) will be common in the later periods."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/78-a-brief-clarification-on-human-behavior> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-19 for Frankly_78. An earlier form is in Frankly_55 (March 2024), where advance policy needs 'three timelines of interventions': preparing now, the bend-or-break moment when the financial musical chairs stop, and after (tgs:Concept.AdvancePolicy, tgs:Concept.SevenKeyInterventions). Daniel Schmachtenberger's corresponding frame is triage, transition and new systems (tgs:Concept.ThreeByThreeResponseGrid, TGS_50). Related: tgs:Concept.ProsocialityUnderTheSuperorganism, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ProsocialityUnderTheSuperorganism a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Prosociality Under the Superorganism"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Suppressed prosociality"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's clarification (Frankly_78) of why so few people act for the greater good today. Human behavior is a product of 290,000 years in small Pleistocene bands, and most of that past was prosocial, held together by strong reciprocity that punished cheaters and those who failed to punish them; caring for nature and for each other are intrinsic and 'quite dominant' values. But the economic superorganism exerts 'downward causation': because everyone must live within a profit-seeking, extractive, growing institutional structure, it is 'sucking out the best of our natural behaviors,' and these values have been 'systematically suppressed and betrayed,' especially in the global north. So only 3 or 4 percent of people buck the trend now -- not because humans are innately selfish (Homo economicus, he says, is 'a desolate and dismal view' and untrue) but because of the structure. Since 'we are plastic' and the biggest predictor of behavior is conforming to what others do, prosocial behavior will return as conditions change."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/78-a-brief-clarification-on-human-behavior> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-19 for Frankly_78, a retraction of the 3-to-4-percent remark in Frankly_76. Daniel Schmachtenberger's corresponding account is that game theory, the logic of win-lose, came to define a good choice in modern institutions (tgs:Concept.GameTheoryFillsTheOughtGap, TGS_126). Related: tgs:Concept.Wetware, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.TraitsOutcompeteValues (Frankly_85), tgs:Concept.ShadowOfTheSuperorganism (Frankly_73), tgs:Concept.ReverseDominanceHierarchy, tgs:Concept.ThreeTimelinesOfResponse."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.WideBoundary a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wide Boundary"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Wide-boundary lens"@en, "Wide-boundary thinking"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's most-used methodological term: drawing the boundary of an analysis wide enough to include the second-, third- and nth-order consequences of an action -- across energy, materials, ecology, money, behavior and time -- rather than stopping at its immediate, narrow-boundary benefit. In Frankly_65 (And Then What?: Using Wide-Boundary Lenses) he grounds it in Garrett Hardin's 'ecolate' habit of asking 'and then what?' and works through cases of widening boundary: corn ethanol (narrow, cheaper gasoline; wide, diverted food and more nitrogen runoff into the Gulf of Mexico), sovereign debt (narrow, consumption now; wide, a claim on future biophysical resources), electric vehicles and renewable buildout, and both growth and degrowth. The opposite is narrow-boundary analysis, which he treats as the default of markets and most policy."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/65-and-then-what-using-wide-boundary-lenses> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.Ecolate, tgs:Concept.UnintendedConsequences ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-19; the gap was surfaced by tgs:Work.TGSGlossary, which lists the wide-boundary lens with Frankly 65. The term recurs across the graph in compounds such as tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySovereignty and tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySunkCost, in Nate's 'Wide Boundary News' Franklys, and in 'wide-boundary EROI.' Hardin's underlying term is tgs:Concept.Ecolate. Daniel Schmachtenberger's corresponding method is tgs:Concept.HolisticExternalityAnalysis (TGS_50); in Frankly_65 Nate credits Daniel's naive-versus-authentic progress distinction as an influence."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.WileECoyoteMoment a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.OvershootAndLimits ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wile E. Coyote Moment"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Wile E. Coyote reconnection date"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's recurring image, from the cartoon coyote who runs off a cliff and falls only when he looks down, for the moment when financial and paper claims, having run far beyond the real biophysical means that back them, are suddenly seen to have nothing beneath them. Growth can continue on credit for a time, but when it stops the reconnection between claims and reality is abrupt rather than gradual. He uses it for the end of embedded exponential growth (TGS_50), for the precipice that the few able to steer must avoid (Frankly_53), for a financial scenario alongside AI-driven growth and collapse (Frankly_67), for the bend-not-break moment that rising rates and debt bring closer (Frankly_90), for compounding instruments that become 'Wile E. Coyote traps' in contraction (Frankly_151), and for the 'reconnection date' at which a deflationary depression becomes likely, most probably first in bond and currency markets (Frankly_159)."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/90-net-zero-and-other-delusions> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.MusicalChairsMoment, tgs:Concept.MoneyAsClaimOnEnergy, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-19; the gap was surfaced by tgs:Work.TGSGlossary. The earliest use in the graph is TGS_50 (December 2022). Nate did not coin the image: a Wile E. Coyote moment is a common economic metaphor for realizing one has run off a cliff (the glossary quotes Time magazine on the dot-com bust in 2000); his use ties it to biophysical claims. Close kin: tgs:Concept.MusicalChairsMoment, which describes who is left without a chair rather than the fall itself."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.MusicalChairsMoment a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.MonetarySystemAndGrowth ;
    skos:prefLabel "Musical Chairs Moment"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Financial musical chairs"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's long-running metaphor for the gap between financial claims and the world's real source and sink capacity: there are far more claims (debt, unfunded promises, asset prices) than there are chairs (real goods, energy and ecological capacity) to honor them, and while the music of credit and growth plays the gap goes unnoticed. When the music stops -- prior claims can no longer be serviced -- not everyone finds a chair. He raises it with Daniel Schmachtenberger as a financial 'musical chairs moment' (TGS_42), places the bend-or-break moment 'when the financial musical chairs stop' (Frankly_55), warns that degrowth risks a sudden musical-chairs collapse given the West's financial overhang (Frankly_65), says that without broadly shared productivity gains society plays musical chairs with fewer chairs (Frankly_115), and counts describing the shortage of chairs among information hazards, since it can itself accelerate a stampede (Frankly_153)."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/42-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.WileECoyoteMoment, tgs:Concept.MoneyAsClaimOnEnergy, tgs:Concept.TwelveInformationHazards ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-19; the gap was surfaced by tgs:Work.TGSGlossary. The earliest use in the graph is TGS_42 (October 2022), whose show notes link a 'financial musical chairs moment' video (hYQDEa99pYU); Frankly_90's show notes link the same video as 'Financial Overshoot.' That video is not yet identified in the graph. Frankly_94's title also uses musical chairs, there for relationships and Dunbar's number."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.OvertonWindow a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Overton Window"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Window of discourse"@en ;
    skos:definition "The range of ideas and policies that the mainstream public treats as acceptable at a given time, named after Joseph Overton of the Mackinac Center for Public Policy in the 1990s; the window can shift, widen or narrow. Nate Hagens describes his own role in its terms: a 'golden retriever catalyst' and 'Overton window biophysical analyst' whose work is to widen the range of what can be discussed about energy, ecology and the human predicament (Frankly_53, Frankly_80, TGS_205), and in Frankly_68 he frames the show's purpose as expanding the window fast enough to keep pace with a shrinking pool of effective solutions."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Overton_window> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-19; the gap was surfaced by tgs:Work.TGSGlossary. Typed thinkr:Concept, not thinkr:HagensConcept: the term is Overton's. Guests also use it: Indy Johar expects crises to be Overton-window moments (TGS_147, TGS_238), and Nate frames the Reality Roundtables as an Overton-window mechanism (RR_12). Related: tgs:Concept.TheoryOfChange, tgs:Concept.NamedSolutionsDrawCounterResponse."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.DissipativeStructures a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dissipative Structures"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Degrading energy gradients"@en, "Nature abhors a gradient"@en ;
    skos:definition "The thermodynamic account of life and self-organization behind much of Nate Hagens's biophysical framing. Systems held far from equilibrium by a flow of energy -- a living organism, an ecosystem, an economy -- can spontaneously organize into ordered 'dissipative structures' (Ilya Prigogine's term, Nobel Prize 1977) that maintain themselves by degrading the gradient that feeds them, exporting entropy as waste heat; life does not violate the second law but rides gradients 'the way a surfer rides a wave' (Frankly_117). Eric Schneider and James Kay proposed that life is a way of dissipating the solar-induced energy gradient and that ecosystems develop by increasing their dissipation, summed up as 'nature abhors a gradient.' Nate builds on it toward Odum's maximum power principle (old-growth redwoods dissipating the most energy in a forest transect, TGS_5), describes civilization as an energy-dissipating structure (Frankly_23), and proposes that humans too travel down gradients (TGS_205; tgs:Concept.FifthLawOfThermodynamics)."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dissipative_system> ;
    thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.FifthLawOfThermodynamics, tgs:Concept.LocalizedSyntropySystemicEntropy, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-19; the gap was surfaced by tgs:Work.TGSGlossary ('Degrading Energy Gradients'). Typed thinkr:Concept, not thinkr:HagensConcept: the ideas are Prigogine's and Schneider and Kay's. William Rees supplies the dissipative-structures backbone in tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_2_DeeperEcology; tgs:Human.IlyaPrigogine exists (no Persona yet)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.LossAversion a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.BehavioralEconomicsAndCognition, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Loss Aversion"@en ;
    skos:definition "The cognitive bias by which people weigh a loss more heavily than an equal gain -- in the research Nate Hagens cites, about twice as strongly. Formalized in Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky's prospect theory (1979). Nate gives it an evolutionary explanation: for an animal with enough food, a little extra made no difference, but a loss could mean death. In Frankly_25 he argues it will be one of the main biases of the coming financial and biophysical contraction, when claims exceed what reality can service and people lose what they thought they owned; that the story of the end of growth itself triggers it, by threatening identities built on progress; that on social media identity becomes a supernormal stimulus defended like calories; and that it keeps cultures choosing fixes that ask them to give up nothing. His counters are to measure self-worth by natural, social, built and human capital rather than net worth, and to widen it into a deep-time loss aversion for the species and ecosystems we are losing slowly."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://read.realityblind.world/view/975731937/82/> ;
    thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli, tgs:Concept.CulturalAntibodies, tgs:Concept.AggregateCognitiveDissonance, tgs:Concept.MusicalChairsMoment ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-19 for Frankly_25, whose title it is; the gap was first surfaced by tgs:Work.TGSGlossary. Typed thinkr:Concept, not thinkr:HagensConcept: the bias is Kahneman and Tversky's, and Nate applies it (the dcterms:source is the Reality Blind page on it). Nate's own image for the counter is in tgs:Concept.RichInSocialCapital and tgs:Concept.HavesAndHaveNots. In TGS_174 (April 2025) Nate and Maren Urner use it to explain why news turns negative: a brain tuned to survival clicks on bad news first, so 'what bleeds leads.'"@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfTheApocalypse a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Four Horsemen of the Apocalypse"@en ;
    skos:definition "The four riders of Revelation 6:1-8 -- on white, red, black and pale horses, usually read as conquest, war, famine and death -- released at the opening of the first four seals; the source of the modern figure of speech for converging catastrophes."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four_Horsemen_of_the_Apocalypse> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-19 at MJSullivan's direction as the canonical reference behind Nate's tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s, so the borrowing is an explicit link rather than only a label. Interpretations vary: the white rider is also read as pestilence or as Christ, and Revelation 6:8 says Hades followed Death, which some count as a fifth figure. Other modern uses of the phrase -- the 'Four Horsemen' of New Atheism, John Gottman's four destructive communication patterns -- are unrelated to Nate's and are not modeled."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.DominationPartnershipContinuum a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Domination-Partnership Continuum"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Partnership-domination social scale"@en, "Biocultural partnership-domination lens"@en ;
    skos:definition "Riane Eisler's lens for societies, families and relationships: a continuum between domination systems (top-down rule, rigid gender roles, violence) and partnership systems (caring, gender balance, power that empowers), which cuts across right and left, capitalist and socialist, religious and secular."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "In TGS_116 Eisler says the inherited categories -- right and left, religious and secular, Eastern and Western, Northern and Southern, capitalist and socialist -- 'fragment our consciousness' and hide a deeper pattern. Hitler's Germany, Stalin's Soviet Union, the Taliban, Iran and Putin's Russia differ on the surface but share one configuration: authoritarian, punitive family and state, rigid gender stereotypes, and violence ('man over man, man over woman, race over race, religion over religion, nation over nation'). North Korea is socialist and pure domination; the Nordic nations have market economies yet lean to partnership. Neither pure end exists: 'it's always a matter of degree.' Partnership keeps hierarchies -- parents, teachers, leaders -- but leaders inspire and empower rather than control, and transparency becomes possible. She dates the turn toward domination to only 5,000 to 10,000 years ago, and says there never was a matriarchy: the opposite of patriarchy is partnership. Her earlier term for a partnership society is 'gylany' (in the transcript, 'Gaelanic')."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RianeEisler ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.TheChaliceAndTheBlade ;
    thinkr:hasAlternateTerm [ a thinkr:AlternateTerm ;
        thinkr:hasAlternateTermType tgs:AlternateTermType.NarrowMatch ;
        skos:prefLabel "Gylany"@en ;
        skos:scopeNote "Eisler's coinage in The Chalice and the Blade (1987) for a partnership society, from gy- (woman) and an- (man) linked by l; adjective gylanic. Transcribed as 'Gaelanic' in the TGS_116 transcript." ] ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-19 for TGS_116. Related: tgs:Concept.ReverseDominanceHierarchy (Boehm's egalitarian bands), tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.PowerVersusLife."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.FourCornerstonesOfPartnership a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Four Cornerstones of Partnership"@en ;
    skos:definition "Riane Eisler's four places where a society's position on the domination-partnership continuum is set, and where change must work: childhood and family, gender, economics, and story and language."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Eisler's theory of change (TGS_116; the last chapter of tgs:Work.NurturingOurHumanity): short-term tactics are not enough without long-term strategies aimed at the four cornerstones. Childhood and family come first, because the first five years shape the brain, and a child in a domination family learns that violence by the stronger against the weaker is normal and moral, and learns the hidden system of gendered values; her first priority is ending violence against children in families, backed by authoritative rather than authoritarian parenting. Gender is 'a primary social, economic, and family organizing principle,' not a women's issue: as the status of women rises, so does the status of values coded feminine, such as caring and nonviolence, so men vote for caring policies too. Economics must value care (tgs:Concept.CaringEconomics). Story and language must change: original sin and the selfish gene are 'the same story' -- we are bad, so we must be controlled from the top. She notes that those pushing back against partnership invest heavily in exactly these long-term strategies -- family control, rigid gender roles."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RianeEisler ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.NurturingOurHumanity ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-19 for TGS_116. Related: tgs:Concept.DominationPartnershipContinuum."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ChaliceAndBladePower a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Chalice and Blade Power"@en ;
    skos:definition "Riane Eisler's two kinds of power: the blade's power to control and take life, which defines power in domination systems, and the chalice's power to give, nurture and illuminate life, which domination systems code as feminine but everyone has."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "From The Chalice and the Blade (1987), restated in TGS_116. The symbols are often misread as standing for the sexes; Eisler says that misreading itself comes from the rigid gender stereotypes of domination systems. 'The battle for our future is not between right and left... It's between these two configurations.' Her advice to listeners: 'remember that you have power, the power of the chalice' -- the advances against child labor and for human rights came from small, usually unpopular groups."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RianeEisler ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.TheChaliceAndTheBlade ;
    thinkr:convergesWith tgs:Concept.PowerVersusLife ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-19 for TGS_116. Converges with Nate's tgs:Concept.PowerVersusLife (Frankly_82); see that link's LinkNote."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CaringEconomics a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Caring Economics"@en ;
    skos:definition "Riane Eisler's economics that counts and rewards the work of caring for people and nature, which both capitalism and socialism left out: unpaid care, done mostly by women, may equal about half of reported GDP."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "In TGS_116 Eisler says economics is about what we value and what we measure. Smith challenged the mercantilist control of kings and Marx challenged the bourgeoisie, but both came from early industrial times and both kept the 'hidden system of gendered values': for both, nature was there to be exploited, and care for children, the old and the sick was to be done for free by women in male-controlled households (as late as Marx's day, in many jurisdictions only a husband could sue for injury to his wife, for loss of her services). An Australian study found care work would be about 50 percent of reported GDP if counted. Economists call high-quality human capital the key to a post-industrial economy, yet neuroscience shows it depends on the care and education children receive early. The Nordic nations show caring policies are compatible with, and help, a competitive market economy."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RianeEisler ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.TheRealWealthOfNations ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-19 for TGS_116. The Six Fronts subdomain that most closely matches it is tgs:InterventionSubdomain.CareEconomy. Related: tgs:Concept.TransmutingWealthIntoIncome, tgs:Concept.PriceCostAndValue."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.DenialLearnedInDominationFamilies a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Denial Learned in Domination Families"@en ;
    skos:definition "Riane Eisler's claim that denial -- of climate change, election results or COVID -- is a trait learned early: a child who depends for food and shelter on the people causing it pain has to deny, and is taught to blame an out-group instead."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RianeEisler ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-19 for TGS_116, where Eisler says the common trait of those pushing back is 'called denial,' as she elucidates in tgs:Work.NurturingOurHumanity. A psychological account of individual denial; distinct from tgs:SchoolOfThought.OrganizedClimateDoubt, which is organized and funded. Useful in WWNS for why doubt campaigns find a receptive audience."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.Pragmatopia a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pragmatopia"@en ;
    skos:definition "Riane Eisler's coinage for a practical, achievable society oriented toward partnership, as opposed to a utopia ('no place'): not a perfect society, but one that leans to the partnership end of the continuum."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RianeEisler ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-19 for TGS_116. Related: tgs:Concept.DominationPartnershipContinuum."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.OstromDesignPrinciples a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ostrom's Design Principles for Commons Governance"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Governing the commons"@en ;
    skos:definition "Elinor Ostrom's eight conditions under which communities govern shared resources well without privatization or state control: clear boundaries, rules fitted to local conditions, users' say in changing the rules, monitoring, graduated sanctions, cheap conflict resolution, recognized rights to organize, and nested governance for larger systems."@en ;
    thinkr:extendedDefinition "Ostrom's empirical answer to Hardin (tgs:Concept.TragedyOfTheCommons). Hardin's model assumes strangers with open access and no rules; real commons usually have governance, and users who meet again and again can build and enforce it. Her field studies found the same eight features in commons that lasted for generations: (1) clearly defined boundaries of who may use what; (2) rules congruent with local conditions; (3) collective-choice arrangements, so those bound by the rules can change them; (4) monitoring, usually by the users themselves; (5) graduated sanctions, mild for a first lapse and escalating for repeat defection; (6) accessible, low-cost conflict resolution; (7) recognition by outside authorities of the users' right to organize; (8) nested enterprises, with local rules for local matters and higher tiers for coordination across communities. Commons governance tends to fail where groups are too large or heterogeneous for reputation and repeated dealings to work, where outside authorities override local rules or grant access to outsiders (colonial regimes often created open access where none existed), and where resources change faster than rules can adapt. Hardin later accepted the narrowing: his argument applies to unmanaged, open-access commons."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.ElinorOstrom ;
    thinkr:definedIn tgs:Work.GoverningTheCommons ;
    thinkr:contrastsWith tgs:Concept.TragedyOfTheCommons ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-19 at MJSullivan's request as the counterpoint to tgs:Concept.TragedyOfTheCommons, prompted by Ryan Collison's explainer 'Elinor Ostrom: Governing the Commons' (ideasthesia, February 6, 2026; a secondary summary, supplied by MJSullivan as a PDF). A qualification of Hardin more than a refutation: the tragedy holds for ungoverned open access, which is why Daniel Schmachtenberger's planetary-scale multipolar traps (no shared rules, no monitoring, no sanctions across nations) remain traps. In TGS, Nate draws on Ostrom's monitoring and sanctioning findings in Frankly_108 (tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths). Related: tgs:Concept.RestraintByDesign, tgs:Concept.CircleOfTrustLocalism, tgs:InterventionSubdomain.CommonsBasedOwnership, tgs:PracticeTerm.CommonsGovernanceProtocols."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.Bioregion a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bioregion"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Bioregionalism"@en ;
    skos:definition "A region defined by nature rather than politics -- by watersheds, landforms, climate and the communities of life they support -- and, in bioregionalism, the scale at which people can organize economies, governance and culture around the places that sustain them."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-20 with the import of tgs:Work.OneEarthBioregions2023, whose 185 bioregions are modeled as thinkr:Bioregion individuals (bioregions.ttl). In TGS, bioregionalism is the subject of tgs:Interview.TGS_139_DanielChristianWahl ('Bioregional Futures'). Related: tgs:Work.BioregioningEarthOntology, tgs:InterventionSubdomain.ReconnectionToPlace, tgs:InterventionSubdomain.SubsidiarityAndLocalGovernance, tgs:Concept.CircleOfTrustLocalism."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.BattlesOfOurTime a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Battles of Our Time"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Polarities of our time"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's list (Frankly_76, November 2024) of the tensions of the coming decades, from geopolitics down to the nervous system: a unipolar versus a multipolar world, elites versus the common man, masculine versus feminine traits, individual versus collective, humans versus non-humans, present versus future, dopamine versus the other neurotransmitters, extraction versus regeneration, things versus connections, activism versus effectivism, brainstem versus neocortex, fantasy versus reality, sympathetic versus parasympathetic, and behaviors versus values. The morning after recording he reframed them as polarities to be navigated and harmonized rather than won. All are part of one greater battle, power versus life, and beyond it the superorganism versus the cultural 'mitochondria' that could outlive it."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/76-the-battles-of-our-time> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-20 for Frankly_76, whose title it is. The culture Nate hopes to seed sits on the second side of most pairings: more feminine, more ecological and less mechanistic, more right-brain, and valuing the more-than-human world in its prices and decisions. Related: tgs:Concept.PowerVersusLife (named here as the umbrella battle, two months before Frankly_82 built on it), tgs:Concept.CulturalMitochondria (the mitochondria image, developed in Frankly_98), tgs:Concept.DividedBrain (left versus right brain), tgs:Concept.GhostOfDopaminePast and tgs:Concept.DopamineHijackedCulture, tgs:Concept.MultilevelSelection (cooperation and competition both hardwired), tgs:Concept.Effectivism. Frankly_74 opens with an earlier list of dualities: pro-human versus anti-human, mechanistic versus animistic, natalist versus anti-natalist."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.Effectivism a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Activism Versus Effectivism"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Effectivism"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's contrast (Frankly_76): activism is backward-looking, destructive and blaming; effectivism is creative, forward-looking and planning. Because activism on the issues of the day has not worked, he hopes more people will work on effectivism -- owning who we are as an evolved, clever, hubristic species and planning ahead. His image for where to put the effort: rather than train a thousand sheep, educate and inspire one border collie."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/76-the-battles-of-our-time> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    rdfs:seeAlso tgs:Archetype.Effectivist ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-20 for Frankly_76, one of the battles of our time (tgs:Concept.BattlesOfOurTime). The same idea names the Effectivist, one of the nine Bend Not Break archetypes of Frankly_58 (tgs:Archetype.Effectivist), and the title of tgs:Interview.TGS_51_DJWhite ('Ocean Effectivism'). In the same passage Nate said appeals to 'the better angels of our nature' will only ever reach a few percent of people; he retracted that framing two weeks later in Frankly_78 (tgs:Concept.ProsocialityUnderTheSuperorganism)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EnergyGDPCoupling a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Energy-GDP Coupling"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Energy and economic growth"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's account (Frankly_70) of how tightly economic growth tracks energy. Globally, GDP is essentially 100 percent correlated with material use and 99 percent correlated with energy use, but correlation is not one-for-one: over the past 50-plus years GDP grew about 3.2 percent a year and energy about 2.04 percent, so roughly 70 units of new energy accompany each 100 units of new GDP. Service economies such as the United States and United Kingdom look less coupled because the energy behind their imports is burned elsewhere: the average American uses about 57 barrels of oil equivalent of fossil fuels a year directly, plus about 17 more embodied in imports."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/70-ask-me-anything> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-20 for Frankly_70, answering a listener who had seen EPA figures suggesting US power use had fallen. Electric power is only about 20 percent of global energy use, and US electricity use has flattened rather than fallen. The show notes cite Nature Communications (2020) for the GDP, materials and energy correlations, Our World in Data on energy embodied in imports, and 17 barrels of oil equivalent embodied in imports; Nate says 15 aloud, and the show notes are taken as authoritative. Related: tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness (the cultural failure to see this dependence), tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.HeadHeartAndHands a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Head, Heart and Hands"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's description (Frankly_70) of the three things The Great Simplification tries to hold in one conversation. The head is the intellectual framing of the biophysical constraints we face. The heart is the change in consciousness and values: a wider definition of 'we,' the recognition that this is not who we have to be as a species, of what we are doing to the planet, and of the colonial impulse and hierarchical, male-dominated culture. The hands are the practical work that follows; the episode description names the podcast's goal as integrating all three through a generative conversation among many more people. He says the predicament cannot be analysed 'like an accountant or even an ecologist.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/70-ask-me-anything> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-20 for Frankly_70, where Nate names it as the main thing he has learned in two and a half years of the podcast. Daniel Schmachtenberger's comparable triad in TGS_31 is heart, will and mind, where any two without the third fail (tgs:Concept.HeartWillMind). Related: tgs:Concept.DividedBrain (left and right brain, which Nate pairs with the brain and the body in the same answer)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.RealityParty a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Reality Party"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Reality platform"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate Hagens's thought experiment (Frankly_66) of a nonpartisan political platform built on shared observations of reality rather than on values. He describes three layers. Preferences most people share across politics: sunsets with loved ones, birdsong, healthy food and clean water, feeling safe, pets, old friends, music, the freedom to choose. Values beneath them, which carry political leanings and are pulling further apart: abortion, fairness, rule of law, fiscal responsibility, immigration, climate, guns, free markets and more. And reality, which 'doesn't care about political affiliation.' His short list of bipartisan observations: things aren't working for most people; the stock market no longer measures well-being; energy underpins the economy, with money and technology sitting on top of it; drilling holes and borrowing on the national credit card are unsustainable; renewables must be rebuilt; the world is turning multipolar; current lifestyles are unsustainable and may change abruptly; humans are animals with a human nature; we are crossing planetary boundaries beyond climate; once basic needs are met most of the best things in life are free; technology is part of the response, not the solution; and we face a predicament, not a problem. The best decisions ahead will be 'country over party.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/66-reality-party> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-20 for Frankly_66, recorded two days after the July 2024 attempt on Donald Trump's life. Nate distinguishes observing and describing reality, which is his work, from proposing the policies that might face it, which he hopes more people will coalesce around. He named his university course Reality 101 fourteen years earlier. Related: tgs:Concept.HumanPredicament, tgs:Concept.PredicamentNotProblem, tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries, tgs:Concept.Rebuildables. In Frankly_75 (October 2024) he returns to the stance as 'apolitical, nonpartisan,' with a four-part filter for judging people that sets left and right aside."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ConstructiveJournalism a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Constructive Journalism"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Solutions journalism"@en ;
    skos:definition "Journalism that reports problems together with context, responses and possible solutions, so that audiences come away informed and able to act rather than helpless. Maren Urner (TGS_174) argues that neutral reporting does not exist -- choosing what to report always changes the reader's brain -- and that a profit-driven competition for attention has pushed news toward fear and anger, which exploit the brain's survival bias toward bad news. Early research suggests people engage more deeply with constructive reporting and share it more."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.mediasupport.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/A-Handbook-for-Constructive-Journalism-IMS-and-CI-2022.pdf> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.MarenUrner ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-20 for TGS_174; the dcterms:source is the constructive journalism handbook the show notes link. Urner co-founded Perspective Daily (tgs:Organization.PerspectiveDaily) in 2016 as the first ad-free online magazine for it, after learning from David Bornstein's Solutions Journalism Network and New York Times Fixes column and from the Dutch De Correspondent (none built). Nate calls his own listener-funded podcast 'a sort of constructive journalism.' Related: tgs:Concept.AttentionEconomy (the incentive it pushes against), tgs:Concept.LearnedHelplessness (the harm it aims to avoid)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.AttentionEconomy a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Attention Economy"@en ;
    skos:definition "An economy in which human attention is the scarce resource that media and platforms compete for and sell, so content is shaped by what captures attention rather than by what informs. Maren Urner (TGS_174) calls attention 'more valuable than anything else on this planet' -- everyone gets the same 24 hours -- and says that in journalism the elephant in the room is money: more clicks mean more revenue, and negative, fear- and anger-driven content gets the most clicks."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.un.org/sites/un2.un.org/files/attention_economy_feb.pdf> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-20 for TGS_174; the dcterms:source is the UN policy brief the show notes link. Nate frames it as the superorganism having 'eaten media and journalism,' with profit as the main goal and good journalism secondary (tgs:Concept.Superorganism), and calls social media with AI 'the perfect monkey trap for a social primate like us.' Related: tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli, tgs:Concept.DopamineHijackedCulture, tgs:Concept.ConstructiveJournalism."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.LearnedHelplessness a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Learned Helplessness and Self-Efficacy"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Learned helplessness"@en, "Self-efficacy"@en ;
    skos:definition "Learned helplessness is the conviction that nothing one does will change anything; its psychological antidote is self-efficacy, the feeling that one's own action makes a difference, built by seeing even a small action work. Maren Urner (TGS_174) links it to news avoidance: in the Reuters Institute's Digital News Report people say they turn away from news because it is too negative, offers no solutions and leaves them unable to do anything. 'The worst thing you can do to people is make them feel that whatever they do, it doesn't matter. That makes people sick.'"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Learned_helplessness> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-20 for TGS_174. Standard psychology, not Urner's coinage: learned helplessness is associated with Martin Seligman and self-efficacy with Albert Bandura (neither built). Her examples of self-efficacy: hanging a picture, signing a petition, going to a demonstration, talking to others. Nate sees apathy and checking out as a real risk for the coming decade. Related: tgs:Concept.PersonalAgency, tgs:Concept.ConstructiveJournalism."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ConfirmationBias a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.BehavioralEconomicsAndCognition, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Confirmation Bias"@en ;
    skos:definition "The tendency to seek, notice and believe information that confirms what we already think, and to discount what contradicts it. Maren Urner (TGS_174) calls it 'the father or mother of all the biases' and stresses its social side: we trust information more when it comes from people we already trust, such as our own party."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.britannica.com/science/confirmation-bias> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-20 for TGS_174; the dcterms:source is the Britannica entry the show notes link. Urner's example: during the COVID pandemic UK politicians had medical staff, not party figures, announce new rules so that supporters of both parties would follow them. In the same passage Nate raises authority bias, now earned by confidence and charisma rather than credentials. Related: tgs:Concept.LossAversion."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.Rewilding a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rewilding"@en ;
    skos:definition "Restoring large territories to fully functioning ecosystems, not only protecting the land but bringing back the species that have gone missing, starting from the top predators down. Kristine Tompkins (TGS_170) describes the method: acquire or aggregate large tracts, donate them as national parks, then ask who is missing -- in Argentina's 2-million-acre Iberá wetlands, 27 species from jaguars and pumas to giant anteaters and marsh deer. Without top predators, prey such as capybaras explode and change grasslands and forests; when wolves returned to Yellowstone, elk were pushed back out of the forests and clogged rivers began to recover."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://rewilding.org/what-is-rewilding/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.KristineTompkins ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-20 for TGS_170; the dcterms:source is the rewilding page the show notes link. Tompkins Conservation (tgs:Organization.TompkinsConservation) and its partners have protected about 15 million acres in Chile and Argentina; reintroducing jaguars to northern Argentina required the world's first jaguar breeding center, and almost 40 now roam wild. Tompkins says the scale of the work has had to shift to whole continents. Related: tgs:Concept.DeepEcology, tgs:Concept.RightsOfNature."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.DeepEcology a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Deep Ecology"@en ;
    skos:definition "The philosophy, named by the Norwegian philosopher Arne Naess in the 1970s, whose central idea is that all life has intrinsic value, independent of its usefulness to humans. Kristine Tompkins (TGS_170) calls herself a deep ecologist: 'if you really believe it, then that influences the decisions you make about what your work is every day.' Nate answers that deep ecology brings deep responsibility, which we mostly abdicate."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://earth.org/deep-ecology-an-often-misunderstood-theory/> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-20 for TGS_170; the dcterms:source is the article the show notes link, with an open-air-philosophy page on Naess also linked (Naess is not built). Tompkins adds that in recent years she has extended intrinsic value back to humankind as well, after a conversation with Pope Francis. Related: tgs:Concept.Rewilding, tgs:Concept.RightsOfNature, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament; the Reality Roundtable tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_2_DeeperEcology."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EarnedHope a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Earned Hope"@en ;
    skos:definition "Kristine Tompkins's view (TGS_170) that hope must be earned by action. When people ask whether she has hope, she hears 'if you have hope, I'm going to relax a little bit'; but 'one doesn't deserve to have hope unless they're doing something,' and 'the heaviest shovel we raise is to ask someone else if they have hope.' Her alternative to the question 'what can I do?' is another question: what are you really good at? Do that, out your door, this afternoon."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/170-kristine-tompkins> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.KristineTompkins ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-20 for TGS_170. Nate agrees, noting that environmental films end with a formulaic note of hope, 'a social filter on what is acceptable to be said.' Tompkins pairs it with abdication: to abdicate dignified lives for the future is 'the most crushing, fatal lack of a decision you will ever make.' Related: tgs:Concept.RealityBasedHope (Nate's later framing, TGS_205), tgs:Concept.LearnedHelplessness (self-efficacy as the antidote), tgs:Concept.Effectivism."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CarryingCapacity a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Carrying capacity"@en ;
    skos:definition "The largest population, or load of human activity, an environment can support indefinitely without degrading its ability to support it; William Catton's 'maximum permanently supportable load'."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carrying_capacity>, <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/the-7-fundamental-drivers-of-overshoot> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.WilliamCatton ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 from review sheet 4 (Bill Baue's r3.0 deck traces the term from Plato to Catton). The graph used the phrase in several descriptions without a node: Nate defines overshoot as a population exceeding carrying capacity (Frankly_70) and describes stored energy inflating the biosphere's apparent carrying capacity (Frankly_68). Bioregional carrying capacity, in Baue's framing, combines the doughnut's thresholds with fair-share allocations (tgs:Concept.ContextBasedSustainability). Nate uses the term throughout the essay version of Frankly_68 ('The 7 Fundamental Drivers of Overshoot', Substack, 14 March 2025), where a footnote gives his own definition: the maximum population an ecosystem can support. He frames overshoot as growing beyond it, and money creation as untethered from it."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.PhantomCarryingCapacity a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:prefLabel "Phantom carrying capacity"@en ;
    skos:definition "William Catton's term for carrying capacity that exists only while a temporary resource lasts: the part of a population that could not be supported once that resource is gone. Fossil energy is the main example."@en ;
    dcterms:source tgs:Work.BioregionalCarryingCapacitiesPluriversalMRV ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.WilliamCatton ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 from review sheet 4. Nate does not use the phrase, but describes the idea in Frankly_68: ancient stored energy has inflated the biosphere's apparent carrying capacity. Related: tgs:Concept.CarryingCapacity, tgs:Concept.Overshoot, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse. The essay version of Frankly_68 (Substack, 14 March 2025) makes the same point without the term: the cheap fossil energy of the carbon pulse let humanity grow its population and mammalian biomass far beyond what the land alone supports, and as that energy depletes we will contract, perhaps back in line with the planet's carrying capacity."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ContextBasedSustainability a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Context-based sustainability"@en ;
    skos:definition "Measuring an organization's or place's impact against what the system can bear: an ecological or social threshold, and that actor's fair share (allocation) of it. A bioregional carrying capacity uses the bioregion as the reference community for the shares."@en ;
    dcterms:source tgs:Work.BioregionalCarryingCapacitiesPluriversalMRV ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Organization.R30 ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 from review sheet 4, rated possible. Developed by Mark McElroy (Social Footprints, 2008) and Bill Baue at r3.0 and UNRISD; Baue notes that almost all corporate sustainability reporting omits thresholds. The bridge between the Doughnut and bioregionalism: tgs:ConceptScheme.DevonDoughnut."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CollapseResponseContinuum a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Collapse Response Continuum"@en ;
    skos:definition "Six stances people and institutions take toward ecological and social collapse: ignorance, awareness, denial, avoidance (believing it can be averted by working harder within the same paradigms), acceptance, and resilience."@en ;
    dcterms:source tgs:Work.GiftOfTheTurtleIslandBioregionalCongress ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Organization.R30 ;
    skos:relatedMatch tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 from review sheet 4. Bill Baue and Ralph Thurm (r3.0). Nate generally says 'simplification' rather than 'collapse', so the link to tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification is skos:relatedMatch, not an equivalence."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CollapseResilience a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Collapse resilience"@en ;
    skos:definition "The last stance on the Collapse Response Continuum: accepting that collapses are under way or coming and building resilience to lessen them, adapt to what follows, and move away from the social patterns that drive collapse, such as competition for power ('de-patterning'), toward prosocial ones ('re-patterning')."@en ;
    dcterms:source tgs:Work.GiftOfTheTurtleIslandBioregionalCongress ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Organization.R30 ;
    skos:broader tgs:Concept.CollapseResponseContinuum ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 from review sheet 4. Baue reads the TIBC11 peacemaking (tgs:Initiative.TurtleIslandBioregionalCongress11) as collapse resilience at small scale. Related, as candidates: tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak, tgs:Concept.ThreeTimelinesOfResponse, tgs:Concept.ProsocialityUnderTheSuperorganism."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EliteOverproduction a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.Geopolitics, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Elite overproduction"@en ;
    skos:definition "Peter Turchin's term: a society producing more aspirants for elite positions than it can absorb, so that competition among them destabilizes it; in his historical data the most consistent driver of state breakdown."@en ;
    dcterms:source tgs:Interview.TGS_164_PeterTurchin ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.PeterTurchin ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 from review sheet 4. Already described in tgs:Human.PeterTurchin and tgs:Interview.TGS_164_PeterTurchin (including Turchin's essay on AI accelerating elite competition) but had no node. Cited in Bill Baue's TIBC11 post as the factor dooming hierarchical civilizations."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.GameTheoretic ;
    .

tgs:Concept.HonorableHarvest a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Honorable Harvest"@en ;
    skos:definition "A code of practical harvesting ethics that Robin Wall Kimmerer finds, in some version, in every Indigenous society she knows: ask permission and abide by the answer; never take the first, so you never take the last; never take more than half; take only what is given; use it respectfully; share what you take; be grateful; and reciprocate the gift. It follows from treating the plants and animals one must eat, as heterotrophs, as relatives and teachers rather than resources."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.yesmagazine.org/issue/good-health/2015/11/26/the-honorable-harvest-lessons-from-an-indigenous-tradition-of-giving-thanks> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RobinWallKimmerer ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_239. Nate had not heard of it and answers that our economic system is 'effectively running a dishonorable harvest at full speed'. Kimmerer insists such ethics 'don't come with a chromosome': they come from relationships with land and from learning from mistakes (taking too much, then a hungry winter), so anyone can learn them. Nate connects it to wisdom as restraint (tgs:Concept.RestraintByDesign, TGS_126) and to fossil energy as principal treated as interest, which removed the need for restraint."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SevenFiresProphecy a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Seven Fires prophecy"@en ;
    skos:definition "An Anishinaabe teaching, carried by the Potawatomi, that names seven eras of the people's history; its earlier fires foretold the missionaries, removal from homelands, loss of language and the taking of children. The seventh fire is a time when river water can no longer be drunk, the air is too thick to breathe and plant and animal relatives turn away. All peoples then stand at a fork between a burnt, dead-end path and a green one, and can reach the green path only by walking back along the ancestor path to pick up what was dropped: language, stories, land and more-than-human relatives. What is carried through 'the bottleneck of the great narrowing' lights an eighth fire that burns forever."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seven_fires_prophecy> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RobinWallKimmerer ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_239. Nate asked about it because Kimmerer had told him before recording that her people had prophecies about a great simplification. He reads it as a microcosm of a species-wide rite of passage and of Bill Plotkin's ecological adulthood (TGS_146), and admits to some shame that when he started the podcast Indigenous wisdom seemed to him an irrelevant distraction. The teaching that only what is picked up and carried makes it through the bottleneck closely parallels Nate's own aim of carrying things of value through the bottlenecks (tgs:Concept.ThingsOfValueThroughTheBottlenecks)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.AgeOfRemembering a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Age of Remembering"@en ;
    skos:definition "Robin Wall Kimmerer's name for the present moment at the fork in the road of the Seven Fires prophecy: after some 500 years of dismembering the community of life and Indigenous communities, a time both to remember how to live in right relationship with land and to 're-member' -- to become members again of the family of life, taking humans off the top of the pyramid of human exceptionalism and back 'into the arms of our relatives'."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://plantbabyplant.com/about/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RobinWallKimmerer ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_239. She prefers it to names like the Anthropocene or the sixth extinction as a way to navigate the crisis; Plant Baby Plant (tgs:Organization.PlantBabyPlant) calls itself the motive force of the Age of Remembering. Asked whether this remembering can happen at scale before a disruption, she answers 'I don't know', though she sees it rising at the grassroots and in Indigenous communities, not among leaders."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.GrammarOfAnimacy a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Grammar of animacy"@en ;
    skos:definition "Robin Wall Kimmerer's term for the way languages such as Potawatomi, which is verb-based, conjugate verbs as animate or inanimate: a truck passing takes an inanimate verb, a bird singing an animate one, and plants and squirrels the same respectful grammar as family members. English reserves that grammar for human beings and calls every other living being 'it', which places humans atop a pyramid and makes it hard to treat plants as teachers or elders."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://orionmagazine.org/article/speaking-of-nature/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RobinWallKimmerer ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_239. Nate notes that several guests, including Victoria Sweet the day before, have discussed English as a noun-based language, and that he only recently learned the idea came from tgs:Work.BraidingSweetgrass. Kimmerer adds that the 'Three Sisters' name is itself a Native kinship term."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.KinshipWorldview a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Kinship worldview"@en ;
    skos:definition "Seeing other beings as relatives within a web of kin rather than as property beneath humans: the self extends, through permeable boundaries, to the plants that feed us, the minerals in our bones and the forest we live in -- what Robin Wall Kimmerer calls 'the great green we' -- so that self-interest becomes the interest of the whole. Expressed in the phrase 'all my relations', and set against the pyramid of human exceptionalism."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://doi.org/10.1890/1051-0761(2000)010[1327:KEIPOT]2.0.CO;2> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RobinWallKimmerer ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_239. The dcterms:source is Enrique Salmón's 'Kincentric Ecology' (2000), linked in the show notes. Kimmerer offers it as one explanation for why biodiversity declines less on lands stewarded by Indigenous peoples; Nate asks whether lower population density might also explain it, and she agrees many factors contribute. Nate compares it to Thich Nhat Hanh's interbeing and to the Reality Roundtable's 'when does the apple become me' (RR_2). Related but distinct: tgs:Concept.KinshipJourney (Samantha Sweetwater's alternative to the hero's journey)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.HumanExceptionalism a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Human exceptionalism"@en ;
    skos:definition "The story that humans stand apart from and above the rest of life -- descended from Aristotle's ladder of nature, with humans just below the angels and plants at the bottom -- so that other beings are property, not kin. Robin Wall Kimmerer traces economic extraction, industrialization and colonization to it, and calls it 'laughable' in the Indigenous communities she knows, where humans are 'the younger brothers of creation' who should learn from their more-than-human relatives."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthropocentrism>, tgs:Work.TheArrogantApe ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RobinWallKimmerer, tgs:Persona.ChristineWebb ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_239. Christine Webb's TGS_213 and her book The Arrogant Ape: The Myth of Human Exceptionalism (tgs:Work.TheArrogantApe) argue the same from primatology, which had no concept node until now; TGS_213 now discusses it. Kimmerer frames the polycrisis -- biodiversity loss, climate injustice -- as rooted in this story of what it means to be human."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.GiftEconomy a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "Gift economy"@en ;
    skos:definition "An economy in which whatever one has in abundance is given away, so that the whole web flourishes: plants, running on the free commons of sunlight, pass their berries, leaves, blossoms and roots on to pollinators, birds, foxes and soil instead of hoarding them, and abundance comes from that unblocked flow. Robin Wall Kimmerer contrasts it with a market economy that encloses the commons, privatizes and hoards, manufactures scarcity and degrades community."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://emergencemagazine.org/essay/the-serviceberry/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RobinWallKimmerer ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_239. From tgs:Work.TheServiceberry, which began when Emergence Magazine asked a botanist to write about economics. Nate's clarification: nature extracts too, but gives back, while humans extract and return only pollution -- 'that's where the break is'. Kimmerer's ask is to 'poke at' the market economy by building reciprocity, and she says people have forgotten that humans can be medicine for the land."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.Enoughness a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Enoughness"@en ;
    skos:definition "Robin Wall Kimmerer's sense of abundance: not material plenty but enough -- sufficiency for oneself and one's relatives, human and more-than-human -- with wealth measured by how much one has to give away, because 'all flourishing is mutual'. It accepts real physical scarcity ahead: having depleted the planet's abundance, we can ask less of her, and may have all we need though not all we want."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://emergencemagazine.org/conversation/practical-reverence/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RobinWallKimmerer ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_239. Nate says that as a biophysical economist he has an aversion to the word abundance, expecting less material abundance on average; Kimmerer agrees and redefines it. A practical counterpart already in the graph: tgs:PersonalIntervention.WriteDownYourEnough."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.RestorationAndRestoryation a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Restoration and re-story-ation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Robin Wall Kimmerer's pairing: restoring land's structure and function with the tools of science and engineering is doable, but Sisyphean unless the broken relationship between people and land is restored too, and that needs new stories to replace those of human exceptionalism and extractive capitalism -- stories, like the Honorable Harvest, that were deliberately hidden and are rarely taught. Plant Baby Plant puts it into practice: science to restore land, the arts to restore relationship."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://atmos.earth/podcast/robin-wall-kimmerer-the-land-loves-you-back/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RobinWallKimmerer ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_239. Her landmark example of restoration at scale is tallgrass prairie, brought back on schoolyards, former army munitions sites and abandoned airports. Related practice terms: tgs:PracticeTerm.ArtAndStorytelling, tgs:PracticeTerm.StoryingPlace."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SuccessAsLongTermPersistence a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Success as long-term persistence"@en ;
    skos:definition "Robin Wall Kimmerer's lesson from mosses: among the first plants on land and little changed since, while more than 99% of all species that ever lived are extinct, mosses mark a kind of success measured by persistence and biodiversity rather than size or energy throughput. They succeed through 'the power of being small' -- cooperating rather than competing, and living within the boundary layer, working within the laws of physics rather than trying to conquer them."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://emergencemagazine.org/essay/ancient-green/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RobinWallKimmerer ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_239. Nate links it to Olivier Hamant's robustness over performance (tgs:Concept.RobustnessVsPerformance, TGS_230) and asks whether mosses simply have a wider niche. Kimmerer closes the episode with the same lesson for humans: be small and cooperative for 'enduring success, not flash-in-the-pan success'. She also notes that mosses in boreal and tundra ecosystems hold more carbon than the forests, much of it as peat."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SevenGenerationsThinking a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Seven generations thinking"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Indigenous practice, a hallmark of Haudenosaunee and other governance, of weighing decisions by their effect on future generations, contrasted with the quarterly profits report. Robin Wall Kimmerer's reading is not a mystical forecast seven generations ahead but care for the seven generations one is connected to: from one's great-grandparents to one's great-grandchildren, 'the ones whose faces are still coming toward us'."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seven_generation_sustainability> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RobinWallKimmerer ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_239. Raised when Nate asks about Indigenous governance; Kimmerer declines to generalize across the 574 Indigenous nations within the United States (575 as of January 2026, per the show notes) but notes that women's leadership is often at the forefront. Nate asks how it pairs with climate models seven generations out; she points to innovative Indigenous work on climate resilience."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EnergySymbiosis a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Energy symbiosis"@en ;
    skos:definition "Jean-Baptiste Fressoz's thesis that energy sources and materials do not succeed one another but grow together, each depending on the others: coal mining consumed ever more timber for pit props (Britain used more wood for pit props around 1900 than it burned as firewood in the 18th century); oil depends on coal through steel (pipes, engines) and on cement (roads); a 1930s Ford took seven tons of coal to build, and a Chinese car still takes two to three; and in China, much new solar and wind capacity has been built together with coal plants to share grid connections. Wood use has doubled since the 1960s and wood energy is now about twice nuclear energy."@en ;
    dcterms:source tgs:Work.MoreAndMoreAndMore ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.JeanBaptisteFressoz ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_162. Nate's own framing is energy additions (tgs:Concept.EnergyAdditionsNotTransitions); Fressoz answers 'it's more than an addition, it's a symbiosis'. It extends to all raw materials: very few have ever declined in absolute use (asbestos, by prohibition, and sheep's wool are the exceptions he finds), and car headlights alone now burn more oil than the whole economy of 1900 used for kerosene lighting. Related: tgs:Concept.Superorganism, Olivia Lazard's rematerialization (TGS_58)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EnergyTransitionNarrative a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "The energy transition narrative"@en ;
    skos:definition "Jean-Baptiste Fressoz's history of the idea of an 'energy transition': a phrase borrowed from nuclear physics (an electron's change of state) and taken up by atomic scientists in the 1950s and 1960s, who imagined fossil fuels as a brief episode before breeder reactors -- the timescale of M. King Hubbert's famous graph, which Fressoz identifies as the origin of the carbon-pulse picture. Experts long knew energies added up and did not speak of transitions until the 1970s. For Fressoz the phrase is 'a slogan, not a serious scientific word': what solar panels and electric motors actually do is lower the carbon intensity of the economy, continuing an old history of technological progress, and Schumpeter's creative destruction, carried into climate economics, does not describe materials."@en ;
    dcterms:source tgs:Work.MoreAndMoreAndMore ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.JeanBaptisteFressoz ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_162. Fressoz notes that the US Atomic Energy Commission raised climate change as early as 1953, and that the industrial elites who took the 1970s energy crisis seriously (Jimmy Carter's 1977 address; European Commission vice-president Sicco Mansholt after reading The Limits to Growth) have not taken climate change or biodiversity loss as seriously. His examples of the narrative in politics: John Kerry calling the energy transition a new industrial revolution. Related: tgs:Concept.EnergyAdditionsNotTransitions, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CarbonAllocationByUse a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:prefLabel "Allocating carbon by use"@en ;
    skos:definition "Jean-Baptiste Fressoz's argument that carbon should not be demonized but allocated: with little CO2 left to emit, the question should be where emissions do the most good -- cement for a needed bridge or a water network in a poor city rather than another Manhattan skyscraper -- whereas markets put it where the most money can be made. He faults climate economics for promising the same world decarbonized by green innovation instead of discussing the quantity and distribution of what is produced."@en ;
    dcterms:source tgs:Work.MoreAndMoreAndMore ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.JeanBaptisteFressoz ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_162. Fressoz links the absence of this discussion to capitalism; Nate answers that the deeper driver is the maximum power principle (tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple), and Fressoz agrees the problem is deeper than capitalism. Fressoz supports putting degrowth at the center of economics (tgs:SchoolOfThought.DegrowthMovement) without claiming it solves the problem. Compare the allocation step of tgs:Concept.ContextBasedSustainability."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.NaturalDeclineRate a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:prefLabel "Natural decline rate"@en ;
    skos:definition "The rate at which output from existing oil and gas wells falls once they are producing, because extraction releases the reservoir pressure that drives fluids to the surface: about 10 to 15% a year worldwide, so that without continual new drilling and investment, production would fall by around 90% within 20 to 30 years. Shale wells decline much faster than conventional ones -- peaking and falling within two or three years, with a long tail -- so shale output needs ever more wells."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://corporate.exxonmobil.com/-/media/global/files/global-outlook/2024/global-outlook-executive-summary.pdf> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.ScottTinker ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_161. Nate cites ExxonMobil's 2024 Global Outlook, whose supply chart implies about a 15% annual decline from existing fields; Tinker explains the physics (pressure release, porosity and permeability; shale nanopores, 200 across a human hair) and says production must be replaced every year, which he calls neither 'it's over' nor 'unlimited'. Related: tgs:Concept.PeakOil."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ResourcesVersusReserves a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:prefLabel "Resources versus reserves"@en ;
    skos:definition "The distinction between a resource -- the total oil or gas in the ground, which geologists estimate reasonably well -- and reserves, the part that can be produced with today's technology at today's price. Because technology improves and prices move, reserves rise and fall ('reserve growth'), so a static count of reserves understates future supply, while the resource sets the ultimate bound."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.norskpetroleum.no/en/petroleum-resources/resource-classification/> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.ScottTinker ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_161. Scott Tinker's framing for why peak oil keeps being pushed back: technology, pulled by demand, turns resource into reserves (US shale has yielded perhaps 5 to 10% of the oil in its source rocks so far, and the source rocks of the Middle East and Russia are untapped). Nate's reply: there is a huge resource (even oil shale, with 'the energy density of a baked potato'), but the prices needed to develop it would break today's cultural and financial arrangements, which is why he measures energy costs in energy terms (tgs:Work.TowardsAnAppliedNetEnergyFramework, tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EnergyPoverty a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Energy poverty"@en ;
    skos:definition "Lacking affordable, reliable energy consistently -- distinct from having no energy at all. Scott Tinker estimates that about 1 billion people have no energy access, while some 4 to 5 billion more have insecure access that comes and goes; in about a quarter of the world's countries, average energy use per person is less than his refrigerator's. Energy access correlates tightly with economic well-being."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://ourworldindata.org/energy-access> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.ScottTinker ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_161. Tinker's argument: energy underpins healthy economies, and it is healthy economies that invest in clean land, air and water, so energy poverty and environmental protection must be solved together. Nate agrees ethically that billions should rise but doubts systemically that they will, and expects average energy use per person to fall. The Switch Energy Alliance's student competition, which studies energy poverty, saw Nigerian teams miss deadlines because of seven-hour power cuts."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.DenseToMoreDenseTransition a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:prefLabel "From dense to more dense"@en ;
    skos:definition "Scott Tinker's thesis that the real energy transition runs from less dense to more dense fuels -- hay and wood to coal, oil, methane, hydrogen and uranium -- by energy per unit weight, so a single uranium fuel pellet holds the energy to drive a car from New York to Los Angeles and back to Dallas. By surface power density (watts per square meter), solar and wind are near the bottom, needing far more materials to collect a unit of energy; moving to them is 'going from dense to less dense, backwards in time of physics'."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://drexel.edu/~/media/Files/greatworks/pdf_sum10/WK8_Layton_EnergyDensities.ashx> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.ScottTinker ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_161. A view Nate partly shares and partly contests: he agrees we would go from dense to more dense if geopolitics and finance hold together, but expects average energy use per person to fall, and doubts nuclear can scale because of cost. Tinker adds that solar and wind collection systems are not renewable, since they are mined and wear out in 10 to 15 years, and that an electric car uses far more metal than a combustion car. Contrast tgs:Concept.EnergyAdditionsNotTransitions and tgs:Concept.EnergySymbiosis."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.RadicalMiddle a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:prefLabel "Radical middle"@en ;
    skos:definition "Scott Tinker's term for the position he urges on young people and experts: rejecting binary framings -- natural gas against solar and wind, coal against nuclear, 'climate change is real, so the answer is solar, wind and batteries' -- in favor of trade-offs, compromises and common ground, argued in a way that is 'not just completely factual, but factually complete' about the pros and cons of every source."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://switchon.org> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.ScottTinker ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_161. In his closing advice he asks listeners to speak in classrooms, scout troops and church groups, and young people to 'join that radical middle' despite fear of online attack. He frames youth climate fear as imposed by powerful voices and wrong; Nate attributes much youth distress to social media, with climate a part."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.Polycrisis a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    skos:prefLabel "Polycrisis"@en ;
    skos:definition "A situation in which crises in several global systems -- climate, food, energy, finance, health, geopolitics -- become causally entangled, so that each amplifies the others and their combined harm is greater than the sum of the separate crises. Polycrisis analysis maps those systems and the links between them, to treat many challenges as one connected challenge rather than as isolated problems."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://cascadeinstitute.org> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Organization.CascadeInstitute, tgs:Persona.BirgitteBischoff ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_160. Developed by the Cascade Institute (tgs:Work.IntroductionToPolycrisisAnalysis). Birgitte Bischoff describes how COVID showed the IFRC that supply-chain, financial and mental-health crises had to be seen 'as one challenge', and how the IFRC Europe region began a polycrisis 'think and do tank'. Distinct from tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, which concerns the shared root causes beneath the crises; see also tgs:Concept.CoupledSystemicRisks and tgs:Work.GlobalPolycrisisCausalMechanisms."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.BundledPreparedness a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Proactive about being proactive (bundled preparedness)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Birgitte Bischoff's approach to preparing for an uncertain, crisis-prone future: rather than forecasting specific numbers or planning for each hazard separately ('11 different scenarios'), an organization prepares for interconnected crises in bundles -- spotting signals, mapping systemic risks, exploring several cross-sector scenarios through strategic foresight, and readying local people and tools that can switch to whatever comes ('if we can't fly, we roll in a car')."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.oecd.org/en/about/programmes/strategic-foresight.html> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.BirgitteBischoff ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_160. 'Proactive about being proactive' is Nate's recollection of her phrase from their first conversation. She doubts long-range migration forecasts are meaningful -- 'whatever we plan for, something else is happening' -- yet argues bundled preparation is also less resource-hungry. For individuals it means taking responsibility locally: knowing your community and neighbors, preparing for the crises you know will hit your area, welcoming newcomers. Related: tgs:Concept.ScenarioThinking, tgs:Concept.Polycrisis."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.HumanitarianNeutralityAndAccess a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.Geopolitics, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Humanitarian neutrality and access"@en ;
    skos:definition "The standing of the Red Cross and Red Crescent: neither an NGO nor part of the UN, each national society works in its country as an independent auxiliary to the government under the Movement's principles of neutrality and impartiality. That standing gives it access to both sides of a conflict and a trusted, post-partisan brand that outlasts elections -- an asset built over decades that can be lost through a single wrongdoing, or by being misquoted or used in a political campaign."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.icrc.org/sites/default/files/topic/file_plus_list/4046-the_fundamental_principles_of_the_international_red_cross_and_red_crescent_movement.pdf> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.BirgitteBischoff ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_160. Nate frames the Red Cross's unique assets as knowledge, network, brand and trust, and speed ('from crisis to response there's like one or two steps'). Bischoff adds that the IFRC also uses its reputation to advocate for rights-based, people-centred policy, including migration policy aligned with humanitarian principles. Source of the principles: tgs:Work.FundamentalPrinciplesOfTheRedCross."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.SelfActivatingVolunteerNetwork a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Self-activating volunteer network"@en ;
    skos:definition "A standing network of local volunteers, trained and prepared in calm times, that mobilizes itself when a crisis hits instead of waiting for a headquarters or government to act. Volunteering rises in crises -- during COVID more people volunteered than Red Cross societies could absorb -- so an existing, trusted, locally present structure gives pro-social impulses somewhere to go."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.ifrc.org/our-work/health-and-care/community-health/communicable-diseases/covid-19> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.BirgitteBischoff ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_160. Birgitte Bischoff's example: as people fled Ukraine, volunteers in Ukraine, Poland, Romania and Moldova handed out tea, biscuits and diapers within days -- 'the power of having a network that can self activate.' Local volunteers are always the first responders. For her, volunteering is 'taking responsibility at local level'; 'you don't need to be... a humanitarian to be a human.' Related: tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EnvironmentAsDevelopment a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    skos:prefLabel "Environment as development (the politics of inclusion)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Sunita Narain's position, which she sees as distinguishing the Indian environmental movement from Western environmentalism: environment is not a separate concern beyond development but the question of how to make development sustainable, and development cannot be sustainable unless it is affordable -- that is, inclusive of the majority. Hence 'you cannot neuter politics from environment': the right to sanitation is the right to environment, and clean air in Delhi depends on the right to walk, cycle and take the bus."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.downtoearth.org.in/climate-change/we-need-politics-of-inclusion> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.SunitaNarain ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_159. Her examples: a sewerage network only a few can afford leaves most excreta untreated, so India's rivers cannot be cleaned; in Delhi only about 20% drive, yet cars take most of the road space and the airshed. She challenges the idea that wealthy countries have solved pollution: they cleaned local air by exporting the harm as climate change. Related: tgs:Concept.LeapfroggingDevelopment, tgs:Concept.CarbonBudgetInequity."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional ;
    .

tgs:Concept.LeapfroggingDevelopment a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:prefLabel "Leapfrogging (business unusual)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Skipping the capital- and resource-intensive infrastructure that rich countries built when they had more money and less pressure, in favour of decentralized systems that can serve everyone -- as India skipped landlines for mobile phones. Sunita Narain applies it to sanitation (non-sewered systems that collect septic-tank waste by tanker, treat it and reuse it on land), water (harvesting every drop of rain where it falls, recharging groundwater, rebuilding lakes and tanks) and energy: 'business unusual'."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.cseindia.org/waste-to-worth-managing-india-s-urban-water-crisis-through-wastewater-reuse-12527> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.SunitaNarain ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_159. She notes that the Government of India has made non-sewered sanitation part of its policy and now talks about circularity 'not because it's a buzzword' but because India lacks the luxury of 'wasting land for waste'. India will do things differently 'not to save the world, but to save ourselves'. Nate connects it to the Paani Foundation's water and permaculture work (tgs:Interview.TGS_64_AndrewMillison); she cites natural farming at scale in Andhra Pradesh, adopted because it cuts farmers' costs. Related: tgs:Concept.EnvironmentAsDevelopment."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.CarbonBudgetInequity a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    skos:prefLabel "Carbon budget inequity"@en ;
    skos:definition "The unequal division of the world's remaining carbon budget -- the finite amount of carbon dioxide that can still be emitted into one shared atmosphere while holding warming below a given level. Sunita Narain's figures: at current rates the 1.5 degree budget will be exhausted by the end of this decade, by which time 30% of the world's population, including the United States and China, will have appropriated 70% of it, leaving 70% of people with access to 30%. 'We exist. You can't wish us away': the majority will still develop and emit, so finance for developing differently is the core of climate justice."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://oxfamilibrary.openrepository.com/bitstream/handle/10546/621305/bn-carbon-inequality-2030-051121-en.pdf> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.SunitaNarain ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_159. She ties it to COP29 in Baku and its 'means of implementation' (climate finance). The equity argument is long associated with CSE: Anil Agarwal and Sunita Narain's 'Global Warming in an Unequal World' (1991) argued for equal per-capita rights to the atmosphere. Climate change, she says, is 'nature's way of telling us we live in one planet'. Related: tgs:Concept.HavesAndHaveNots, tgs:Concept.EnvironmentAsDevelopment."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Institutional, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.Biophysical ;
    .

tgs:Concept.DutyToHope a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Duty to hope"@en ;
    skos:definition "Sunita Narain's injunction, repeated through TGS_159, that those who work on environment and development have an obligation to create hope -- to show people that there is a way ahead and that it is better than today -- grounded not in a magic wand but in persistence, perseverance and tenacity, learned above all from the world's poor, who keep coping, surviving and working through every extreme weather event."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.cseindia.org/page/sunita-narain> ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.SunitaNarain ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-21 for TGS_159. She counts her generation's biggest failure as 'not being able to capture the imagination or the policy space' with a better way ahead. Asked what young people should understand, she answers 'the despair of the poor' and the tenacity of the poor woman who cannot afford fuelwood but is 'still smiling'. Related: tgs:Concept.RealityBasedHope, tgs:Concept.EarnedHope."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal ;
    .

tgs:Concept.FiveThingsThatKeepUsWhole a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:inScheme tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    skos:topConceptOf tgs:ConceptScheme.TGSConcepts ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Five Things That Keep Us Whole"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Nate's five things"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's personal compass (Frankly_163) for staying grounded and authentic, and able to contribute, in turbulent times: (1) self-care -- sleep, exercise and nutrition, because burning out in this work undermines it; (2) slack with routine -- unstructured time to process and reflect without stimulation, held in daily routines; (3) daily time in nature, normal stimuli in a life full of supernormal ones; (4) deep friendship with people who see the unfolding tragedy, with whom he can be himself; and (5) meaningful work that tries to make the future better than the default. Together they let him be 'not my best self, but more my true self'."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Frankly-163-Transcript-The-5-Most-Important-Things.docx.pdf> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Minted 2026-10-23 for Frankly_163. Nate stresses these are his five, learned through 'reflection, practice, observation, failure, pattern recognition', not a universal formula, and that each listener's will differ; he asks what 'little Nate on your shoulder' knows. He pairs fun, joy and relaxation with deep meaningful work 'in a barbell way', and calls deep friendship his 'true north, burnout compass'. Related: tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli (nature as normal stimuli), tgs:Concept.AllostaticLoad and tgs:Concept.DefaultModeNetwork (why slack matters), tgs:Concept.RobustnessVsPerformance (Olivier Hamant on slack), tgs:Concept.FourCategoriesOfRealWork (the meaningful work), tgs:Concept.PersonalAgency. It is the personal layer beneath tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction, whose foundation is inner work."@en ;
    thinkr:hasAnalyticalLens thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PracticalPersonal, thinkr:AnalyticalLensType.PsychologicalInner ;
    .

